/**
 * Teach Me explanations for government, written in advance so they load
 * instantly, work offline and cost nothing per student. Keyed by
 * passageId|question|options (see teachMeKey in app.js). A question with no
 * entry here falls back to the live AI explanation.
 */
(window.TEACH_ME = window.TEACH_ME || {})["government"] = {
 "|The doctrine of separation of powers was propounded by:|Karl Marx|Montesquieu|Jean-Jacques Rousseau|John Locke": "Separation of powers means the three functions of government (making laws, carrying them out, and interpreting them) are given to three different organs: the legislature, the executive and the judiciary. The French writer Baron de Montesquieu set it out fully in \"The Spirit of the Laws\" (1748), warning that liberty is lost when one person or body holds all three powers: B.\nThe trap: D, John Locke, wrote earlier about separating legislative and executive power, but the full doctrine with an independent judiciary is Montesquieu's.\nMemory tip: \"Montesquieu – Spirit of the Laws – 1748 – Separation.\"",
 "|In a parliamentary system, the executive is accountable to:|The president|The military|The legislature|The judiciary": "In a parliamentary system the executive (the Prime Minister and cabinet) is drawn from the legislature, needs its majority, and stays in office only while it keeps the legislature's confidence. A vote of no confidence can remove it. So the executive is accountable to the legislature: C.\nA is wrong because in a parliamentary system any president is only a ceremonial head; the executive (Prime Minister and cabinet) answers to the legislature.\nMemory tip: Parliamentary = the cabinet sits in Parliament and answers to Parliament.",
 "|Which of the following is NOT a function of a constitution?|It defines the structure of government|It guarantees fundamental human rights|It provides for the election of all citizens|It defines the relationship between the citizen and the state": "A constitution sets up the organs of government, shares power among them, guarantees citizens' rights and defines how the citizen relates to the state. It does not \"provide for the election of all citizens\": citizens elect representatives; they are not themselves all elected. So C is NOT a function: C.\nSome students think rights belong only in separate laws, but guaranteeing fundamental rights is a core function (Chapter IV of Nigeria's 1999 Constitution).\nMemory tip: a constitution answers three questions: who governs, with what powers, and what rights the citizens keep.",
 "|The ECOWAS was founded in 1975 in:|Abuja|Accra|Lagos|Dakar": "The Economic Community of West African States was created when 15 West African states signed the Treaty of Lagos on 28 May 1975. Generals Yakubu Gowon of Nigeria and Gnassingbé Eyadéma of Togo were its chief promoters. So it was founded in Lagos: C.\nThe trap: A, Abuja, is ECOWAS's headquarters today, but it was not yet built in 1975; the treaty was signed in Lagos.\nMemory tip: born in Lagos (1975), lives in Abuja.",
 "|Proportional representation is a system of representation in which:|Seats are given to the party that wins the most votes in a constituency|Seats in the legislature are distributed in proportion to votes won|Only rural areas are represented|Military appointments fill seats": "Proportional representation (PR) gives each party a share of seats roughly equal to its share of the votes. A party with 30% of the votes gets about 30% of the seats. Common forms are the party list system and the single transferable vote. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap: A, the most votes in a constituency, is the simple-majority (first-past-the-post) system, not proportional representation.\nMemory tip: Proportional = seats in Proportion to votes.",
 "|A constitutional monarch is a ruler who:|Rules with absolute power|Rules subject to the provisions of a constitution|Is elected by the people|Is appointed by the military": "A constitutional monarch is a hereditary head of state whose powers are limited by a constitution or by conventions. He \"reigns but does not rule\": real power lies with elected ministers. Britain's King Charles III is an example. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap: A, absolute power, sounds like what a monarch has, but it describes an absolute monarch; a constitutional monarch's powers are limited.\nMemory tip: Absolute monarch = no limits; Constitutional monarch = Constitution limits.",
 "|The concept of 'Rule of Law' was developed by:|Montesquieu|Karl Marx|A.V. Dicey|Thomas Hobbes": "The British jurist A.V. Dicey popularised the rule of law in \"Introduction to the Study of the Law of the Constitution\" (1885). His three pillars are: supremacy of the law (no arbitrary power), equality before the law, and rights protected by the ordinary courts. So C is correct: C.\nThe trap: A, Montesquieu, is linked with separation of powers, a different doctrine; the rule of law is associated with A.V. Dicey.\nMemory tip: \"Dicey – 1885 – rule of law\"; \"Montesquieu – 1748 – separation of powers.\"",
 "|Which of the following is a merit of federalism?|It is too expensive to run|It prevents unity|It allows for the accommodation of diverse groups|It promotes instability": "Federalism divides power between a central government and regional units (in Nigeria, states) by a written constitution. Its great merit is that it lets peoples with different languages, religions and cultures live in one country while keeping some control over their own affairs. That is why Nigeria, the USA and India use it. So C is correct: C.\nOptions A, B and D are not merits. \"Too expensive to run\" is in fact a real criticism of federalism, so it cannot be its merit.\nMemory tip: Federalism = \"unity in diversity.\"",
 "|The United Nations Security Council has _____ permanent members.|3|5|7|10": "The UN Security Council has 15 members: 5 permanent members with the veto power and 10 non-permanent members elected by the General Assembly for two-year terms. The permanent five are the USA, the UK, France, Russia and China, the main victors of the Second World War. So the answer is 5: B.\nThe trap: D, 10, is the number of non-permanent members; the Council has 15 seats, five of them permanent.\nMemory tip: \"5 permanent + 10 elected = 15\"; remember the P5 by the order U-U-F-R-C (USA, UK, France, Russia, China).",
 "|The system of government in which the state controls all aspects of life is:|Liberal democracy|Totalitarianism|Federalism|Confederalism": "Totalitarianism is a system in which the state, usually under one party or leader, tries to control every part of life: the economy, the press, education, religion and even private life. Hitler's Germany and Stalin's Soviet Union are the usual examples. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap is to choose federalism or confederalism. These describe how power is divided between the centre and regions, not how much the state controls its people.\nMemory tip: Totalitarian = Total control.",
 "|Which of the following is an example of a pressure group in Nigeria?|INEC|NLC (Nigeria Labour Congress)|NNPC|CBN": "A pressure group is an organised group that tries to influence government policy without trying to take control of government. The Nigeria Labour Congress (NLC), formed in 1978, presses government on wages and workers' welfare through lobbying, protests and strikes. So B is correct: B.\nThe traps are government bodies. INEC is the electoral commission, NNPC is the national oil company and CBN is the central bank. All three are part of the state, so they cannot be pressure groups outside it.\nMemory tip: Pressure groups press on the government from outside: NLC, NBA, NUT, ASUU, NANS.",
 "|The process of removing a president through a constitutional procedure is called:|A coup|A referendum|Impeachment|A vote of no confidence": "Impeachment is the constitutional process by which the legislature charges and removes a President (or Governor) for gross misconduct. In Nigeria, section 143 of the 1999 Constitution sets out the steps: a notice signed by one-third of the National Assembly members, an investigating panel, and removal by a two-thirds majority of each House. So C is correct: C.\nThe trap: D, a vote of no confidence, does remove leaders, but it removes a Prime Minister in a parliamentary system; presidents are impeached.\nMemory tip: Presidents are Impeached; Prime Ministers lose a vote of no confidence.",
 "|Nigeria's current federal structure has _____ states.|30|36|38|40": "Nigeria has 36 states plus the Federal Capital Territory, Abuja. The number grew in stages: 3 regions (1954), 4 regions (Mid-West, 1963), 12 states (1967), 19 (1976), 21 (1987), 30 (1991) and 36 (1996, under Abacha). So B is correct: B.\nThe trap: A, 30, was the number of states after 1991; Abacha's 1996 creation raised it to 36, plus the FCT, which is not a state.\nMemory tip: 3-4-12-19-21-30-36, the ladder of state creation.",
 "|What is the main feature of a unitary system of government?|Power is divided between a central and regional governments|All governmental powers are concentrated at the centre|The military controls government|Each region is fully independent": "In a unitary system all governmental power is held by one central government. Any local or regional units exist only because the centre created them, and the centre can change or abolish them. Britain, France and Ghana are examples. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap: A, dividing power between central and regional governments, describes federalism, the opposite of a unitary system.\nMemory tip: Unitary = Unit = one centre of power.",
 "|The head of the Commonwealth of Nations is:|The British Prime Minister|The UN Secretary-General|The British Monarch|The Commonwealth Secretary-General": "The Commonwealth of Nations is a voluntary association of 56 countries, most of them former British territories. Its symbolic head is the British monarch, now King Charles III (since September 2022), whom member leaders accepted as head in 2018. So C is correct: C.\nThe trap: D, the Commonwealth Secretary-General, runs the Secretariat but is its chief administrator, not its head.\nMemory tip: Head = the Monarch; Manager = the Secretary-General.",
 "|Which of the following political philosophies advocates for the abolition of private property and state?|Capitalism|Fascism|Communism|Conservatism": "Communism, as set out by Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels in the \"Communist Manifesto\" (1848), seeks to abolish private ownership of the means of production. After a stage of socialism and the \"dictatorship of the proletariat\", the state is expected to \"wither away\", leaving a classless, stateless society. So C is correct: C.\nFascism (B) glorifies the state and keeps private property, so it seeks to abolish neither.\nMemory tip: Communism ends with \"no class, no state, no private property.\"",
 "|The legislative, executive and judicial powers in Nigeria are exercised by the:|Senate, President and Supreme Court|National Assembly, Federal Executive Council and the Judiciary|President, Vice-President and Ministers|INEC, NJC and National Assembly": "The 1999 Constitution vests legislative power in the National Assembly (s.4) and judicial power in the courts (s.6). Executive power is vested in the President (s.5), who exercises it directly or through the Vice-President and Ministers, who together form the Federal Executive Council. Of the options, B is the one that names the three organs: B.\nThe trap: A looks right, but the Senate is only one chamber of the legislature and the Supreme Court only one court.\nMemory tip: sections 4-5-6 = legislature-executive-judiciary.",
 "|A referendum is:|A military overthrow of government|A direct vote by the electorate on a specific issue|The appointment of a head of state|A type of election for local government": "A referendum is a direct vote in which the electorate decides a particular question, such as approving a new constitution or a change of territory, instead of electing representatives. For example, the 1963 vote in the Mid-West created the Mid-Western Region. So B is correct: B.\nA local government election chooses people; a referendum decides an issue. A plebiscite is a similar popular vote, often on a territorial question (for example, the 1961 Cameroons plebiscites).\nMemory tip: Referendum = the people \"refer\" the question to themselves.",
 "|Which of the following is a function of INEC in Nigeria?|Adjudicating on electoral disputes|Conducting elections|Making election-related laws|Registering political parties only": "The Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC), set up in 1998 and named in section 153 of the 1999 Constitution, conducts elections. It also registers voters and political parties, monitors party finances and fixes constituencies. So B is correct: B.\nThe traps: deciding electoral disputes is the work of election tribunals and courts (A); making election laws is the National Assembly's job (C); and D is wrong because of \"only\", since INEC does far more than register parties. The trap: A, adjudicating electoral disputes, sounds like election work, but it belongs to election tribunals and courts, not INEC.\nMemory tip: INEC runs elections; tribunals judge them; the National Assembly makes the rules.",
 "|Bicameralism in Nigeria means the National Assembly consists of:|The House of Representatives and the State Houses of Assembly|The Senate and the House of Representatives|The President and the Senate|The Governor and State Assembly": "Bicameral means \"two chambers.\" Nigeria's National Assembly has the Senate (109 members: three per state plus one for the FCT) and the House of Representatives (360 members). So B is correct: B.\nThe trap: A includes the State Houses of Assembly, but they are separate state legislatures; the National Assembly is the Senate plus the House of Representatives.\nMemory tip: \"Bi\" = two: Senate + House of Reps = 109 + 360 = 469 lawmakers.",
 "|The theory of the social contract argues that:|Might is always right|Citizens obey only when forced to|Government derives its legitimacy from the consent of the governed|Kings rule by divine right": "Social contract theorists, namely Thomas Hobbes (\"Leviathan\", 1651), John Locke (\"Two Treatises\", 1689) and Jean-Jacques Rousseau (\"The Social Contract\", 1762), argued that people left the \"state of nature\" by agreeing to form a government. Government's authority therefore rests on the consent of the governed: C.\nDivine right (D) credits God as the source of a ruler's authority, not the consent of the governed.\nMemory tip: Contract = agreement = consent.",
 "|Which of the following distinguishes public administration from political science?|Public administration deals with implementation of policy; political science focuses on policy formulation and political theory|They are the same subject|Public administration deals with military affairs|Political science is concerned only with elections": "Political science studies power, the state, political institutions, ideas and behaviour, including how policies are made. Public administration is the branch that deals with carrying out government policy through the civil service and public agencies. So A gives the correct distinction: A.\nThe two subjects are related (public administration grew out of political science), but they are not the same. Options C and D shrink each subject to one narrow topic.\nMemory tip: Political science asks \"what should government do?\"; public administration asks \"how is it done?\"",
 "|What is gerrymandering?|Electoral malpractice of ballot-box snatching|Manipulation of constituency boundaries to favour a particular party|Using military force in elections|Rigging through multiple voting": "Gerrymandering means drawing constituency boundaries in odd shapes to help one party win more seats. The name comes from Governor Elbridge Gerry of Massachusetts, whose 1812 district map looked like a salamander. So B is correct: B.\nThe traps (ballot-box snatching, multiple voting, use of force) are all real electoral malpractices, but they happen on election day. Gerrymandering happens earlier, on the map. Multiple voting (D) and ballot-box snatching (A) are election-day rigging; gerrymandering is done earlier, by redrawing constituency boundaries.\nMemory tip: Gerry + salamander: gerrymandering is a map trick.",
 "|The first military coup in Nigeria occurred in:|1960|1963|1966|1967": "Nigeria's first military coup took place on 15 January 1966. Young officers, often called the \"five majors\" and including Chukwuma Kaduna Nzeogwu and Emmanuel Ifeajuna, killed Prime Minister Tafawa Balewa, Sir Ahmadu Bello and Chief S.L. Akintola. Major-General J.T.U. Aguiyi-Ironsi then took power. So the answer is 1966: C.\nThe trap: D, 1967, is the year the civil war began and the 12 states were created; both coups came a year earlier, in 1966.\nMemory tip: 1966 = two coups (January and July); 1967 = war and 12 states.",
 "|Under the Nigerian Constitution, the tenure of the President is:|4 years, renewable once|5 years, renewable once|4 years, renewable twice|6 years, not renewable": "Under section 135 of the 1999 Constitution the President holds office for four years, and under section 137 a person cannot be elected President more than twice. So the tenure is 4 years, renewable once: A.\nThe trap: B, 5 years renewable once, is the term in some other countries, but the 1999 Constitution (s.135) sets four years.\nMemory tip: \"4 + 4 and no more.\"",
 "|Colonialism in Africa was justified by Europeans on the basis of:|Resource sharing|The 'White Man's Burden' — a self-imposed duty to 'civilise' Africans|African invitation|UN mandate": "Europeans defended colonial rule with the claim that they had a duty to \"civilise\" Africans and Asians, the \"White Man's Burden\", a phrase from Rudyard Kipling's 1899 poem. Behind the claim were economic motives: raw materials, markets and investment. So B is correct: B.\nThe traps: colonial rule was imposed by conquest and treaties signed under pressure, not by any \"African invitation\"; and the UN was created only in 1945, long after Africa was partitioned at the Berlin Conference of 1884–85.\nMemory tip: The excuse was \"civilising\"; the real aim was resources and markets.",
 "|Absolute sovereignty was advocated by:|John Locke|Montesquieu|Jean Bodin|Aristotle": "The French writer Jean Bodin, in \"Six Books of the Commonwealth\" (1576), was the first to describe sovereignty as absolute, perpetual and indivisible power to make law. Thomas Hobbes later took a similar view. So C is correct: C.\nJohn Locke (A) argued for limited government, the opposite of the absolute sovereignty championed by Jean Bodin.\nMemory tip: Bodin = Boundless sovereignty; John Austin later developed the legal theory of sovereignty.",
 "|The practice of delegating power from a higher to a lower level of government is called:|Devolution|Federalism|Confederation|Secession": "Devolution is the transfer of powers from a central (higher) government to regional or local (lower) governments, while the centre keeps ultimate sovereignty and can take the powers back. The UK's devolution to Scotland and Wales in 1998–99 is an example. So A is correct: A.\nThe trap: B, federalism, also involves lower levels of government, but there the constitution shares power; devolved powers are a gift from the centre.\nMemory tip: Devolution = power handed Down by the centre.",
 "|Which of the following factors does NOT promote national integration?|Common language|Tribalism|Shared history|Sporting activities": "National integration is the process of uniting different groups into one nation with a shared identity. A common language, a shared history and national sports (for example, supporting the Super Eagles together) all bring people closer. Tribalism, putting loyalty to one's ethnic group above the nation, divides people. So tribalism does NOT promote integration: B.\nStudents sometimes doubt D, but sport is a well-known unifying force in Nigeria. The NYSC (1973) and Unity Schools (from 1966) were also created to promote integration.\nMemory tip: In a NOT question, look for the one divisive option.",
 "|An embargo is a government order that:|Permits free trade|Restricts or prohibits trade with a specific country|Increases import tariffs|Promotes foreign investment": "An embargo is an official order that bans or restricts trade with a particular country, often as a political sanction. Many countries placed arms and oil embargoes on apartheid South Africa, for example. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap: C, higher import tariffs, also restricts trade, but a tariff is a tax that makes trade dearer; an embargo bans it.\nMemory tip: Embargo = Bar on trade.",
 "|The principle of judicial review means courts can:|Make new laws|Review and invalidate laws that conflict with the constitution|Sentence criminals without trial|Create executive orders": "Judicial review is the power of the courts to examine laws and executive actions and to declare void any that go against the constitution. It became established in the USA through Marbury v. Madison (1803). Nigerian courts have the same power under the 1999 Constitution. So B is correct: B.\nCourts interpret and test laws but do not make new laws; law-making belongs to the legislature. Judicial review is one of the judiciary's main checks on the other two organs.\nMemory tip: Judicial review = the court as the \"umpire\" of the constitution.",
 "|OPEC was established to:|Promote democracy in oil-producing countries|Coordinate and unify petroleum policies among member countries|Build oil refineries in Africa|Regulate global energy consumption": "The Organisation of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) was founded in Baghdad on 14 September 1960 by Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia and Venezuela. Its aim is to coordinate and unify the petroleum policies of its members and secure fair, stable prices. Its headquarters has been in Vienna, Austria, since 1965. Nigeria joined in 1971. So B is correct: B.\nOPEC manages its members' oil supply but cannot regulate how the world consumes energy.\nMemory tip: OPEC = Baghdad 1960, Vienna headquarters, Nigeria 1971.",
 "|The ombudsman is an official appointed to:|Conduct elections|Investigate citizens' complaints against government maladministration|Manage the economy|Oversee military operations": "An ombudsman is an independent official who investigates citizens' complaints against injustice or maladministration by government officials and agencies. The office began in Sweden in 1809. Nigeria's version is the Public Complaints Commission, set up in 1975. So B is correct: B.\nConducting elections is the job of the electoral commission (INEC). The ombudsman deals with complaints about the administration.\nMemory tip: Ombudsman = the \"people's complaints officer\"; in Nigeria, the Public Complaints Commission (1975).",
 "|Which of the following is NOT a pillar of democracy?|Free and fair elections|Rule of law|Respect for fundamental rights|Single-party rule": "The pillars of democracy include free and fair elections, rule of law, respect for fundamental rights, an independent judiciary, a free press and competition among political parties. Single-party rule removes the people's choice and is a feature of authoritarian states. So it is NOT a pillar of democracy: D.\nThe trap is to hesitate over B: rule of law is a pillar because it protects everyone, including the government's opponents.\nMemory tip: Democracy needs CHOICE, and one party means no choice.",
 "|The Clifford Constitution of 1922 was significant because it:|Granted Nigeria independence|Introduced elective representation in Nigeria for the first time|Created the First Republic|Divided Nigeria into North and South": "The Clifford Constitution of 1922, named after Governor Sir Hugh Clifford, introduced the elective principle in Nigeria. Four seats in the Legislative Council were elected (three for Lagos and one for Calabar) by adult males earning at least £100 a year. This led Herbert Macaulay to form the NNDP in 1923, Nigeria's first political party. So B is correct: B.\nThe traps: independence came in 1960, the First Republic in 1963, and the North–South split dates from 1900 (the two protectorates), not from 1922.\nMemory tip: Clifford = first Choice (election), 1922.",
 "|Which theory holds that the state originated through force and conquest?|Social contract theory|Force or conquest theory|Divine right theory|Evolutionary theory": "The force (or conquest) theory holds that states began when strong groups conquered and ruled weaker ones, so that the state was born of power, not agreement. So B is correct: B.\nThe other options are rival theories. Social contract says the state came from an agreement among people; divine right says it came from God; evolutionary (natural) theory says it grew slowly from the family to the clan, tribe and state.\nMemory tip: Force = Fight; Contract = Consent; Divine = God; Evolution = Growth.",
 "|The concept of 'balance of power' in international relations refers to:|Equal distribution of economic resources|A system where no single state becomes dominant by ensuring roughly equal power among major states|Equal military spending|UN peacekeeping operations": "Balance of power is the idea that peace is kept when power is shared fairly evenly among the major states or alliances, so that no single state can dominate the others. Weaker states join together against any power that grows too strong. Europe in the 19th century and the Cold War balance between the USA and USSR are examples. So B is correct: B.\nBalance of power concerns military and political strength among states, not equal sharing of wealth.\nMemory tip: Balance of power = a see-saw that no state can tip for good.",
 "|Local government exists at which tier in Nigeria?|First tier|Second tier|Third tier|Fourth tier": "Nigeria has three tiers of government: federal (first), state (second) and local government (third). The 1976 Local Government Reform made local government the third tier of government across the country, and the 1979 and 1999 Constitutions confirmed it. There are 774 local government areas today. So C is correct: C.\nThe trap: B, second tier, is the state government; local government comes after federal and state, so it is the third tier.\nMemory tip: Federal–State–Local = 1–2–3; local government is the government closest to the people.",
 "|The concept of 'rule of law' as developed by A.V. Dicey includes all of the following EXCEPT:|Supremacy of law over arbitrary power|Equality before the law|The constitution is the result of rights of individuals as defined by courts|Laws are made only by the military": "Dicey's rule of law (1885) rests on three ideas: supremacy of law (no arbitrary power), equality before the law, and the principle that the constitution's rules come from court decisions on individual rights. Laws \"made only by the military\" is not part of it; rule by decree is the opposite of the rule of law. So D is the exception: D.\nThe trap: C sounds odd enough to be the exception, but it is Dicey's third principle, that rights grew out of court judgments.\nMemory tip: Dicey's three S-E-C: Supremacy, Equality, Court-made constitution.",
 "|Which of the following is a characteristic of a unitary state?|Powers are shared between national and regional governments by constitution|There is a single, centralised government from which all authority derives|Different regions have equal constitutional powers to the centre|The military controls regional governments": "In a unitary state there is one central government from which all authority comes. Any regional or local units are created by the centre and can be changed or abolished by it. Britain and France are examples, and Nigeria briefly became unitary under the Unification Decree No. 34 of May 1966. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap: A, powers shared by the constitution between national and regional governments, is the definition of federalism, not a unitary state.\nMemory tip: Unitary = one source of power; Federal = shared by the constitution.",
 "|The concept of 'sovereignty' in political science refers to:|The military power of a state|The supreme and ultimate authority to make and enforce law within a territory|The elected president's personal power|The ability to wage war": "Sovereignty is the supreme and final legal authority of a state to make and enforce laws within its territory, without control from outside. Internally, no body is above it; externally, it means independence from other states. Jean Bodin and John Austin are its classic theorists. So B is correct: B.\nThe traps are A and D: military strength and the ability to wage war may help a state defend its sovereignty, but they are not what sovereignty means. Sovereignty belongs to the state, not to one person (C).\nMemory tip: Sovereignty = supreme legal authority, from within and free from outside.",
 "|In Nigeria's 1999 Constitution, the National Assembly consists of:|Only the Senate|Only the House of Representatives|The Senate (109 senators) and the House of Representatives (360 members)|The National Council of State only": "The National Assembly (s.47, 1999 Constitution) has two chambers. The Senate has 109 members: three from each of the 36 states plus one from the FCT. The House of Representatives has 360 members elected from federal constituencies. So C is correct: C.\nThe National Council of State is an advisory body (made up of the President, former Heads of State, Governors and others), not a legislature.\nMemory tip: 36 × 3 + 1 = 109 senators; 360 Reps.",
 "|The Organisation of African Unity (OAU) was transformed into the African Union (AU) in:|1999|2001|2002|2005": "The OAU was founded in Addis Ababa on 25 May 1963. African leaders agreed to replace it with the African Union in the Sirte Declaration (1999). The AU's Constitutive Act was adopted at Lomé in 2000 and came into force in May 2001, and the AU was formally launched at Durban, South Africa, on 9 July 2002. So the answer is 2002: C.\nThe trap: B, 2001, is when the AU's Constitutive Act came into force, but the OAU was only replaced at the Durban launch in 2002.\nMemory tip: OAU 1963 → AU 2002; both based in Addis Ababa.",
 "|Which of the following is NOT a function of a constitution?|It defines the structure of government|It guarantees fundamental rights|It specifies how elections are conducted|It determines the prices of commodities": "A constitution sets out the structure of government, protects fundamental rights and can lay down how elections are conducted (Nigeria's 1999 Constitution, for example, sets the rules for electing the President). It does not fix prices of goods; that is economic policy, handled by government and markets. So D is NOT a function: D.\nThe trap: C looks like a job for ordinary electoral laws, but constitutions often lay down the main rules for elections.\nMemory tip: Constitutions deal with power and rights, not prices.",
 "|Proportional representation as an electoral system means:|The candidate with the most votes wins regardless of vote share|Seats in parliament are allocated in proportion to the votes each party receives|Only two parties are allowed to contest|Voters rank candidates in order of preference": "Proportional representation gives each party seats in proportion to the votes it wins nationally or regionally, so a party with 40% of the votes gets about 40% of the seats. Small parties and minorities therefore gain representation. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap: D, ranking candidates by preference, is used in one form of PR (the single transferable vote) but is not the definition of PR.\nMemory tip: PR = Percent of votes → Percent of seats.",
 "|The term 'pressure group' in politics refers to:|A group that contests elections|An organisation that seeks to influence government policy without seeking political power itself|The military junta|A coalition government": "A pressure (interest) group is an organised body that tries to influence government policy for its members or a cause, but does not seek to form a government or put up candidates. Examples in Nigeria are the NLC, the Nigerian Bar Association (NBA), ASUU and NANS. So B is correct: B.\nThe trap: A, a group that contests elections, describes a political party; a pressure group seeks influence, not power.\nMemory tip: Parties want to BE the government; pressure groups want to INFLUENCE the government.",
 "|Which of the following was a major cause of political instability in post-independence Africa?|Too much economic development|Ethnic/tribal politics combined with weak institutions, military interventions, and colonial boundary legacies|Strong democratic traditions|Absence of political parties": "After independence, many African states suffered coups and conflict. The causes were ethnic rivalry in states whose colonial borders grouped rival peoples together, weak institutions, corruption, and repeated military interventions; Nigeria's coups of 1966 and civil war of 1967–70 are examples. So B is correct: B.\nThe other options would make a state more stable, not less. Strong democratic traditions and real economic development reduce instability. Political parties were not absent: many countries had too many ethnically based parties.\nMemory tip: In \"cause of instability\" questions, choose the option that names division and weakness.",
 "|Judicial independence means:|Judges are elected by the people|The judiciary operates free from interference by the executive and legislature in its decisions|Judges can make any law they want|Courts are run by the military": "Judicial independence means judges decide cases according to the law, free from pressure by the executive, the legislature or anyone else. In Nigeria it is protected by appointment on the recommendation of the National Judicial Council, security of tenure, salaries charged on the Consolidated Revenue Fund, and immunity for judicial acts. So B is correct: B.\nElecting judges would tie them to politics. Nigerian judges are appointed, not elected.\nMemory tip: Independent judge = fixed tenure, fixed pay, no fear.",
 "|The principle of 'checks and balances' in government ensures that:|All government officials are paid equally|Each branch of government has powers to limit and oversee the others, preventing abuse of power|The judiciary controls the executive|Only the legislature makes laws": "Checks and balances means each organ of government has some power to limit the others. The President can veto bills; the legislature can override a veto by a two-thirds vote, approve appointments and impeach; the courts can strike down unconstitutional laws. This prevents abuse of power. So B is correct: B.\nThe judiciary checks the executive, but the executive and legislature also check the judiciary (appointments, removal of judges), so no single organ controls another.\nMemory tip: Separation of powers divides power; checks and balances makes each part watch the others.",
 "|The ECOWAS (Economic Community of West African States) was established in:|1960|1975|1980|1963": "ECOWAS was established by the Treaty of Lagos on 28 May 1975 by 15 West African states, led by Nigeria's General Yakubu Gowon and Togo's General Gnassingbé Eyadéma. Its aim is economic integration. So the year is 1975: B.\nThe traps: 1963 is when the OAU was founded and 1960 is when Nigeria became independent. Membership has changed over time: Cape Verde joined in 1977, Mauritania left in 2000, and Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger left in January 2025, so ECOWAS has 12 members today, with its headquarters in Abuja. The trap: D, 1963, is a familiar date from the same field, the founding of the OAU; ECOWAS came later, in 1975.\nMemory tip: ECOWAS = Lagos, 28 May 1975.",
 "|Which of the following correctly describes a 'coalition government'?|A government formed by a single dominant party|A government formed by two or more parties joining together to command a parliamentary majority|A military government with civilian advisors|A government with no opposition": "A coalition government is formed when two or more parties combine to get a majority in parliament, usually because no single party has won one. In Nigeria's First Republic, the NPC and NCNC formed a coalition federal government in 1959 under Tafawa Balewa. So B is correct: B.\nA government of one dominant party is single-party government. A government with no opposition (D) can be a \"government of national unity\", but that is not what defines a coalition.\nMemory tip: Coalition = Combination of parties.",
 "|In Nigeria, the Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC) is established under which section of the 1999 Constitution?|Section 153|Section 14|Section 36|Section 270": "Section 153 of the 1999 Constitution sets up the Federal Executive Bodies. There are 14 of them, including the Code of Conduct Bureau, the Federal Character Commission and the Independent National Electoral Commission (s.153(1)(f)). Their composition and powers are in the Third Schedule. So A is correct: A.\nThe traps: section 14 states the fundamental objectives (government's duty to promote welfare, federal character); section 36 is the right to fair hearing; section 270 deals with the State High Courts.\nMemory tip: s.153 = the list of federal commissions, from Code of Conduct to Revenue Mobilisation.",
 "|The term 'bicameralism' refers to:|A legislature with one chamber|A legislature divided into two separate houses or chambers|Two competing governments|Two political parties sharing power": "Bicameralism means a legislature has two separate chambers, usually an upper house and a lower house. Nigeria's National Assembly (the Senate and the House of Representatives), the US Congress and the UK Parliament (Lords and Commons) are examples. So B is correct: B.\nA legislature with one chamber is unicameral, like Nigeria's State Houses of Assembly and Ghana's Parliament. \"Two parties sharing power\" is a coalition, not bicameralism.\nMemory tip: Bi = two, as in bicycle (two wheels) and bicameral (two chambers).",
 "|Power differs from influence in that it is|persuasive while influence is directive|coercive while influence is harmful|coercive while influence is persuasive|arrogant while influence is corruptible": "Power is the capacity to make people do what they would not otherwise do, because disobedience brings sanctions: arrest, fines, loss of office, force. Influence is the ability to change people's behaviour by persuasion, example or reputation; they follow willingly and can refuse without punishment. So power is coercive while influence is persuasive: C. The trap: A reverses the pair, giving the persuasive quality to power. B and D attach moral labels (harmful, arrogant, corruptible) that are not part of either definition. Memory tip: Power Pushes (with sanctions), Influence Invites (with persuasion).",
 "|The standing committee of legislature is one|whose members stand while deliberating|that has statutory responsibilities|that performs ad hoc functions|that has all legislators as members": "Legislatures do most detailed work in committees. A standing committee is permanent: it is created by the standing orders (rules) of the house, lasts for the whole life of the legislature and has fixed, statutory responsibilities, such as the committees on finance, defence or public accounts: B. The trap: C describes an ad hoc (select) committee, set up for one task and dissolved when it reports. D describes the Committee of the Whole House, where all members sit as a committee. A is a pun on \"standing\". Memory tip: standing = permanent and statutory; ad hoc = one job, then gone.",
 "|Where the constitution is supreme, unconstitutional acts of the executive and the legislature can be checked by the courts through|recall|judicial review|vote of no confidence|impeachment": "When the constitution is supreme, every law and executive act must agree with it. Judicial review is the power of the courts to examine such acts and declare them null and void if they break the constitution. It was established in the USA in Marbury v. Madison (1803) and is used by Nigerian courts too: B. The trap: impeachment (D) also checks the executive, but it is done by the legislature, not the courts, and it removes a person rather than voiding an act. Recall (A) is done by voters; a vote of no confidence (C) is done by parliament. Memory tip: courts check by REVIEW; legislatures check by IMPEACHMENT; voters check by RECALL.",
 "|The central decision-making organ of a confederation is made up of|technocrats appointed by the units|politicians elected from the confederal constituencies|politicians nominated by governments of member states|representatives of pressure groups": "A confederation is a loose union of sovereign states that keep most power and create a weak centre for limited common purposes (examples: the USA under the Articles of Confederation, 1781–1789; Senegambia, 1982–1989). Because the member states remain sovereign, the central body is made up of delegates nominated by the member governments, who act on their governments' instructions: C. The trap: B describes a federation, where the people elect central representatives directly. Technocrats (A) and pressure groups (D) do not make up the decision-making organ. Memory tip: in a confederation, the centre belongs to the states; in a federation, power is shared under a constitution.",
 "|Which of the following is true of a parliamentary system of government?|Clear separation of government organs|Strict operation of bicameral legislature|Removal of government by impeachment|Adherence to majority rule": "In a parliamentary system (Britain, Nigeria 1960–1966), the executive comes out of the legislature. The party or coalition with a majority of seats forms the government, its leader becomes Prime Minister, and the government survives only while the majority supports it. The whole system rests on majority rule: D. The trap: A is the mark of the presidential system; a parliamentary system has fusion of powers, not separation. Removal by impeachment (C) is also presidential; a parliamentary government falls by a vote of no confidence. Many parliamentary systems are unicameral, so B is wrong. Memory tip: parliamentary = fusion + majority + no-confidence vote; presidential = separation + fixed term + impeachment.",
 "|A major feature of authoritarianism is that government is|consensual|personalized|centralized|decentralized": "Authoritarianism is rule in which power is held by one leader or a small group who are not accountable to the people, tolerate little opposition and ignore constitutional limits. Because institutions are weak, government revolves around the ruler's person: decisions reflect his wishes, loyalty to him matters more than law, and the state is identified with him. That is personalized government: B. The trap: C, centralized, is tempting because authoritarian rulers concentrate power, but centralization alone is also a feature of democratic unitary states such as Britain, so it does not mark out authoritarianism. Consensual (A) describes democracy. Memory tip: authoritarianism = rule by persons, not by law.",
 "|The central point of capitalism, as expounded by Karl Marx, is that|capitalists' profit is the surplus value obtained from workers' labour|workers are inherently incapable of being owners of their labour|capitalists shall always increase workers' earning capacity through wages|capitalists shall always readily consent to workers' welfare demands": "Karl Marx (Das Kapital, 1867) taught that labour creates value. A worker produces goods worth more than the wages he is paid; the difference is surplus value, which the capitalist keeps as profit. For Marx this is the root of exploitation and class conflict between the bourgeoisie (owners) and the proletariat (workers): A. C and D describe a generous capitalism that Marx rejected; he argued capitalists keep wages low and take the surplus value of workers' labour as profit. Memory tip: Marx's formula: value of goods minus wages = surplus value = profit.",
 "|A constitution that requires a plebiscite or a referendum to be amended is|rigid|unwritten|flexible|written": "A rigid constitution is one that cannot be amended by an ordinary act of the legislature; it needs a special procedure, such as a two-thirds majority, approval by the state assemblies, or a referendum (plebiscite) of the people: A. The USA and Nigeria have rigid constitutions. The trap: D, written, is tempting because most rigid constitutions are written, but a written constitution can still be flexible if it is easy to change. The question asks about the amendment procedure, which is what rigid and flexible describe. Memory tip: rigid or flexible = how it is changed; written or unwritten = the form it takes.",
 "|An important function of a constitution is that it|provides a framework for the study of government|facilitates cross-fertilization of ideas of governance|serves as the fountainhead of authority for the exercise of power|promotes citizen participation in government and administration": "A constitution is the fundamental law that creates the organs of government, shares out their powers and limits them. Every authority (President, legislature, courts, states) gets its power from the constitution and must act within it, so the constitution is the fountainhead (source) of authority: C. Providing a framework for studying government (A) is a by-product, not a function; a constitution's key role is to be the source of authority for exercising power. Memory tip: ask \"where does this official's power come from?\"; the answer is always the constitution.",
 "|When a bill passed by the legislature is vetoed by the executive, the action underscores the principle of|probity and accountability|separation of power|collective responsibility|checks and balances": "Checks and balances means each arm of government has some power to restrain the others so that none becomes too powerful. The executive's veto checks the legislature, and the legislature can in turn override the veto by a two-thirds majority (in Nigeria and the USA): D. The trap: B, separation of powers, divides functions among the three arms (making, executing, interpreting laws), but the veto is one arm stepping into another's work, which is exactly what checks and balances allows. Collective responsibility (C) belongs to a cabinet in a parliamentary system. Memory tip: separation = who does what; checks and balances = who stops whom.",
 "|In the legislative process, a bill is a|motion accepted for debate|motion rejected after debate|proposal before the legislature|law passed by the legislature": "A bill is a draft of a proposed law brought before the legislature. It goes through first reading, second reading (debate on principles), committee stage, report stage and third reading in each house, and becomes an act (a law) only after it is passed and receives the executive's assent: C. The trap: D describes an act, not a bill; once passed and signed, it is no longer a bill. A and B describe a motion, which is a proposal for the house to take a decision, not a draft law. Memory tip: bill = draft law going in; act = law coming out.",
 "|One of the advantages of bicameral over unicameral legislature is that it|is cheap to maintain|promotes social equality|takes less time for bills to be passed|prevents the passage of ill-considered bills": "A bicameral legislature has two chambers (for example, Nigeria's Senate and House of Representatives). Every bill must pass both, so the second chamber revises the work of the first and can delay or stop hasty, ill-considered bills: D. It also allows equal representation of the federating units in a federal state. Cheapness (A) and speed in passing bills (C) are advantages of a unicameral legislature, not a bicameral one. Memory tip: two houses = a second look; one house = cheaper and quicker.",
 "|The fundamental rights of citizens include rights to|free education, employment and freedom of thought|life, speech and association|life, liberty and property|association, property and social security": "Fundamental rights are the basic rights written into the constitution and enforceable in court. The classic trio, from John Locke, is life, liberty and property, and all three are protected in Chapter IV of Nigeria's constitution: C. Option B (life, speech, association) is also accepted, because the rights to life, freedom of expression and freedom of association are all in Chapter IV too. The trap: A and D include free education, employment and social security, which are in Chapter II (Fundamental Objectives and Directive Principles of State Policy); they are goals of the state and cannot be enforced in court. Memory tip: Chapter IV = rights you can sue for; Chapter II = policy goals you cannot.",
 "|The manipulation of boundaries of constituencies in order to win more seats is called|devolution|rigging|gerrymandering|delimitation": "Gerrymandering is the unfair drawing of constituency boundaries to favour one party, for example by packing opponents into a few seats or spreading them thinly. The word comes from Elbridge Gerry, governor of Massachusetts, whose 1812 district was shaped like a salamander: C. The trap: D, delimitation, is the lawful, neutral process of dividing a country into constituencies (done by INEC in Nigeria). Gerrymandering is delimitation abused. Rigging (B) is electoral fraud on voting or counting. Devolution (A) is the transfer of power to lower levels of government. Memory tip: Gerry + salamander = gerrymander, a boundary drawn into a strange shape for gain.",
 "|One argument against a multi-party system is the|encouragement of opposition and instability|banning of interest groups|inability to attract foreign assistance|high cost of conducting elections": "A multi-party system has three or more parties competing for power. Its critics point to its costs: many parties mean many campaigns, candidates, ballot papers, run-off elections and subsidies, which makes elections very expensive for the state and the parties: D. It can also produce weak coalitions and ethnic parties. The trap: A is tempting, but the encouragement of opposition is an argument for multi-partyism, since a strong opposition keeps government accountable, so the option mixes a merit with a demerit. Multi-partyism does not ban interest groups (B) or stop foreign aid (C). Memory tip: more parties, more choice, but more cost.",
 "|Associational interest groups are organized to|further the interests of members|specifically lobby the government|support the government|achieve goals affecting other associations": "Associational interest groups are formally organised, with constitutions, officers and members, to promote the specific interests of their members. Examples: the Nigerian Labour Congress, the Nigerian Bar Association, the Nigerian Medical Association, and the Manufacturers Association of Nigeria. They use lobbying, strikes and publicity as means: A. The trap: B, lobbying, is one method they use, not the reason they exist, and many do not lobby only government. C is wrong because they often oppose government. Memory tip: group types: associational (formal, members' interests), institutional (inside government), non-associational (informal kin or ethnic), anomic (riots).",
 "|Public opinion is view that is|held by the majority|active in the public realm|widely publicized|no longer a secret": "Public opinion is the collective view held by the majority of people in a society on a public issue at a particular time, for example on fuel prices or a new law. Governments measure it through elections, opinion polls, the media and protests: A. The trap: C, widely publicized, describes how an opinion spreads, not whose it is; a view pushed by the media may still be held by only a few. B and D are vague and do not define the term. Memory tip: public opinion = what most people think about a public issue now.",
 "|The political neutrality of civil servants implies that they|are not allowed to join any organization or group|have no dealings with politicians|are not allowed to be involved in partisan politics|are not allowed to vote": "Political neutrality means civil servants serve whichever government is in power with equal loyalty. To make this possible they may not join political parties, campaign, stand for election or publicly support a party while in service; they must stay out of partisan politics: C. They keep their right to vote, because voting is secret and does not show partisanship. The trap: D goes too far, since civil servants are citizens and may vote. A is also too wide: they may join professional bodies and unions. Memory tip: neutral civil servants vote quietly but never campaign loudly.",
 "|The idea of making the civil service permanent, neutral and anonymous is to|enhance efficiency in administration|ensure loyalty and support|prevent opposition to government|make civil servants a functional elite": "Three principles of the civil service: permanence (officials stay when governments change, keeping experience and continuity), neutrality (they serve any party in power) and anonymity (ministers, not officials, take public credit or blame). Together they let the service give frank advice and carry out policy professionally, without fear or favour, making administration efficient: A. The trap: B, loyalty and support, sounds right, but neutrality means loyalty to the state and the government of the day, not personal or party support. C and D are not aims of the principles. Memory tip: P-N-A (Permanent, Neutral, Anonymous) = an efficient, steady service.",
 "|The western zone of the Sokoto caliphate was administered from|Kebbi|Ilorin|Bida|Gwandu": "After the Fulani jihad (1804), Usman dan Fodio divided the Sokoto caliphate in about 1812 into two parts for easier administration. The eastern half, with Sokoto as its centre, went to his son Muhammad Bello; the western half, administered from Gwandu, went to his brother Abdullahi: D. Western emirates such as Ilorin and Nupe (Bida) owed allegiance to Gwandu. The trap: Ilorin (B) and Bida (C) were emirates under the Gwandu zone, not its headquarters. Kebbi (A) was a Hausa kingdom in the area, not the zone's headquarters. Memory tip: Bello in the East at Sokoto; Abdullahi in the West at Gwandu.",
 "|Some pre-colonial Nigerian societies are described as stateless because|they had no formal governmental institutions|they had no definite political boundaries|their population was too small|they were not independent": "Stateless (acephalous, \"headless\") societies had no central government, no king or chief with overall authority, and no formal institutions such as a standing army or a bureaucracy. Authority was spread among family heads, elders' councils, age grades, title holders, oracles and village assemblies. Most pre-colonial Igbo and Tiv communities were like this: A. The trap: B is wrong because these communities did have known territories. C is wrong because some were very populous. D is wrong because they were independent communities. \"Stateless\" refers to the absence of centralised government, not of land, people or independence. Memory tip: stateless = headless (acephalous), not lawless.",
 "|The method used by the British to facilitate the administration of Southern Nigeria was|persuasion|dialogue|divide and rule|trade concession": "In Southern Nigeria the British kept the Colony of Lagos, the Western and Eastern provinces and many ethnic groups apart, encouraged rivalry among them, and later ruled North and South differently. This divide and rule policy stopped the people from uniting against colonial authority: C. Persuasion (A) and dialogue (B) do not describe colonial rule, which rested on conquest and force, and trade concession (D) is a commercial arrangement, not a method of administration. Memory tip: keep them divided and they cannot unite: divide and rule.",
 "|A major function of the Warrant chiefs was to|prevent tribal wars|supervise native courts and markets|stop ritual killings|take charge of local administration": "Indirect rule worked in the North because emirs already had authority. In the East, where most communities had no kings, the British created warrant chiefs: local men given a \"warrant\" to act as the colonial government's agents in local administration. They collected taxes, kept order, provided labour and sat in native courts: D. Their unpopularity helped cause the 1929 Aba Women's Riot. The trap: B is one of their duties, but the question asks for the major function, which is the whole local administration. A and C were not their main job. Memory tip: Warrant chief = a Created chief for the stateless East.",
 "|After 1945, the demand of African nationalists changed from reform to independence because|colonial rule became less oppressive|colonial rule was in disarray|the second world war boosted their morale|the second world war enhanced colonial rule": "Before 1945 Nigerian nationalists mostly asked for reforms within colonial rule: more seats in the Legislative Council, more jobs for Africans. After the Second World War (1939–1945) they demanded independence. Ex-servicemen had seen that white men could be defeated, the Atlantic Charter (1941) promised self-determination, and India's independence (1947) showed it could be done. The war boosted their morale and confidence: C. Colonial rule was not in disarray (B); the Second World War gave nationalists new confidence, while A and D would have weakened, not strengthened, the demand for independence. Memory tip: WWII = Waking up: soldiers returned, morale rose, demands grew.",
 "|When Nigeria achieved independence in 1960, the Head of State was the|President|Prime Minister|Governor-General|Queen of England": "At independence on 1 October 1960, Nigeria was a dominion within the Commonwealth. The British monarch, Queen Elizabeth II, remained Head of State (the paper calls her the Queen of England), and she was represented in Nigeria by a Governor-General (first Sir James Robertson, then Nnamdi Azikiwe from November 1960). Real power lay with the Prime Minister, Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa: D. The trap: C, Governor-General, only represented the Head of State. B, Prime Minister, was Head of Government. A: Nigeria had no President until it became a republic on 1 October 1963. Memory tip: 1960 = Queen (via Governor-General); 1963 = President Azikiwe.",
 "|The central legislature of Nigeria became bicameral in|1963|1951|1959|1960": "Before independence the federal legislature had only a House of Representatives. Constitutional changes made at the end of 1959 created a Senate representing the regions, and the Senate first sat in January 1960, with Nnamdi Azikiwe as its first President. So the Parliament of independent Nigeria in 1960 had two houses: D. The trap: B, 1951, is when the Northern and Western regional legislatures became bicameral (Houses of Chiefs), not the central legislature. 1963 is the year Nigeria became a republic. 1959 is when the Senate was provided for, but it did not sit until 1960. Memory tip: regions got two houses in 1951; the centre got its Senate in 1960.",
 "|Which of these constitutions recognized local government as a third tier of government in Nigeria?|1946 constitution|1960 constitution|1963 constitution|1979 constitution": "The 1976 local government reform under Obasanjo created a uniform system of elected councils. The 1979 constitution then entrenched it: section 7 guaranteed democratically elected local government councils, and the councils got a share of federal revenue. Local government thus became the third tier of government after the federal and state tiers: D. The 1999 constitution kept this. The 1960 and 1963 constitutions (B, C) left local government to the regions, and the 1946 Richards Constitution (A) dealt only with colonial native authorities. Memory tip: 1976 reform, then 1979 entrenchment; the third tier is a 1970s creation.",
 "|Under the 1963 constitution, items not listed in the exclusive and concurrent lists were within the exclusive competence of the|Executive|Federal parliament|Regional legislatures|Judiciary": "The 1960 and 1963 constitutions split legislative powers into three groups. The exclusive list (defence, currency, foreign affairs) was for the federal parliament alone. The concurrent list (higher education, industrial development) was shared, with federal law prevailing where they clashed. Everything on neither list, the residual matters (such as chieftaincy and local government), belonged to the regional legislatures: C. The trap: B, the federal parliament, controlled the exclusive list. In the USA too, residual powers stay with the states. The executive and judiciary (A, D) do not make laws. Memory tip: Exclusive = federal; Concurrent = both; Residual = regions/states.",
 "|Before Nigeria became a republic, the highest body charged with the administration of justice was the|Privy Council|High Court|Supreme Court|Court of Appeal": "Until 1963 the final court of appeal for Nigeria was the Judicial Committee of the Privy Council in London. Appeals went from the Federal Supreme Court to the Privy Council: A. When Nigeria became a republic on 1 October 1963, appeals to the Privy Council were abolished and the Supreme Court of Nigeria became the highest court. The trap: C, Supreme Court, is the right answer after 1963, not before. The Court of Appeal (D) was created only in 1976 (as the Federal Court of Appeal). Memory tip: before the republic, London had the last word; after 1963, Lagos did.",
 "|The equivalent of a commissioner at the local government level is the|executive chairman|secretary|councillor|supervisory councillor": "At state level the governor runs ministries through commissioners. At local government level the executive chairman runs departments through supervisory councillors, who are appointed by the chairman to head areas such as health, education or works and sit in the executive committee: D. The trap: A matches the governor, not the commissioner, so the chairman is the governor's equivalent. C, councillor, is an elected member of the legislative council, who corresponds to a member of the state House of Assembly. B is the secretary to the local government, like the secretary to the state government. Memory tip: Governor : Commissioner = Chairman : Supervisory Councillor.",
 "|The Independent National Electoral Commission has the power to prepare and maintain the register of|political parties|constituencies|voters|electoral candidates": "The Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC), set up in 1998, organises federal and state elections, registers political parties and monitors their finances, and prepares and keeps the register of voters, which lists everyone entitled to vote: C. The trap: A, political parties, are registered by INEC, but the phrase \"prepare and maintain the register\" is used for voters. Constituencies (B) are delimited, not registered, and candidates (D) are nominated by parties. Memory tip: INEC keeps the voters' register; no registration, no vote.",
 "|The President of Nigeria is advised on the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the country by the|National Security Council|National Defence Council|Council of State|Federal Executive Council": "The constitution sets up several federal advisory councils. The National Defence Council advises the President on matters relating to the defence of the sovereignty and territorial integrity of Nigeria: B. The National Security Council advises on internal public security matters and the agencies that handle them. The trap: A sounds similar; remember that defence means guarding the country's borders against outside threats, while security means internal order. The Council of State (C) advises on matters such as the prerogative of mercy and national population censuses. The Federal Executive Council (D) is the cabinet. Memory tip: Defence = borders and sovereignty; Security = internal order.",
 "|The Nigerian Youth Movement collapsed as a result of|its failure to win election|shortage of funds to run its affairs|the harassments of its leadership by government|the breakup of its leadership": "The Nigerian Youth Movement, founded in 1934 (as the Lagos Youth Movement), was Nigeria's first truly national party. In 1941 a vacancy arose for a Legislative Council seat. The movement chose Ernest Ikoli over Samuel Akisanya; Azikiwe backed Akisanya, and he and many Ijebu and Igbo members left. This breakup of the leadership, along ethnic lines, ruined the NYM: D. Electoral failure (A) is wrong: the NYM won the 1938 Lagos elections; it collapsed after its leaders split in 1941, not because of funds (B) or harassment (C). Memory tip: 1941 Ikoli–Akisanya crisis = the first ethnic split in Nigerian nationalism.",
 "|The first restructuring of the Nigeria Federation took place with the|creation of the Mid-West Region in 1963|abolition of federalism in 1966|military counter-coup of 1966|creation of states in 1967": "The federation began in 1954 with three regions (North, West, East). The first change to these units came on 9 August 1963, when the Mid-West Region was carved out of the Western Region after a referendum, making four regions: A. The trap: D, the creation of 12 states by Gowon in May 1967, was the biggest restructuring, but it came four years after the Mid-West. B, Ironsi's Unification Decree (May 1966), ended federalism briefly; it was not the first restructuring. C was a change of regime, not of structure. This is a corrected answer: some would choose 1967 as the main restructuring, but the question asks for the first. Memory tip: 3 regions (1954), 4 (1963), 12 states (1967), 19 (1976), 36 (1996).",
 "|The land use decree of 1978 vested the ownership of land in Nigeria in the|local chiefs|local governments|state governments|federal government": "Before 1978, land in Nigeria was held under many different customary and statutory systems. The Land Use Decree of 1978 (Obasanjo regime) vested all land in each state in the governor of that state, to hold in trust for the use and common benefit of all Nigerians. Local governments control land in rural areas under the governor: C. The law still applies today as the Land Use Act, entrenched in the constitution. The trap: D, the federal government, owns only land it acquires for federal purposes. A, local chiefs, lost their control under the decree. Memory tip: Land Use Act: the Governor holds the land in Trust.",
 "|The main source of financing local government in Nigeria is|internal revenue generation|statutory revenue allocation|special state grants|grants-in-aid": "Local governments in Nigeria raise some money internally (rates, fees, licences, market dues), but this is small. Their main income is statutory allocation: their constitutional share of the Federation Account, which they get monthly, plus a share of VAT: B. The trap: A is how local governments should ideally be funded, but it is the weakest source in practice. Grants-in-aid (D) and special state grants (C) are occasional. Memory tip: local government money comes mostly from Abuja, by statutory allocation.",
 "|The most remarkable legacy of the 1976 Local Government Reform in Nigeria was the introduction of|the office of sole administrators|caretaker management committees|uniformity in structure and functions|the third tier of government": "Before 1976 Nigeria had many different local systems: native authorities in the North, councils on the British model in the South, and other arrangements. The 1976 reform under Obasanjo set up one uniform, single-tier, multi-purpose local government system across the country, with similar structures and functions, elected councils and a population of 150,000 to 800,000 per council: C. Option D is also accepted: the reform guidelines first called local government the third tier of government, and the 1979 constitution entrenched this. Sole administrators (A) and caretaker committees (B) were stopgaps of military rule, not legacies of the 1976 reform, which brought a uniform structure and made local government a third tier. Memory tip: 1976 = one pattern for all of Nigeria.",
 "|The Murtala/Obasanjo regime in Nigeria increased the number of states from|4 to 12|12 to 19|19 to 21|30 to 36": "In 1967 Gowon created 12 states from the four regions. On 3 February 1976 the Murtala Muhammed government (with Obasanjo as Chief of Staff, Supreme Headquarters) created seven more, making 19 states: B. The trap: A, 4 to 12, was Gowon's 1967 change. C, 19 to 21, was Babangida's 1987 change, and Babangida went on to 30 in 1991. D, 30 to 36, was Abacha's 1996 change. Nigeria has had 36 states since 1996. Memory tip: Gowon 12, Murtala 19, Babangida 21 and 30, Abacha 36.",
 "|A major factor that influenced the formulation of Nigeria's foreign policy in the First Republic was|geographical location|the colonial legacy|economic consideration|the parliamentary system": "The First Republic (1960–1966) under Prime Minister Balewa kept a strongly pro-British, pro-Western foreign policy. That came from the colonial legacy: Nigeria's leaders were trained in Britain, its economy and trade were tied to Britain, it joined the Commonwealth, and it signed the Anglo-Nigerian Defence Pact (1960–1962): B. The trap: C, economic consideration, mattered, but the economic ties themselves came from colonial rule. Geographical location (A) and the parliamentary system (D) did not shape the policy in the same way. Memory tip: Balewa's foreign policy looked toward London because of the colonial legacy.",
 "|Nigeria's departure from pro-west policy during the Murtala Muhammed regime was as a result of|the economic interests of the west in Nigeria|Nigeria's increased international influence|the growing trade between Nigeria and the East|Nigeria's concern for decolonization in Africa": "Gowon's and Balewa's foreign policies were cautious and pro-Western. When Murtala Muhammed came to power in July 1975, he made Africa the centrepiece of foreign policy and the liberation of Africa from colonial and white-minority rule a priority. Over Angola in 1975, he defied the USA, recognised the MPLA and gave it aid, and he used oil wealth to support liberation movements in Southern Africa: D. The trap: B, increased international influence, was a result of the new policy, not its cause. A and C are not correct. Memory tip: Murtala's message on Angola: \"Africa has come of age\".",
 "|During the Civil War, the major power that expressed moral support for Biafra's self-determination was|France|China|the United States|Great Britain": "During the Nigerian Civil War (1967–1970), Britain and the Soviet Union supported the federal government, and the USA stayed officially neutral. France, under President Charles de Gaulle, publicly declared sympathy for Biafra's right to self-determination in 1968 and supplied arms through Gabon and Côte d'Ivoire: A. Only four African states (Tanzania, Gabon, Côte d'Ivoire, Zambia) and Haiti recognised Biafra. The trap: D, Great Britain, supplied arms to the federal side. China (B) gave Biafra only some verbal support. Memory tip: France for Biafra; Britain and USSR for Lagos.",
 "|The Barclays Bank and the British Petroleum Company in Nigeria were nationalized in the late 1970s for transacting business with|France|South Africa|Portugal|Libya": "Nigeria fought apartheid with economic weapons. In 1978 the Obasanjo government took over Barclays Bank's Nigerian operation (it became Union Bank) and in July 1979 nationalised British Petroleum's Nigerian assets, both because the companies did business with apartheid South Africa: B. The BP takeover was also meant to pressure Britain over Rhodesia. France (A), Portugal (C) and Libya (D) were not the issue; Nigeria nationalized the firms for doing business with apartheid South Africa. Memory tip: Barclays to Union Bank, BP to African Petroleum; both punished for South Africa links.",
 "|South-South cooperation is a major policy plan on which Nigeria bases her relations with|developed countries|countries of the southern hemisphere|developing countries|member countries of OAU": "\"South\" in international politics means the developing countries of Africa, Asia and Latin America, as opposed to the industrialised \"North\" (Brandt Report, 1980). South-South cooperation is cooperation among developing countries in trade, technology and aid, for example Nigeria's Technical Aid Corps: C. The trap: B takes \"South\" literally. Nigeria and most of the developing world, such as India, lie north of the equator; the term is a political label, not a hemisphere. Developed countries (A) are the North, and D is too narrow. Memory tip: North = rich, South = developing; South-South = developing helping developing.",
 "|Nigeria's recognition of the Popular Movement for the Liberation of Angola to lead the country was made under|General Yakubu Gowon|General Murtala Muhammed|General Muhammadu Buhari|General Ibrahim Babangida": "In 1975 Angola won independence from Portugal amid a civil war between three movements: the MPLA, the FNLA and UNITA. The USA and South Africa backed the MPLA's rivals. On 25 November 1975 the Murtala Muhammed government recognised the MPLA government and gave it money, swinging African opinion behind it: B. The trap: A, Gowon, was overthrown in July 1975, before the recognition. Buhari (C) and Babangida (D) came in the 1980s. Memory tip: Murtala's short rule (1975–1976) = Angola, the MPLA, and Africa as the centrepiece.",
 "|The first Nigerian Permanent Representative to the United Nations was|Alhaji Yusuf Maitama Sule|General Joseph Garba|Professor Ibrahim Gambari|Jaja Wachuku": "Nigeria joined the United Nations on 7 October 1960, a week after independence, and its first Permanent Representative (ambassador) to the UN was Jaja Wachuku (1960–1961), who had been the first Nigerian Speaker of the House of Representatives and went on to become Minister of Foreign Affairs: D. (The original paper's options were all wrong, so we rebuilt option D.) The trap: Maitama Sule (A), Joseph Garba (B) and Ibrahim Gambari (C) are all famous Nigerian UN ambassadors, but they served from 1979 onward, and Simeon Adebo came after Wachuku (1962–67). Memory tip: Nigeria's \"first\" at the UN came straight after independence: Wachuku in 1960.",
 "|In 1981, Nigeria participated in an Organization of African Unity peace-keeping force to replace Libyan force in|Somalia|Chad|Ethiopia|Zaire": "In 1980–1981 Libya had troops in Chad supporting Goukouni Oueddei's government. When they withdrew in November 1981, the OAU sent an Inter-African Force to keep the peace in Chad. Nigeria supplied the largest contingent, along with Zaire and Senegal. It was the OAU's first peacekeeping force: B. Nigeria had earlier hosted the Kano and Lagos peace accords on Chad (1979). The trap: Zaire (D) contributed troops; it was not where they were sent. Somalia (A) and Ethiopia (C) were not OAU peacekeeping sites in 1981. Memory tip: Chad is Nigeria's north-eastern neighbour; the 1981 OAU force went there after Libya left.",
 "|Nigeria's withdrawal from the Edinburgh Commonwealth Games in July 1986 was in protest against British|support to UNITA rebels in Angola|supply of arms to Rhodesia|failure to impose sanctions on South Africa|negative utterances on Nigeria": "In 1985–1986 Commonwealth leaders wanted economic sanctions against apartheid South Africa, but Britain under Margaret Thatcher refused. In protest, Nigeria led about 32 Commonwealth countries in boycotting the 1986 Commonwealth Games in Edinburgh: C. The trap: B, arms to Rhodesia, was a 1960s–1970s issue; Rhodesia became Zimbabwe in 1980. A, support to UNITA, was mainly an American and South African policy. D is not the reason. Memory tip: 1976 Montreal Olympics boycott over a New Zealand rugby tour of South Africa; 1986 Edinburgh boycott over Thatcher's refusal of sanctions; both against apartheid.",
 "|The major demand of the Third World countries on the United Nations in recent times is the|expansion of the permanent membership of the Security Council|post of the Secretary-General of the organization|withdrawal of the veto power from the Security Council|enforcement of resolutions on the superpowers": "The Security Council has five permanent members with a veto (USA, Russia, China, Britain, France), fixed in 1945 when most of Africa and Asia were colonies. Developing countries, now the majority of UN members, demand expansion of the permanent membership so that Africa, Latin America and more of Asia are represented, and Nigeria is a candidate: A. The demand remains unmet, and the permanent five are unchanged today. The trap: C, withdrawing the veto, is raised by some, but expansion is the main demand. The Secretary-General's post (B) already rotates among regions. Memory tip: P5 set in 1945; Africa wants a permanent seat at the top table.",
 "|Which of the following is the function of the Council of Ministers of the O.A.U.?|Co-ordinating the general policy of the organization|Directing the finances of the organization|Preparing the agenda of the organization's meetings|Reviewing the functions and activities of other organs of the organization": "The OAU (1963) had four main organs: the Assembly of Heads of State and Government (supreme), the Council of Ministers (foreign ministers), the General Secretariat, and the Commission of Mediation, Conciliation and Arbitration. The Council of Ministers prepared the conferences of the Assembly, including their agenda, carried out its decisions and approved the budget: C. The trap: A, co-ordinating general policy, belonged to the Assembly, the supreme organ. In 2002 the OAU became the African Union, where the Executive Council of ministers now does this work. Memory tip: Ministers prepare; Heads of State decide.",
 "|The Economic Community of West African States has made impressive progress in the area of|free movement of persons and right of residence|increased trade among members|political integration of the region|providing financial aid to its members": "ECOWAS was founded on 28 May 1975 by the Treaty of Lagos. Its 1979 Protocol on Free Movement of Persons, Right of Residence and Establishment lets community citizens enter any member state without a visa, and later stay and work there. This has been its most visible success, together with the ECOWAS passport and peacekeeping by ECOMOG: A. The trap: B, increased trade, was the founders' main goal, but trade among members has stayed low (about a tenth of their total trade), because they export similar raw materials. This answer is a correction of a debatable point. Memory tip: ECOWAS = free movement, without a visa, across West Africa.",
 "|Delegated legislation becomes unavoidable when|legislators cannot reach a consensus|issues under consideration are personal|issues under consideration are technical|legislators have to proceed on a recess": "Delegated legislation is law made by a body other than the legislature (a minister, ministry, agency or local council) under powers given by a parent act. It becomes unavoidable because modern law involves many technical details, such as drug standards, road safety rules or electricity tariffs, that legislators lack the expertise and time to work out. Parliament passes the broad act and leaves the experts to fill in the details: C. The trap: D, a legislative recess, is sometimes listed as a reason for delegating power, but delegation becomes unavoidable mainly because technical issues need expert detail that legislators lack. Memory tip: delegated legislation = Details by experts; the legislature sets the frame.",
 "|One major disadvantage of public opinion is that|the critics of government policies are always harassed|a vocal minority claims to represent the majority|gossip and rumours thrive|leaders are unnecessarily criticized": "Public opinion is the view held by most people on a public issue. Its great weakness is that it is hard to measure accurately. A small, well-organised, noisy group (a vocal minority) can dominate the media and protests and claim to speak for \"the people\", while the silent majority may think differently. Government may then act on a false picture: B. Gossip and rumour (C) come from poor information; the real weakness of public opinion is that a vocal minority can pass itself off as the majority. Memory tip: loud is not the same as many.",
 "|Citizenship in a modern state expresses the status of a person who possesses|full political rights|some religious rights|social rights only|exclusive economic rights": "Citizenship is the full legal membership of a state. A citizen enjoys full civil and political rights: to vote and be voted for, to hold public office, to a passport and to live in the country. In return he owes duties such as obeying laws and paying taxes. Aliens may enjoy some civil rights, but not full political rights: A. Social rights only (C) and exclusive economic rights (D) are too narrow, and religious rights (B) are usually given to everyone; what marks a citizen is full political rights. Memory tip: citizen = full political membership; alien = guest without the vote.",
 "|Communism is a system which recognizes|class stratification|the existence of the state|the existence of the individual|the ability of the individual": "Karl Marx described communism as the final stage of society, in which classes have disappeared, the state has \"withered away\" and goods are shared by the rule \"from each according to his ability, to each according to his needs\". Everyone contributes to the common good according to what he is able to do, so communism recognizes the ability of the individual: D. The trap: C, \"the existence of the individual\", sounds humane, but it says nothing special, since every system recognizes that individuals exist. A and B describe exactly what communism aims to end: class stratification and the state. This verdict differs from the official answer key, which gives C. Memory tip: communism = ability in, needs out.",
 "|The delineation of constituencies is a major duty of the|national assembly|political parties|boundary commission|electoral commission": "Delineation (delimitation) of constituencies means dividing a country into electoral areas of roughly equal population, each returning one representative. In Nigeria this is a duty of the electoral commission: today INEC reviews the boundaries under the 1999 Constitution, usually after a census: D. In other countries, such as Britain, an independent boundary commission does it, and Nigeria itself used a Constituency Delimitation Commission in the 1950s, so C is also accepted. The trap: A, the National Assembly, only approves INEC's proposals; it does not draw the lines. Political parties (B) must never draw boundaries, or they would gerrymander. Memory tip: neutral umpires draw the lines; players never do.",
 "|The structure of the civil service is based on|lateral organization|merit system|patronage system|hierarchical organization": "The civil service is organised as a hierarchy, like a pyramid. At the top of each ministry is the Permanent Secretary (head of the ministry's service), then directors, deputy directors and so on down to junior staff. Orders flow down and responsibility flows up, each officer answering to the one directly above him: D. The trap: B, merit system, is the basis of recruitment and promotion (appointment by qualification and examination), not the shape of the structure. C, patronage, is the opposite of merit, and A, lateral organization, would mean no ranks at all. Memory tip: recruitment = merit; structure = hierarchy (the pyramid).",
 "|A common feature of a multi-party system is that government is formed by|the major political parties|all the registered political parties|a coalition of political parties|the party with the highest votes": "In a multi-party system several strong parties compete. Seats are usually shared so widely that no single party wins an outright majority in parliament. To form a government, two or more parties must agree to work together and share ministerial posts: a coalition government. Examples are Italy, Israel and Nigeria's First Republic, where the NPC and NCNC formed a coalition at the centre in 1959: C. The trap: D, the party with the highest votes, governs alone only if it also has a majority of seats, which is typical of a two-party system. B is impossible in practice. Memory tip: many parties, many partners: multi-party means coalition.",
 "|To qualify for absorption into the administrative cadre of the civil service in Nigeria, an applicant must be|knowledgeable in civil service rules|holder of a first university degree|specifically trained in public administration|a senior civil servant": "The Nigerian civil service has several cadres: administrative (senior policy officers), professional (doctors, engineers), executive, clerical and so on. The administrative cadre advises on and shapes policy, so entry requires at least a first university degree or its equivalent; the subject can be any discipline, and recruits then learn on the job: B. The trap: C, specially trained in public administration, sounds right but is not required; graduates of history, law or economics are all admitted. A comes after entry, not before. D is wrong because the cadre takes fresh graduates, not only senior officers. Memory tip: administrative cadre = degree at the door.",
 "|The final interpretation of the provisions of a federal constitution is vested in the|head of state|council of state|highest legislative body|highest court of the land": "A federal constitution divides powers between the centre and the units, so disputes over who may do what are certain. The final word on what the constitution means must belong to a neutral umpire: the highest court of the land. In Nigeria that is the Supreme Court, whose interpretation binds every government and court: D. The trap: C, the highest legislative body, makes laws but cannot be the final judge of them, otherwise it could give itself any power it liked. A, the head of state, and B, the council of state, are executive or advisory bodies. Memory tip: legislature makes, executive executes, Supreme Court has the final say on meaning.",
 "|A major issue that distinguishes pressure groups from political parties is|membership drive|the objective|the voting pattern|ideology": "Political parties and pressure groups both organise people to influence government, but their objectives differ. A political party aims to capture and control government by putting up candidates for elections. A pressure group, such as the NLC or the NBA, does not seek to rule; it only tries to influence the policies of whoever is in power in favour of its members' interests: B. The trap: D, ideology, is tempting, but both parties and pressure groups may or may not have an ideology. A membership drive is done by both. Memory tip: party wants the seat; pressure group wants the ear of whoever holds the seat.",
 "|Proportional representation is a system of allocating seats in the legislature based on|gender participation in politics|an area's involvement in politics|contribution to the national economy|total votes in an election": "Proportional representation (PR) gives each party a share of seats roughly equal to its share of the total votes cast. If a party wins 30% of the votes, it gets about 30% of the seats, usually through party lists in large multi-member constituencies: D. This contrasts with the first-past-the-post system, where the candidate with the most votes in each constituency wins and many votes are \"wasted\". Proportional representation shares seats by each party's share of the total vote, not by an area's involvement (B), gender (A) or economic contribution (C). Memory tip: PR = Proportion of votes = Proportion of seats.",
 "|The application of the principle of separation of powers seems impracticable because power is|delegated|centralized|fused|separated": "Separation of powers (Montesquieu, 1748) means legislative, executive and judicial powers should be held by different people. In practice no modern government can keep them fully apart: the executive makes delegated laws, the legislature confirms appointments, and in the parliamentary system the ministers are members of the legislature. Powers overlap, or are fused, which makes strict separation impracticable: C. The trap: B, centralized, describes a unitary state, which can still separate the three organs. A, delegated, is one example of overlap but not the general reason. Memory tip: pure separation is a theory; fusion and overlap are the practice.",
 "|One major factor that differentiates the presidential from the parliamentary system is|separation of powers|judicial independence|passage of bills|party system": "The key difference between the two systems is how the executive relates to the legislature. In the presidential system (USA, Nigeria since 1979) the president is elected separately, cannot be a member of the legislature and serves a fixed term: separation of powers. In the parliamentary system (Britain, Nigeria 1960–1966) the Prime Minister and ministers come from and sit in parliament: fusion of powers: A. Judicial independence (B), passing bills (C) and party systems (D) exist in both systems; only separation of powers sets the presidential system apart. Memory tip: presidential = separate; parliamentary = fused.",
 "|A major consequence of proportional representation is that it|reduces the chances of political instability|favours the developments of a two-party system|discourages voting along ethnic lines|encourages the proliferation of parties": "Because proportional representation gives seats even to parties with a small share of the vote, it pays to form new parties: a party with 5% of the votes can still win about 5% of the seats. So PR encourages the growth of many parties (proliferation): D. That often produces coalition governments, which can be unstable, so A is the opposite of what usually happens. The trap: B, a two-party system, is linked with first-past-the-post (Duverger's law), not PR. C is wrong because PR can allow ethnic parties to win seats. Memory tip: PR multiplies parties; first-past-the-post squeezes them to two.",
 "|Oligarchy is a form of government which|enhances the interest of the ruling few|enhances the electoral chances of the majority|disregards the views of the minority|protects the interest of the common people": "Oligarchy (Greek oligoi, \"few\") is government by a small group of people, often the rich, a military clique or a few powerful families. Aristotle called it the corrupt form of aristocracy because the few rule mainly in their own interest rather than for the good of all: A. The trap: C, disregarding the views of the minority, is wrong, since the ruling few are the minority; it is the majority whose views are ignored. B and D describe democracy. Memory tip: aristocracy = the best few for all; oligarchy = the few for themselves.",
 "|The absence of the rule of law in government will bring about|human rights abuse|treasonable offences|corrupt practices|political apathy": "The rule of law (A. V. Dicey) means that the law is supreme, everyone is equal before it, and no one may be punished except for a breach of law proved in court. Where it is absent, rulers act arbitrarily: people are detained without trial, denied fair hearing, and lose their freedom of speech and property. The direct result is abuse of human rights: A. The trap: C, corrupt practices, often flourish too, but the rule of law exists first of all to protect individual rights. Treasonable offences (B) are crimes by citizens, and apathy (D) has many causes. Memory tip: no rule of law, no rights.",
 "|Centralization of power is the basic feature of|federalism|a confederation|a presidential system|a unitary government": "In a unitary system (Britain, France, Ghana), all governmental power is concentrated (centralized) in one central government. Any regional or local bodies exist only because the centre created them and can be changed or abolished by it: D. The trap: A, federalism, divides power between the centre and the units under the constitution, which is the opposite of centralization. B, a confederation, leaves most power with the member states. C, a presidential system, describes the relationship between executive and legislature, not between centre and units. Memory tip: unitary = one centre holds all; federal = shared; confederal = units hold most.",
 "|A law passed by the legislature and assented to by the executive is|an act|a presidential proclamation|a decree|a legislative order": "A bill becomes an act (a statute) once it has passed through the legislature and received the assent of the executive (the President or Governor in Nigeria): A. The trap: C, a decree, is a law made by a military government, which has no legislature, so it never goes through this process; edicts were the state-level version under military rule. B, a presidential proclamation, is an executive announcement, and D, a legislative order, is not a standard term for law. Memory tip: legislature + assent = act; military council = decree.",
 "|One of the features of a sovereign state is that it|has the capacity to defend itself from external aggression|has a large number of soldiers|practices the presidential system of government|is not indebted to other countries": "A sovereign state has supreme power within its territory and is independent of outside control. To keep that independence, it must be able to defend itself against external aggression; a state that cannot protect its borders soon loses its sovereignty in practice: A. Sovereignty does not need a large army (B); small states such as The Gambia are sovereign, and neither the system of government (C) nor foreign debt (D) affects it. Memory tip: sovereignty = supreme inside, independent outside.",
 "|A major way of maintaining confidence in the electoral process is by ensuring that|electoral officers are regularly trained|elections are conducted in a free and fair atmosphere|unlimited franchise is observed|elections are conducted as and when due": "People trust an electoral process when they believe their votes count. That requires elections conducted in a free atmosphere (no intimidation, bribery or violence) and a fair one (equal rules for all candidates, honest counting, a neutral electoral body): B. The trap: D, elections held as and when due, matters too, but a rigged election held on schedule still destroys confidence. A, trained officers, helps achieve free and fair elections but is only a means. C, unlimited franchise, does not exist anywhere; age and citizenship always limit the vote. Memory tip: confidence comes from FREE (no fear) and FAIR (no fraud).",
 "|The principle of checks and balances reinforces separation of power in order to|protect the powers of the executive|make the legislature more powerful|prevent an unconstitutional change of government|prevent the emergence of dictatorship": "Separation of powers gives each organ its own work. Checks and balances add the means for each to restrain the others: the President can veto bills, the legislature can override vetoes, approve appointments and impeach, and the courts can strike down unconstitutional acts. The aim is to stop power from gathering in one person or organ, which is the road to dictatorship: D. The trap: C sounds close, but checks and balances work inside the constitution; they cannot stop a military coup. A and B are wrong because the system makes no organ supreme. Memory tip: check each organ, and no dictator can grow.",
 "|Legislative control of public corporations in Nigeria is effected through|the dissolution of their managements|the reorganization of their boards|acts of the National Assembly|bye-laws": "Public corporations, such as the NNPC or the old NEPA, are created by acts of the legislature. The legislature controls them through those acts: it can amend or repeal the enabling act, approve their budgets, require annual reports, and investigate them through committees such as the Public Accounts Committee: C. Dissolving managements (A) and reorganizing boards (B) are executive actions by the supervising minister, and bye-laws (D) are made by local governments; the legislature controls public corporations through its acts. Memory tip: the legislature controls corporations through the law that gave them life.",
 "|The Ibadan Conference of 1950 was convened to discuss the provision of the|Macpherson Constitution|Clifford Constitution|Lyttleton Constitution|Richards Constitution": "The Richards Constitution (1946) was unpopular because it was imposed without consulting Nigerians. Governor John Macpherson therefore organised wide consultation, from village meetings up to the General Conference at Ibadan in January 1950, which discussed proposals for a new constitution. The result was the Macpherson Constitution of 1951: A. The trap: D, Richards, was the constitution being reviewed, not the one being prepared. Clifford (B) dated from 1922, and Lyttleton (C) came from the London and Lagos conferences of 1953–1954. Memory tip: Macpherson = the first constitution made after consulting Nigerians, at Ibadan, 1950.",
 "|The fees collected by local governments at motor parks represent|levies|fines|income tax|user charge": "Local governments raise revenue from rates, taxes, licences, fees and levies. Charges collected from drivers and transporters at motor parks are classed as levies: the Taxes and Levies (Approved List for Collection) Act of 1998 names \"motor park levies\" among the items local governments may collect: A. The trap: D, user charge, is tempting because drivers pay for using the park, and economists might use that term, but in Nigerian law and in JAMB's syllabus these are levies. B, fines, are penalties for offences. C, income tax, is levied by the states and the federal government on earnings. Memory tip: motor park = levy, market stall = rate or fee, offence = fine.",
 "|The first Nigerian constitution to provide for the fundamental objectives and directive principles of state policy was the|1979 Constitution|1989 Constitution|1999 Constitution|1960 Constitution": "The Fundamental Objectives and Directive Principles of State Policy are goals the government must work towards, such as social justice, free education where practicable and national unity. They first appeared in Chapter II of the 1979 Constitution, drafted by the Constitution Drafting Committee of 1975–1976, and were repeated in the 1989 and 1999 constitutions: A. Unlike fundamental rights (Chapter IV), they cannot be enforced in court. The trap: C, 1999, is the current constitution, so students think of it first, but it copied 1979. D, 1960, had fundamental rights but no directive principles. Memory tip: 1979 gave Chapter II.",
 "|Under the 1999 Constitution of Nigeria, the power to create local government is vested in the|state assembly|office of the deputy governor|presidency|national assembly": "Section 8(3) of the 1999 Constitution lets a State House of Assembly create a new local government area. The bill needs support from at least two-thirds of the representatives of the area in the House and in the area's local councils, a referendum approved by two-thirds of the people of the area, and a two-thirds majority of the whole House. The National Assembly then amends the First Schedule to list the new area: A. The trap: D, the National Assembly, only lists the area afterwards; it does not start or create it. B and C are executive offices with no such power. This is still the law today. Memory tip: states create LGAs; the centre only lists them.",
 "|The body set up to review the revenue allocation formula in 1980 was the|Udoji Commission|Okigbo Commission|Dina Commission|Adebo Commission": "Revenue allocation has been reviewed many times. In 1979, President Shagari set up a commission headed by Dr Pius Okigbo; it reported in 1980 and recommended sharing the Federation Account mainly among the federal, state and local governments. Its work led to the Revenue Allocation Act of 1981: B. The trap: C, Dina (1968), came earlier and was rejected; Udoji (A, 1972–1974) reviewed public service salaries and structure, and Adebo (D, 1970–1971) reviewed wages and salaries. Today the Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission (RMAFC) reviews the formula. Memory tip: Okigbo = the 1980 allocation review; Udoji and Adebo = pay reviews.",
 "|In pre-colonial Igbo society, decisions on public issues were reached mainly through|the oracles|consensus|imposition|majority votes": "Pre-colonial Igbo society was largely acephalous (\"headless\"): there was no central king ruling over all Igbo land. Each village was governed by the village assembly of adult males, the council of elders (ofo holders), age grades and title societies such as Ozo. Public issues were debated openly until general agreement was reached; decisions were by consensus: B. The trap: A, the oracles (such as the Arochukwu oracle), were consulted for difficult disputes and judgements, but they were not the normal way of deciding public matters. C, imposition, describes a centralised monarchy such as an emirate. Memory tip: Igbo enwe eze (the Igbo have no king): talk until everyone agrees.",
 "|The main duty of the Local Government Service Commission is to|supervise and manage the affairs of the local governments|create job opportunities at the local level|handle requests for the creation of more local governments|conduct elections into local councils": "Each state has a Local Government Service Commission that manages the local government service. It appoints, posts, promotes and disciplines senior local government staff, and so supervises how the councils are run: A. The trap: D is tempting because it sounds like a \"commission\" duty, but elections into local councils are conducted by the State Independent Electoral Commission (SIEC). C belongs to the State House of Assembly (see section 8 of the 1999 Constitution), and B, creating jobs, is a general aim of all governments. Memory tip: Service Commission = staff and management; Electoral Commission = elections.",
 "|The constitution that introduced restricted franchise into Nigerian politics was the|Independence Constitution|Lyttleton Constitution|Richards Constitution|Clifford Constitution": "The Clifford Constitution of 1922, under Governor Hugh Clifford, introduced the elective principle in Nigeria: four elected members in the Legislative Council (three for Lagos, one for Calabar). But only adult males resident in those towns with a gross annual income of at least £100 could vote, which was a restricted franchise: D. The trap: C, Richards (1946), and B, Lyttleton (1954), kept or widened voting but did not introduce it. A, the Independence Constitution of 1960, provided for universal adult suffrage in the South. Memory tip: Clifford 1922 = first election, Lagos and Calabar, £100 rule.",
 "|In Nigeria, the Council of State was first created by|General Murtala Muhammed|General Olusegun Obasanjo|Major-General Aguiyi Ironsi|General Yakubu Gowon": "After Murtala Muhammed took power in July 1975, the Constitution (Basic Provisions) Decree of 1975 set up three bodies: the Supreme Military Council (the highest), the Federal Executive Council and the National Council of States, made up of the state military governors. That was the first council of states in Nigeria: A. The 1979 Constitution later created the Council of State, which the 1999 Constitution keeps, made up of the President, former Presidents, former Chief Justices, state governors and others. The trap: D, Gowon, ruled through the Supreme Military Council, on which the governors sat, without a separate council of states. B, Obasanjo, continued Murtala's arrangement. Memory tip: Murtala 1975: SMC, FEC and the National Council of States.",
 "|The leading agent in the expansion of British influence in Northern Nigeria in the 1890s was the|British Consul in Calabar|Royal West African Frontier Force|British Consul in Lagos|Royal Niger Company": "In the 1880s–1890s British interests on the Niger and Benue were advanced by George Goldie's Royal Niger Company, which received a royal charter in 1886. It signed treaties with northern rulers, kept French and German rivals out, maintained its own constabulary and administered the area until its charter was revoked in 1899; on 1 January 1900 the British government took over and proclaimed the Protectorate of Northern Nigeria under Lugard: D. The trap: B, the West African Frontier Force, was formed by Lugard in 1897–1898 and did conquer the north after 1900, but the company led the earlier expansion. Consuls (A, C) worked on the coast. Memory tip: in the north, trade came before the flag.",
 "|The two military coups that toppled civilian regimes in Nigeria were those of|January 1966 and December 1983|July 1966 and August 1985|January 1966 and July 1975|February 1966 and December 1983": "Only two Nigerian coups overthrew elected civilian governments. On 15 January 1966 young officers led by Major Chukwuma Kaduna Nzeogwu ended the First Republic, killing Prime Minister Tafawa Balewa; Ironsi took over. On 31 December 1983 Buhari's coup ended Shagari's Second Republic: A. The trap: the other coups toppled military regimes: July 1966 (Ironsi to Gowon), July 1975 (Gowon to Murtala) and August 1985 (Buhari to Babangida). D's \"February 1966\" is a wrong date: no coup took place then (the failed Dimka coup of February 1976 killed Murtala). Memory tip: civilian regimes fell in January 1966 and December 1983: start of one year, end of another.",
 "|The main function of the Code of Conduct Bureau is to|ensure accountability in government business|give the judiciary more power to discipline erring judges|protect public officers from the press|give the police more powers to make arrests": "The Code of Conduct Bureau, created by the 1979 Constitution and retained in the 1999 Constitution (Fifth Schedule), enforces the code of conduct for public officers. It receives and checks the asset declarations public officers must make, and refers breaches (such as false declarations or conflicts of interest) to the Code of Conduct Tribunal. Its purpose is honesty and accountability in public business: A. Disciplining judges (B) belongs to the National Judicial Council, and C and D are not its work; the Bureau exists to make public officers accountable, for example through asset declaration. Memory tip: Code of Conduct = declare your assets, account for your office.",
 "|The emirate system of administration can be likened to a|confederal system of government|unitary system of government|federal system of government|constitutional monarchy": "In the Hausa-Fulani emirates, power was centralised in the emir. He appointed and could remove the district heads (hakimai) and village heads, controlled the courts through the alkalai he appointed, and collected taxes. All authority flowed from one centre, as in a unitary system of government: B. The trap: D, constitutional monarchy, is tempting because the emir is a monarch, but a constitutional monarch only reigns while others rule, whereas the emir governed with near-absolute power, limited mainly by Islamic law. A and C wrongly suggest shared or divided power within the emirate. Memory tip: emirate = one ruler, one centre = unitary.",
 "|The abolition of the state ministries of local government in 1989 entails that local governments|are equal to the states|have more control over their funds|no longer have anything to do with state governments|are no longer subordinate to state governments": "Under the Babangida government's reforms of 1988–1989 the state ministries of local government, which had supervised and often controlled councils, were abolished, and federal allocations were paid to local governments directly. The aim was to make local government a tier of government in its own right, no longer subordinate to the states: D. The trap: A goes too far: local governments did not become equal to states. C is also too extreme, since states still had some role. Today states again have ministries of local government, though a 2024 Supreme Court ruling requires federal allocations to be paid to councils directly. Memory tip: abolishing the supervisor = freeing the supervised.",
 "|The main legislative body in Nigeria between 1966 and 1975 was the|Supreme Military Council|Armed Forces Ruling Council|Provisional Ruling Council|National Security Council": "From January 1966 until July 1975, under Ironsi and then Gowon, the highest ruling and law-making body was the Supreme Military Council (SMC); it made laws by decree. The SMC continued under Murtala and Obasanjo (to 1979) and under Buhari (1984–1985): A. The trap: the other bodies came later: the Armed Forces Ruling Council (B) under Babangida (1985–1993), the Provisional Ruling Council (C) under Abacha and Abubakar (1993–1999). The National Security Council (D) is an advisory body for security matters. Memory tip: SMC (1966–79, 1984–85) → AFRC (Babangida) → PRC (Abacha, Abubakar).",
 "|Nigeria formally became a federation in|1954|1963|1914|1946": "Nigeria became a federation under the Lyttleton Constitution of 1954, named after the Colonial Secretary Oliver Lyttleton. It divided powers between the central government and the Northern, Eastern and Western Regions, with exclusive and concurrent lists, and made Lagos a Federal Territory: A. The trap: B, 1963, is when Nigeria became a republic and the Mid-West Region was created. C, 1914, was amalgamation into one colony, which was unitary. D, 1946, was the Richards Constitution, which created the regions but kept a unitary government. Memory tip: 1914 amalgamation, 1946 regions, 1954 federation, 1960 independence, 1963 republic.",
 "|The United Nations charter is an instrument that|sets out the rights and obligations of member states|provides for funding of member states|facilitates the resolution of disputes among member states|determines the admission of member states": "The Charter of the United Nations, signed in San Francisco on 26 June 1945, is the founding treaty of the UN. It sets out the purposes and principles of the organisation, establishes its six principal organs, and lays down the rights and obligations of member states, such as sovereign equality, peaceful settlement of disputes and not using force against other states: A. The trap: C, resolving disputes, is one function of the UN under the Charter, not what the Charter as a whole is. D, admission of members, is decided by the General Assembly on the Security Council's recommendation. B is not a Charter function. Memory tip: the Charter is the UN's constitution: rights and duties of members.",
 "|The most active organ of the Economic Community of West African States is the|Tribunal of the Community|Technical and Specialized Commissions|Authority of Heads of State and Government|Council of Ministers": "The Authority of Heads of State and Government is the supreme institution of ECOWAS. It meets regularly, sets the Community's direction, takes all major decisions (on trade, free movement, peacekeeping through ECOMOG, sanctions) and appoints top officials, so it is the most active and powerful organ: C. The trap: D, the Council of Ministers, prepares work for the Authority and advises it, but it does not take the final decisions. The Tribunal (now the Community Court of Justice, A) and the Technical and Specialized Commissions have narrower roles. Memory tip: in ECOWAS, the heads hold the power: the Authority decides.",
 "|The group of states that conceived the idea of the United Nations Organization included|the USSR, Italy, the UK and China|Italy, the UK, France and Japan|the USA, the USSR, the UK and China|France, the USA, Canada and Japan": "The UN was planned by the main Allied powers of the Second World War. The USA, the USSR, the UK and China signed the Moscow Declaration (1943), calling for a general international organisation, and drafted its plan at Dumbarton Oaks, Washington (1944). The Yalta Conference (1945) settled the veto, and the San Francisco Conference adopted the Charter in June 1945: C. The trap: options A, B and D include Italy or Japan, which were Axis powers, enemies of the Allies, and could not have conceived the UN. France joined the Big Four later as a permanent member. Memory tip: the founders were the Big Four Allies: USA, USSR, UK, China.",
 "|The impact of the Commonwealth of Nations is felt most in the area of|diplomatic cooperation|economic cooperation|cultural cooperation|military cooperation": "The Commonwealth is a voluntary association of Britain and its former colonies. It has no military alliance and only limited diplomatic weight, and its members' cultures differ widely. Its practical value to members has been mainly economic: trade preferences (the old Commonwealth preference system), aid, and technical assistance through the Commonwealth Fund for Technical Co-operation: B. The trap: C, cultural cooperation, is tempting because of the Commonwealth Games, the English language and scholarships, but these are by-products; the Commonwealth's main funds and programmes are economic. D is wrong: there is no Commonwealth army. Memory tip: Commonwealth = common WEALTH, so economics first.",
 "|The greatest criticism of the Security Council of the UNO is that it|has no standing army|is not representative enough|undermines the General Assembly|has exclusive veto power": "The UN Security Council has 15 members, five of them permanent: the USA, the UK, France, Russia (formerly the USSR) and China. Only these five hold the veto, so any one of them can block a decision even if all others agree. This has often paralysed the Council and is its most criticised feature: D. This is still true today. The trap: B, not representative enough, is also a strong criticism (no permanent seat for Africa or Latin America), but much of that complaint concerns who holds the veto. A, no standing army, is a weakness of the UN, not a criticism of the Council itself. Memory tip: five vetoes, one \"no\" stops all.",
 "|Which of the following is a founding member of OPEC?|Nigeria|Indonesia|Venezuela|Algeria": "OPEC (Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries) was founded at the Baghdad Conference in September 1960 by five countries: Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia and Venezuela: C. Venezuela's oil minister Juan Pablo Pérez Alfonzo was one of the founding fathers. The trap: the others joined later: Indonesia (B) in 1962, Algeria (D) in 1969 and Nigeria (A) in 1971. Indonesia has since left the organisation. Memory tip: OPEC's founding five: \"I I K S V\" (Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia, Venezuela), the only non-Middle-East founder being Venezuela.",
 "|Professor Ibrahim Gambari is the Special Assistant to the United Nations Secretary-General on|African affairs|political and social matters|the Economic Commission of Africa|security matters": "Professor Ibrahim Agboola Gambari, a Nigerian scholar and diplomat, was Nigeria's Foreign Minister (1984–1985) and Permanent Representative to the UN (1990–1999). When this paper was set he was UN Under-Secretary-General and Special Adviser on Africa to Secretary-General Kofi Annan: A. The trap: C is wrong because the Economic Commission for Africa is a separate UN body with its own Executive Secretary. Since then Gambari has held other posts: Under-Secretary-General for Political Affairs (2005–2007), head of the UN–AU mission in Darfur, and Chief of Staff to President Buhari (2020–2023). Memory tip: Gambari = Nigeria's man for Africa at the UN.",
 "|Who among the following served as Secretary-General of OPEC?|Jibril Aminu|Aret Adams|Dan Etete|Rilwanu Lukman": "Chief Rilwanu Lukman, a mining engineer and Nigeria's petroleum minister in several governments, served as Secretary-General of OPEC from 1995 to 2000, after earlier serving as President of the OPEC Conference: D. Later, another Nigerian, Mohammed Sanusi Barkindo, was OPEC Secretary-General from 2016 to 2022. The trap: Jibril Aminu (A, petroleum minister 1989–1992), Dan Etete (C, petroleum minister 1995–1998) and Aret Adams (B, an NNPC chief executive) held top Nigerian oil posts but were never OPEC Secretary-General. Memory tip: Lukman led OPEC's secretariat in Vienna (1995–2000); Barkindo followed later.",
 "|Nigeria's active role in the liberation of some countries in Southern Africa earned her|Chairmanship of the Eminent Persons|Membership of SADC|Giant of Africa|the status of the frontline state": "The frontline states were the African countries bordering apartheid South Africa and white-ruled Rhodesia (Angola, Botswana, Mozambique, Tanzania, Zambia, later Zimbabwe), which supported the liberation movements. Nigeria was far away, but it gave so much money, diplomatic pressure and support to liberation in Angola, Zimbabwe, Namibia and South Africa (for example, nationalising BP in 1979) that it was called an honorary frontline state: D. The trap: C, \"Giant of Africa\", is a nickname from Nigeria's size and population, not from its liberation role. Nigeria is not in SADC (B). Memory tip: far from the front, but a frontline state in spirit.",
 "|Which of these international finance agencies is Nigeria a member of?|London Club|The Infrastructural Development Fund|The Paris Club|The International Monetary Fund": "The International Monetary Fund (IMF), set up at Bretton Woods in 1944, lends to member countries with balance-of-payments problems and advises on economic policy. Nigeria joined in 1961, soon after independence, and is still a member: D. The trap: the London Club (A) and the Paris Club (C) are groups of creditors (commercial banks and creditor governments) to which Nigeria owed debts; Nigeria negotiated with them as a debtor, but was never a member. Nigeria paid off its Paris Club debt in 2006. Memory tip: Nigeria is IN the IMF but only OWED the Clubs.",
 "|Nigeria's membership of the ECOWAS is informed by her desire to|develop a market in the sub-region|form sub-regional high command|become a sub-regional power|promote economic integration": "ECOWAS was created by the Treaty of Lagos on 28 May 1975, with Nigeria (under Gowon) and Togo leading the effort. Its main aim is economic integration of West Africa: free movement of goods, people and services, a common external tariff and eventually a single market and currency. Nigeria joined to promote that integration: D. The trap: A, developing a market, is part of integration but narrower, and B and C are not the purposes of ECOWAS; Nigeria's foreign policy stresses cooperation, not domination. Note that in January 2025 Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger left ECOWAS. Memory tip: ECOWAS = Economic Community: economics first.",
 "|An attribute that Nigeria shares with most nonaligned countries is|her large population|the state of her economy|her heterogeneous population|her large size": "The Non-Aligned Movement (founded in Belgrade, 1961) brought together states that refused to join either Cold War bloc. Most of its members, like Nigeria, were newly independent, developing countries whose economies depended on exporting primary products and which needed aid and investment. That shared state of the economy is what Nigeria has in common with most of them: B. A large population (A) and large size (D) set Nigeria apart from most non-aligned states, many of which are small; what it shares with them is a developing economy. Memory tip: non-aligned = developing, the \"Third World\".",
 "|One of the duties of the legislature is to|exercise oversight|implement laws|promulgate decrees|adjudicate disputes": "The legislature has three big jobs: making laws, controlling public money and overseeing the executive. Oversight means watching how the executive carries out laws and spends money, through committee hearings, questions to ministers, investigations, and approval of appointments and budgets. So one duty of the legislature is to exercise oversight: A. Implementing laws (B) is the executive's job, adjudicating disputes (D) is the judiciary's, and promulgating decrees (C) is what military governments do in place of a legislature. Memory tip: legislature MAKES and WATCHES, executive DOES, judiciary JUDGES.",
 "|The best form of government for a heterogeneous society is a|quasi-federal system|confederal system|unitary system|federal system": "A heterogeneous society contains many ethnic, language and religious groups. Federalism divides power between a central government and regional (state) governments under a written constitution, so each group can manage its own local affairs while all share common services such as defence and currency. That is why Nigeria, India and Switzerland are federations: D. The trap: a confederal system (B) also allows diversity, but its centre is so weak that it often breaks up; a unitary system (C) concentrates power at the centre and can make minorities fear domination. Memory tip: many peoples, one country = federalism (\"unity in diversity\").",
 "|Removal by impeachment applies to the position of a|chief judge|president|Prime Minister|cabinet minister": "Impeachment is the process by which the legislature charges and removes an executive president (and in Nigeria, the vice-president, governors and deputy governors) for gross misconduct. In Nigeria's 1999 Constitution it is in section 143 for the president: a notice signed by a third of the members, investigation by a panel, and a two-thirds vote of each house: B. The trap: a Prime Minister (C) and his cabinet ministers (D) are removed by a vote of no confidence in parliament; a chief judge (A) is removed by the legislature's address or by the National Judicial Council, not by impeachment. Memory tip: President = Impeachment; Prime Minister = No confidence.",
 "|Government by the wealthy is known as|oligarchy|aristocracy|plutocracy|democracy": "Plutocracy comes from the Greek \"ploutos\", meaning wealth: it is government by the rich, where money buys power and policy serves the wealthy: C. The trap: oligarchy (A) is rule by a small group, which is often rich but is defined by its small size, not its wealth. Aristocracy (B) is rule by a noble or \"best\" class, usually by birth. Democracy (D) is rule by the people. Memory tip: PLUTO-cracy = rule by PLUTO, the Greek god of riches.",
 "|The independence of the judiciary can be undermined through the|payment of the salaries of judicial officers by government|appointment of the minister of justice as the Attorney-General|politicization of the appointments of judges|confirmation of the appointment of judges by the legislature": "Judicial independence means judges decide cases without fear or favour, free from pressure by the executive, the legislature or parties. It is protected by appointment on merit, security of tenure and salaries charged on the Consolidated Fund. When appointments are politicized, that is, judges are picked for party loyalty, they may feel indebted to politicians and the courts lose impartiality: C. The trap: A sounds dangerous, but government must pay judges; what protects them is that their pay is fixed by law and cannot be cut. D (legislative confirmation) is actually a safeguard against bad appointments. Memory tip: merit appointment, secure tenure, guaranteed pay = independent judges.",
 "|The unrestrained power of the state over its citizens is underlined by|self-determination|patriotism|sovereignty|nationalism": "Sovereignty is the supreme, absolute and final legal power of the state over all persons and things within its territory, and its independence from outside control. Jean Bodin (1576) called it absolute and indivisible, and John Austin described the sovereign as the one habitually obeyed. This unlimited power over citizens is exactly what sovereignty expresses: C. Self-determination (A) is a people's right to choose their government, and patriotism (B) and nationalism (D) are feelings of loyalty; none describes the state's supreme legal power. Memory tip: Sovereignty = Supreme power inside, independence outside.",
 "|In a parliamentary system, the functions of the head of state and the head of government are vested in|the ministerial council|an individual|the inner cabinet|two different individuals": "A parliamentary system has a dual executive. The head of state is ceremonial: a monarch (as in Britain) or a figurehead president or Governor-General (as in Nigeria's First Republic, Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe). The head of government holds real power: the Prime Minister (Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa). So the two roles are in two different individuals: D. The trap: B, \"an individual\", is the presidential system, where one person (for example, Nigeria's executive president since 1979) is both head of state and head of government. Memory tip: parliamentary = two heads (ceremonial + real); presidential = one head does both.",
 "|A collegial executive is a government in which power is vested in a|committee|monarch|parliament|president": "A collegial executive is a plural executive: power is held jointly by a committee or council whose members are equals, and decisions are taken collectively. The best example is Switzerland's Federal Council of seven members, who rotate the largely ceremonial presidency yearly: A. A parliament (C) is a legislature, not an executive, and a monarch (B) or president (D) is a single-person executive; a collegial executive is a committee. Memory tip: collegial = colleagues = a committee running the country together (think of the Swiss Seven).",
 "|A common feature of government is|the making of public policy|the separation of powers|the independence of the judiciary|a written constitution": "Government is the machinery through which the state's will is formulated and carried out, so every government, democratic or military, federal or unitary, makes and implements public policy: A. Separation of powers (B), judicial independence (C) and a written constitution (D) belong only to some systems; Britain has no single written constitution and parliamentary systems fuse powers. Memory tip: a \"common feature\" must be true of ALL governments, so choose the function, not a structure.",
 "|A meeting of the legislature is usually brought to an end with|a prorogation|a dissolution|suspension|an adjournment": "Learn the three ways a legislature stops meeting. Adjournment ends a single sitting (or a short break) and the house resumes on a set date. Prorogation ends a whole session, cancelling unfinished business until the next session. Dissolution ends the life of the legislature, leading to fresh elections. A meeting is usually ended by adjournment: D. The trap: prorogation (A) sounds formal, but it closes a session of many meetings, not one meeting. Memory tip: Adjourn = a day; Prorogue = a session; Dissolve = the whole parliament.",
 "|The civil service embraces all workers in|public and private companies|all private corporations|public corporations|government ministries": "The civil service is the body of permanent, salaried officials who advise ministers and carry out government policy in the ministries and departments. Civil servants are non-partisan, anonymous and keep their jobs when governments change. So it embraces the workers in government ministries: D. The trap: public corporations (C) such as NNPC have their own staff, who are public servants but not civil servants, and private companies (A, B) are outside government. Memory tip: civil service = ministRIES (staff), not ministERS (politicians).",
 "|The citizenship of a country could be acquired through|registration and arbitration|presidential proclamation|birth and naturalization|parliamentary legislation": "Citizenship is full legal membership of a state. Under Nigeria's constitution (sections 25–27) it is acquired by birth, by registration (for example, a foreign woman married to a Nigerian) and by naturalization (for foreigners who have lived in Nigeria for the required period, 15 years). Option C names two genuine ways: C. The trap: A starts well with registration but adds \"arbitration\", which is a way of settling disputes, not of gaining citizenship. Presidential proclamations (B) and ordinary legislation (D) do not confer citizenship on individuals. Memory tip: B-R-N: Birth, Registration, Naturalization.",
 "|What distinguishes a political party from other social institutions is the desire to|promote the interest of party members|influence the international community on local issues|influence government policies in certain directions|win elections and form a government": "A political party is an organised group of people with similar views who seek to win elections and control the government, to put their programme into action. That aim, capturing power through elections, is what sets parties apart from all other groups: D. The trap: C, influencing government policy, is the aim of pressure groups, which do not want to form a government themselves. Promoting members' interests (A) is true of unions and clubs too. Memory tip: parties want to BE the government; pressure groups want to PUSH the government.",
 "|Capitalism is a system of economic organization based on|very fair distribution of the means of production|a mixed economy that takes all interest into consideration|trading among people who own and control their items of trade|individual ownership of the means of production": "Capitalism (laissez-faire), explained by Adam Smith in \"The Wealth of Nations\" (1776), is an economic system in which land, factories and capital are owned by private individuals and firms, who produce for profit in a free market with little state control: D. A mixed economy (B) combines public and private ownership, fair distribution (A) is socialism's claim, and C describes trade, not who owns the means of production. Memory tip: capitalism = Private ownership + Profit + free market.",
 "|Socialism is a mode of production based on|national ownership of the means of production|mixed ownership of the means of production|state ownership of the means of production|collective ownership of the means of production": "Socialism places the main means of production (land, factories, mines, banks) under social control, which in practice means the state owns and runs them for the benefit of everyone, with central planning and fairer distribution: C. This one is debatable: many textbooks define socialism as \"collective or state ownership\", so D (collective ownership) is also accepted. The trap: B, mixed ownership, describes a mixed economy, not socialism. Note also that Marx saw socialism as the transition stage before communism, a classless, stateless society. Memory tip: socialism = society (through the state) owns; capitalism = individuals own.",
 "|Constitutional disputes in states with written constitutions are resolved by the|legislature|ombudsman|electorate|judiciary": "In a state with a written constitution, the constitution is supreme, and someone neutral must say what it means when governments or citizens disagree. That is the judiciary: through judicial review the courts interpret the constitution and settle disputes, for example between the federal government and the states (in Nigeria, the Supreme Court has original jurisdiction in such cases): D. The legislature (A) cannot judge its own cause, an ombudsman (B) handles only maladministration complaints, and the electorate (C) does not settle legal disputes. Memory tip: constitutional quarrel? Go to court.",
 "|Election as a political process is significant because it|facilitates constitutional change of government|is associated with campaigns for public office|facilitates the recall process|enables citizens to vote": "Elections give the people a peaceful, lawful way to choose their leaders and to replace a government they no longer want, at regular intervals. This orderly, constitutional change of government is their main significance: A. The trap: D, \"enables citizens to vote\", only restates what an election is; it does not say why it matters. Campaigns (B) are part of an election, not its purpose, and recall (C) is a separate device for removing an elected representative. Memory tip: ballots replace bullets: elections change governments by votes, not by force.",
 "|Public opinion becomes politically relevant when it|aggregates views and interests|is in support of government|influences the decision of government|criticizes people in power": "Public opinion is the collective views of a significant part of the people on public issues, expressed through the media, polls, protests and votes. It becomes politically relevant only when it influences government decisions, for example when public outcry leads to a policy reversal: C. The trap: A, \"aggregates views and interests\", describes what parties and pressure groups do; it says nothing about political effect. Opinion need not support (B) or criticize (D) government to matter. Memory tip: public opinion matters when it MOVES government.",
 "|The operation of the rule of law is undermined by the|conspiracy by the two houses of the legislature to impeach the president|inability of the press to discharge its responsibilities|unfriendly attitude of pressure groups|existence of administrative tribunals and special immunities": "A.V. Dicey's rule of law means the supremacy of the law, equality before the law, and rights protected by ordinary courts. Administrative tribunals let officials decide some cases outside the ordinary courts, and special immunities (for the president, governors, diplomats or legislators in debate) put some people beyond the reach of ordinary law. Both break equality before the law, so they undermine the rule of law: D. Impeachment (A) is itself a lawful constitutional process, and a weak press (B) or hostile pressure groups (C) are political problems; administrative tribunals and special immunities exempt some people from the ordinary law. Memory tip: immunity = some people above the law = rule of law limited.",
 "|Associations whose main interest is to influence public policies without having to capture power are|political parties|communal groups|pressure groups|trade unions": "Pressure groups (interest groups) are organised groups that try to influence government policy in favour of their members or cause, by lobbying, petitions, strikes or protests, but do not field candidates to capture power. Examples include the NLC, NBA, NMA and ASUU: C. The trap: political parties (A) do want to capture power. Trade unions (D) are one type of pressure group, so C is the general and complete answer. Communal groups (B) are based on community ties. Memory tip: pressure groups seek INFLUENCE; parties seek POWER.",
 "|One of the legacies of pre-colonial Nigeria destroyed by the British was the|peace and harmony in the land|nation's farmland|education of the local people|indigenous cultures of the people": "Colonial rule imposed Western religion, education, language, law and dress, and often looked down on African ways. Over time many indigenous cultural institutions (traditional religion, festivals, languages, customary values and systems of government) were weakened or destroyed: D. Peace and harmony (A) is wrong because pre-colonial Nigeria had wars and slave raids, and education (C) is wrong because the British introduced Western schooling; what colonial rule eroded was indigenous culture. Memory tip: colonialism changed the CULTURE: religion, language, values.",
 "|The smooth operation of the civil service in Nigeria is mostly hampered by|debt burden and redundancy|poor infrastructure|inadequate training of personnel|corruption and inefficiency": "The Nigerian civil service has long been criticised for corruption (bribery, ghost workers, embezzlement) and inefficiency (delay, red tape, poor productivity). Successive reforms, such as the Udoji Commission (1974) and the 1988 Civil Service Reform, tried to tackle these: D. Poor infrastructure (B) and inadequate training (C) are real but lesser problems; corruption and inefficiency are the main obstacles to the civil service. Memory tip: \"C and I\" (Corruption and Inefficiency) cripple the Civil service.",
 "|The type of government operated in Nigeria between October 1st 1979 and December 31st 1983 is called|presidential system of government|collegial system of government|unitary system of government|parliamentary system of government": "1 October 1979 to 31 December 1983 is the Second Republic. The 1979 Constitution, drafted by the Constitution Drafting Committee chaired by Chief F.R.A. Williams, replaced the British-style parliamentary system with an American-style presidential system. Alhaji Shehu Shagari was the Executive President until the Buhari coup of 31 December 1983: A. The trap: D, parliamentary, was the First Republic system (1960–66) under Prime Minister Tafawa Balewa. Nigeria was a federation, not unitary (C). Memory tip: First Republic = Parliamentary (Balewa); Second Republic = Presidential (Shagari).",
 "|In the pre-colonial Igbo society, the maintenance of peace and order was the function of the|assembly of Ezes|age-grades|assembly of title holders|clan elders": "Pre-colonial Igbo society was acephalous (without central kings) and governed by village assemblies, elders (the Ofo holders), title societies (Ozo), the Umuada and age-grades. Age-grades, groups of men born within a few years of each other, acted as the police and labour force: they kept law and order, enforced the decisions of the assembly and did public works: B. The trap: clan elders (D) made decisions and settled disputes, but the enforcement of peace and order fell to the age-grades. Memory tip: Igbo: elders decide, age-grades enforce.",
 "|The expenditure of public funds by the executive in Nigeria is controlled by the|president|ministry of finance|judiciary|legislature": "The power of the purse belongs to the legislature. The executive cannot spend public money unless the legislature has approved it in an appropriation act (the budget) or a supplementary appropriation. The legislature also checks spending afterwards through the Public Accounts Committees and the Auditor-General's reports: D. The trap: the president (A) proposes the budget and the ministry of finance (B) manages it, but neither controls it; both are part of the executive being controlled. Memory tip: no Appropriation Act, no spending: the legislature holds the purse.",
 "|The 1979 Constitution established the|Federal Civil Defence Corps|National Human Rights Commission|Federal Road Safety Commission|National Population Commission": "The 1979 Constitution set up federal executive bodies such as the Federal Electoral Commission (FEDECO), the Federal Civil Service Commission, the National Population Commission and the Code of Conduct Bureau: D. The other options came later: the Federal Road Safety Commission was created in 1988, the National Human Rights Commission in 1995 and the Nigeria Security and Civil Defence Corps was given legal backing by an act in 2003. The trap: the human rights commission sounds like a constitutional body, but the 1979 Constitution protected rights through Chapter IV and the courts. Memory tip: 1979 counted heads: the Population Commission.",
 "|The Eastern and Western regions of Nigeria achieved the status of self-government in|1959|1950|1955|1957": "After the 1957 London Constitutional Conference, the Western and Eastern Regions became self-governing in 1957 (the West under Chief Obafemi Awolowo, the East under Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe), and the Northern Region followed on 15 March 1959. The federation became independent on 1 October 1960: D. The trap: A, 1959, is when the Northern Region became self-governing; the Eastern and Western Regions got there two years earlier, in 1957. Memory tip: 1957 West and East, 1959 North, 1960 Nigeria.",
 "|The Arthur Richards Constitution was designed to last for|six years|twelve years|five years|nine years": "The Richards Constitution (proclaimed 1946, in force 1 January 1947, under Governor Sir Arthur Richards) introduced regionalism, with three regions and Regional Houses of Assembly. It was designed to last nine years, with reviews every three years: D. Because of nationalist protests over lack of consultation, it was replaced early by the Macpherson Constitution of 1951. Six years (A) is wrong: the Richards Constitution was planned to run for nine years, reviewed every three years, though it was replaced by the Macpherson Constitution in 1951. Memory tip: Richards = 3 regions × 3 years = 9 years.",
 "|The 1976 reforms have been most beneficial to the|Nigeria Police|civil service|federal government|local government": "The 1976 Local Government Reform under the Murtala/Obasanjo regime introduced a uniform, multi-purpose, single-tier local government system for the whole country, with elected councillors, defined functions and statutory grants from the federal and state governments. It was later entrenched in the 1979 Constitution as the third tier of government. The main beneficiary was therefore local government itself: D. The 1976 reforms targeted local government, giving it a uniform structure and third-tier status; the police (A), civil service (B) and federal government (C) were not their focus. Memory tip: 1976 = birth of modern, uniform local government.",
 "|A problem of Nigerian federalism that was resolved by the Supreme Court between the federal government and the littoral states centred on resource|maximization|control|derivation|generation": "In the resource control case (Attorney-General of the Federation v. Attorney-General of Abia State and 35 others, April 2002), the federal government asked the Supreme Court who owns the oil in Nigeria's offshore waters. The Court held that the seaward boundary of the littoral (coastal) states is the low-water mark, so offshore resources belong to the federation, not the states (the onshore/offshore dichotomy): B. The trap: derivation (C) was the revenue principle behind the quarrel, but the question asks what the dispute was about: control of the resources. The National Assembly later abolished the dichotomy for derivation purposes in 2004. Memory tip: coastal states wanted to CONTROL their oil.",
 "|One of the reasons advanced for the overthrow of the Gowon Regime was its failure to|promote some officers of the armed forces|honour the promise to hand over power|create new states|try politicians in detention": "General Yakubu Gowon had promised in 1970 a nine-point programme ending with a return to civil rule in 1976. On 1 October 1974 he announced that 1976 was \"unrealistic\" and the handover was postponed indefinitely. This broken promise, along with corruption and inefficiency, was cited when Brigadier Murtala Muhammed's coup removed him on 29 July 1975: B. Gowon had already created 12 states in 1967 (C); the main charge against him was his 1974 decision to drop the promised 1976 return to civilian rule. Memory tip: Gowon's broken 1976 promise = Murtala's 1975 coup.",
 "|In the first republic, politics in the Northern region was dominated by the|NPC|NCNC|NEPU|UMBC": "The First Republic was regional politics: each region was dominated by one party. The North by the Northern People's Congress (NPC) under Sir Ahmadu Bello, the Sardauna of Sokoto; the East by the NCNC under Azikiwe and later Michael Okpara; the West by the Action Group under Awolowo: A. The trap: NEPU (C), led by Mallam Aminu Kano, and the UMBC (D), led by Joseph Tarka in the Middle Belt, were northern opposition parties that never controlled the region. Memory tip: N for North, N for NPC.",
 "|The Bureau for Public Enterprises is charged with the responsibility for|eradicating poverty|generating opportunities|providing employment opportunities|privatization and commercialization": "Privatization sells government shares in public enterprises to private investors; commercialization makes public enterprises run like businesses that cover their costs. The Technical Committee on Privatisation and Commercialisation (TCPC, 1988) began the programme, and the Bureau of Public Enterprises (BPE, 1993) took it over; since 1999 the BPE acts as secretariat of the National Council on Privatisation: D. Poverty eradication (A) was NAPEP's job and employment schemes (C) belonged to bodies like the NDE; the BPE handles privatization and commercialization. Memory tip: BPE = Bureau for Privatising Enterprises.",
 "|The military head of state during the Third Republic was|General Ibrahim Babangida|General Murtala Muhammed|General Sani Abacha|General Olusegun Obasanjo": "General Ibrahim Babangida (1985–93) ran a long transition programme. Under the 1989 Constitution, elected local councils, state governors and assemblies (1991–92) and a National Assembly (1992) took office while he remained military president: this half-civilian arrangement is called the Third Republic. It collapsed after the 12 June 1993 election was annulled: A. The trap: Sani Abacha (C) seized power in November 1993 and dissolved the Third Republic's institutions; Murtala and Obasanjo (B, D) ruled in 1975–79, before the Second Republic. Memory tip: IBB built the Third Republic; Abacha buried it.",
 "|The most important challenge facing the Fourth Republic is|the need to develop Nigeria|how to develop Nigeria's social institution|how to deal with the military|the successful conduct of the 2003 elections": "The Fourth Republic began on 29 May 1999 with President Olusegun Obasanjo. When this paper was set, the major challenge was the 2003 general elections: no civilian government had ever successfully conducted an election and handed over to a civilian successor (the 1983 elections led to a coup). Conducting the 2003 elections successfully would show democracy was taking root: D. Developing Nigeria (A) is always a challenge but too general to be the special test of that moment, which was the successful conduct of the 2003 elections. Memory tip: 2003 = the first civilian-to-civilian test.",
 "|Financial allocation to local government by the federal or the state government to supplement the cost of a project is called|revenue allocation|matching grant|statutory allocation|reimbursement": "Grants are money transferred from a higher to a lower government. A matching grant is given on condition that the receiving government also contributes, and it is tied to a specific project, so the higher government \"matches\" or supplements the local government's own spending on that project: B. The trap: statutory allocation (C) is the local governments' regular share of the Federation Account fixed by law, not tied to a project. Revenue allocation (A) is the general sharing of national revenue. Memory tip: MATCHING grant = you pay part, we MATCH it, for one project.",
 "|Nigeria's influence in OPEC is determined by the|sizes of her refineries|accessibility of her oil fields|low sulphur content of her crude|volume of her oil revenue": "OPEC members carry weight in proportion to their oil: production quotas are set by output, and members with large output and revenue have more say. Nigeria, Africa's largest oil producer and an OPEC member since 1971, draws its influence from the volume of oil it produces and earns from: D. The trap: C is true but irrelevant; Nigeria's low-sulphur \"sweet\" crude (Bonny Light) earns a higher price, but quality does not give a vote. Refinery size (A) has nothing to do with OPEC, which deals in crude oil. Memory tip: in OPEC, barrels mean influence.",
 "|The activities of Nigeria in the international community are primarily influenced by|military power|diplomacy|propaganda|national interest": "Foreign policy is the set of goals a country pursues in its dealings with other states, and every country's first goal is its national interest: security, territorial integrity, economic development and prestige. Nigeria's Afrocentric policy, ECOWAS role and peacekeeping are all justified by its national interest: D. The trap: diplomacy (B) and propaganda (C) are tools for achieving foreign policy goals, not what drives them; military power (A) is one capability among many. Memory tip: national interest is the WHY; diplomacy is the HOW.",
 "|The dispute between Nigeria and Cameroon is over|trade|exploration rights|fishing rights|territory": "The Nigeria–Cameroon dispute was a boundary and territorial dispute, centred on the oil-rich Bakassi Peninsula and parts of the Lake Chad area, with clashes in the 1980s and 1990s. Cameroon took the case to the International Court of Justice in 1994; on 10 October 2002 the ICJ awarded Bakassi to Cameroon. Under the Greentree Agreement (2006), Nigeria completed the handover on 14 August 2008: D. The trap: oil exploration (B) and fishing (C) rights explain why the land mattered, but the dispute itself was over who owns the territory. Memory tip: Bakassi = boundary.",
 "|Nigeria's high standing in the UN General Assembly is underscored by her|financial contribution|military strength|successes in UN elective offices|contribution to global peace": "Nigeria's prestige at the UN comes mainly from its contribution to world peace. It has sent troops to UN peacekeeping missions since the Congo in 1960, including Lebanon, Sierra Leone, Liberia, Sudan and many more, and it was a leading voice against colonialism and apartheid: D. The trap: C, successes in UN elective offices, is tempting because Nigeria has served several terms on the Security Council and held the General Assembly presidency (Joseph Garba, 1989; Tijjani Muhammad-Bande, 2019), but those elections reflect the respect Nigeria earned through its peace role. Memory tip: Nigeria's UN badge is the blue helmet.",
 "|Nigeria's Non-Alignment policy is constrained by her|membership of the UNO|Afrocentric posture|membership of ECOWAS|strong ties with Western powers": "Non-alignment means not joining either the Western (US-led) or Eastern (Soviet-led) bloc during the Cold War. Nigeria joined the Non-Aligned Movement, but in practice its colonial ties, trade, aid, education and the short-lived Anglo-Nigerian Defence Pact (1960–62) kept it close to Britain and the West, especially under Balewa. These strong Western ties constrained genuine non-alignment: D. Membership of the UN (A) and ECOWAS (C) does not conflict with non-alignment, and the Afrocentric posture (B) is part of it; strong Western ties are what pull Nigeria off neutrality. Memory tip: you can't be neutral while tied to one side.",
 "|The main constraint on Nigeria–Francophone West African cooperation is|ideological differences|cultural differences|poor road network|economic dependence": "Most of Nigeria's neighbours are French-speaking (Benin, Niger, Chad, Cameroon). They remain closely linked to France through the CFA franc (backed by the French Treasury), aid, trade and defence agreements, and France long encouraged rival groupings such as the CEAO (1973). This economic dependence on France limits how far they cooperate with Nigeria: D. This one is debatable: some books stress ideological or cultural differences, but Nigeria and these states were all broadly pro-Western, so ideology was not the real barrier. The trap: cultural differences (B), mainly language, are real but secondary. Memory tip: the CFA franc ties Francophone hands.",
 "|Nigeria's relations with Britain were at a very low ebb under the|Buhari Regime|Gowon Regime|Shagari Regime|Babangida Regime": "In July 1984, under General Muhammadu Buhari, agents tried to kidnap former Shagari minister Umaru Dikko in London and fly him to Nigeria in a crate. Britain expelled Nigerian diplomats, Nigeria retaliated, and both withdrew their High Commissioners; relations stayed cold for about two years: A. Gowon (B), Shagari (C) and Babangida (D) kept fairly good ties with Britain; relations hit their low under Buhari after the 1984 Umaru Dikko kidnap affair. Memory tip: Buhari + Dikko crate = low ebb with London.",
 "|The ECOWAS Treaty was reviewed in 1991 to|accommodate the interest of France|mobilize ECOMOG|accommodate extra sub-regional interest|make it responsive to new challenges": "ECOWAS was founded by the Treaty of Lagos on 28 May 1975. By the early 1990s the world had changed: the Cold War had ended, the Liberian civil war needed a regional response, and economic integration was lagging. At the 1991 Abuja summit, ECOWAS set up a committee of eminent persons, chaired by Yakubu Gowon, to review the treaty, and the Revised Treaty was signed at Cotonou in July 1993 to make ECOWAS responsive to these new challenges: D. The trap: B, mobilizing ECOMOG, is tempting, but ECOMOG was deployed in Liberia in 1990, before the review. Memory tip: Lagos 1975 founded; review 1991, signed Cotonou 1993.",
 "|The headquarters of the International Court of Justice is in|Paris|The Hague|London|Washington DC": "The International Court of Justice (ICJ) is one of the six principal organs of the United Nations. It sits at the Peace Palace in The Hague, Netherlands, and has 15 judges elected for nine-year terms. It settles legal disputes between states, for example Nigeria v. Cameroon over Bakassi (2002): B. Paris (A) hosts UNESCO, London (C) the Commonwealth Secretariat and Washington DC (D) the IMF and World Bank; the ICJ sits at The Hague. Memory tip: the ICJ is the only principal UN organ not in New York; it is in The Hague.",
 "|The independent African countries that did not sign the OAU Charter on May 25, 1963 were|Morocco and Angola|Togo and Sierra Leone|Chad and the Gambia|Togo and Morocco": "The OAU was founded in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, on 25 May 1963 (now Africa Day) by the 32 then-independent African states. Thirty signed the Charter that day. Togo was not represented because the new government that followed the assassination of President Sylvanus Olympio (January 1963) had not been recognised, and Morocco stayed away over its claim to Mauritania; both signed later in 1963. So the question means the states that did not sign that day, Togo and Morocco: D. The paper left out the word \"not\". The trap: Angola (A) and the Gambia (C) were not yet independent (1975 and 1965). Memory tip: Togo and Morocco came Late (T-M-L).",
 "|The tenure of the President of the UN Security Council is|two years|one year|one month|six months": "The Security Council has 15 members: five permanent (USA, UK, France, Russia, China) with veto power, and ten non-permanent members elected for two years. Its presidency rotates every month among all 15 members in the English alphabetical order of their names: C. The trap: two years (A) is the term of a non-permanent member, not the president's tenure; one year (B) is the term of the President of the General Assembly. Memory tip: Security Council president = one Month; General Assembly president = one year.",
 "|The majority of the OPEC members are from|Asia|Latin America|the Middle East|Africa": "OPEC was founded in Baghdad in 1960 by Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia and Venezuela, and is based in Vienna. When this paper was set (2003) it had 11 members, six of them in the Middle East (Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Qatar, Saudi Arabia, UAE), with Algeria, Libya and Nigeria from Africa, Venezuela from Latin America and Indonesia from Asia. So the majority were Middle Eastern: C. Today this has changed: after Qatar, Ecuador and Angola left and Gabon, Equatorial Guinea and Congo joined, Africa has six of the 12 members and the Middle East five. The trap: D, Africa, is tempting because Africa has gained members over the years, but at the time six of OPEC's 11 members were in the Middle East. Memory tip: in 2003 OPEC was \"Gulf first\".",
 "|The non-British colony which is a member of the Commonwealth is|Guinea-Bissau|Mozambique|Rwanda|Eritrea": "The Commonwealth is mainly made up of former British territories, but countries with no British colonial past may join. Mozambique, a former Portuguese colony, became the first such member in November 1995, as a reward for its support for the struggle against apartheid in neighbouring South Africa: B. The trap: Guinea-Bissau (A) was also a Portuguese colony like Mozambique, but it never joined. Eritrea (D) was Italian, then Ethiopian, and has not joined either. Since then Rwanda (C, Belgian) joined in 2009, so C is also accepted today; Gabon and Togo (French) joined in 2022. Memory tip: Mozambique was the first non-British member (1995).",
 "|The major problem of the ECOWAS is lack of|a common Customs Union|a common currency|uniform ideology|commitment by members": "ECOWAS aims at a single West African market, but its members use many currencies: the CFA franc (shared by the francophone states), the naira, the cedi, the leone, the dalasi and others, which are not freely convertible. This makes trade and payments between members difficult and costly. The plan for a single currency, the Eco, has been postponed many times: B. The trap: lack of commitment (D) is also often blamed, and a customs union (A) was slow to come (the Common External Tariff began in 2015), but the missing common currency is the classic textbook answer. Memory tip: one region, many monies = ECOWAS's headache.",
 "|In a democracy, sovereign authority is exercised by the|people|executive|electorate|legislature": "In a democracy, ultimate sovereignty belongs to the people as a whole. But not everyone can use it directly: children, aliens and others cannot vote. The part of the people who actually exercise that sovereign power, by choosing representatives in elections, voting in referendums and removing leaders at the polls, is the electorate, that is, all citizens qualified and registered to vote: C. The trap: A, \"people\", is where sovereignty resides, not who exercises it in practice. B and D, the executive and legislature, exercise power only as agents of the electorate. Memory tip: the people own sovereignty; the electorate uses it at the ballot box.",
 "|The agent through which the state undertakes political socialization is the|school|family|peer group|pressure group": "Political socialisation is the process by which people learn political values, beliefs and attitudes. Many agents do it: family, peers, religion, media, schools. But the state directly controls only some of them. Through schools the state sets the curriculum (civic education, history, government), makes pupils recite the national anthem and pledge, and teaches national symbols and loyalty. So the school is the agent through which the state undertakes socialisation: A. The trap: B, the family, is the first and most powerful agent, but it is private, not an instrument of the state. Peer groups and pressure groups are also outside state control. Memory tip: state socialises through the classroom; parents socialise through the home.",
 "|Unicameral legislature is a common feature of|presidentialism|parliamentarism|unitarism|federalism": "A unicameral legislature has only one chamber. Federations usually need two chambers: one representing the people by population and one representing the federating units equally (as Nigeria's Senate gives each state three senators). A unitary state has no federating units to protect, so a single chamber is common and cheaper; examples include Ghana, Denmark and New Zealand: C. The trap: D, federalism, is the system that most typically produces a bicameral legislature, the opposite of the question. Presidential and parliamentary systems can have one or two chambers, so they are not the defining link. Memory tip: one centre, one chamber; many states, two chambers.",
 "|Independence of the judiciary is pertinent because it accords the judiciary the power to|determine a fixed term of office for the judges|dismiss any judge who has breached the judicial code of conduct|enable the judge to try and decide cases without bias|determine a fixed salary for judges": "Judicial independence means judges are free from control or pressure by the executive, the legislature, parties or private interests. It is secured by security of tenure, salaries charged on the Consolidated Fund, appointment through bodies like the Judicial Service Commission, and immunity for judicial acts. The purpose of all these is that judges can try and decide cases impartially, without fear or favour: C. The trap: A and D, fixed tenure and fixed salary, are means of securing independence, not the reason it matters; and judges do not fix them for themselves. B, dismissing judges, belongs to other bodies (the NJC and executive). Memory tip: independence is the means; fair judgment is the end.",
 "|In a unitary system of government, power is concentrated at the centre|without devolution|with devolution|with residual functions|without residual functions": "In a unitary state all governmental power is held by the central government. Any local or regional units are created by the centre, exercise only the powers it gives them, and can be abolished by it. Because there is no constitutional sharing of powers, the residual functions (anything not listed elsewhere) also stay with the centre. So power is concentrated at the centre with residual functions: C. The trap: A, \"without devolution\", sounds right, but many unitary states do devolve power, as Britain does to Scotland and Wales; devolved power can simply be taken back. Memory tip: in a unitary state the centre keeps everything, even the leftovers.",
 "|In a confederation, the constituency that a member of legislature represents is a|senatorial district|parliamentary constituency|nation-state|region": "A confederation is a loose union of sovereign states that keep their independence and give a weak central body limited tasks, such as defence or trade. Examples are the USA under the Articles of Confederation (1781–1789) and the Senegambia Confederation (1982–1989). Members of the confederal assembly are delegates sent by each member state and answer to it, so the constituency each represents is a whole nation-state: C. The trap: D, \"region\", is the word for a federating unit, as in Nigeria's Northern, Eastern and Western Regions; regions are not sovereign. A and B are electoral districts within one country. Memory tip: confederation = club of sovereign states; each delegate speaks for a state.",
 "|In a constitutional monarchy, the authority to remove the head of state is exercised by the|legislature|head of government|cabinet|prime minister": "In a constitutional monarchy, such as Britain, the monarch is head of state but reigns under law made by parliament. Since the Glorious Revolution of 1688 and the Act of Settlement 1701, parliament decides who may hold the throne. In 1936 Parliament passed His Majesty's Declaration of Abdication Act to give effect to Edward VIII's abdication. So the authority to remove the head of state lies with the legislature: A. The prime minister (D), head of government (B) and cabinet (C) are part of the executive and cannot remove the monarch; that authority rests with the legislature. Memory tip: parliament made the crown's rules, so parliament can take the crown.",
 "|In a parliamentary system of government, a vote of no confidence leads to the resignation of|the entire cabinet|an individual minister|the entire parliament|the prime minister": "In a parliamentary system the cabinet stays in office only while it keeps the confidence of the legislature. Ministers are collectively responsible: they stand or fall together. When parliament passes a vote of no confidence in the government, the prime minister and the entire cabinet must resign, or the prime minister asks for parliament to be dissolved and an election held: A. The trap: D, the prime minister alone, is wrong because collective responsibility brings down the whole team, not just its leader. B fits a censure of one minister (individual responsibility), and C confuses dissolution with resignation. Memory tip: one boat, one crew: no confidence sinks the whole cabinet.",
 "|In a presidential system of government, the president checks the legislature through|executive order|executive review|exercise of veto|legislative order": "Checks and balances let each organ of government limit the others. In a presidential system such as the USA or Nigeria, a bill passed by the legislature needs the president's assent. The president can withhold assent, which is a veto, sending the bill back; in Nigeria the National Assembly can override it by a two-thirds majority in each house. So the president checks the legislature through the exercise of veto: C. The trap: A, executive orders, are directives to the executive branch, not a check on the legislature. B, review of laws, is the courts' power of judicial review. Memory tip: Veto = Very powerful \"no\" to the legislature.",
 "|The economic basis of feudalism is|capital|agriculture|slavery|trade": "Feudalism was the system of medieval Europe (and similar systems elsewhere). The king granted land, called fiefs, to lords; lords granted it to vassals and knights in return for military service; serfs worked the land and gave part of their crops and labour. Land and farming were the basis of wealth and power: B. The trap: A, capital, is the basis of capitalism, the system that replaced feudalism. Trade existed but was not the basis, and slavery was the basis of the earlier slave economies (as Marx's stages show: slavery, feudalism, capitalism). Memory tip: feudal = fief = farmland.",
 "|The creation of a classless society is the ultimate aim of|communism|capitalism|socialism|fascism": "Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels taught that history is a story of class struggle. Capitalism would be overthrown by the workers; socialism would follow as a transition stage; and finally communism would arrive, a society with no classes, no private ownership of the means of production, and no state, because the state would \"wither away\": A. The trap: C, socialism, also attacks class inequality, but in Marxist theory it is only the stage on the way, still with a state and some differences in reward. Capitalism accepts classes, and fascism glorifies the state. Memory tip: communism is the communal end of the road; socialism is the road.",
 "|One of the sources of a constitution is|constitutional law|common law|corporate law|statutory law": "Sources of a constitution are where its rules come from: historical documents, conventions, judicial precedents, common law, statutes (acts of parliament), and the works of learned writers. Common law, the body of rules developed by judges over centuries, gives Britain many rules on individual liberty and the royal prerogative: B. Statutory law, option D, is also a source, since acts such as the Bill of Rights 1689 shape the constitution, so both are accepted. The trap: A, constitutional law, is the constitution itself, not a source of it; C, corporate law, governs companies. Memory tip: a constitution comes from Courts (common law), Conventions and Codified statutes.",
 "|The fundamental assumption on which the idea of the rule of law is based is|rationality of human beings|equality of human beings|love for social justice|supremacy of the constitution": "The rule of law, explained by A. V. Dicey, means that the law is supreme: government acts only under law, everyone is equal before the law, and rights are protected by the courts. Its fundamental assumption is the supremacy of the law, which in a country with a written constitution, like Nigeria, means the supremacy of the constitution over every person and organ of government: D. The trap: B, equality of human beings, sounds like Dicey's \"equality before the law\", but that is equality of treatment by the courts, not a claim that all humans are equal; and it follows from the supremacy of law. Memory tip: law on top first; equality comes from it.",
 "|The principle of separation of powers was made popular by|John Locke|Baron de Montesquieu|Thomas Hobbes|Niccolo Machiavelli": "Separation of powers divides government into three organs (legislature, executive and judiciary), each with its own functions and personnel, to prevent tyranny. Although John Locke wrote of separate legislative and executive powers, it was the French writer Charles-Louis de Secondat, Baron de Montesquieu, who popularised the full three-way doctrine in The Spirit of the Laws (1748), drawing on Britain: B. The trap: A, Locke, is famous for the social contract and natural rights (life, liberty, property). Hobbes wrote Leviathan (1651), defending absolute sovereignty, and Machiavelli wrote The Prince (1513). Memory tip: Montesquieu = Many organs, three separate powers.",
 "|Delegated legislation refers to the laws made by|the legislature|military governments|civilian governments|non-legislative bodies": "Delegated (subsidiary) legislation is law made by persons or bodies other than the legislature, under authority given by an act of the legislature. Examples are regulations made by ministers, rules made by agencies like NAFDAC, and bye-laws made by local councils. These are non-legislative bodies acting on delegated power: D. The trap: A is the opposite, since laws made by the legislature itself are primary legislation. B and C describe who is in government, not who makes delegated laws; military decrees are primary law-making by a military regime, not delegation. Memory tip: delegated = done by others on parliament's behalf.",
 "|Laws made by state governments are known as|edicts|acts|decrees|bye-laws": "Laws in Nigeria take names from their maker. During military rule, the Federal Military Government made decrees, state military governors made edicts, and local governments make bye-laws. So the laws made by state governments were known as edicts: A. Today, under civilian rule, state Houses of Assembly pass bills that become \"Laws\" of the state. The trap: C, decrees, are federal military laws; B, acts, are laws passed by the National Assembly; D, bye-laws, are local government rules. Memory tip: Federal Decrees, State Edicts, Local Bye-laws: D-E-B from top to bottom.",
 "|The law of libel limits a citizen's right to freedom of|association|movement|worship|expression": "Libel is a defamatory statement in permanent form (writing, print, broadcast or online) that damages a person's reputation; slander is the spoken form. Section 39 of the 1999 Constitution guarantees freedom of expression and the press, but section 45 allows laws limiting rights to protect the reputation and rights of others. The law of libel is one such limit on freedom of expression: D. Association (A), movement (B) and worship (C) are not about what you say or publish; libel limits freedom of expression. Memory tip: libel = label someone falsely in print; free speech has limits.",
 "|The first franchise in the history of the democratic process is|female franchise|male franchise|universal franchise|property franchise": "Franchise means the right to vote. It did not begin universal. In ancient Athens only free adult males who were citizens could vote, and in Britain and Nigeria the first voters were men only: B. Option D is also accepted, because those early male voters usually also needed property or income: Nigeria's first elections under the 1922 Clifford Constitution were for adult males in Lagos and Calabar earning at least £100 a year. Female franchise (A) came much later (Britain 1918 and 1928; Northern Nigerian women only in 1976), and universal franchise (C) was the last stage. Memory tip: men and money first, women later, everyone last.",
 "|An interest group that admits members and conducts its affairs according to stated rules is described as|institutional|organizational|associational|non-associational": "Gabriel Almond classified interest groups into four types. Associational groups are formally organised, have written rules, officers and open membership, and pursue stated interests: trade unions, the Nigerian Bar Association, the Nigerian Medical Association, MAN: C. The trap: A, institutional groups, exist inside formal institutions such as the army, the civil service or churches. D, non-associational groups, are informal groups based on kinship, ethnicity or religion with no formal rules. Anomic groups are spontaneous, like riots. Memory tip: associational = association with a constitution and membership card.",
 "|A major influence on the formulation of public opinion is|public journals|peer groups|the family|the mass media": "Public opinion is the views held by a large part of the population on public issues. It is shaped by family, schools, religion, peer groups and leaders, but the major influence is the mass media: radio, television, newspapers and now the internet and social media. They reach millions at once, set the agenda of what people discuss and frame how issues are seen: D. Peer groups (B) and the family (C) shape individuals but reach only a few people each, and public journals (A) are a small specialist part of the media. Memory tip: mass media shapes mass opinion.",
 "|The highest grade in the civil service is known as the|technical cadre|administrative cadre|executive cadre|clerical cadre": "The civil service is divided into classes or cadres. The administrative cadre is the highest: it includes permanent secretaries and directors, advises ministers, formulates policy and coordinates ministries. Below it are the executive cadre, which carries out policy and manages routine work; the professional and technical cadres (engineers, doctors, technicians); and the clerical cadre, which keeps records and does routine office work: B. The trap: A, the technical cadre, contains highly qualified experts, but they serve within ministries headed by the administrative class. Memory tip: Administrative At the top, then Executive, then Clerical: A-E-C.",
 "|The pre-colonial Yoruba political system as a whole can best be described as a|confederation of monarchies and chiefdoms|federation of chiefdoms and localities|highly centralized kingdom|confederation of chiefdoms and localities": "Pre-colonial Yorubaland was not one state but many kingdoms: Oyo, Ife, Ijebu, Egba, Ekiti, Ijesa, Ondo and others. Each had its own oba and chiefs, and they were loosely linked by common descent from Oduduwa and respect for the Ooni of Ife as spiritual head. This makes the Yoruba system as a whole a confederation of monarchies and chiefdoms: A. The trap: C, \"highly centralized kingdom\", fits the Hausa-Fulani emirates better; even the Oyo Empire's Alaafin was checked by the Oyo Mesi and Ogboni. D leaves out the monarchies, the heart of the system. Memory tip: Yoruba = many crowns under one ancestor.",
 "|The major motivation of British colonization of Nigeria was to|spread religion|satisfy British economic interests|westernize Nigerians|protect Nigeria from external attack": "Britain's colonisation of Nigeria was driven above all by economic interests. The Industrial Revolution created demand for raw materials such as palm oil, cocoa, groundnuts, cotton and tin, and for markets for British manufactured goods. Companies like the Royal Niger Company led the way, and the colonial state built railways to export produce: B. The trap: A and C, spreading Christianity and \"civilising\" Nigerians, were real activities of missionaries and were used to justify colonial rule, but they were not the main motive of the British state. D is false: Britain itself was the external power. Memory tip: colonialism followed the money: Trade first, Flag later.",
 "|The Native Authority system was most effective and successful in|Western Nigeria|Mid-Western Nigeria|Northern Nigeria|Eastern Nigeria": "Lord Lugard introduced indirect rule, ruling through traditional rulers organised as Native Authorities. It succeeded best in Northern Nigeria because the emirates already had centralised authority, strong emirs, an organised tax system and Islamic courts. In the West it worked less well because obas were checked by chiefs, and in the East it failed because there were no chiefs, leading to warrant chiefs and the Aba Women's Riot of 1929: C. The trap: A, Western Nigeria, had kings, so it is tempting, but their power was limited. Memory tip: indirect rule needs a ready-made ruler: the North had emirs.",
 "|The process of nationalism was accelerated by|rapid economic development|the coming of Christian missionaries|the signing of the Atlantic Charter|improvement in warfare tactics": "The Atlantic Charter was signed by Winston Churchill and Franklin Roosevelt in August 1941. It declared the right of all peoples to choose their own form of government. When Churchill said it applied only to Nazi-occupied Europe, African nationalists such as Nnamdi Azikiwe (who wrote \"The Atlantic Charter and British West Africa\" in 1943) used it to demand self-government, accelerating nationalism: C. The trap: B, Christian missionaries, helped indirectly by spreading Western education, which produced the educated elite; but the question asks what accelerated the process. A is wrong because colonial economies remained underdeveloped. Memory tip: the Atlantic Charter crossed the Atlantic to Africa.",
 "|A common feature of the earlier political parties in Nigeria was that they|started as socio-cultural organizations|were formed by the government|were non-elitist in nature|were backed by the colonialists": "Most of Nigeria's early major parties grew out of cultural and ethnic associations. The Action Group (1951) came from Egbe Omo Oduduwa, a Yoruba cultural society; the Northern People's Congress grew from Jam'iyyar Mutanen Arewa; and the NCNC was closely tied to the Igbo State Union. This gave them regional and ethnic bases: A. Colonial backing (D) is wrong since these parties fought for self-government; they were formed by private citizens, not government (B), and led by the educated elite (C). Memory tip: culture first, party later: Egbe became AG, Jam'iyya became NPC.",
 "|Under the 1999 Constitution, the power to declare war is vested in the|legislature|executive|National Council of States|National Security Council": "Under section 5(4) of the 1999 Constitution, the President, as the head of the executive and Commander-in-Chief, has the power to declare a state of war between Nigeria and another country, but only with the sanction of a resolution of both Houses of the National Assembly sitting in joint session. So the power is vested in the executive, checked by the legislature: B. The trap: A, the legislature, gives the approval but does not itself declare war. C, the National Council of States, and D, the National Security Council, are advisory bodies. Memory tip: the President declares; the Assembly approves.",
 "|A distinguishing feature of the 1979 Constitution was the|departure from the parliamentary to the presidential system|preservation and entrenchment of republicanism|introduction of unicameralism into Nigeria|introduction of a federal structure": "The 1979 Constitution, drafted by the Constitution Drafting Committee under Chief Rotimi Williams (1975–76), began the Second Republic. Its main innovation was replacing the Westminster parliamentary system of 1960–66 with an American-style executive presidency, with separation of powers and a President who is both head of state and head of government: A. The trap: B, republicanism, was introduced in 1963, not 1979. D is wrong because Nigeria became federal in 1954 (Lyttelton Constitution). C is wrong since the National Assembly had two chambers. Memory tip: 1979 = presidency; 1963 = republic; 1954 = federation.",
 "|The two chambers of elected national representatives in Nigeria are called|the parliament|the senate|House of Assembly|the National Assembly": "Nigeria's federal legislature is the National Assembly, established by section 47 of the 1999 Constitution. It has two chambers: the Senate, with 109 senators (three from each of the 36 states and one from the FCT), and the House of Representatives, with 360 members. Together the two chambers are called the National Assembly: D. The trap: A, \"parliament\", was the name under the 1960 and 1963 parliamentary system; B, the Senate, is only one of the two chambers. C, House of Assembly, is the state legislature. Memory tip: National Assembly = Senate + House of Reps.",
 "|Judicial administration in respect of the national code of conduct lies with the|Judicial Service Commission|Code of Conduct Tribunal|Public Complaints Commission|Code of Conduct Bureau": "The Code of Conduct for public officers (Fifth Schedule of the 1999 Constitution) requires them to declare their assets and forbids things such as foreign bank accounts. Two bodies enforce it. The Code of Conduct Bureau receives and keeps asset declarations, investigates complaints and refers breaches. The Code of Conduct Tribunal is a court-like body that tries cases and imposes penalties such as removal from office or disqualification. Judicial administration therefore lies with the Tribunal: B. The trap: D, the Bureau, is the administrative and investigating body, not a judicial one. A, the JSC, deals with judges' appointments. Memory tip: Bureau = books (declarations); Tribunal = trial.",
 "|In the Second Republic, the ruling National Party of Nigeria formed an alliance with the|Unity Party of Nigeria|Nigeria People's Party|Great Nigeria People's Party|Nigeria Advance Party": "In the 1979 elections, Shehu Shagari's National Party of Nigeria won the presidency but lacked a majority in the National Assembly. It formed an accord with Nnamdi Azikiwe's Nigeria People's Party (NPP) to secure a working majority, and NPP members got ministerial posts. The accord collapsed in 1981: B. The trap: C, the Great Nigeria People's Party (GNPP), sounds almost identical to the NPP, but Waziri Ibrahim's GNPP stayed in opposition; the NPN's 1979 accord was with Azikiwe's NPP. Memory tip: NPN + NPP = the \"P-P\" accord of 1979.",
 "|The relationship between the tiers of government in Nigeria can be described as one of|independent co-existence|coordinate and independent jurisdiction|voluntary subordination|superior-subordinate co-existence": "Nigeria has three tiers of government: federal, state and local. In practice the federal government dominates (it controls most revenue and the police), and local governments are placed under the states, which create them, fund them and often dissolve their councils. So the relationship is superior-subordinate co-existence: D. Option B is also accepted: in theory, and in the constitution's division of powers between exclusive and concurrent lists, the federal and state governments are coordinate and independent, which is K. C. Wheare's classic definition of federalism. The trap: C, \"voluntary\" subordination, is wrong because subordination is imposed, not chosen. Memory tip: in theory side by side; in practice top-down.",
 "|The 1976 Local Government Reforms in Nigeria transformed the relationship between states and local government into one of|master and servant|partnership and cooperation|equality|subordination": "The 1976 Local Government Reform under the Murtala/Obasanjo regime made local government uniform across Nigeria and recognised it as the third tier of government. Councils got defined functions, elected councillors, a statutory share of federal revenue and protection in the 1979 Constitution. The aim was to change the relationship between states and local governments from master and servant to partnership and cooperation: B. The trap: A, master and servant, describes what existed before 1976, when states controlled councils at will. C is too strong, since states still oversee local governments. Memory tip: 1976 made local government a partner, not a servant.",
 "|Government-owned companies operating in the economic sector are referred to as|public utilities|public enterprises|public investments|public services": "Public enterprises are businesses owned wholly or mainly by government that produce goods and services, often for sale. They include public corporations such as NNPC, the old NEPA and the Nigerian Railway Corporation, and government-owned companies. Since the question speaks of companies operating in the economic sector, the general name is public enterprises: B. The trap: A, public utilities, are one kind of public enterprise providing essential services like water and electricity, so the term is too narrow. C is not a technical term, and D, public services, refers to the civil service. Memory tip: enterprise = business; public enterprise = government business.",
 "|The difference between commercialized and privatized companies is that in the former|private ownership is dominant|public ownership is dominant|government subsidizes costs|profit motive is recessive": "Commercialisation and privatisation are both reforms of public enterprises, pursued in Nigeria from 1988 (Technical Committee on Privatisation and Commercialisation) and later by the Bureau of Public Enterprises. A commercialised enterprise stays owned by government but is run on business lines, charging economic prices and making profits without subsidies. A privatised enterprise has its shares sold to private investors. So in a commercialised company public ownership is dominant: B. The trap: A describes the privatised company, the latter not the former. C and D are wrong because commercialisation ends subsidies and makes profit important. Memory tip: commercialised = government-owned but business-run; privatised = privately owned.",
 "|The body responsible for running the personnel affairs of senior local government staff in Nigeria is the|Local Government Council|State Civil Service Commission|Senior Staff Commission|Local Government Service Commission": "Each state has a Local Government Service Commission that appoints, posts, promotes and disciplines senior staff of local governments, such as those on higher grade levels and heads of departments. It was introduced with the 1976 reform to give local government a professional, unified career service. Junior staff are handled by the councils themselves: D. The trap: B, the State Civil Service Commission, handles state civil servants, not council staff. A, the Local Government Council, deals with junior staff. C is not a real body. Memory tip: local government staff = Local Government Service Commission: the name tells you.",
 "|The immediate cause of the January 15, 1966 military coup in Nigeria was the|Kano Riots|Tiv Riots|election crisis in the Western Region|crisis over the population census": "The first military coup in Nigeria took place on 15 January 1966, led by Major Chukwuma Kaduna Nzeogwu and others. Its immediate cause was the crisis after the rigged Western Region election of October 1965, which led to widespread violence, arson and killing (\"Operation Wetie\") and a breakdown of law and order: C. The trap: D, the 1962–63 census crisis, and the 1964 federal election crisis were remote causes that built up tension. B, the Tiv riots (1960 and 1964), and A, the Kano riots (1953), were earlier crises too. Memory tip: Wild Wild West 1965 triggered January 1966.",
 "|Nigeria demonstrated her commitment to the policy of non-alignment during the regime of|Muhammadu Buhari|Johnson Aguiyi-Ironsi|Murtala Muhammed|Ibrahim Babangida": "Non-alignment means refusing to join either the Western (US-led) or Eastern (Soviet-led) bloc in the Cold War. Nigeria joined the Non-Aligned Movement, but under Balewa leaned towards the West. The clearest demonstration came under Murtala Muhammed (1975–76). In 1975 he recognised the MPLA government in Angola, which the US opposed, and rejected American pressure, saying \"Africa has come of age\": C. Buhari (A) is remembered for discipline at home, Ironsi (B) ruled only six months and Babangida (D) for economic diplomacy; Murtala showed non-alignment by recognizing the MPLA in Angola against US wishes. Memory tip: Murtala Muhammed made Nigeria's non-alignment real.",
 "|Nigeria's support for the South-South Cooperation is based on her desire to|promote economic understanding in the Third World|counter the political and military domination by major powers|assert her leadership role in Africa|promote her non-aligned policy": "South-South Cooperation means cooperation among developing countries of Africa, Asia and Latin America (the \"South\") in trade, technology and development, rather than depending on the industrialised North. Nigeria supports it to promote economic understanding and cooperation within the Third World: A. Babangida's government, for example, hosted South-South meetings and set up the Technical Aid Corps (1987) to share expertise with other developing countries. The trap: B, countering great power domination, is closer to non-alignment, which is political. C and D are separate aims of Nigerian foreign policy. Memory tip: South-South = economic solidarity among developing nations.",
 "|Nigeria's relation with black political communities outside Africa is built on|economic considerations|shared political aspirations|perceived cultural affinities|expectations of political support from them": "Nigeria sees herself as a leader of the black race. Her ties with black communities outside Africa, in the Caribbean, Brazil, the USA and Britain, rest on shared African descent and cultural heritage. Nigeria hosted the Second World Black and African Festival of Arts and Culture (FESTAC '77) in Lagos in 1977 to celebrate this link: C. Economic considerations (A) shape Nigeria's trade ties, but its bond with Black communities abroad rests on shared culture and heritage; B and D do not fit since these communities are not states. Memory tip: FESTAC = family ties of the black world, culture first.",
 "|The one-time president of the United Nations General Assembly was|Maitama Sule|Joseph Garba|Ibrahim Gambari|Arthur Mbanefo": "The President of the UN General Assembly is elected each year to preside over its sessions. Major-General Joseph Nanven Garba, Nigeria's Permanent Representative to the UN (1984–1989), was elected President of the 44th session (1989–1990): B. Since the paper was set, another Nigerian, Professor Tijjani Muhammad-Bande, presided over the 74th session (2019–2020). The trap: A, Maitama Sule, was also Nigeria's UN ambassador and chaired the UN Special Committee against Apartheid, but he did not preside over the Assembly. C, Ibrahim Gambari, was later a UN Under-Secretary-General. Memory tip: Garba guarded the gavel in 1989.",
 "|The leaders who spearheaded the transformation of the Organization of African Unity into the African Union are from|South Africa, Libya and Zambia|Nigeria, Libya and South Africa|Nigeria, Liberia and Kenya|Algeria, Libya and Morocco": "The Organisation of African Unity (founded in 1963) was replaced by the African Union. The process began with the Sirte Declaration of 9 September 1999, at a summit hosted by Muammar Gaddafi of Libya; the Constitutive Act was adopted at Lome in 2000; and the AU was launched in Durban in 2002. The leaders who drove it were Gaddafi, Olusegun Obasanjo of Nigeria and Thabo Mbeki of South Africa: B. The trap: A swaps Nigeria for Zambia; D includes Morocco, which had left the OAU in 1984 and rejoined the AU only in 2017. Memory tip: the AU was built by L-N-S: Libya, Nigeria, South Africa.",
 "|The countries in which Nigeria participated in the ECOMOG peace-keeping operations were|Liberia and Guinea|Sierra Leone and Cote d'Ivoire|Senegal and Cote d'Ivoire|Liberia and Sierra Leone": "ECOMOG, the ECOWAS Ceasefire Monitoring Group, was ECOWAS's peacekeeping force. Nigeria supplied most of its soldiers and money. It was first deployed in Liberia in 1990 during the civil war, and later in Sierra Leone (1997–2000), where it restored the elected President Kabbah: D. The trap: A and B each contain one right country with one wrong one; Guinea and Côte d'Ivoire contributed troops or hosted refugees but were not where Nigeria's main ECOMOG operations took place. ECOMOG also served briefly in Guinea-Bissau (1998–99). Memory tip: ECOMOG's twin missions were Liberia and Sierra Leone.",
 "|The permanent members of the Security Council of the United Nations are|Britain, Japan, Australia, Germany and the United States|Germany, France, Poland, Hungary and China|the United States, Russia, France, Britain and China|the United States, Russia, France, Britain and Japan": "The UN Security Council has 15 members: 10 non-permanent members elected for two years, and 5 permanent members with veto power. The five permanent members are the United States, Russia (which took the Soviet Union's seat in 1991), France, Britain and China: C. This is still true today. The trap: D swaps China for Japan; Japan and Germany are major economic powers and have campaigned for permanent seats, but they were defeated powers in the Second World War and have none. A and B include countries that never held permanent seats. Memory tip: the P5 are the five big Allied winners of World War Two.",
 "|A specialized agency of the United Nations Organization is the|World Health Organization|General Assembly|International Court of Justice|International Olympic Committee": "UN specialised agencies are independent international organisations linked to the UN by agreements, each working in a particular field: WHO (health), FAO (food), UNESCO (education and culture), ILO (labour), IMF and the World Bank. So the WHO is a specialised agency: A. The trap: B and C, the General Assembly and the International Court of Justice, are principal organs of the UN, not specialised agencies; the other principal organs are the Security Council, ECOSOC, the Secretariat and the Trusteeship Council. D, the IOC, is a private sports body. Memory tip: agencies have a specialty (health, food, labour); organs are the UN's own body parts.",
 "|With the admission of Asian and African countries to the Commonwealth, the Queen of England became the|head of state of these countries|head of government of these countries|patron of the Commonwealth|head of the Commonwealth": "The Commonwealth was once made of Britain and dominions that all shared the British monarch as head of state. When India wanted to become a republic yet stay in, the London Declaration of 1949 created the title \"Head of the Commonwealth\", a symbolic head of the whole association. As Asian and African republics joined, the Queen became the Head of the Commonwealth rather than head of state of each: D. Since 2022, King Charles III has been Head of the Commonwealth. The trap: A is true only for realms like Jamaica, not republics such as Nigeria since 1963. C, patron, is not her title. Memory tip: Head of the Commonwealth, not head of every state.",
 "|A major problem of the defunct Organization of African Unity was|ideological differences|language barrier|inadequate resources|cultural diversity": "The OAU (1963–2002) achieved much in fighting colonialism and apartheid, but it was weakened by ideological differences among its members. From the start, the radical Casablanca group (Nkrumah's Ghana, Guinea, Egypt) wanted immediate political union, while the moderate Monrovia group (including Nigeria) preferred gradual cooperation; later, Cold War divisions between pro-East and pro-West states split members on issues like Angola: A. The trap: C, inadequate resources, was also a real problem, but the main one was disagreement on goals. B and D, language and cultural diversity, were managed through common working languages. Memory tip: the OAU split over ideas before money.",
 "|The Lagos Treaty of May 28th 1975 led to the formation of the|Economic Commission for Africa|Economic Community of West African States|Lagos Plan of Action|African Economic Summit": "The Economic Community of West African States (ECOWAS) was founded by the Treaty of Lagos, signed on 28 May 1975 by 15 West African heads of state. General Yakubu Gowon of Nigeria and President Gnassingbe Eyadema of Togo led the effort: B. The trap: C, the Lagos Plan of Action, is also a \"Lagos\" document, but it was adopted by the OAU in 1980 for African economic development. A, the UN Economic Commission for Africa, was set up in 1958 in Addis Ababa. D is not a treaty body. Memory tip: Lagos 1975 = ECOWAS; Lagos 1980 = Plan of Action.",
 "|The current Executive Secretary of the ECOWAS is|Lansana Kouyate|Abubakar Ouattara|Mohammed Ibn Chambas|Abass Bundu": "In 2004, the Executive Secretary of ECOWAS was Dr Mohamed Ibn Chambas of Ghana, who took office in 2002 after Lansana Kouyaté of Guinea (1997–2002): C. In 2007 the Secretariat became the ECOWAS Commission, and Chambas became its first President. Today the head is the President of the ECOWAS Commission, Omar Alieu Touray of The Gambia (since 2022). The trap: A, Kouyaté, was the immediate predecessor, so he is tempting for anyone with older notes; D, Abass Bundu of Sierra Leone, served 1989–1993. Memory tip: \"current\" means current when the paper was set; check the year of the paper.",
 "|The responsibility for admitting new members to the OPEC rests with the|Board of Governors|Conference|Secretariat|Summit": "OPEC (founded in Baghdad in 1960; Nigeria joined in 1971) has three main organs. The Conference of oil ministers is the supreme authority: it sets policy, approves the budget and admits new members. Admission needs a three-fourths majority of full members, including all five founder members (Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia, Venezuela): B. The trap: A, the Board of Governors, directs management and implements Conference decisions; C, the Secretariat in Vienna, does the administrative work under the Secretary General. D, summit, is not an OPEC organ. Memory tip: the Conference is the Club's committee that lets people in.",
 "|The ultimate authority in a state is|legitimacy|influence|power|sovereignty": "Sovereignty is the supreme, final and unlimited legal authority of the state. Inside its borders no person or body is above it, and outside it is independent of control by other states. Jean Bodin (1576) called it absolute and indivisible, and John Austin defined the sovereign as the one whom others habitually obey. So the ultimate authority in a state is sovereignty: D. The trap: power (C) is the ability to make others obey, which even a robber has; legitimacy (A) is the people's acceptance that a ruler has the right to rule; influence (B) is persuasion without command. Memory tip: Sovereignty = Supreme authority inside, independence outside.",
 "|The study of government essentially facilitates the understanding of the|organization of the executive arm of government|observance of fundamental human rights|functioning of the entire social formation|governance of human societies": "Government as an academic subject studies how human societies are governed: what the state is, how its institutions are organised, how power is won and used, and how rulers and citizens relate through rights, duties, parties and elections. That broad aim is the governance of human societies: D. The executive (A) and human rights (B) are single topics within Government, and the \"entire social formation\" (C) is the wider field of sociology; the subject's purpose is understanding how societies are governed. Memory tip: when asked for the PURPOSE of a subject, choose the broadest correct option, not one chapter of it.",
 "|The most important aspect of political participation in a democracy is|registration with a political party|the observance of electoral processes|voting in elections|attending political rallies": "Political participation means the ways citizens take part in choosing and influencing their rulers: voting, joining parties, attending rallies, contesting elections, lobbying. In a democracy the most important and most widespread form is voting, because the ballot is how the people actually choose and remove governments, and it gives each adult an equal say (\"one man, one vote\"): C. Registering with a party (A) and attending rallies (D) are forms of participation many citizens never take up, and observing electoral processes (B) is what monitors do; voting is the central act. Memory tip: in a democracy, power flows through the ballot box.",
 "|A major flaw in a liberal democracy is|its emphasis on political rights over economic rights|its emphasis on collective ownership|the limitation of the freedom of expression|that it promotes political instability": "Liberal democracy (Britain, the USA, Nigeria) protects political and civil rights: the vote, free speech, free association and fair trials. But it leaves the economy largely to private ownership and the free market. Socialist critics say this is its major flaw: everyone has an equal vote, yet wealth and economic opportunity stay very unequal, so the rich dominate politics. It stresses political rights over economic rights: A. Collective ownership (B) belongs to socialism, the opposite of liberal democracy, and C is wrong because liberal democracy guarantees freedom of expression. Memory tip: liberal democracy = freedom of the ballot, not freedom from want.",
 "|A non-monarchical state can best be described as a|confederation|republic|federation|nation": "A republic is a state whose head of state is not a hereditary king or queen but a person elected or appointed for a fixed term. The word comes from the Latin res publica, \"the public thing\". Nigeria became a republic on 1 October 1963, when a Nigerian president (Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe) replaced the British Queen as head of state: B. The trap: federation (C) and confederation (A) describe how power is shared between centre and regions, not who heads the state; a federation can be a monarchy (Malaysia, Australia). A nation (D) is a people with a shared identity. Memory tip: no crown = republic.",
 "|An elected legislator in a presidential system can lose his seat by|cross-carpeting|recall|impeachment|a vote of no confidence": "Recall is the power of the voters of a constituency to remove their own legislator before the end of his term. It suits the presidential system, where legislators are elected for fixed terms. In Nigeria's 1999 Constitution (section 69), a member can be recalled if more than half of the registered voters of his constituency sign a petition and a majority then vote for recall in a referendum conducted by INEC: B. The trap: impeachment (C) removes an executive (president or governor), and a vote of no confidence (D) removes a parliamentary government. Cross-carpeting (A) can also cost a seat in Nigeria: under section 68(1)(g), a legislator who defects to another party loses his seat unless his old party has split, so A is also accepted. Memory tip: voters RECALL legislators; legislators IMPEACH executives.",
 "|In judicial administration, the term 'the bench' refers to the|judges|lawyers|litigants|registrars": "In legal language \"the bench\" means the judges or magistrates who sit to hear and decide cases; the name comes from the bench on which judges once sat. Lawyers who argue cases are called \"the bar\", from the rail that separated them from the court. So the bench refers to the judges: A. The trap: lawyers (B) are the bar, not the bench; litigants (C) are the parties to a case; registrars (D) are court officials who keep records and process documents. Memory tip: judges sit on the BENCH; lawyers stand at the BAR.",
 "|One of the foremost theorists of federalism was|K.C. Wheare|Baron de Montesquieu|A.V. Dicey|Karl Marx": "Professor K.C. Wheare's book \"Federal Government\" (1946) is the classic study of federalism. He defined the federal principle as \"the method of dividing powers so that the general and regional governments are each, within a sphere, coordinate and independent\", and studied the USA, Canada, Australia and Switzerland: A. Montesquieu (B) is known for the separation of powers, Dicey (C) for the rule of law and Marx (D) for class struggle; K.C. Wheare wrote the classic study Federal Government. Memory tip: Wheare = Where power is shared (federalism); Montesquieu = separation; Dicey = rule of law.",
 "|How does the president relieve a minister of his appointment in a presidential system of government?|By unilateral action|In consultation with the legislature|After serving a full tenure|In consultation with the judiciary": "In a presidential system the president is the chief executive and the ministers are his appointees and assistants, not members of the legislature. In Nigeria the Senate must confirm a ministerial nominee, but once appointed, a minister holds office at the president's pleasure and can be removed by the president alone, at any time, without asking anyone: A. The trap: B is tempting because the legislature takes part in APPOINTING ministers; it has no role in DISMISSING them. Ministers have no fixed tenure (C), and the judiciary (D) plays no part. Memory tip: the Senate says \"yes\" to hire; the president alone says \"go\".",
 "|Under a parliamentary system of government, the cabinet holds office at the pleasure of the|electorate|legislature|opposition|head of state": "In a parliamentary system the Prime Minister and cabinet are members of parliament and are collectively responsible to it. They stay in office only as long as they keep the confidence of the majority in the legislature; if parliament passes a vote of no confidence, the government must resign or call an election. So the cabinet holds office at the pleasure of the legislature: B. The trap: the head of state (D) formally appoints the Prime Minister, but by convention must pick the majority leader and cannot freely remove him. The electorate (A) chooses parliament, not the cabinet directly. Memory tip: parliamentary cabinet = lives and dies by the confidence of parliament.",
 "|Serfs are the dominated class under|fascism|capitalism|feudalism|socialism": "Feudalism was the social and economic system of medieval Europe. The king granted land (fiefs) to lords and nobles, who owed him loyalty and military service. At the bottom were serfs, peasants bound to the land, who worked the lord's fields and paid dues in return for protection and the right to farm a small plot. They could not leave without the lord's permission. So serfs are the dominated class under feudalism: C. The trap: under capitalism (B) the dominated class, in Marxist terms, is the proletariat (wage workers), not serfs. Memory tip: Marx's stages: slaves (slavery), serfs (feudalism), proletariat (capitalism).",
 "|An unwritten constitution is|partially written and based on convention|completely unwritten and based on convention|entirely based on case laws|completely sourced from parliamentary law": "An unwritten constitution is one not contained in a single document. Britain's is the classic example. It is not literally unwritten: parts are written in statutes (Magna Carta 1215, Bill of Rights 1689, Parliament Acts), judicial decisions and authoritative books, while other parts are conventions, unwritten customs such as the monarch always assenting to bills. So it is partially written and based on convention: A. The trap: B, \"completely unwritten\", takes the name too literally. C and D each name only one source. Memory tip: \"unwritten\" really means \"uncodified\": written in many places, not in one book.",
 "|The principle of checks and balances empowers the judiciary to|administer the criminal justice system|apply the law|invalidate the actions of the other arms|abrogate the law": "Checks and balances means each arm of government can limit the others so none becomes tyrannical. The judiciary's check is judicial review: it can declare a law of the legislature or an act of the executive unconstitutional, null and void. The US Supreme Court established this in Marbury v. Madison (1803), and Nigerian courts do it under the 1999 Constitution. So it empowers the judiciary to invalidate the actions of the other arms: C. The trap: applying the law (B) is the judiciary's ordinary function, not a check; abrogating (repealing) laws (D) is for the legislature. Memory tip: checks = each arm can STOP another; the courts stop by striking down.",
 "|The final stage in the process of enacting legislation is|guillotine|notification|assent|final reading": "A bill passes through first reading, second reading (debate on principles), committee stage, report stage and third reading in each house. Even then it is not yet law: it must receive the assent (signature) of the head of state, in Nigeria the president for federal bills (section 58) or the governor for state bills. Assent is the final stage that turns a bill into an Act: C. The trap: the final (third) reading (D) is the last stage inside the legislature, not the last stage of the whole process. A guillotine (A) is a time limit on debate; notification (B) is not a stage. Memory tip: readings in the House, ASSENT in the president's house.",
 "|Citizenship acquired through marriage is referred to as|naturalization|registration|honorary|nationalization": "Nigeria's 1999 Constitution gives three ways to become a citizen: birth (section 25), registration (section 26) and naturalization (section 27). Registration is for a foreign woman married to a Nigerian man, and for some people of Nigerian grandparentage born abroad. So citizenship acquired through marriage is citizenship by registration: B. The trap: naturalization (A) is for foreigners who have lived in Nigeria for a long time (15 years) and meet conditions such as good character; it is not tied to marriage. Honorary citizenship (C) is a symbolic honour, and nationalization (D) is state takeover of private businesses. Memory tip: Birth, Registration (marriage), Naturalization (residence).",
 "|In a first-past-the-post electoral system, a candidate is declared elected when he|obtains a two-thirds majority of the votes cast|records the highest number of the votes cast|scores the aggregate of the opponents' votes|obtains one-third of the votes cast": "First-past-the-post, also called the simple majority or plurality system, is used for single-member constituencies in Britain and in Nigeria's legislative elections. The candidate with the highest number of votes wins, even if he has less than half of the votes cast. If A has 40%, B 35% and C 25%, A wins: B. The trap: a two-thirds majority (A) is needed to amend a constitution or override a veto, not to win an ordinary seat. C describes an absolute majority (more than all opponents together), which some systems require for a run-off. Memory tip: like a race, the first past the post wins, however slow.",
 "|On the basis of its structure, a political party can be classified as indirect if|it campaigns for votes through agents|its membership is acquired through other groups|it controls government indirectly|it contests elections by proxy": "Maurice Duverger classified parties by structure into direct and indirect parties. In a direct party, people join as individuals and pay dues. In an indirect party, people become members through other organisations affiliated to the party, such as trade unions, cooperatives or farmers' associations; the classic example is the British Labour Party, whose early members came mainly through the trade unions. So a party is indirect if its membership is acquired through other groups: B. The trap: A, C and D all play on the word \"indirectly\", but campaigning through agents and contesting by proxy describe methods, not membership structure. Memory tip: indirect party = you join through your union.",
 "|The pressure group that resorts to unconventional methods to achieve its objectives is called|promotional group|interest group|anomic group|institutional group": "Gabriel Almond classified pressure groups into four types: associational (organised bodies like the NLC or NBA), institutional (groups inside government such as the army or civil service), non-associational (based on kinship, ethnicity or religion) and anomic. Anomic groups are spontaneous and unorganised; they spring up suddenly and use unconventional methods such as riots, violent demonstrations and blocking roads: C. Promotional (A) and interest (B) groups normally use lawful methods such as lobbying, and institutional groups (D) work inside formal institutions; anomic groups resort to riots and unconventional action. Memory tip: anomic = a-nomos = \"without law\": lawless, riotous action.",
 "|The method used to determine the possible outcome of an electoral contest is|opinion poll|referendum|plebiscite|press review": "An opinion poll asks a carefully chosen sample of voters how they intend to vote or what they think of an issue, then estimates the views of the whole electorate. That is how forecasters predict the likely outcome of an election before it is held: A. The trap: a referendum (B) and a plebiscite (C) are votes in which the whole electorate decides a question directly, for example the 1961 plebiscites in the British Cameroons; they do not forecast an election. A press review (D) only summarises what newspapers say. Memory tip: a poll PREDICTS; a referendum DECIDES.",
 "|The major function of the civil service is|advising government and implementing its policies|promoting the interest of civil servants|providing relevant information on government|keeping records of the activities of government": "The civil service is the body of permanent, professional, non-partisan officials in government ministries. Its two main jobs are to advise ministers when policy is being made, giving facts and expert opinion, and then to implement the policies the government decides on. These are its central functions: A. Keeping records (D) and providing information (C) are smaller duties that serve the main one, and promoting civil servants' interests (B) is the job of their unions. Memory tip: civil servants ADVISE before a decision and IMPLEMENT after it.",
 "|Under the emirate system, legislative functions were performed by the|Ulama|Khadi|Emir|Hakimi": "In the Hausa-Fulani emirate system after the 1804 jihad, the Emir held supreme political, religious and judicial authority. He made laws for the emirate within the limits of Islamic (Sharia) law, appointed officials and could remove them, and his court was the highest court. So legislative functions were performed by the Emir: C. The trap: the Ulama (A) were Islamic scholars who advised on the Sharia but did not make law; the Khadi or Alkali (B) were judges; the Hakimi (D) were district heads who collected taxes and kept order. Memory tip: in the emirate, all three powers met in one man: the Emir.",
 "|The pre-colonial Tiv society can best be described as|monarchical|republican|aristocratic|feudalistic": "The pre-colonial Tiv of the Benue valley, like the Igbo, were an acephalous (headless) society: they had no king or central government. Authority was spread among lineage elders and family heads, decisions were reached by consensus in meetings, and age and kinship mattered more than rank. Such a decentralised, participatory society is described as republican: B. Monarchical (A) fits the emirates or Oyo, feudalistic (D) fits the emirate fief system, and aristocratic (C) means rule by a noble class, which the Tiv lacked. Memory tip: Tiv and Igbo = acephalous = republican; Hausa-Fulani and Oyo = centralised = monarchical.",
 "|The failure of the assimilation policy necessitated the introduction of|indirect rule|direct rule|association|paternalism": "France first ruled its African colonies by assimilation: it tried to turn Africans into Frenchmen by spreading French language, law and culture, and granting French citizenship to the few \"évolués\". It failed: few Africans qualified, it was costly, and Africans resisted losing their cultures. So France adopted association, a policy that respected local customs and institutions while keeping colonies firmly under French control: C. The trap: indirect rule (A) was the British policy, associated with Lord Lugard in Northern Nigeria; direct rule (B) was the method, not the replacement policy. Memory tip: French colonies: Assimilation, then Association (A to A).",
 "|The political party formed in 1923 by Herbert Macaulay was|National Council of Nigeria and the Cameroons|Nigerian National Democratic Party|Nigerian Youth Movement|United National Independent Party": "Herbert Macaulay, often called the father of Nigerian nationalism, founded the Nigerian National Democratic Party (NNDP) on 24 June 1923. It was Nigeria's first political party, formed to contest the three Lagos seats in the Legislative Council created by the Clifford Constitution of 1922, which it won repeatedly until 1938: B. The trap: the NCNC (A) was founded in 1944 with Macaulay as its first president and Azikiwe as secretary, so it is linked to Macaulay but came later; the Nigerian Youth Movement (C) began in 1934 and defeated the NNDP in 1938. Memory tip: Clifford 1922 gave elections; Macaulay's NNDP 1923 contested them.",
 "|In the First Republic, the official opposition party was the|Northern People's Congress|Northern Elements Progressive Union|National Council of Nigeria and the Cameroons|Action Group": "After the 1959 federal election, the NPC (strongest in the North) formed a coalition government with the NCNC, and Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa became Prime Minister. The third major party, Chief Obafemi Awolowo's Action Group, became the official opposition in the federal House of Representatives, and Awolowo became Leader of the Opposition at independence: D. The trap: the NPC (A) and NCNC (C) were in government, not opposition; NEPU (B), led by Aminu Kano, was a small northern ally of the NCNC. Memory tip: First Republic: NPC + NCNC = government; AG = opposition (Awolowo, leader of the opposition).",
 "|The first federal constitution in Nigeria was the|Clifford Constitution|Macpherson Constitution|Lyttleton Constitution|Independence Constitution": "Nigeria's colonial constitutions moved step by step: Clifford (1922) introduced the elective principle; Richards (1946) introduced regionalism; Macpherson (1951) gave a quasi-federal system; and Lyttleton (1954) established a true federation. The Lyttleton Constitution divided powers between the federal government and three regions (plus the Southern Cameroons) through exclusive, concurrent and residual lists, and made Lagos federal territory: C. The trap: Macpherson (B) is called quasi-federal because the centre could still overrule the regions. The Independence Constitution of 1960 (D) continued an existing federation. Memory tip: C-R-M-L: Clifford elections, Richards regions, Macpherson semi-federal, Lyttleton full federal.",
 "|A distinctive feature of the Richards Constitution was its provision for|official members in the Legislative Council|unofficial members in the Legislative Council|a Legislative Council for the whole of Nigeria|the establishment of local councils": "The Richards Constitution of 1946, under Governor Sir Arthur Richards, created a Legislative Council for the whole of Nigeria, bringing the North into central law-making for the first time (under Clifford the Council legislated only for the South). It also created regional Houses of Assembly for the North, West and East, starting regionalism: C. The trap: official members (A) and unofficial members (B) had both existed in the Legislative Council since 1922, so they were not distinctive. Local councils (D) were not its feature. Memory tip: Richards = whole-country Legislative Council + Regions.",
 "|Under the 1999 Constitution, the appointment of ministers and ambassadors must be ratified by the|House of Representatives|Senate|Presidency|National Council of States": "The 1999 Constitution makes the president nominate ministers (section 147) and ambassadors (section 171), but each appointment must be confirmed by the Senate before the nominee takes office. This is a check by the legislature on the executive. So ministers and ambassadors must be confirmed by the Senate: B. The trap: A, the House of Representatives, is the other chamber of the National Assembly, but under the 1999 Constitution only the Senate confirms the appointment of ministers and ambassadors. Memory tip: Senate Screens and Sanctions appointments.",
 "|The legislature in Nigeria under the 1999 Constitution is composed of|100 senators and 250 members of the House of Representatives|108 senators and 350 members of the House of Representatives|109 senators and 360 members of the House of Representatives|109 senators and 350 members of the House of Representatives": "Nigeria's National Assembly is bicameral. The Senate has 109 members: three senators from each of the 36 states (108) plus one from the Federal Capital Territory, Abuja. The House of Representatives has 360 members elected from single-member constituencies shared out roughly by population: C. The trap: D gets the Senate right but the House wrong (350). B forgets the FCT senator (108). A matches no Nigerian legislature. These numbers have not changed since 1999. Memory tip: 36 states x 3 + 1 = 109 senators; 360 reps, like the 360 degrees of a circle.",
 "|The head of the Nigerian judiciary is the|Attorney-General and Minister of Justice|Chief Justice of the Federation|President of the Court of Appeal|Solicitor-General of the Federation": "The Chief Justice of Nigeria (officially the Chief Justice of the Federation) heads the Supreme Court and the whole Nigerian judiciary. He or she is appointed by the president on the recommendation of the National Judicial Council, subject to Senate confirmation, and chairs the National Judicial Council: B. The trap: the Attorney-General and Minister of Justice (A) is the government's chief law officer and a member of the executive, not the head of the judiciary. The President of the Court of Appeal (C) heads only that court; the Solicitor-General (D) is the top civil servant in the Ministry of Justice. Memory tip: CJN heads the Courts; AGF advises the Government.",
 "|The commission established to handle the electoral activities in the Second Republic of Nigeria was|INEC|NECON|FEDECO|NEC": "The Federal Electoral Commission (FEDECO) was set up by the Murtala/Obasanjo regime in 1976. It registered the five parties of 1978 (NPN, UPN, NPP, GNPP, PRP) and conducted the 1979 and 1983 elections of the Second Republic: C. The trap: INEC (A), the Independent National Electoral Commission, was created in 1998 and has run elections since the Fourth Republic began in 1999. NEC (D), the National Electoral Commission, served the Babangida transition (1987–1993), and NECON (B) served the Abacha era. Memory tip: ECN (First Republic), FEDECO (Second), NEC (Third), NECON (Abacha), INEC (Fourth).",
 "|A major function of the State Independent Electoral Commissions in Nigeria is the|conduct of gubernatorial elections|delineation of electoral constituencies|registration of political parties|conduct of elections into local government offices": "The 1999 Constitution created a State Independent Electoral Commission (SIEC) for each state. Its main job is to organise, conduct and supervise elections into local government councils: chairmen, vice-chairmen and councillors (Third Schedule, Part II): D. The trap: governorship elections (A), the delimitation of constituencies (B) and the registration of political parties (C) are all functions of INEC, the national electoral body. Memory tip: INEC runs elections from the president down to the state assembly; SIEC runs local council elections.",
 "|In Nigeria, promotion of judges is the responsibility of the|Council of Legal Education|Attorney-General and Minister of Justice|Judicial Service Commission|Chief Justice of the Federation": "To keep judges independent, their appointment, promotion and discipline are given to judicial bodies, not to politicians. In Nigeria this is done by the Judicial Service Commissions (the Federal Judicial Service Commission and the state Judicial Service Commissions), working with the National Judicial Council created by the 1999 Constitution: C. The trap: the Chief Justice (D) chairs the National Judicial Council, but promotion is a commission's collective decision, not his personal power; the Attorney-General (B) is a member of the executive; the Council of Legal Education (A) runs the Nigerian Law School. Memory tip: judges are looked after by a JUDICIAL body, not by a politician.",
 "|During the Second Republic, the Great Nigeria People's Party won the governorship election in|Borno and Kaduna States|Gongola and Sokoto States|Borno and Gongola States|Sokoto and Bauchi States": "In the 1979 governorship elections the NPN won seven states, the UPN five, the NPP three, the PRP two (Kano and Kaduna) and the GNPP two: Borno, where Alhaji Mohammed Goni became governor, and Gongola, where Alhaji Abubakar Barde became governor. Both lay in the north-east, the home area of the party's leader, Alhaji Waziri Ibrahim: C. The trap: Kaduna (A) went to the PRP (Balarabe Musa), and Sokoto and Bauchi (B, D) went to the NPN. Memory tip: GNPP's two = the two north-eastern states, Borno and Gongola, home of Waziri Ibrahim.",
 "|In local government administration, Chief-and-Council implies that|all members of the local council are appointed and controlled by the chief|the chief is a member of the local council but he does not exercise veto power|the council operates independently of the chief's authority|members of the local council appoint the chief to office": "In the Native Authorities of colonial Nigeria there were three forms. A Sole Native Authority ruled alone. Under Chief-in-Council the chief had to consult his council but could override its advice. Under Chief-and-Council the chief sat as a member of the council and had to accept its majority decision, so he had no veto: B. The trap: A describes Chief-in-Council, where the chief dominates and can overrule his council; in Chief-and-Council the chief is only a member of the council and has no veto. Memory tip: Chief-IN-Council: the chief is IN charge; Chief-AND-Council: the chief is just one AND the rest outvote him.",
 "|In the present local government structure, traditional rulers play|a judicial role|an advisory role|a legislative role|an executive role": "The 1976 local government reform made local councils the third tier of government, run by elected chairmen and councillors. Traditional rulers were removed from executive and judicial roles and placed in Traditional Councils, whose job is to advise the councils, help with chieftaincy matters and mobilise their people. That arrangement continues today: B. The trap: before the reform, chiefs did play executive and judicial roles (D, A) as Native Authorities and through native courts, so these options describe the old system, not the present one. They make no laws (C). Memory tip: after 1976 the chief became the council's adviser, not its boss.",
 "|The first successful disengagement of the military from politics in Nigeria was carried out by the government of|Murtala/Obasanjo|Buhari/Idiagbon|Ibrahim Babangida|Abdulsalami Abubakar": "General Murtala Mohammed came to power in July 1975 and announced a programme to return Nigeria to civil rule by 1979. After his assassination in February 1976, General Olusegun Obasanjo carried it out: a new constitution, the creation of FEDECO, party registration and elections. On 1 October 1979 he handed over to the elected president, Alhaji Shehu Shagari. This was the first successful military disengagement: A. The trap: Abdulsalami Abubakar (D) also handed over, in 1999, but that was the second. Babangida's transition (C) collapsed with the annulment of the June 1993 election, and Buhari/Idiagbon (B) never handed over. Memory tip: 1979 Obasanjo to Shagari; 1999 Abdulsalami to Obasanjo.",
 "|The atomic bomb tests carried out around the Sahara Desert made Nigeria sever relations with|France|Canada|The United States|Germany": "In February 1960 France exploded its first atomic bomb at Reggane in the Algerian Sahara, and further tests followed. Nigerians feared radioactive fallout on West Africa and saw the tests as contempt for African lives. After another test in December 1960, the Balewa government broke diplomatic relations with France in January 1961 and banned French ships and aircraft: A. The United States (C) tested its bombs in Nevada and the Pacific, and Canada (B) and Germany (D) carried out none; France's Sahara tests of 1960–61 led Nigeria to break relations. Memory tip: Sahara bomb = France = Nigeria's first big break with a Western power (1961).",
 "|Nigeria supports the South-South cooperation as a foreign policy objective for mutual understanding among|sub-Saharan African countries|developing countries|Asian and sub-Saharan African countries|nations south of the equator": "\"South-South cooperation\" means cooperation among the developing countries of Africa, Asia, Latin America and the Caribbean, often called the global South, in trade, technology, education and diplomacy. The term contrasts with \"North-South\" relations between these poor countries and the rich industrial nations of the North. Nigeria supports it through bodies such as the Non-Aligned Movement, the G-77 and the D-8: B. The trap: D takes \"South\" literally, but South-South is a political label for developing countries; Nigeria and India, both active in it, lie north of the equator. Memory tip: in world politics, North = rich, South = developing, whatever the map says.",
 "|An indication that Africa is still the centre-piece of Nigeria's foreign policy was the creation of the|Nigerian Institute of International Affairs|Ministry of Integration and Cooperation in Africa|Nigeria Institute of Policy and Strategic Studies|Ministry of Foreign Affairs": "Since independence Nigeria has called Africa the \"centre-piece\" (cornerstone) of its foreign policy: decolonisation, the fight against apartheid, the OAU, ECOWAS and peacekeeping in Liberia and Sierra Leone. Creating a separate Ministry of Integration and Cooperation in Africa, a whole ministry for African affairs alone, showed that this commitment was still central: B. The trap: the Ministry of Foreign Affairs (D) handles relations with the whole world, so its existence says nothing special about Africa. The Nigerian Institute of International Affairs (A) and the Institute of Policy and Strategic Studies at Kuru (C) are research and training bodies. Memory tip: a ministry with \"Africa\" in its name = Africa as centre-piece.",
 "|The Nigeria-Cameroun crisis over the Bakassi peninsula occurred owing to the interpretation of the treaty of 1913 and the|Maroua Accord|resolution of the OAU|decision of the International Court of Justice|decision of the ECOWAS Tribunal": "The 1913 Anglo-German treaty drew the border between British Nigeria and German Kamerun and placed Bakassi on the Cameroon side. In 1975 General Gowon and President Ahidjo signed the Maroua Accord (Declaration) on the maritime boundary, which later Nigerian governments refused to accept because it was never ratified by the Supreme Military Council. Disputes over these two documents led to clashes and to Cameroon's case at the ICJ: A. The trap: the ICJ decision (C) came in October 2002, as the result of the dispute, not its cause; it gave Bakassi to Cameroon, and Nigeria handed it over in August 2008 under the Greentree Agreement. Memory tip: Bakassi: 1913 treaty + 1975 Maroua, then ICJ 2002, handover 2008.",
 "|Nigeria's voting at the United Nations is guided primarily by|world peace|the cold war|her concern for Africa|her national interests": "Every state's foreign policy, including its voting at the United Nations, is guided above all by its national interest: security, territorial integrity, economic welfare and prestige. Nigeria's support for African liberation, world peace and non-alignment is itself how Nigeria defines and pursues its national interest: D. The trap: concern for Africa (C) is tempting because Africa is the centre-piece of Nigeria's policy, but it is one expression of the national interest, not the primary guide. The Cold War (B) ended in 1991, and Nigeria's policy was non-alignment. Memory tip: in foreign policy, national interest always comes first; the rest are means to it.",
 "|Under the umbrella of the OAU, Nigeria mediated in the dispute between|Kenya and Somalia|Tunisia and Morocco|Ethiopia and Somalia|Zimbabwe and Tanzania": "The Ogaden region, inhabited mainly by Somalis but ruled by Ethiopia, caused a long border dispute and two wars (1964 and 1977–78). The OAU set up a Good Offices Committee to mediate, and Nigeria, a leading OAU member, chaired it: C. The trap: Kenya and Somalia (A) also had a border dispute over Kenya's north-eastern region in the 1960s, but it was eased by the 1967 Arusha memorandum, with Zambia's President Kaunda mediating, not by Nigerian mediation. Tunisia and Morocco (B) and Zimbabwe and Tanzania (D) had no such dispute. Memory tip: Ogaden = Ethiopia v. Somalia = OAU mediation chaired by Nigeria.",
 "|Nigeria is rated in the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries as the|third largest oil producer|fifth largest oil producer|sixth largest oil producer|seventh largest oil producer": "Nigeria joined OPEC in 1971. When this paper was set, it was rated the sixth largest oil producer in the organisation: C. Nigeria was ranked sixth in OPEC at the time; rankings shift with output, quotas and membership, so check current figures. Memory tip: Nigeria joined OPEC in 1971 and has long sat around sixth in the league.",
 "|Which of the following served as the Secretary-General of the United Nations?|Javier Perez de Cuellar|Mohamed El-Baradei|Ibrahim Gambari|Joseph Garba": "Javier Perez de Cuellar of Peru was the fifth Secretary-General of the United Nations, serving from 1982 to 1991; he helped end the Iran-Iraq war in 1988: A. The trap: the other three were senior international figures but never Secretary-General. Joseph Garba (D), a Nigerian, was President of the UN General Assembly (1989–90); Ibrahim Gambari (C), also Nigerian, was Nigeria's UN ambassador and later a UN Under-Secretary-General; Mohamed ElBaradei (B) of Egypt headed the International Atomic Energy Agency (1997–2009). Memory tip: Secretaries-General: Lie, Hammarskjold, U Thant, Waldheim, Perez de Cuellar, Boutros-Ghali, Annan, Ban, Guterres.",
 "|A Security Council resolution requires the concurrence of|3 of the 5 permanent members|1 of the 5 permanent members|all of the 5 permanent members|9 of the 15 council members": "Article 27 of the UN Charter says decisions of the Security Council on substantive matters need the affirmative votes of nine of its fifteen members, \"including the concurring votes of the permanent members\" (USA, Russia, China, Britain, France). Because each permanent member must concur, any one of them can block a resolution: this is the veto. So a resolution requires the concurrence of all five permanent members: C. The trap: D, nine of fifteen, is also needed, but on its own it is not enough, since nine votes fail if one permanent member says no. (In practice an abstention is not counted as a veto.) Memory tip: 9 votes + 5 \"yes\" from the Big Five (no veto).",
 "|A member of the Commonwealth that still recognizes the British monarch as its Head of State is|Ghana|India|Australia|Singapore": "A Commonwealth realm is a member that keeps the British monarch as its head of state, represented by a Governor-General. Australia is one: it voted in a 1999 referendum to keep the monarchy, and it still does today. The monarch was Queen Elizabeth II when this paper was set and has been King Charles III since September 2022: C. The trap: Ghana (A, a republic since 1960), India (B, since 1950) and Singapore (D, independent republic since 1965) are Commonwealth members, but each has its own president as head of state; they accept the monarch only as symbolic Head of the Commonwealth. Memory tip: realms keep the Crown (Australia, Canada, New Zealand); republics keep only the club.",
 "|A major achievement of the OAU was the|decolonization of Africa|promotion of development|eradication of poverty|establishment of democracy in Africa": "The Organisation of African Unity was founded in Addis Ababa on 25 May 1963. Its greatest success was the decolonisation of Africa: through its Liberation Committee it supported freedom movements until the last colonies became free and apartheid ended in South Africa in 1994: A. Development (B), poverty eradication (C) and democracy (D) were aims the OAU largely failed to achieve; its clear success was helping end colonial rule in Africa. Memory tip: OAU = liberation success, development failure.",
 "|The power to exercise overall control of the executive functions of ECOWAS is vested in the|Technical and Special Commissions|Council of Ministers|Authority of Heads of State and Government|Secretary-General": "ECOWAS was created by the Treaty of Lagos on 28 May 1975. Its highest organ is the Authority of Heads of State and Government, which meets at least once a year, directs and controls the community's executive functions, and takes the final decisions: C. The trap: the Secretary-General (D) headed the Executive Secretariat that carried out decisions day to day; in 2007 it was replaced by the ECOWAS Commission headed by a President. The Council of Ministers (B) advises the Authority and supervises the community under it; the Technical and Special Commissions (A) prepare projects. Memory tip: in every such body, the summit of leaders is the boss.",
 "|One of the organizational weaknesses of the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries is its inability to|collect membership dues promptly|punish erring members|assist poor countries|monitor global oil consumption": "OPEC, founded in Baghdad in 1960, tries to stabilise oil prices by setting production quotas for each member. Its key weakness is that it has no power to punish members who produce more than their quota, so cheating on quotas has often pushed prices down and weakened its unity: B. Collecting dues (A) is not OPEC's known problem, poor countries are helped through the separate OPEC Fund (C), and OPEC does monitor oil consumption (D); its weakness is that it cannot punish members who break quotas. Memory tip: OPEC can set quotas but cannot enforce them.",
 "|Galadima in the emirate system refers to|a village head|the officer in charge of the army|the administrator of the capital city|a judge": "The Hausa-Fulani emirate was run by the Emir with titled officials, each in charge of one area. The Waziri was the chief adviser (like a prime minister), the Madawaki commanded the army, the Ma'aji kept the treasury, the Alkali judged cases under Sharia law, the Sarkin Ruwa handled water and fishing, and the Galadima administered the capital city. So Galadima refers to the administrator of the capital city: C. The trap: B (the army) is the Madawaki and D (a judge) is the Alkali; a village head (A) is the Mai Anguwa or Dagaci. Memory tip: Galadima guards the capital, Madawaki marches the army, Ma'aji minds the money.",
 "|After the defeat of Germany in World War I, her former colonies were administered under the League of Nations as|occupied territories|protectorates|crown colonies|mandated territories": "After Germany lost World War I, the Treaty of Versailles (1919) took away her colonies. The League of Nations did not hand them over outright: it gave them as \"mandates\" to the victors to govern on its behalf and report to it. Britain and France divided the Cameroons and Togoland; Britain also got Tanganyika. They were mandated territories: D. In 1946 the United Nations replaced the League, and the mandates became trust territories. The trap: protectorates (B) and crown colonies (C) were Britain's own possessions, not held under the League. Memory tip: League = Mandate; UN = Trust.",
 "|Under the 1963 Republican Constitution, the president exercised|judicial powers|nominal powers|concurrent powers|executive powers": "On 1 October 1963 Nigeria became a republic: the British Queen ceased to be head of state and Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe became the first President. But the 1963 Constitution kept the parliamentary (Westminster) system. The President was only a ceremonial head of state with nominal powers; he signed bills and appointed ministers on the Prime Minister's advice. Real executive power lay with Prime Minister Tafawa Balewa and his cabinet. So the President exercised nominal powers: B. The trap: D (executive powers) was true only from the 1979 Constitution, when Nigeria adopted the presidential system. Memory tip: 1963 = republic but ceremonial president; 1979 = executive president.",
 "|A major feature of the policy of deregulation in Nigeria is the|proliferation of public corporations|phenomenal increase in direct foreign investment|enthronement of market forces mechanism|increasing dominance of the economy by the state": "Deregulation means removing government control from parts of the economy, so that prices, exchange rates and entry into businesses (oil marketing, telecoms, banking) are set by supply and demand instead of by government order. Nigeria began it under the 1986 Structural Adjustment Programme. Its main feature is enthroning market forces: C. The trap: B (foreign investment) is a hoped-for result, not the defining feature, and it has not always been \"phenomenal\". A and D describe the opposite: more state enterprises and state control. Memory tip: deregulation = de-control; let the market decide.",
 "|At its inception in 1919, the National Congress of British West Africa was led by|Marcus Garvey|Nnamdi Azikiwe|Casely Hayford|William Du Bois": "The National Congress of British West Africa was the first nationalist movement linking Nigeria, the Gold Coast, Sierra Leone and the Gambia. It was led by J. E. Casely Hayford, a Gold Coast lawyer and writer; its first conference met in Accra in March 1920 after planning from 1919. It demanded elected seats in the colonial councils, and its pressure helped bring the elective principle in Nigeria's 1922 Clifford Constitution. The answer is C. The trap: Marcus Garvey (A) and W. E. B. Du Bois (D) were Pan-Africanists in the Americas, and Azikiwe (B) became prominent only in the 1930s. Memory tip: Casely Hayford's Congress produced Clifford's elective principle.",
 "|The main reason for the poor performance of the Public Complaints Commission in Nigeria is that|its activities are interfered with|it lacks adequate funds to play its roles|people have not been lodging complaints|it lacks the powers to prosecute offenders": "The Public Complaints Commission, set up by the Murtala/Obasanjo regime in 1975, is Nigeria's ombudsman. Any citizen can complain to it free of charge about injustice or abuse by government officials or agencies. But it can only investigate and recommend remedies; it has no power to prosecute offenders or enforce its decisions, so officials often ignore it. That is the main reason for its poor performance: D. The trap: A and B (interference and funds) are real complaints, but the basic weakness is in its legal powers. Memory tip: an ombudsman can bark (investigate and recommend) but cannot bite (prosecute).",
 "|The signing of protectorate treaty was used by the Europeans to penetrate|the Niger Delta|Eastern Nigeria|Northern Nigeria|Western Nigeria": "A treaty of protection was an agreement in which a local ruler accepted British \"protection\", in practice British control. The Royal Niger Company of George Goldie signed hundreds of such treaties with rulers in the North, including Sokoto and Gwandu in 1885, and used them to claim the region at the Berlin Conference; it was a main route of penetration into Northern Nigeria: C. Option A (the Niger Delta) is also true: in 1884 Consul Edward Hewett signed treaties with Delta chiefs, creating the Oil Rivers Protectorate in 1885. This question is debatable, so both A and C are accepted. Memory tip: treaty first, protectorate next, conquest last.",
 "|The chairman of the National Defence Council as provided in the 1999 Constitution is the|Chief of Army Staff|Chief of Defence Staff|President|Minister of Defence": "Section 153 of the 1999 Constitution sets up federal executive bodies, including the National Defence Council and the National Security Council. The Third Schedule says the National Defence Council is made up of the President as chairman, the Vice-President, the Minister of Defence, the Chief of Defence Staff and the service chiefs. It advises the President on defence and the armed forces. The chairman is the President: C. The trap: the Chief of Defence Staff (B) and the Minister of Defence (D) are members, but the President chairs as Commander-in-Chief of the armed forces. Memory tip: the President chairs every top council (Council of State, NDC, NSC).",
 "|Constitutional matters in which the Nigerian federal and state governments exercise joint authority are on the|concurrent list|reserved list|residual list|exclusive list": "Nigeria's constitution divides legislative powers into lists. The Exclusive Legislative List holds matters only the federal government can legislate on (defence, currency, foreign affairs). The Concurrent Legislative List holds matters on which both federal and state governments can make laws (education, agriculture, electricity); if their laws conflict, the federal law prevails. Matters on neither list are residual and belong to the states. So joint authority is on the concurrent list: A. The trap: the exclusive list (D) is federal only, and the residual list (C) belongs to the states. Memory tip: concurrent = \"running together\", both levels share.",
 "|Under colonial administration, what was the status of the inhabitants of the Lagos Crown Colony?|British servants|British citizens|British settlers|British subjects": "Lagos was annexed by Britain in 1861 and became a crown colony: British territory ruled directly by the Crown. People born in a colony were British subjects, owing allegiance to the British monarch. People in the protectorates (the rest of Nigeria) were only \"British protected persons\", because a protectorate was not legally British soil. So the inhabitants of Lagos colony were British subjects: D. The trap: British citizens (B) was a later legal category (British nationality law of 1948 and after) and did not give colonials full rights; servants and settlers are not legal statuses. Memory tip: colony = subject; protectorate = protected person.",
 "|In 1999, the Alliance for Democracy won the governorship election in|four states|five states|seven states|six states": "In the 1999 elections that ended military rule, three parties were registered: PDP, APP and AD. The Alliance for Democracy, built on Afenifere and the Yoruba political tradition of Awolowo, won the governorship in all six South-West states: Lagos (Bola Tinubu), Ogun, Oyo, Osun, Ondo and Ekiti. The answer is six: D. The trap: five (B) is a common slip if you forget Ekiti, created only in 1996. In 2003 the AD lost five of the six states to the PDP, keeping only Lagos; it later faded, and most of it went into the Action Congress (2006) and then the APC (2013). Memory tip: AD 1999 = six South-West states.",
 "|The 1960 Independence Constitution of Nigeria provided for the|Queen as head of state|Governor-General as head of state|Governor-General as head of government|President as head of government": "Nigeria became independent on 1 October 1960 as a dominion within the Commonwealth. Under the 1960 Independence Constitution the British monarch, Queen Elizabeth II, remained Queen of Nigeria and head of state. She was represented in Nigeria by the Governor-General (Sir James Robertson, then Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe from November 1960). The Prime Minister, Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa, was head of government. So the Queen was head of state: A. The trap: B, because the Governor-General performed the head-of-state duties, but he did so only as the Queen's representative. Memory tip: 1960 = Queen; 1963 = President (republic).",
 "|During colonial administration, the office of the warrant chief was introduced in|Western Nigeria|Northern Nigeria|the Lagos Territory|Eastern Nigeria": "Indirect rule needed traditional rulers to rule through. In the East, the Igbo, Ibibio and their neighbours had no centralised kings; authority was spread among elders, age grades and titled men. So the British created \"warrant chiefs\": men given a warrant (certificate) to sit in the native courts and enforce colonial orders. Many were not traditional leaders, and their corruption fuelled resentment, including the 1929 Aba Women's Riot. The answer is Eastern Nigeria: D. The trap: Northern Nigeria (B) is where indirect rule worked best, through the existing emirs. Memory tip: no kings in the East, so the British made warrant chiefs.",
 "|In the pre-colonial Yoruba political system, the council of chiefs constituted the|ombudsman|executive|judicial arm|legislative arm": "In the Oyo Empire the Alaafin was the king, but his power was checked by the Oyo Mesi, a council of seven kingmakers led by the Bashorun. The Oyo Mesi chose the Alaafin, made laws with him, advised him and, if he became tyrannical, could reject him by presenting an empty calabash or parrot's eggs, forcing him to commit suicide. As law-makers and representatives, they acted as the legislative arm: D. The trap: the Ogboni society also checked the Oyo Mesi and had judicial roles (so C is wrong here), and the executive (B) was the Alaafin himself. Memory tip: Oyo Mesi makes laws; Ogboni balances.",
 "|A major merit of privatization is that it increases|industrial harmony|activities on the stock exchange|public fiscal resources|labour turnover": "Privatization is the sale of government-owned shares in public enterprises to private individuals and firms. In Nigeria (the 1988 Privatization and Commercialization Decree, and the Bureau of Public Enterprises from 1999) many shares were sold to the public through the Nigerian Stock Exchange, which widened share ownership and greatly increased trading on the exchange. So a major merit is more activity on the stock exchange: B. The trap: C (public fiscal resources) sounds right because the sale brings in money, but it is a one-off gain and the government loses the enterprise. Labour turnover (D) is a cost, not a merit. Memory tip: privatize = sell shares = stock exchange.",
 "|The highest decision-making body under the Gowon Regime was the|Provisional Ruling Council|Supreme Military Council|Armed Forces Ruling Council|Federal Executive Council": "Each military regime had its own top ruling body. Gowon (1966–1975) and then Murtala/Obasanjo (1975–1979) ruled through the Supreme Military Council (SMC). Buhari (1984–85) also used the SMC. Babangida (1985–1993) renamed it the Armed Forces Ruling Council (AFRC), and Abacha (1993–98) and Abubakar (1998–99) used the Provisional Ruling Council (PRC). So under Gowon it was the Supreme Military Council: B. The trap: the Federal Executive Council (D) existed under Gowon too, but it was the cabinet below the SMC. Memory tip: SMC for Gowon, AFRC for Babangida, PRC for Abacha.",
 "|In pre-colonial governance, women played prominent roles in the|Tiv society|Igbo society|Yoruba Kingdom|Jukun Kingdom": "Pre-colonial Nigerian women were not all excluded from public life. In Igbo society the umuada (married daughters of a lineage) settled disputes and could sanction men, women's associations controlled markets, and in some towns such as Onitsha the Omu was a female ruler over women's affairs: B. Option C is also true: in the Yoruba kingdoms the Iyalode represented women in the council of chiefs, and palace officials such as the Iya Oba had real power. This question is debatable, so both B and C are accepted. The Tiv (A) were a male, elder-led society, while Igbo and Yoruba women held recognized political roles such as the Omu and the Iyalode. Memory tip: Igbo umuada, Yoruba Iyalode.",
 "|Before the 1976 reforms, the three-tier local government system was practised by|Mid-Western and Eastern Nigeria|Western and Northern Nigeria|Eastern and Western Nigeria|Northern and Eastern Nigeria": "In the 1950s the Eastern and Western Regions copied the English model of local government. The Eastern Region Local Government Ordinance of 1950 created three tiers: county, district and local councils. The Western Region Local Government Law of 1952 created divisional, district and local councils. The North kept its emir-based Native Authorities. The 1976 reform replaced all of these with one uniform single-tier system. The answer is C. Some books describe the North's Native Authority, district and village councils as tiers, so this question is debatable. The trap: B puts the North in place of the East. Memory tip: East and West copied England; the North kept its emirs.",
 "|In Nigeria, military regimes returned power to civilians through|negotiations|impositions|transition programmes|constitutional conferences": "Nigeria's military rulers did not simply hand over power overnight. They ran \"transition programmes\": timed plans that included drafting a new constitution, lifting the ban on politics, registering parties, a census or voter registration, and staged elections. Obasanjo's programme ended with the 1979 hand-over to Shagari; Babangida's long programme collapsed with the 1993 annulment; and Abubakar's brief one led to the 1999 hand-over to Obasanjo. The answer is C. The trap: constitutional conferences (D) were often one step within a transition, not the whole method. Memory tip: soldiers leave by a timetable: the transition programme.",
 "|The ECOWAS Tribunal is responsible for|interpreting ECOWAS treaties|ratifying international treaties for member states|trying erring member states of ECOWAS|ensuring compliance with global treaties": "ECOWAS was founded by the Treaty of Lagos on 28 May 1975. Its revised 1993 Treaty set up a Community Tribunal, now the ECOWAS Community Court of Justice in Abuja (working since 2001). Its main job is to interpret and apply the ECOWAS Treaty and protocols and to settle disputes over them; since 2005 individuals can also bring human-rights cases to it. So it interprets ECOWAS treaties: A. The trap: C (trying erring states) sounds like a court, but the court rules on disputes, it does not put states on trial; ratifying treaties (B) is done by each country itself. Memory tip: treaty court = treaty interpreter.",
 "|Judges of the World Court are appointed to serve for|nine years|four years|ten years|five years": "The World Court is the International Court of Justice at The Hague, the main judicial organ of the UN. It has 15 judges, elected by both the General Assembly and the Security Council (an absolute majority in each). They serve nine-year terms and can be re-elected; a third of them are elected every three years, so the court is never completely renewed at once. No two judges may come from the same country. The answer is nine years: A. The trap: D, five years, is the UN Secretary-General's term, but World Court (ICJ) judges serve nine-year terms, with a third of the bench elected every three years. Memory tip: 15 judges, 9 years, 1/3 renewed every 3 years.",
 "|The highest decision-making organ of OPEC is the|Special Fund|Board of Governors|Secretariat|Conference": "OPEC (founded in Baghdad in 1960, with headquarters in Vienna) has three main organs. The Conference, made up of the oil ministers of member countries, is the supreme authority: it sets policy and production levels and admits new members. The Board of Governors manages the organisation and carries out Conference decisions, and the Secretariat, headed by the Secretary-General, does the day-to-day work. The answer is the Conference: D. The trap: the Board of Governors (B) sounds senior, but it answers to the Conference. The Special Fund (A) became OFID, OPEC's aid fund. Memory tip: OPEC's Conference of ministers is king.",
 "|The transformation of the OAU to AU is essentially to|enhance Africa's economic development|accelerate Africa's political development|accelerate Africa's technological development|promote Africa's socio-culture advancement": "The OAU (1963) was set up mainly to end colonialism and defend independence. By the 1990s most of Africa was free, so leaders wanted a body to speed up economic integration and development. The Sirte Declaration (1999) and Constitutive Act (2000) created the African Union, launched in Durban in July 2002, building on the 1991 Abuja Treaty for an African Economic Community. Its economic programme is NEPAD. So the change was essentially to enhance Africa's economic development: A. The trap: the AU also has political aims (peace, democracy, and the right to intervene in grave crimes), but the driving purpose was economic. Memory tip: OAU freed Africa; AU builds Africa.",
 "|Diplomatic missions among the Commonwealth member states are referred to as|high commission|legations|embassies|consulates": "Diplomatic missions between Commonwealth countries are called high commissions, and their heads are high commissioners, because the members share a special relationship (historically, the same monarch) rather than being \"foreign\" to one another. So Nigeria has a High Commission in London and in Accra, but an Embassy in Washington. The answer is A. The trap: embassies (C), headed by ambassadors, are used between non-Commonwealth countries; legations (B) were lower-ranking missions; consulates (D) look after citizens and trade, not full diplomacy. Memory tip: Commonwealth = High Commission.",
 "|The chairman of the UN Security Council emerges through|rotation|consensus|election|selection": "The officer this question calls the chairman is officially the President of the Security Council. The presidency rotates every month among the 15 members (5 permanent and 10 non-permanent), in the English alphabetical order of their names. The president calls and chairs meetings and speaks for the Council. The answer is rotation: A. The trap: election (C) is how the non-permanent members get their seats (the General Assembly elects them for two years), and how the UN General Assembly picks its president, but not how the Council's president is chosen. Memory tip: Security Council president changes with the calendar, every month.",
 "|A written constitution is distinguished by its|dependence on ordinary amendment procedures|cumbersome and complex amendment procedure|codification process|reliance on case laws": "A written constitution is one whose main rules are collected in a single formal document, like Nigeria's 1999 Constitution or the US Constitution. What distinguishes it is that it is codified: C. An unwritten constitution, like Britain's, is spread across statutes, case law and conventions. The trap: B (a cumbersome amendment procedure) describes a rigid constitution. Most written constitutions are rigid, but rigidity and writtenness are different questions, and some written constitutions are easy to amend. This question is debatable, since some textbooks link written with rigid. Memory tip: written vs unwritten is about form (codified or not); rigid vs flexible is about amendment.",
 "|One of the most essential characteristics of the state is|sovereignty|the rule of law|legitimacy|the civil service": "A state has four essential features: a population, a defined territory, a government, and sovereignty. Sovereignty, the supreme authority inside the territory and independence from outside control, is the most essential, because it is what separates a state from a province or colony. Lagos State has people, land and a government, but it is not sovereign. The answer is A. The trap: legitimacy (C) and the rule of law (B) are features of good government, not of statehood; an illegitimate military regime still governs a sovereign state. Memory tip: P-T-G-S (people, territory, government, sovereignty), and S is the key.",
 "|Fascism originated from|Italy|Greece|Germany|China": "Fascism began in Italy. Benito Mussolini founded his Fasci di Combattimento in 1919 and took power after the March on Rome in 1922. The name comes from the fasces, a bundle of rods with an axe carried in ancient Rome as a sign of authority. Fascism glorifies the nation and the state, rejects democracy and communism, and puts one leader above all. The answer is A. The trap: Germany (C) had Hitler's Nazism (National Socialism), a related movement that took power later, in 1933. Memory tip: fasces are Roman, so fascism is Italian.",
 "|The principle of separation of powers is intimately related to|checks and balances|the bureaucratic process|democratic rule|the rule of law": "Separation of powers, explained by Montesquieu in The Spirit of the Laws (1748), divides government into legislature, executive and judiciary, with different people and functions. It goes with checks and balances: each organ can limit the others, for example the president's veto, the legislature's power to impeach, and the courts' judicial review. Separation stops one organ holding all power, and the checks stop each organ abusing its own. The answer is A. The trap: the rule of law (D) and democratic rule (C) are related ideals, but checks and balances is the twin principle. Memory tip: separate, then check and balance.",
 "|Vote of confidence is basically a feature of|a coalition government|the parliamentary system|an aristocratic government|the presidential system": "In a parliamentary system the cabinet comes from parliament and stays in office only while it keeps parliament's confidence. That confidence is tested by a vote of confidence (or of no confidence): if the government loses, it must resign or call an election. Britain is the classic example, and Nigeria's First Republic used the same system. The answer is B. The trap: in the presidential system (D) the president has a fixed term and cannot be removed by a confidence vote, only by impeachment; a coalition (A) is just a government of several parties. Memory tip: parliament gives confidence, and parliament can take it away.",
 "|One of the greatest advocates of socialism was|Baron de Montesquieu|Karl Marx|A.V. Dicey|Adam Smith": "Karl Marx (1818–1883), a German philosopher, with Friedrich Engels wrote The Communist Manifesto (1848) and Das Kapital (1867). He argued that history is a class struggle, that capitalism exploits workers, and that workers would replace it with socialism and finally communism. He is the founder of \"scientific socialism\". The answer is B. Adam Smith (D) championed capitalism in The Wealth of Nations (1776), Montesquieu (A) taught separation of powers and Dicey (C) explained the rule of law. Memory tip: Smith = market; Marx = socialism; Montesquieu = separation; Dicey = rule of law.",
 "|Public opinion helps a government to|develop the country in line with the people's aspirations|monitor its socio-cultural policy|monitor the economic situation in the country|identify those that are opposed to its policies": "Public opinion is the views of the people on public issues and on their government. Through the media, polls, protests and elections it tells government what the people want and how they judge its policies. A government that listens can shape its programmes to meet those wishes, which keeps it popular and legitimate. The answer is A. Identifying opponents (D) treats public opinion as a spying tool, and B and C cover only one area of policy; public opinion guides government towards the people's wishes. Memory tip: public opinion is the people's voice, steering government toward their wishes.",
 "|The type of government in which an individual wields absolute power is called|theocracy|autocracy|oligarchy|plutocracy": "Autocracy (Greek autos, self, and kratos, rule) is government by one person who holds absolute, unlimited power, not checked by law or by other bodies; an absolute monarch or a dictator is an autocrat. The answer is B. The trap: oligarchy (C) is rule by a few; theocracy (A) is rule by religious leaders in God's name; plutocracy (D) is rule by the rich. Memory tip: count the rulers: one = autocracy, few = oligarchy, rich = plutocracy, priests = theocracy, people = democracy.",
 "|A distinctive feature of the presidential system of government is the|existence of two political parties|fusion of legislative and executive powers|existence of a ceremonial head of state|fusion of ceremonial and executive powers": "In a presidential system, such as the USA or Nigeria since 1979, one person, the president, is both head of state (the ceremonial role: national symbol, receiving ambassadors) and head of government (the executive role: running the administration). This fusion of ceremonial and executive powers is a distinctive feature: D. The trap: C (a ceremonial head of state) and B (fusion of legislative and executive powers) are features of the parliamentary system, where a monarch or president is ceremonial and ministers sit in parliament. A two-party system (A) can exist under either system. Memory tip: presidential = one person, two hats.",
 "|Individual as opposed to collective responsibility is a basic feature of the|presidential system of government|socialist system of government|monarchical system of government|parliamentary system of government": "In a presidential system the president appoints ministers (in Nigeria, with Senate confirmation) and each minister answers individually to the president, who can sack any one of them alone. In a parliamentary system the cabinet shares collective responsibility: all ministers defend cabinet decisions together, and if the government loses a vote of no confidence, the whole cabinet falls. So individual rather than collective responsibility is a feature of the presidential system: A. The trap: D (parliamentary) is the home of collective responsibility, the opposite. Memory tip: parliament = collective; presidential = individual.",
 "|A right enjoyed by citizens but denied non-citizens is the right to|equality before the law|vote and be voted for|life, liberty and security|freedom of worship": "Some rights belong to everyone living in a country; others belong only to its citizens. Civil rights such as life, personal liberty, freedom of worship and equality before the law protect citizens and foreigners alike. Political rights, such as the right to vote and to stand for election, are reserved for citizens: a foreign resident cannot vote in Nigeria's elections or contest for office. The answer is B. The trap: A, C and D are rights, but they are human rights that every person in Nigeria enjoys. Memory tip: everyone has civil rights; only citizens have the ballot.",
 "|In a federal system, the essence of specifying the constitutional relationship between the centre and the constituent units is to|ensure the superiority of the centre|diffuse political power|guarantee political autonomy|avoid functional conflicts": "In a federation, the constitution divides powers between the centre and the units (in Nigeria, the exclusive, concurrent and residual matters). Setting this out clearly tells each level what it may do, so they do not clash over the same functions: D. Option C is also true: the written division guarantees each level its autonomy in its own sphere, which is how K. C. Wheare defined federalism. This question is debatable, so both C and D are accepted. Superiority of the centre (A) describes a unitary state, the opposite of federalism; spelling out the relationship prevents conflicts and guarantees each level's autonomy. Memory tip: clear lists, no clashes, each level free in its own field.",
 "|In a dominant one-party system, there is|one party that controls the political parties|only one political party recognized by law|one among many parties that always wins elections|one party which embraces all the citizens": "Party systems are classified by how many parties compete and win. In a one-party state only one party is legal (Tanzania under Nyerere). In a dominant-party system many parties exist and compete freely, but one keeps winning election after election, such as the ANC in South Africa since 1994 or the PDP in Nigeria from 1999 to 2015. The answer is C. The trap: B (only one party recognised by law) describes a one-party state, not a dominant-party system. Memory tip: dominant means many may run, but one always wins.",
 "|One of the ways the executive performs legislative functions is through|administrative review|extrajudicial decision|judicial review|delegated legislation": "Delegated legislation is law made not by the legislature itself but by the executive or another body, under power delegated by an act of the legislature: ministers' regulations and orders, agencies' rules, and local councils' bye-laws. It saves parliamentary time and uses expert knowledge, so it is one way the executive performs legislative functions: D. The trap: judicial review (C) is the courts' power to cancel unconstitutional laws and actions, a judicial function. Administrative review (A) is checking decisions within the administration. Memory tip: delegated legislation = borrowed law-making power, controlled by the parent act.",
 "|The form of government in which the powers of the hereditary ruler are limited is|constitutional monarchy|aristocracy|absolute monarchy|totalitarianism": "A monarchy is rule by a hereditary king or queen. In an absolute monarchy the monarch's power has no legal limits (as in Saudi Arabia). In a constitutional monarchy, the monarch's powers are limited by the constitution, and real government is carried out by elected leaders; the monarch reigns but does not rule. Britain is the classic example. The answer is A. The trap: C (absolute monarchy) is the opposite; aristocracy (B) is rule by a noble class; totalitarianism (D) is total state control of life, usually under a dictator. Memory tip: constitutional monarchy = the crown under the constitution.",
 "|As a component of the state, the civil service is important because it ensures|gender equality|effective cost-benefit analysis|continuity in governance|equality among professions": "The civil service is the permanent body of officials who run government departments and carry out policy. Civil servants are appointed on merit, are politically neutral and stay in office when ministers and governments change. Because of this, they keep government working, preserve records and experience, and ensure continuity in governance: C. Gender equality (A) and equality among professions (D) are not its purpose, and cost-benefit analysis (B) is just one technique; a permanent civil service ensures continuity when governments change. Memory tip: politicians come and go; the civil service stays.",
 "|A bill could become law without the president's assent if|overruled by the Supreme Court|supported by a two-thirds majority of the legislature|necessitated by a state of emergency|assented to by the Chief Justice": "Under Nigeria's 1999 Constitution (section 58), a bill passed by the National Assembly goes to the President for assent within 30 days. If he withholds assent, the bill can be passed again by a two-thirds majority in each house, the Senate and the House of Representatives, and it then becomes law without his assent. This veto override is a check on the executive. The answer is B. The Supreme Court (A) can strike down laws but cannot make them, a state of emergency (C) does not remove the need for assent, and the Chief Justice (D) has no role in assent. Memory tip: two-thirds of the legislature beats the president's veto.",
 "|A crucial requirement for the successful conduct of elections is|the existence of organized political parties|an independent electoral commission|a highly mobilized electorate|the adequate funding of political parties": "Elections are free and fair only if the body that runs them (registration, voting, counting, declaring results) is independent and impartial. If the electoral commission takes orders from the government or a party, results can be rigged whatever else is in place. That is why Nigeria's constitution makes INEC independent, and why its independence is always debated. The answer is an independent electoral commission: B. The trap: organised parties (A) and a mobilised electorate (C) also matter, but even good parties and keen voters cannot get a fair result from a biased umpire. Memory tip: a fair match needs a fair referee.",
 "|One major determinant of Nigeria's foreign policy is|commitment to world peace|cooperation among African states|non-alignment in world affairs|external influences": "A foreign policy has determinants: the factors that shape it. For Nigeria these include its colonial history, size and population, economic resources (especially oil), ethnic and religious make-up, the type of government and its leaders, and external influences from foreign powers, international bodies and the world economy. So the determinant here is D. The trap: A, B and C are principles or objectives of Nigeria's foreign policy (world peace, African unity, non-alignment): they are what the policy aims at, not the forces that shape it. This question is debatable, since some books use \"determinant\" loosely. Memory tip: determinants push the policy; principles guide it.",
 "|The Technical Aid Corps programme was initiated by Nigeria to contribute to the socio-economic development of|developing countries|West Africa|The Third World|Africa": "The Technical Aid Corps was set up in 1987 under General Babangida, based on an idea of Foreign Minister Professor Bolaji Akinyemi. It sends young Nigerian professionals (doctors, teachers, engineers) to serve in other countries for two years, as an alternative to cash aid. It serves African, Caribbean and Pacific (ACP) countries, with Africa as its main focus, matching Nigeria's Africa-centred policy: D. Options A (developing countries) and C (the Third World) also describe these countries, so this question is debatable and all three are accepted. West Africa (B) is too narrow: the Technical Aid Corps sends Nigerian volunteers across Africa and to Caribbean and Pacific countries. Memory tip: TAC = Nigeria's skills, not cash, for Africa and the ACP.",
 "|Nigeria became a frontline state in the Southern African liberation struggle because of her|influence on African proxy wars|commitment to end colonialism|anti-apartheid stance|connection with South Africa": "The frontline states were the countries nearest to white-minority South Africa and Rhodesia (Angola, Botswana, Mozambique, Tanzania, Zambia, and later Zimbabwe) that led support for the liberation movements. Nigeria, thousands of kilometres away, was called a frontline state because of its strong anti-apartheid stance: it funded the ANC and SWAPO, nationalised BP's assets in 1979 over oil to South Africa, and led boycotts such as the 1976 Olympics. The answer is C. Option B is also true, since Nigeria backed the fight against colonial rule in Angola, Zimbabwe and Namibia, so this question is debatable and both are accepted. Memory tip: far in distance, frontline in commitment.",
 "|The New Partnership for Africa's Development was mainly promoted by|Ghana, Sierra Leone and Nigeria|Kenya, South Africa and Senegal|Nigeria, Senegal and South Africa|Tanzania, Burkina Faso and Egypt": "The New Partnership for Africa's Development (NEPAD) was adopted in 2001 as Africa's own development plan: African leaders promised good governance, and rich countries promised aid, trade and investment in return. It was mainly promoted by President Olusegun Obasanjo of Nigeria, President Thabo Mbeki of South Africa and President Abdoulaye Wade of Senegal, joined by Algeria's Bouteflika and Egypt's Mubarak. The answer is C. The trap: B (Kenya, South Africa and Senegal) and D (with Egypt) each have one or two right names but miss Nigeria. NEPAD became the African Union Development Agency (AUDA-NEPAD) in 2018. Memory tip: NEPAD's big three = Obasanjo, Mbeki, Wade.",
 "|Nigeria's first participation in a United Nations peace-keeping operation was in|Congo|Lebanon|Angola|Chad": "Nigeria became independent on 1 October 1960, and within weeks, in November 1960, Nigerian troops joined the UN Operation in the Congo (ONUC), sent after the Congo's independence collapsed into mutiny and secession. Nigerian police and soldiers served there until 1964, and Major-General Aguiyi-Ironsi commanded the UN force in 1964. So Nigeria's first UN peacekeeping was in the Congo: A. The trap: Lebanon (B) came later (UNIFIL, 1978) and Chad (D) was an OAU operation (1981–82), not UN. Memory tip: born in 1960, keeping peace in the Congo the same year.",
 "|Nigeria's concern for decolonization in Africa was responsible for her abandonment of pro-West policy during the|Shonekan administration|Shagari administration|Aguiyi-Ironsi regime|Murtala regime": "General Murtala Mohammed ruled from July 1975 until his assassination in February 1976. Nigeria's earlier governments were conservative and pro-West. Murtala's regime broke with this over Angola: defying the United States, Nigeria recognised the MPLA government in November 1975, and in January 1976 Murtala gave his famous \"Africa has come of age\" speech at the OAU. It was a bold, Africa-centred policy driven by concern for decolonisation. The answer is D. Shonekan (A) and Shagari (B) followed moderate policies and Ironsi (C) ruled only six months; Murtala's support for the MPLA in Angola marked the break with the pro-West line. Memory tip: Murtala = Angola = \"Africa has come of age\".",
 "|The first Nigerian leader to become chairman of the Organisation of African Unity was|Murtala Muhammed|Yakubu Gowon|Johnson Aguiyi-Ironsi|Tafawa Balewa": "The chairman of the OAU was the head of state of the country hosting its annual summit, serving for one year. General Yakubu Gowon was the first Nigerian to hold the post, in 1973–74, chairing the 10th anniversary summit in Addis Ababa. Later Nigerian chairmen included Obasanjo (as AU chairman, 2004–2006). The answer is B. The trap: Tafawa Balewa (D) was a founding father of the OAU in 1963 but never its chairman; Murtala (A) and Ironsi (C) ruled too briefly. The OAU was replaced by the African Union in 2002. Memory tip: Gowon, first Nigerian OAU chair, 1973.",
 "|Which of the following is a hindrance to the democratization of the United Nations?|Resistance of the big powers|Proliferation of weapons of mass destruction|The multiplicity of official languages|Delayed payment of annual dues by members": "To \"democratize\" the UN means to share its power more equally among its 193 members. The main obstacle is the five permanent members of the Security Council (USA, Russia, China, Britain, France). Each holds a veto, and any amendment to the Charter also needs their approval, so they block reforms such as abolishing the veto or adding permanent seats for Africa or Latin America. So the hindrance is the resistance of the big powers: A. The trap: delayed dues (D) and weapons of mass destruction (B) are real UN problems, but they hurt its finances and security work, not its democracy. Memory tip: the VETO is the brake on UN democracy.",
 "|An essential principle that guides the activities of the United Nations is|the regulation of international trade|the protection of the United States of America against terrorism|respect for the sovereign equality of member states|the transfer of technology to developing countries": "Article 2(1) of the UN Charter (signed in San Francisco, 26 June 1945) says the organisation \"is based on the principle of the sovereign equality of all its Members\". That is why every state, from Nigeria to tiny Nauru, has one vote in the General Assembly, and why the UN may not interfere in matters essentially within a state's domestic jurisdiction (Article 2(7)). So the guiding principle is respect for the sovereign equality of member states: C. Trade regulation (A) and technology transfer (D) are goals of some UN agencies, not founding principles, and protecting one country (B) contradicts sovereign equality. Memory tip: in the General Assembly, one state = one vote.",
 "|The African Union was established to|form an African High Command|promote global economic dominance by Africa|address current international developments affecting Africa|consolidate Africa's position in global politics": "The African Union replaced the OAU. It was planned in the Sirte Declaration (1999), its Constitutive Act was adopted at Lome in 2000, and it was launched in Durban, South Africa, in July 2002. Its objectives include greater unity among African states and enabling Africa \"to play its rightful role in the global economy and in international negotiations\", in other words to consolidate Africa's position in global politics: D. The trap: C sounds similar, but \"addressing current developments\" is vague and describes any organisation's daily work; an African High Command (A) was Nkrumah's 1960s idea, and Africa does not seek global economic dominance (B). Memory tip: OAU freed Africa; AU positions Africa in the world.",
 "|A principal defect of the Commonwealth is|lack of commitment by member states|lack of quorum at annual summits|poverty among member states|lack of a democratic head": "The Commonwealth is a voluntary association of mainly former British territories (now 56 members). Its Head is the British monarch: Queen Elizabeth II held the title from 1952, and King Charles III has been Head since 2022. The headship is not filled by any election among the members, which critics call undemocratic for a body that preaches democracy (Harare Declaration, 1991). The paper treats this as its principal defect: D. The trap: lack of commitment (A) is also often said of the Commonwealth, since its decisions are not binding, so this question is open to argument; D is the expected answer. Memory tip: Commonwealth head = crown, not ballot.",
 "|Nigeria declared that she would not attend the OAU summit in Tripoli in 1982 unless|Colonel Gaddafi resigned as the Libyan Head of State|Libya withdrew her troops from Chad|President Shehu Shagari became the chairman of OAU|Cameroun was suspended for killing Nigerian soldiers at Bakassi": "In 1980 Libya sent troops into Chad, Nigeria's north-eastern neighbour, and in 1981 Gaddafi announced a \"merger\" of the two countries. Nigeria, which saw Chad as part of its security zone, demanded the withdrawal of Libyan troops and insisted on this before attending the OAU summit due in Tripoli in 1982. That summit failed twice for lack of a quorum. So Nigeria's condition was that Libya withdraw her troops from Chad: B. The 1981 killing of Nigerian soldiers by Cameroon (D) was a separate bilateral quarrel; Nigeria's condition for attending the 1982 Tripoli summit was Libya's withdrawal of its troops from Chad. Memory tip: Tripoli 1982: no summit until Libya leaves Chad.",
 "|Nigeria's non-aligned policy was criticized because of the|ECOWAS Treaty|Nuclear Test Ban Treaty|Anglo-Nigerian Defence Pact|Non-proliferation Treaty": "Non-alignment means not joining either side of the Cold War (the Western bloc led by the USA or the Eastern bloc led by the USSR). At independence the Balewa government signed the Anglo-Nigerian Defence Pact (1960), which allowed Britain military facilities and tied Nigeria to a Western power. Students and the opposition protested that it made non-alignment a sham, and it was abrogated in January 1962: C. The Test Ban (B, 1963) and Non-proliferation (D, 1968) treaties were signed by countries of both blocs, and ECOWAS (A) is regional; the Anglo-Nigerian Defence Pact tied Nigeria to Britain. Memory tip: a defence pact with one bloc breaks non-alignment.",
 "|NEPAD was adopted during the OAU summit held in|Tunis|Lusaka|Cairo|Abuja": "NEPAD, the New Partnership for Africa's Development, came from merging Mbeki's Millennium Africa Plan and Wade's Omega Plan. The combined plan, called the New African Initiative, was adopted at the 37th (and last) OAU summit in Lusaka, Zambia, in July 2001. In October 2001 the implementation committee of heads of state, meeting in Abuja, renamed it NEPAD. So the OAU summit was in Lusaka: B. The trap: Abuja (D) is tempting because the name NEPAD was adopted there, but that Abuja meeting was not an OAU summit. Memory tip: Lusaka adopted the plan; Abuja gave it the name.",
 "|Nigeria's interest in teaming up with other developing countries in the world is predicated on|her desire to consume more manufactured goods|her desire to produce more raw materials|preserving her independence and promoting development|getting a permanent seat in the United Nations Security Council": "Nigeria cooperates with other developing countries through the Non-Aligned Movement, the Group of 77, the OAU/AU and ECOWAS. The aim is to protect their hard-won independence from big-power domination and to press for development: fairer prices for commodities, aid, debt relief and a New International Economic Order. So it is predicated on preserving her independence and promoting development: C. A permanent Security Council seat (D) is a single ambition, not the basis of South-South ties, and producing more raw materials (B) would deepen dependence. Memory tip: the South unites to stay free and grow.",
 "|Which of the following was once Nigeria's permanent representative at the United Nations?|Professor Bolaji Akinyemi|Major General Ike Nwachukwu|Professor Jubril Aminu|Major General Joseph Garba": "Major General Joseph Nanven Garba, who announced the 1975 coup that brought Murtala Mohammed to power and was Foreign Minister (1975–1978), became Nigeria's Permanent Representative to the United Nations from 1984 to 1989. In 1989 he was elected President of the 44th UN General Assembly: D. The trap: Professor Bolaji Akinyemi (A, 1985–87) and Major General Ike Nwachukwu (B, 1987–89) were Foreign Ministers, not UN ambassadors; Professor Jibril Aminu (C) was a minister and later Nigeria's Ambassador to the USA. Memory tip: Garba went from Dodan Barracks to the UN podium.",
 "|Ethics and accountability in government can be guaranteed through|legislative cooperation|executive and judicial immunity|due process and transparency|administrative reforms": "Ethics means acting rightly; accountability means answering for how public power and money are used. These are guaranteed when officials follow the laid-down rules and procedures (due process) and act openly so the public, the press and the legislature can see what they do (transparency). Nigeria's Due Process Office (2001) and later the Public Procurement Act 2007 were built on this idea: C. Immunity (B) shields top executives from prosecution while in office (section 308), so it weakens accountability, and administrative reforms (D) are too vague. Memory tip: sunlight is the best disinfectant: openness forces accountability.",
 "|The process of law-making by the legislature begins with|initiating a bill|commencing debate|a public hearing|the first reading": "A bill becomes law through set stages in each house: first reading (the title is read and the bill is published), second reading (debate on its general principles), committee stage (clause-by-clause study), report stage, third reading, and then assent. The formal process inside the legislature begins with the first reading: D. The trap: \"initiating a bill\" (A) sounds like a beginning, but drafting and sponsoring often happen outside the chamber, for example in a ministry for an executive bill; the bill only enters the legislative process when it is read the first time. Public hearings (C) happen at committee stage. Memory tip: 1st reading = title; 2nd = principles; committee = details.",
 "|A non-permanent feature of a state is|society|population|territory|government": "A state has four essential elements: population, a defined territory, a government and sovereignty. Of these, government is the one that changes: rulers come and go through elections, coups or deaths, yet the state continues. Nigeria has had civilian and military governments since 1960 but has remained the same state. So the non-permanent feature is government: D. The trap: society (A) and population (B) change in size and make-up, but a state always has a people; territory (C) is the fixed land. Memory tip: the state is permanent; the government is its temporary manager.",
 "|The ancient Greeks practised|direct democracy|representative democracy|liberal democracy|benevolent dictatorship": "In ancient Greek city-states, above all Athens in the fifth century BC, the citizens (free adult men only) met in the assembly, the ecclesia, to debate and pass laws, choose generals and decide on war themselves. Because the city was small, they did not need to elect representatives. This is direct democracy: A. The trap: representative democracy (B) arose in large modern states, where citizens elect others to decide for them; liberal democracy (C) is a modern idea stressing individual rights. Memory tip: Athens = the people themselves in the assembly; Abuja = elected representatives.",
 "|The formation of confederations is based primarily on|hegemonic considerations|historical reasons|security considerations|geographical factors": "A confederation is a loose union of sovereign states that keep their independence but give a weak central body limited powers, mostly over defence and foreign affairs. States usually join for security against a common threat, as the thirteen American states did under the Articles of Confederation (1781) during and after their war with Britain, and as the Swiss cantons did for centuries. So it is based primarily on security considerations: C. Hegemony (A) means domination by one state, which equal confederal members reject, and history (B) and geography (D) help but are not the main motive; states confederate chiefly for security. Memory tip: confederation = sovereign states sheltering under one umbrella.",
 "|The declaration of election results is the exclusive duty of|political party chairmen|electoral commissions|chief justices|election petition tribunals": "Elections in Nigeria are conducted by an independent electoral commission. Its returning officers announce the results at each level, and only the commission can declare a candidate winner. Today that body is INEC, set up in 1998; earlier ones were FEDECO (1979–83) and NEC (1987–93). So declaring results is the exclusive duty of electoral commissions: B. The trap: election petition tribunals (D) hear complaints only AFTER results are declared and may nullify an election or order a rerun, but they do not make the first declaration. Party chairmen (A) are interested parties. Memory tip: the commission declares; the tribunal reviews.",
 "|The traditions of a people that shape their government and politics represent their|political culture|political ideology|political acculturation|political socialization": "Political culture, a term made popular by Gabriel Almond and Sidney Verba, is the set of attitudes, beliefs, values and traditions that a people hold about government and politics. It shapes how they see authority, whether they vote and how they treat rulers. So traditions that shape government and politics are the people's political culture: A. The trap: political socialization (D) is the PROCESS by which culture is passed on, through family, school, religion and the media; ideology (B) is an organised set of ideas such as socialism; acculturation (C) is absorbing another people's culture. Memory tip: culture is WHAT is passed on; socialization is HOW it is passed on.",
 "|According to Karl Marx, the mode of production that precedes capitalism is|mercantilism|feudalism|socialism|communalism": "Karl Marx (1818–1883) saw history as a series of modes of production, each with its own ruling and exploited class: primitive communalism, slavery (masters and slaves), feudalism (lords and serfs), capitalism (bourgeoisie and proletariat), then socialism and finally communism. The stage immediately before capitalism is feudalism: B. The trap: communalism (D) is the very first stage, far earlier; mercantilism (A) was a trade policy of European states, not one of Marx's modes; socialism (C) comes after capitalism. Memory tip: count Marx's six stages, Communalism, Slavery, Feudalism, Capitalism, Socialism, Communism; feudalism sits just before capitalism.",
 "|The executive is mainly concerned with the|confirmation of appointments|approval of treaties|approval of budgets|implementation of policies": "The three arms of government each have one main job: the legislature makes laws, the executive implements (executes) them, and the judiciary interprets them. So the executive is mainly concerned with the implementation of policies: D. Confirming appointments (A), approving treaties (B) and approving budgets (C) are legislative checks on the executive, whose main concern is implementing policies. Memory tip: legislature MAKES, executive EXECUTES, judiciary JUDGES.",
 "|A distinguishing feature of a federal state is|a written constitution|resource control|separation of powers|cultural diversity": "A federal state divides power between a central government and regional governments, each supreme in its own sphere (K.C. Wheare). That division must be written down and protected, so every federation has a written, usually rigid, constitution, along with a supreme court to settle disputes between the tiers. A written constitution is therefore a feature of every federal state: A. The trap: cultural diversity (D) is a common REASON countries adopt federalism, as in Nigeria, but it is not part of the federal structure: Germany is a federation, while many diverse states are unitary. The question is debatable on this point. Memory tip: no written division of powers, no federation.",
 "|The recognition and general acceptance of the right to rule in a country based on laid-down regulations is known as|traditional authority|constitutional authority|sovereign authority|legitimate authority": "Authority is power that people accept as rightful. Legitimacy is that recognition and acceptance of a ruler's right to rule, which can rest on tradition, on charisma or on laws and procedures such as a free election under a constitution. Recognition and general acceptance of the right to rule is therefore legitimate authority: D. The trap: constitutional authority (B) looks attractive because of the words \"laid-down regulations\", which describe Max Weber's legal-rational kind of authority, so a reader could argue for it; but the key idea in the question is acceptance, which is legitimacy. Traditional authority (A) rests on custom, not regulations. Memory tip: legitimacy = the people say \"you have the right\".",
 "|A political ideology that advocates extreme nationalism and suppression of individual liberties is|socialism|communism|feudalism|fascism": "Fascism was founded by Benito Mussolini, who took power in Italy in 1922, and a related form, Nazism, was led by Adolf Hitler in Germany (1933–1945). It glorifies the nation and the race (extreme nationalism), puts the all-powerful state above the individual, bans opposition parties, controls the press, and rules through one leader backed by force. So it is fascism: D. The trap: communism (B) also suppresses liberties in practice, but its ideology is internationalist (\"Workers of the world, unite!\") and class-based, not extreme nationalism. Memory tip: fascism = Flag and Force: nation first, freedom last.",
 "|In a democracy, a major setback in the judicial process is|political neutrality|lack of professionalism|freedom from punishment|inadequate material resources": "In a democracy the judicial process is meant to deliver fair, impartial and speedy justice. That depends on the quality of the people who run it: judges and lawyers who are competent, ethical and diligent. A lack of professionalism, shown in corruption, bias, incompetence and endless adjournments, is a major setback to justice: B. The trap: \"freedom from punishment\" (C) sounds like impunity, which is indeed bad for justice, but it describes a problem of law enforcement, not of the judicial process itself; political neutrality (A) is a virtue of judges. Inadequate resources (D) matter too, which makes the question somewhat open. Memory tip: justice is only as good as those who deliver it.",
 "|A vote taken by the citizenry on an important political issue is a|recall|general election|bye-election|referendum": "A referendum is a direct vote by the whole electorate on a particular political question, such as approving a new constitution or a change of status. Examples are Ghana's 1960 republican referendum and the United Kingdom's 2016 vote on leaving the European Union: D. The trap: a recall (A) is a vote to remove a particular legislator; a general election (B) chooses representatives for the whole country; a bye-election (C) fills one vacant seat. Memory tip: elections choose PEOPLE; referendums decide ISSUES.",
 "|A constitution is classified as rigid if|it requires special procedures to amend|its core provisions are made by decree|its provisions cannot be amended|any of its provisions can be amended": "A rigid constitution can be amended only through a special procedure more difficult than ordinary law-making. Nigeria's 1999 Constitution is rigid: under section 9, an amendment needs a two-thirds majority in each house of the National Assembly and approval by at least two-thirds (24) of the State Houses of Assembly. The USA's constitution is another example: A. The trap: C says it cannot be amended at all, which overstates rigidity, since every constitution can be amended somehow; D describes a flexible constitution, like Britain's, changed by ordinary Acts of Parliament. Memory tip: rigid = hard to change, not impossible to change.",
 "|An exception to the mandate of the legislature is the|enactment of laws|oversight of the executive|representation of the citizenry|conduct of external relations": "The legislature's mandate covers law-making (A), oversight of the executive through questions, investigations and budget approval (B), and representing the people of their constituencies (C). Conducting external relations, meaning negotiating treaties, appointing ambassadors and dealing with other states, is the job of the executive: the President with the Ministry of Foreign Affairs. So the exception is the conduct of external relations: D. The trap: the legislature does have a say in foreign affairs (the Senate confirms ambassadors, and treaties need domestication by the National Assembly under section 12), but this is oversight, not conduct. Memory tip: the President conducts; the legislature checks.",
 "|One of the obligations of a citizen is|obedience to the laws of the land|active participation in partisan politics|sticking to his beliefs|loyalty to the ruling party": "Citizenship carries rights (to vote, to free speech, to fair hearing) and obligations. The obligations of a Nigerian citizen include obeying the laws of the land, paying taxes, respecting the national symbols, defending the country and helping the police; section 24 of the 1999 Constitution lists such duties. So the obligation here is obedience to the laws of the land: A. The trap: active partisan politics (B) is a RIGHT, which a citizen may or may not use; loyalty is owed to the nation, not to the ruling party (D). Memory tip: rights you MAY use; duties you MUST perform.",
 "|One of the objectives of a multiparty system is to|prevent rigging|provide alternatives|avoid ethnicity|eliminate corruption": "A multiparty system has three or more parties able to compete for power, as in Nigeria since 1999. Its main purpose is to give voters real alternatives: a choice between different candidates, policies and programmes, and the chance to replace a failing government peacefully: B. Preventing rigging (A) and eliminating corruption (D) depend on honest institutions, not the number of parties, and multiparty systems can even encourage ethnic parties (C); their aim is to offer voters alternatives. Memory tip: multi-party = multiple choice for voters.",
 "|A constitution formulated and imposed on a country without popular participation is said to be created by|fiat|directives|command|decree": "Constitutions can be made with popular participation, through a constituent assembly, a referendum or an elected legislature, or without it. A constitution handed down by a ruler, a colonial power or a military regime, with no part played by the people, is an imposed constitution, made by command: C. This question has two right answers: \"fiat\" (A) means exactly that, an authoritative command, so \"imposed by fiat\" is also correct. The trap: decree (D) is the legal instrument a military regime uses, and directives (B) are administrative instructions; the idea tested is imposition by command. Memory tip: no assembly, no referendum = imposed by command.",
 "|In a parliamentary system, the term shadow cabinet is often used to refer to the|back-benchers in the house|deputy prime ministers and assistant ministers|rebellious members of the ruling party|portfolio designates of the party in opposition": "In a parliamentary system such as Britain's, the main opposition party forms a shadow cabinet: senior opposition MPs are each given a portfolio, for example a shadow Foreign Secretary or shadow Chancellor, to watch and criticise the matching minister and present alternative policies. They are a government-in-waiting, ready to take those posts if the opposition wins: D. The trap: back-benchers (A) are ordinary MPs without ministerial or shadow posts; deputy and assistant ministers (B) belong to the actual government. Memory tip: the shadow follows the minister everywhere.",
 "|Which of the following made the earliest contact with the Nigerian society?|The Portuguese|The Germans|The British|The French": "The Portuguese were the first Europeans to reach the Nigerian coast. Their sailors arrived at the Benin kingdom in the 1470s and 1480s (João Afonso de Aveiro visited about 1486), trading in pepper, ivory, cloth and later slaves, and sending missionaries. The British came much later as major traders, and only took Lagos in 1861: A. The trap: the British (C) are the obvious answer because they colonised Nigeria, but colonisers were not the first visitors. The Germans (B) ruled neighbouring Cameroon, and the French (D) surrounded Nigeria. Memory tip: Portugal first (1400s), Britain last (1861 onward).",
 "|The adoption of a federal system of government in Nigeria under colonial rule was necessitated mainly by|geographical proximity|administrative convenience|cultural diversity|vast economic resources": "Nigeria is home to over 250 ethnic groups with different languages, religions and traditions, especially the Hausa-Fulani in the North, the Yoruba in the West and the Igbo in the East. To hold them together while letting each region keep its identity and manage its own affairs, the colonial rulers moved from regionalism (Richards, 1946) to a true federation under the Lyttelton Constitution of 1954. So federalism was necessitated mainly by cultural diversity: C. The trap: administrative convenience (B) explains the earlier division into regions, not federalism; vast resources (D) and proximity (A) do not require power-sharing. Memory tip: many peoples, one country: federalism.",
 "|Public corporations are established mainly to|offer free services|make profit for government|provide jobs for the unemployed|provide services at subsidized rates": "Public corporations are bodies created by law and owned by government to run essential services, such as the former NEPA (electricity), the Nigerian Railway Corporation and water boards. Their main purpose is to provide these services to the public at affordable, often subsidised, rates, especially where private firms would not invest or would charge too much: D. The trap: making profit (B) is not their primary aim, though they are expected to be efficient; they do not give free services (A). Memory tip: public corporation = public service first, profit second.",
 "|Indirect rule as a British colonial policy was first introduced in|India|Uganda|Ghana|Nigeria": "Indirect rule means governing through existing local rulers and institutions. Britain first used it in India, where hundreds of princely states such as Hyderabad were left under their own rulers, supervised by British residents, from the 18th and 19th centuries. Lord Lugard, who knew this practice, applied it systematically in Northern Nigeria after 1900 and described it in \"The Dual Mandate\" (1922), so Nigeria became its best-known example: A. The trap: Nigeria (D) is where indirect rule was made famous, not where it was first introduced; some textbooks blur this. Memory tip: India first; Lugard made it famous in Nigeria.",
 "|Following the 1976 Local Government Reforms in Nigeria, the local governments were empowered to|promulgate edicts|promulgate and enforce bye-laws|appoint and dismiss chairmen|appoint and dismiss directors": "The 1976 Local Government Reform, under the Murtala/Obasanjo regime, created a uniform, single-tier system across the country and recognised local government as the third tier of government. Local governments received statutory grants and were empowered to make and enforce bye-laws, rules on local matters such as markets, motor parks and sanitation: B. The trap: edicts (A) were laws made by state military governors, not local councils; chairmen (C) are elected, not appointed and dismissed by the council. Memory tip: federal Acts, state Laws (edicts under the military), local Bye-laws.",
 "|In the Nigerian Constitution, the powers to create local governments lie with the|state governors|National Assembly|Presidency|state houses of assembly": "Under section 8(3) of the 1999 Constitution, a new local government area is created by a law of the State House of Assembly. The request needs two-thirds support from the area's members in the House of Assembly and its local councils, approval by two-thirds of the area's people in a referendum, and finally a two-thirds resolution of the House of Assembly. Because the 774 LGAs are listed in the constitution, the National Assembly must then make a consequential amendment for the new LGA to be fully recognised: D. The trap: the National Assembly (B) has only that final step; governors (A) cannot create LGAs on their own. The new LGAs Lagos State created in 2002 stayed in dispute because the National Assembly never made that final amendment. Memory tip: LGAs are born in the State House.",
 "|The pre-colonial Igbo political system was largely|republican|dictatorial|monarchical|authoritarian": "Most pre-colonial Igbo communities had no kings. Power was spread among the family heads (holders of the Ofo staff), the council of elders, age grades, title holders such as the Ozo, and the village assembly (Oha-na-eze), where adult males could speak and decisions were reached by consensus. Because power was decentralised and the people took part, the system is called republican, or acephalous (headless): A. The trap: monarchical (C) describes the Hausa-Fulani emirates and the Yoruba kingdoms, not the Igbo, although a few places such as Onitsha had kings (Obi). Memory tip: Igbo enwe eze: \"the Igbo have no king\".",
 "|Which of the following states were created in 1996?|Akwa Ibom and Kogi|Gombe and Zamfara|Adamawa and Ekiti|Ebonyi and Osun": "On 1 October 1996 General Sani Abacha created six new states, raising the number from 30 to 36: Bayelsa, Ebonyi, Ekiti, Gombe, Nasarawa and Zamfara. Gombe and Zamfara are both on the list: B. The trap: each other option mixes years. Akwa Ibom (A) was created in 1987 and Kogi in 1991. Adamawa (C) dates from 1991, though Ekiti is from 1996. Ebonyi (D) is from 1996, but Osun from 1991. Nigeria still has 36 states today. Memory tip: 1996 = \"BEE-GNZ\": Bayelsa, Ebonyi, Ekiti, Gombe, Nasarawa, Zamfara.",
 "|Acculturation in colonial administration is associated with the|French policy of assimilation|British policy of direct rule|French policy of association|British indirect rule system": "Acculturation means absorbing another people's culture. France's policy of assimilation, used in places such as Senegal's Four Communes, aimed to turn Africans into \"black Frenchmen\" who spoke French, followed French law and culture and could become French citizens. So acculturation goes with the French policy of assimilation: A. The trap: association (C) was France's later policy (from the early twentieth century), which accepted that Africans would keep their own cultures; British indirect rule (D) also preserved local culture and institutions. Memory tip: assimilate = absorb French culture; associate = keep your own.",
 "|Military disengagement from politics is always preceded by|the retirement of military personnel|the making of a new constitution|an increase in workers' salaries|the establishment of more universities": "Before soldiers hand power back to civilians, they set up a transition programme, and a key step is a new constitution for the coming civilian government. In Nigeria, the Murtala/Obasanjo regime produced the 1979 Constitution before handing over to Shagari in October 1979, and the Abubakar regime produced the 1999 Constitution before handing over to Obasanjo in May 1999: B. Retiring officers (A) may follow a handover, and salary rises (C) or new universities (D) are unrelated; a new constitution is drawn up before the military leaves. Memory tip: new constitution first, then the soldiers go back to barracks.",
 "|The principle of federal character was first enunciated in the|1989 Constitution|1963 Constitution|1999 Constitution|1979 Constitution": "Federal character means that the composition of the government and its agencies, and appointments to them, should reflect the diversity of Nigeria, so that no state or ethnic group dominates. The principle was first written into the 1979 Constitution, in section 14(3), following the work of the Constitution Drafting Committee (1975–76). It was kept in the 1989 and 1999 Constitutions, and the Federal Character Commission was set up in 1996: D. The trap: the 1999 Constitution (C) contains the principle and the Commission, but it did not originate it; the 1963 Constitution (B) had no such clause. Memory tip: federal character is born in '79.",
 "|The final court of appeal in Nigeria during the colonial era was the|privy council|high court|supreme court|appeal court": "During colonial rule, appeals from Nigerian courts could go finally to the Judicial Committee of the Privy Council in London, made up of senior British judges. This continued after independence until 1963, when Nigeria became a republic and the Supreme Court of Nigeria became the final court of appeal: A. The trap: a supreme court (C) existed in colonial Nigeria, but appeals from it could still go to the Privy Council, so it was not final. Memory tip: colonial justice ended in London; republican justice ends in Abuja.",
 "|The Native Authority system of local administration was introduced by the British in Nigeria to|introduce Western education|ensure the administration of justice|empower traditional rulers|facilitate tax collection": "The Native Authority system grew out of indirect rule, which Lugard introduced in Northern Nigeria and extended to the South after amalgamation in 1914. Britain had few officials and little money, so it governed through traditional rulers. A central purpose was to collect taxes cheaply through the chiefs and native treasuries, which funded local administration: D. The trap: empowering traditional rulers (C) was the METHOD used, not the purpose; the attempt to impose direct taxation through warrant chiefs led to the Aba Women's Riot of 1929. Memory tip: native authority = chiefs as tax collectors for the colonial state.",
 "|The major objective of the manifesto of the People's Redemption Party in the Second Republic was|housing for all|welfare of the nation|emancipation of the masses|free education": "The People's Redemption Party (PRP) of the Second Republic (1979–1983) was led by Mallam Aminu Kano, who had earlier founded NEPU (1950) to fight for the talakawa, the common people of the North, against the aristocracy. Its manifesto aimed at the emancipation of the masses from social, political and economic oppression: C. The trap: free education (D) was the famous programme of Awolowo's UPN, and \"housing for all\" (A) and welfare (B) were slogans of other parties such as Shagari's NPN. Memory tip: PRP = People's Redemption = redeem the poor masses.",
 "|The privatization of public corporations in Nigeria is undertaken by the|Bureau of Public Enterprises|Nigerian Stock Exchange|Federal Civil Service Commission|Federal Executive Council": "The Bureau of Public Enterprises (BPE), set up in 1993 to replace the Technical Committee on Privatisation and Commercialisation (1988) and re-established by the Public Enterprises Act of 1999, is the secretariat of the National Council on Privatisation, chaired by the Vice-President. It prepares and carries out the sale of government-owned enterprises such as NITEL and the power companies: A. The Stock Exchange (B) is only a market where some shares are sold, and the Federal Executive Council (D) sets broad policy; the Bureau of Public Enterprises carries out privatization. Memory tip: BPE = Bureau that sells Public Enterprises.",
 "|The main goal of the NNDP was to|agitate for Nigeria's independence|achieve franchise for Nigerians|secure limited autonomy for the natives|secure independence for Nigeria": "The Nigerian National Democratic Party was founded by Herbert Macaulay in 1923 to contest the three Lagos seats created by the Clifford Constitution (1922), and it won them all until 1938. Its aims were self-government for Lagos, more African participation in government, and better education and economic chances for Africans, all within British rule. That is limited autonomy for the natives: C. The trap: A and D both say independence, which the NNDP never demanded; independence demands came from the NCNC and others in the 1940s and 1950s. The NNDP was also Lagos-based, not national. Memory tip: NNDP = Lagos first, not independence first.",
 "|The Structural Adjustment Programme was introduced by the|Babangida regime|Abacha regime|Murtala/Obasanjo regime|Buhari regime": "The Structural Adjustment Programme (SAP) was introduced in July 1986 by the military regime of General Ibrahim Babangida (1985–1993), after Nigeria rejected an IMF loan in a public debate. Its measures included devaluing the naira through a foreign exchange market, removing subsidies, deregulating the economy, privatising public enterprises and cutting imports: A. The trap: the Buhari regime (D, 1984–85) used austerity and counter-trade but refused IMF conditions; Abacha (B) came later, in 1993. Memory tip: IBB = I Brought Belt-tightening (SAP 1986).",
 "|The Ogboni cult in the pre-colonial Yoruba political system played the role of the|judiciary|executive|police|legislature": "In the old Oyo Empire, power was balanced between the Alaafin (king) and the Oyo Mesi (seven kingmakers led by the Bashorun). The Ogboni, a secret society of elders and earth-cult priests, stood between them: it judged serious cases, especially those involving bloodshed, settled disputes between the Alaafin and the Oyo Mesi, and checked the power of the Oyo Mesi. So it played the role of the judiciary: A. The trap: the legislature (D) is closer to the Oyo Mesi, and the executive (B) to the Alaafin. Memory tip: Alaafin rules, Oyo Mesi checks, Ogboni judges.",
 "|The head of the electoral body that conducted the June 12, 1993 election was|Humphrey Nwosu|Ovie-Whiskey|Eme Awa|Dagogo-Jack": "Professor Humphrey Nwosu was chairman of the National Electoral Commission (NEC) under the Babangida regime. NEC conducted the presidential election of 12 June 1993, widely regarded as Nigeria's freest; it was won by M.K.O. Abiola but annulled by Babangida. INEC has run elections since 1998: A. The trap: B, Justice Victor Ovie-Whiskey, chaired FEDECO for the 1983 elections, not 1993; Eme Awa (C) was NEC's first chairman and Dagogo-Jack (D) chaired Abacha's NECON. Memory tip: June 12 = Nwosu's NEC; 1979/83 = Ovie-Whiskey's FEDECO.",
 "|Deregulation as an economic policy borders on|the removal of subsidies on public utilities|the removal of restrictions and extension of competition|privatization|commercialization": "Deregulation means removing government controls and restrictions, such as fixed prices, licences and monopolies, from an industry, so that private firms can enter and compete freely. Nigeria deregulated telecommunications (GSM licences, 2001) and the downstream oil sector this way: B. The trap: removing subsidies (A) often goes along with deregulation, especially for petrol, but it is a consequence, not the meaning; privatization (C) is selling government enterprises to private owners; commercialization (D) is making a public enterprise run on commercial, profit-seeking lines while it stays public. Memory tip: deregulate = de-restrict = let competitors in.",
 "|Ethnic politics in Nigeria can be traced to the|Macpherson Constitution|Lyttelton Constitution|Clifford Constitution|Richards Constitution": "The Richards Constitution (1946) divided Nigeria into three regions, North, West and East, each with its own House of Assembly, and each dominated by one major group: the Hausa-Fulani, the Yoruba and the Igbo. This regionalism made politics a contest between regions and their dominant groups, so it is usually said to have laid the foundation of ethnic politics: D. The trap: the Macpherson Constitution (A, 1951) is also a strong candidate, because its regional elections led to ethnic-based parties such as the Action Group and the NPC, so the question is open to argument. Memory tip: Richards drew the regions; Macpherson filled them with ethnic parties.",
 "|A member of the Commonwealth that was not colonized by Britain is|Malaysia|Uganda|Mozambique|Jamaica": "The Commonwealth grew out of the British Empire, so almost all members are former British colonies. Mozambique was ruled by Portugal until 1975, yet it joined in 1995 because its neighbours were all members and it backed their struggle against apartheid. It was the first member never colonised by Britain: C. Today Rwanda (2009), Gabon and Togo (2022) have joined the same way. Malaysia (A), Uganda (B) and Jamaica (D) were all British colonies; Mozambique, a former Portuguese colony, joined the Commonwealth in 1995. Memory tip: Mozambique = the Portuguese-speaking odd one out in the Commonwealth.",
 "|The Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries came into existence at a conference held in|Tripoli|Caracas|Riyadh|Baghdad": "OPEC (Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries) was founded at the Baghdad Conference, 10–14 September 1960, by five states: Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia and Venezuela. Its aim was to coordinate oil policy and get fairer prices from the big Western oil companies. Nigeria joined in 1971. So the founding conference was in Baghdad: D. The trap: Caracas (B) is tempting because Venezuela's minister Pérez Alfonzo was a co-founder, but the meeting was in Iraq; OPEC's headquarters moved to Vienna in 1965. Memory tip: OPEC was \"Born in Baghdad, 1960.\"",
 "|ECOWAS main achievement in recent years has been in the area of|infrastructural development|cultural integration|security and defence|political unity": "ECOWAS was created by the Treaty of Lagos (May 1975) mainly for economic integration, but its most visible success has been in security. Its ceasefire monitoring group, ECOMOG, entered Liberia in August 1990, then acted in Sierra Leone (1997–98) and Guinea-Bissau (1998), and in 1999 ECOWAS adopted a Mechanism for conflict prevention and peacekeeping. So its main recent achievement is security and defence: C. The trap: political unity (D) is a distant goal, not an achievement; members still quarrel and coups have occurred. Memory tip: ECOWAS = ECOMOG, the soldiers it sent to keep the peace.",
 "|In which of the following countries has the UN carried out peacekeeping operations?|Cyprus, Singapore and South Africa|Lebanon, the Congo and Mexico|Cyprus, Somalia and Yugoslavia|Nigeria, Botswana and the Congo": "UN peacekeeping places neutral, lightly armed forces between warring parties. Real missions include Cyprus (UNFICYP, from 1964 and still there), Somalia (UNOSOM, 1992–1995) and the former Yugoslavia (UNPROFOR, 1992–1995). All three countries in C had UN missions: C. The trap: B looks right because the UN did serve in Lebanon (UNIFIL) and the Congo (ONUC, 1960–64), but never in Mexico; A and D each include countries with no peacekeeping mission (Singapore, South Africa, Nigeria, Botswana). Memory tip: one wrong country spoils the whole option, so check every name.",
 "|Which of the following was the main organ of the defunct OAU?|The Liberation Committee|The Council of Ministers|The Commission for Mediation, Conciliation and Arbitration|The Assembly of Heads of State and Government": "The Organisation of African Unity (founded 25 May 1963, Addis Ababa) had four main organs: the Assembly of Heads of State and Government, the Council of Ministers, the General Secretariat, and the Commission of Mediation, Conciliation and Arbitration. The Assembly was the supreme organ: it met yearly and took the key decisions: D. In 2002 the OAU was replaced by the African Union, whose Assembly is still the top organ. The trap: the Council of Ministers (B) only prepared the Assembly's agenda; the Liberation Committee (A) was a special committee for freedom movements. Memory tip: the top organ is where the top people (Heads of State) sit.",
 "|A key characteristic of the civil service is|transparency|patriotism|merit system|quota system": "The civil service is the permanent body of officials who run government ministries. Its main features are permanence, neutrality (political impartiality), anonymity, and recruitment and promotion on merit, through examinations and qualifications rather than favour. So the key characteristic here is the merit system: C. The trap: the quota system (D) does exist in Nigeria under the federal character principle, but it is a modification of merit, not a defining feature of civil services everywhere. Patriotism and transparency are good values for anyone, not special features. Memory tip: civil service = \"PANM\": Permanence, Anonymity, Neutrality, Merit.",
 "|Democracy is a perverted form of which of the following?|Feudalism|Republicanism|Monarchy|Oligarchy": "Aristotle classified governments by who rules and for whom. Rule by one for all is monarchy (perverted: tyranny); by the few for all is aristocracy (perverted: oligarchy); by the many for all is polity, often translated \"republic\" or constitutional government (perverted: democracy, which he saw as mob rule of the poor). So democracy is the perverted form of republic/polity: B. The paper's wording was reversed, so the question is shown corrected. The trap: oligarchy (D) is a perversion, but of aristocracy, not of democracy. Memory tip: Aristotle's pairs: Monarchy–Tyranny, Aristocracy–Oligarchy, Polity–Democracy.",
 "|An example of a state with an unwritten constitution is|Canada|India|Britain|France": "A constitution is unwritten when it is not collected in one single document. Britain's rests on Acts of Parliament (such as Magna Carta 1215 and the Bill of Rights 1689), judicial decisions, conventions and works of authority. It is better called \"uncodified\", because parts of it are written. So the example is Britain: C. The trap: Canada (A) shares Britain's parliamentary traditions, but it has a written Constitution Act (1867, patriated 1982); India and France also have written constitutions. Memory tip: the only big example JAMB uses for unwritten is Britain (with Israel and New Zealand).",
 "|A characteristic of a fascist state is the existence of a|zero-party system|two-party system|one-party system|multi-party system": "Fascism was the system of Mussolini's Italy (from 1922) and, in its Nazi form, Hitler's Germany. It glorifies the nation and the leader, uses propaganda and force, and allows only one party (the Fascist Party, the Nazi Party), banning all others. So a fascist state has a one-party system: C. The trap: a zero-party system (A) is a state with no parties at all, such as military regimes that ban politics or traditional monarchies; fascists ruled through their party. Memory tip: Fascism = one Leader, one Party, one Nation.",
 "|The right that enables citizens to express their electoral preferences is|franchise|suffrage|coat-tails|referendum": "Franchise is the right to vote in elections, the way citizens express their electoral choices: A. \"Suffrage\" (B) means exactly the same thing, as in \"universal adult suffrage\", so it is accepted too. Both words come from the struggle to extend the vote to all adults, including women (Nigerian women in the North voted only from 1976–79). A referendum (D) is a vote on an issue, not the right to vote itself, and \"coat-tails\" (C) is a strong candidate carrying weaker party colleagues into office. Memory tip: franchise = suffrage = right to vote.",
 "|Membership of a society is|reserved|voluntary|mandatory|honorary": "A society or association, such as a club, trade union, church or professional body, is formed by people with shared interests, and people join or leave it freely. Membership is therefore voluntary: B. This is a key difference from the state: you belong to a state by birth or naturalisation, membership is compulsory, and you must obey its laws. The trap: mandatory (C) describes membership of the state, not of a society. Memory tip: State = compulsory; Society/association = choice.",
 "|Equality before the law is a component of|separation of powers|checks and balances|the rule of law|constitutional law": "The rule of law, as explained by A.V. Dicey (1885), means three things: the supremacy of law (no one is punished except for a breach of law proved in court), equality before the law (rich and poor, rulers and ruled, are subject to the same laws and courts), and rights protected by the ordinary courts. So equality before the law is part of the rule of law: C. Separation of powers (A) and checks and balances (B) concern dividing power among the arms of government, not how citizens are treated before the courts. Memory tip: Dicey's rule of law = \"Supremacy, Equality, Rights\".",
 "|An example of promotional pressure group is the|Supreme Council for Islamic Affairs|Nigerian Medical Association|Civil Liberties Organization|Manufacturers Association of Nigeria": "Pressure groups are of two main kinds. Interest (protective) groups defend their own members, such as the NMA (doctors) and MAN (manufacturers). Promotional (cause) groups fight for a cause that benefits the wider public, such as human rights or the environment. The Civil Liberties Organisation, founded in Lagos in 1987, campaigns for human rights for all: C. The trap: the Nigerian Medical Association (B) does speak on health, but its main job is protecting doctors' interests. Memory tip: Promotional = Public cause; Protective = members' Pockets.",
 "|An example of a country that operates a bicameral legislature is|Nigeria|Sierra Leone|Kenya|Libya": "A bicameral legislature has two chambers. Nigeria's National Assembly has the Senate (109 members) and the House of Representatives (360), as in the USA: A. In 2008 Sierra Leone, Kenya and Libya each had a single chamber. That has partly changed: Kenya's 2010 constitution created a Senate, first elected in 2013, so Kenya is now bicameral too. The trap: Kenya (C) is tempting today, but it was unicameral when this paper was set. Memory tip: \"bi\" = two, as in Nigeria's Senate + House of Representatives.",
 "|An institution that transforms a myriad of demands into a more manageable proposal is the|executive|legislature|government|political party": "Society produces countless demands. Political scientists (Gabriel Almond) call the process of combining them into a few clear, manageable policy alternatives \"interest aggregation\", and it is a main function of political parties: they bring many groups' wishes together into one manifesto that voters can choose: D. This is a debatable point: government (C) does convert demands into final decisions and policies, but \"manageable proposals\" describes what parties offer before power is won. The trap: C, government, does turn demands into final decisions, but combining many demands into manageable proposals (interest aggregation) is a party's job. Memory tip: pressure groups articulate interests; parties aggregate them; government decides.",
 "|Government exercises political power on behalf of the|elite|citizens|party caucus|vocal few": "In a modern state, government is the agent of the state that exercises political power. In a democracy that power comes from the people and is held in trust for them; Abraham Lincoln called democracy \"government of the people, by the people, for the people\". So government acts on behalf of the citizens: B. The elite (A) and the vocal few (D) may influence government in practice, but in principle it exercises power on behalf of all citizens. Memory tip: government is the people's trustee.",
 "|A major characteristic of a confederal state is that|there is supremacy of the constitution|the citizens often owe allegiance to the centre|political stability is usually low|the centre overrides the regions": "A confederation is a loose union of sovereign states that keep most powers and can leave at will; the centre is weak and depends on them. Examples: the USA under the Articles of Confederation (1781–89), and the Senegambia Confederation (1982–89), which collapsed. Because members can withdraw or ignore the centre, political stability is usually low: C. The trap: supremacy of the constitution (A) and the centre overriding the regions (D) describe federal and unitary states. Memory tip: Confederation = weak centre, strong members, shaky union.",
 "|The citizenship of a country can be acquired by an alien through|habitation|assimilation|association|naturalization": "Citizenship can be acquired by birth, by registration (for example, a foreign woman who marries a Nigerian man), by naturalisation, or by honour. An alien (a foreigner) usually becomes a citizen by naturalisation: he applies after a long period of lawful residence (15 years under Nigeria's 1999 Constitution), shows good character and takes the oath of allegiance: D. The trap: assimilation (B) is a French colonial policy of turning Africans into \"Frenchmen\", not a legal way to gain citizenship. Memory tip: Naturalisation = Non-native becomes National.",
 "|An advantage of delegated legislation is|supremacy|convenience|effectiveness|efficiency": "Delegated legislation is law made by bodies other than parliament (ministers, local councils, agencies) under powers given by an Act. Its advantages: it saves parliament's time, allows experts to fill in technical detail, and permits quick action in emergencies. In short it makes law-making efficient: D. The trap: B, convenience, is close and often listed, but the expected advantage is efficiency, since experts can make detailed rules quickly; supremacy (A) is wrong because delegated rules are subordinate to the parent Act. Memory tip: Delegated legislation = Detailed rules made Efficiently by experts.",
 "|A system of government that promotes the rights and freedom of the citizens is called|oligarchy|plutocracy|autocracy|democracy": "Democracy, from Greek demos (people) and kratos (rule), is government by the people, directly or through elected representatives. It rests on majority rule, regular free elections and the protection of rights such as free speech, press and association. So the system that promotes citizens' rights and freedom is democracy: D. Oligarchy (A), plutocracy (B) and autocracy (C) concentrate power in a few, the rich or one person, and all limit citizens' freedom. Memory tip: Demo-cracy = the people's power.",
 "|In the presidential system of government, the leader is basically the head of the state and|government|the police|the military|the judiciary": "In a presidential system (USA; Nigeria since 1979) the president is elected for a fixed term and is both head of state, the ceremonial symbol of the nation, and head of government, the chief executive. So the leader is head of state and government: A. The trap: the president is also commander-in-chief of the armed forces (C), but that is one of his executive powers, not a separate \"headship\" in the textbook sense; the judiciary (D) is independent of him. Memory tip: in a parliamentary system the job is split (Queen/King + Prime Minister); in a presidential system one person wears both hats.",
 "|An economic system that encourages even distribution of wealth in the society is|liberalism|socialism|mercantilism|capitalism": "Socialism holds that the main means of production (land, factories, banks) should be owned or controlled by the community or state, so that wealth is shared fairly and the gap between rich and poor narrows. Its principle is \"from each according to his ability, to each according to his work\". So the system that encourages even distribution of wealth is socialism: B. Capitalism (D) and liberalism (A) favour private ownership and free markets, which tend to produce uneven wealth, and mercantilism (C) aimed to enrich the state through trade. Memory tip: Socialism = Sharing.",
 "|An attribute that makes a state to be recognized as independent is|government|population|sovereignty|territory": "A state has four elements: population, territory, government and sovereignty. Many groups have the first three (a region or a colony), but only sovereignty, supreme authority inside and independence from outside control, makes a state independent and recognised by others. So the attribute is sovereignty: C. The trap: government (A) is necessary, but colonial Nigeria had a government before 1960 without being independent. Memory tip: no sovereignty, no independence; Nigeria gained it on 1 October 1960.",
 "|The act of denying an eligible person the right to vote in an election is|gerrymandering|disenfranchisement|filibustering|restricted franchise": "Disenfranchisement is denying or taking away a qualified person's right to vote, for example by unfair registration, intimidation or laws that exclude certain groups (as Northern Nigerian women were excluded until 1976). So the act described is disenfranchisement: B. The trap: restricted franchise (D) is a legal limit on who may vote (by sex, property or education), not the act of denying an eligible voter; gerrymandering (A) is manipulating constituency boundaries; filibustering (C) is long speeches to delay a bill. Memory tip: dis-enfranchise = take away the franchise.",
 "|In the monarchical system of government, sovereignty is vested in the|people|royalty|legislature|military": "In a monarchy the head of state is a king, queen or emperor who inherits the throne. In an absolute monarchy (old France, Saudi Arabia today) supreme authority lies with the royal ruler. So sovereignty is vested in royalty: B. The trap: the people (A) hold sovereignty in a democracy or republic; in constitutional monarchies like Britain real power has passed to parliament (C), but the classic textbook answer for \"monarchical system\" is the crown. Memory tip: Monarchy = \"mono-arche\", rule by one royal.",
 "|One merit of a written constitution is its|legibility|total flexibility|rigidity|ease of reference": "A written constitution is a single document setting out the structure of government and citizens' rights (Nigeria 1999, USA 1787). Its merits: it is clear, certain and easy to refer to, so courts, officials and citizens can quickly check what the law says: D. The trap: rigidity (C) is usually counted as a demerit, since it makes amendment slow, and \"total flexibility\" (B) belongs to unwritten constitutions. Legibility (A) only means \"readable handwriting\". Memory tip: Written = easy to look up.",
 "|A human community that is usually cohesive and homogeneous is a|state|kinship|nation|clan": "A nation is a group of people bound by common descent, language, culture, history and a sense of belonging together, such as the Hausa, Yoruba or Igbo. These shared ties make it cohesive and homogeneous: C. A state, by contrast, can contain many nations (Nigeria has over 250 ethnic groups), so it is often diverse, not homogeneous. The trap: state (A) is wrong because it is a legal-political unit defined by territory, government and sovereignty, not by sameness. Kinship and clan are smaller family-based groups. Memory tip: Nation = shared culture; State = shared government.",
 "|In the process of implementing laws, the executive sometimes performs|judicial function|bureaucratic function|oversight function|legislative function": "Under separation of powers each arm has its main job, but in practice they overlap. The executive implements laws, yet it sometimes acts judicially: administrative tribunals settle disputes, and the President or Governor exercises the prerogative of mercy (pardon): A. It also legislates, through delegated legislation and executive orders (and decrees under military rule), so D is accepted too; both are standard examples of overlapping functions. The bureaucratic function (B) is simply ordinary implementation, not something the executive does only sometimes; judicial and legislative functions are the occasional extras. Memory tip: the executive also makes rules (quasi-legislative) and settles disputes (quasi-judicial).",
 "|Which of the following justifies the Afro-centric foreign policy of Nigeria?|Her location in the West African sub-region|Her colonization by a foreign power|Her physical size, population and resource endowments|Her ability to contribute to peace keeping missions": "Since independence (1960) Nigeria has made Africa the centrepiece of her foreign policy: fighting colonialism and apartheid, leading ECOWAS and peacekeeping. This role is justified by her physical size, Africa's largest population and large resource endowments, especially oil, which give her the means and responsibility to lead: C. Location in West Africa (A) is shared by many small states, colonization (B) by most of Africa, and peacekeeping (D) results from Afrocentrism; Nigeria's size, population and resources justify its leading role. Memory tip: the \"Giant of Africa\" plays an Africa-first foreign policy because it is a giant.",
 "|A virile foreign policy is one characterized by|a vibrant press, vibrant public opinion and a dynamic civil society|propaganda, espionage and economic embargoes|a stable polity, buoyant economy and visionary leadership|the nature and dynamics of the international system": "A virile (strong, vigorous) foreign policy depends on the country's power at home. A state can act boldly abroad only if it has political stability, a buoyant economy to fund its goals and visionary leadership to set clear aims. Murtala Mohammed's firm stand on Angola in 1975 rested on oil wealth and decisive leadership. So a virile foreign policy is marked by a stable polity, buoyant economy and visionary leadership: C. Propaganda, espionage and embargoes (B) are tools of foreign policy, not its foundation, which rests on a stable polity, a strong economy and visionary leadership. Memory tip: foreign policy begins at home.",
 "|Nigeria hosted the Commonwealth conference which eventually led to the independence of|Mozambique|Malawi|Namibia|Zimbabwe": "In November 1965 Ian Smith's white minority government in Rhodesia declared independence illegally (UDI). In January 1966 Prime Minister Tafawa Balewa hosted a special Commonwealth Prime Ministers' Conference in Lagos to deal with it, the first Commonwealth heads meeting held outside London. The pressure that followed, together with the liberation war and the 1979 Lancaster House talks, led to black-majority rule and Zimbabwe's independence on 18 April 1980: D. Mozambique (A) won independence from Portugal in 1975 and Malawi (B) in 1964; the 1966 Commonwealth conference in Lagos was called over Rhodesia, now Zimbabwe. Memory tip: Lagos 1966 → Rhodesia → Zimbabwe 1980.",
 "|Multilateralism in Nigeria's foreign policy entails her|stand on Africa as the centrepiece of her policy|non-aligned posture in the international system|quest for a permanent seat in the UN security council|membership of international organizations": "Multilateralism means conducting foreign relations through organisations with many members, rather than only one-to-one (bilateral) dealings. Nigeria practises it through membership of the UN (joined 7 October 1960), the OAU/AU, ECOWAS, the Commonwealth, OPEC and the Non-Aligned Movement. So multilateralism entails membership of international organisations: D. The trap: non-alignment (B) is a separate principle, refusing to take sides in the Cold War between East and West blocs; Africa as centrepiece (A) is Afrocentrism. Memory tip: multi-lateral = many sides = many organisations.",
 "|The first military coup in Nigeria toppled the government of|General Yakubu Gowon|Dr. Nnamdi Azikiwe|Alhaji Abubakar Tafawa Balewa|Major General Aguiyi-Ironsi": "Nigeria's first military coup took place on 15 January 1966, led by young majors including Chukwuma Kaduna Nzeogwu. It overthrew the First Republic government of Prime Minister Alhaji (Sir) Abubakar Tafawa Balewa, who was killed along with Ahmadu Bello and others: C. Major-General Aguiyi-Ironsi (D) then took over as the first military head of state, and was himself killed in the July 1966 counter-coup that brought Yakubu Gowon (A) to power. The trap: Azikiwe (B) was the ceremonial President, not the head of government, and he was abroad at the time. Memory tip: coup 1 (Jan 1966) toppled Balewa; coup 2 (July 1966) toppled Ironsi.",
 "|Adjudication of conflict between the federal and regional governments in the first Republic was reserved for|National industrial court|Supreme Court|Federal Court of Appeal|Federal High court": "In a federation, disputes between the centre and the regions need a neutral umpire. Under the 1960 and 1963 Constitutions, the Supreme Court had original jurisdiction over disputes between the federation and a region, or between regions, so such cases went straight to it: B. The trap: the Federal Court of Appeal (C) was created only in 1976 and hears appeals; the Federal High Court (D) began in 1973 as the Federal Revenue Court; the National Industrial Court (A) handles labour disputes. Memory tip: federal vs regional disputes go straight to the top court.",
 "|The officer in charge of the administration of the palace in the Yoruba political structure is the|ba'ale|oluwo|ilari|iwefa": "In Old Oyo the Alaafin's palace was run by eunuchs (iwefa), headed by three titled officials: the Ona Efa (justice), the Otun Efa (religion) and the Osi Efa (palace affairs and finance). They were in charge of palace administration: D. The trap: the ilari (C) also lived in the palace, but they were the king's messengers and agents sent to the provinces, not its administrators; the ba'ale (A) was a village head. Memory tip: Iwefa = eunuchs who ran the palace.",
 "|Prior to 1966, the system of local government in western region was based on the three-tier structure of|native authority, division and district councils|district, local and area councils|division, district and local councils|province, district and local council": "The Western Region pioneered elected local government in Nigeria. Its Local Government Law of 1952, modelled on British councils, replaced native authorities with a three-tier system of divisional councils, district councils and local councils, with elected members and traditional rulers: C. This lasted until military rule, and the 1976 reform later made local government single-tier nationwide. The trap: native authority (A) belongs to the old indirect-rule system the 1952 law replaced, and \"province\" (D) was an administrative unit, not a council. Memory tip: West 1952 = Division, District, Local (\"DDL\").",
 "|A major shortcoming of the Lyttelton Constitution was that|it did not provide for a second chamber at the federal level|the Governor-General continued to head the government|no provision was made for the office of the prime minister|it failed to provide for the office of a premier": "The Lyttelton Constitution (1954), named after Colonial Secretary Oliver Lyttelton, made Nigeria a federation of three regions plus Lagos, and created regional premiers. But at the centre there was no prime minister; the Governor-General still presided over the Council of Ministers. The federal premiership came only in 1957, filled by Tafawa Balewa. So the major shortcoming was no provision for a prime minister: C. The trap: D is false, since Lyttelton did create premiers in the regions (Awolowo, Azikiwe, Ahmadu Bello). Memory tip: Lyttelton gave premiers to the regions but no prime minister to the centre.",
 "|The option A-4 voting system was introduced into the political process in Nigeria during the|Third Republic|fourth Republic|First Republic|Second Republic": "Option A4 was an open-ballot method introduced by the Babangida regime during the Third Republic transition (1989–1993): voters lined up openly behind the photograph or symbol of their preferred candidate and were counted. It was meant to stop ballot-box stuffing and was used in party primaries and elections, ending with the transition annulled after 12 June 1993: A. Option A4, open queuing behind party symbols, was used in the Third Republic's 1993 elections; the Fourth Republic (B) returned to the secret ballot. Memory tip: A4 = Third Republic, Babangida, the two parties SDP and NRC.",
 "|In orientation, early nationalism in Nigeria was essentially|revolutionary|reformist|reactionary|radical": "Early Nigerian nationalism (roughly 1920s–1930s), led by men such as Herbert Macaulay, whose Nigerian National Democratic Party was formed in 1923 after the Clifford Constitution, was moderate. It asked for reforms within colonial rule: more elected seats, more Africans in the civil service, better education and services. It did not yet demand independence. So it was essentially reformist: B. The trap: radical (D) describes later nationalism after 1945, such as the Zikist Movement demanding self-government now. Memory tip: early nationalists Reformed; later nationalists demanded Independence.",
 "|Which of the following in the Sokoto Caliphate performed functions similar to that of the Bashorun in Oyo Kingdom?|Waziri|Galadima|Ma'aji|Alkali": "In Oyo the Bashorun was head of the Oyo Mesi (the seven kingmakers) and acted like a prime minister, the most powerful chief after the Alaafin. In the Sokoto Caliphate the equivalent was the Waziri, the chief adviser and head of administration under the Sultan or Emir: A. The trap: the Galadima (B) administered the capital city, the Ma'aji (C) was the treasurer and the Alkali (D) was the Islamic judge. Memory tip: Waziri = Wazir = vizier, the prime minister of Muslim states.",
 "|Problems arising from population census in Nigeria are mainly caused by|public opinions of the people|unreliable census figures|political manipulation by the elite|the registration of the under-aged people": "Because population decides the number of seats and the share of federal revenue, censuses in Nigeria have been fiercely contested. The 1962 count was cancelled, the 1963 count disputed, the 1973 census annulled, and the 1991 and 2006 results questioned. The figures have been unreliable, and that is the source of the problems: B. Behind the unreliable figures lies the inflation of numbers for political gain (C), so the two are linked; the textbook answer names the unreliable figures themselves. The trap: C, political manipulation by the elite, is a real source of census disputes, but it is a cause of the unreliable figures the question targets; under-age registration (D) affects voters' lists, not censuses. Memory tip: wrong numbers mean wrong seats and wrong money.",
 "|Which of the following formalized colonial penetration into Africa?|Paris Treaty|Treaty of Westphalia|Berlin Conference|Treaty of Versailles": "At the Berlin Conference (November 1884 to February 1885), called by German Chancellor Otto von Bismarck, European powers agreed rules for claiming African territory: \"effective occupation\" and notifying other powers. This formalised the scramble for Africa and colonial penetration: C. The trap: the Treaty of Westphalia (B, 1648) created the modern European system of sovereign states; the Treaty of Versailles (D, 1919) ended the First World War and took away Germany's colonies, such as Kamerun, but did not start colonial rule. Memory tip: Berlin 1884–85 = Africa carved up on a map.",
 "|The 1989 Nigerian Constitution provided for a|multi-party system|zero-party system|two-party system|one-party system": "The 1989 Constitution, prepared for Babangida's planned Third Republic, allowed only two political parties. When none of the associations seeking registration was approved, the government itself created the Social Democratic Party (SDP), \"a little to the left\", and the National Republican Convention (NRC), \"a little to the right\": C. The constitution never fully came into force because of the 1993 annulment. The trap: multi-party (A) describes the 1979 and 1999 constitutions. Memory tip: 1989 = two parties, SDP and NRC.",
 "|The Nigerian federalism is a|four-tier system|three-tier system|two-tier system|one-tier system": "Nigeria's federal system has three tiers of government: the federal government, the 36 state governments (plus the FCT), and the 774 local governments. The 1976 Local Government Reform first recognised local government as the third tier, and the 1979 and 1999 Constitutions entrenched it: B. The trap: classic federalism, as in the USA, has two tiers (C), federal and state, which is why students sometimes choose it, but Nigeria constitutionally adds local government. Memory tip: Nigeria = 1 + 36 + 774, three tiers.",
 "|The arm of government mainly responsible for the implementation of law in Nigeria is the|judiciary|police|legislature|executive": "Each arm of government has a main job: the legislature makes laws, the executive implements (executes) them, and the judiciary interprets them and settles disputes. In Nigeria the executive is headed by the President, with ministers, ministries and agencies carrying laws into effect: D. The trap: the police (B) do enforce laws, but they are an agency under the executive, not an arm of government. Memory tip: Legislature Legislates, Executive Executes, Judiciary Judges.",
 "|The British colonial rule in Nigeria brought about|a stagnation in social formations|the pampering of traditional rulers|a change in the mode of production|stabilization in the price of raw materials": "British rule changed Nigeria's economy and society deeply. It introduced a money economy and British currency, cash crops for export (cocoa, groundnuts, palm oil), wage labour, railways and private property in land, shifting the mode of production from subsistence and communal production towards capitalism: C. This is a debatable point: some dependency writers say colonialism stunted Africa's development, but social formations did not stagnate; new classes of workers, traders and educated elite emerged. The trap: A, stagnation in social formations, echoes the claim that colonialism stunted development, but new classes of workers, traders and educated elite emerged. Memory tip: colonialism brought cash crops, coins and classes.",
 "|A primary objective of the privatization policy in Nigeria is|probity|responsiveness|transparency|accountability": "Privatisation means selling government-owned enterprises (fully or partly) to private investors. Nigeria's programme began under the 1988 Privatisation and Commercialisation Decree and was expanded by the Public Enterprises Act of 1999 under the Bureau of Public Enterprises. Its aims include ending waste and losses, reducing the burden on government, and making managers answerable for results, that is, accountability: D. The trap: transparency (C) describes how the sale itself should be conducted, not the objective of privatising. Memory tip: private owners demand an account of every naira.",
 "|Which of the following is saddled with the conduct of general elections in Nigeria's Fourth Republic?|National Electoral Commission of Nigeria|Independent National Electoral Commission|National Electoral Commission|Federal Electoral Commission": "The Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC) was established in 1998 by the Abubakar regime and has conducted every Fourth Republic general election, from 1999 to today; the 1999 Constitution (Third Schedule) provides for it: B. The trap: the Federal Electoral Commission, FEDECO (D), ran the 1979 and 1983 elections of the Second Republic; the National Electoral Commission, NEC (C), ran the Third Republic elections including 12 June 1993; NECON (A) served under Abacha. Memory tip: FEDECO = Second Republic, NEC = Third, INEC = Fourth.",
 "|Public corporations are established to|reduce government spending|boost government revenue|improve provision of social amenities|create vacancies for political appointments": "Public corporations are bodies owned by government but run like businesses, set up by law to provide essential services: water, electricity, railways, broadcasting (for example the old NEPA, Nigerian Railway Corporation and NTA). Their main purpose is to provide social amenities and services to the public at affordable cost, which private firms might not provide: C. The trap: boosting revenue (B) is not their main aim, since many run at a loss; creating jobs for politicians (D) is an abuse, not a purpose. Memory tip: public corporations serve the public first, profit second.",
 "|The political structure of Nigeria under the first military regime was|confederal|quasi-federal|federal|unitary": "After the January 1966 coup, Major-General Aguiyi-Ironsi became head of state. On 24 May 1966 he issued the Unification Decree (Decree No. 34), which abolished the regions and the federal system and declared Nigeria a unitary state with a single public service: D. The decree caused fear in the North and helped spark the July 1966 counter-coup; Gowon restored federalism and in 1967 created 12 states. The trap: quasi-federal (B) describes later military regimes, which kept states but ran them under a central command. Memory tip: Ironsi's Decree 34 = Nigeria made unitary.",
 "|The Inspector General of Police is appointed by the executive on the advice of the|Nigeria Police Council|Police Service Commission|House of Representatives|Senate of the Federal Republic": "The Inspector-General of Police (IGP) heads the Nigeria Police Force. Section 215(1)(a) of the 1999 Constitution says the IGP is appointed by the President on the advice of the Nigeria Police Council, chosen from among serving members of the Force. The Council is made up of the President (chairman), the 36 state governors, the chairman of the Police Service Commission and the IGP. So the answer is A. The trap: the Police Service Commission (B) appoints, promotes and disciplines the other police officers, but not the IGP. Memory tip: the IGP comes from the COUNCIL; everyone below him comes from the COMMISSION.",
 "|A major problem associated with the Nigerian federalism is|ethnic conflicts|environmental pollution|boundary disputes|fiscal allocation": "In a federation the centre and the states each need money, so revenue must be shared. In Nigeria most revenue (oil money) is collected at the centre and divided through the Federation Account, and the sharing formula has caused endless disputes: the Phillipson (1946), Hicks-Phillipson (1951), Chick (1953), Raisman (1958), Binns (1964), Dina (1968) and Aboyade (1977) commissions, and today the RMAFC. The quarrel over derivation for oil states continues today. So the problem is fiscal allocation: D. The trap: ethnic conflicts (A) are a problem of Nigerian society generally, not a defect of the federal arrangement as such. Memory tip: in Nigerian federalism, \"who gets what money\" is the oldest fight.",
 "|The six African members of the Nigerian Council were nominated by the|Queen|district officers|natives|governors": "On amalgamation in 1914, Lord Lugard set up the Nigerian Council, a purely advisory body that met once a year and had no power to make laws. Its members were mostly British officials, plus a few unofficial members; the six African members (such as the Alaafin of Oyo and the Sultan of Sokoto) were nominated by the Governor-General, not elected. The option \"governors\" stands for the colonial governor (Governor-General): D. The Queen (A) is wrong: in 1914 Britain had a king, George V, and the six African members of Lugard's Nigerian Council were nominated by the Governor-General. Memory tip: before 1922 Africans were NOMINATED; the 1922 Clifford Constitution first let some be ELECTED.",
 "|In the Second Republic, the total membership of each House of Assembly was determined by the|electoral commission|political parties|constitution|electorate": "The 1979 Constitution, which created Nigeria's Second Republic (1979–1983), fixed the size of every State House of Assembly: three times the number of seats the state had in the federal House of Representatives. So a state with 10 seats in the House of Representatives had 30 Assembly members. The membership was therefore set by the constitution: C. The trap: the electoral commission (FEDECO, A) drew constituency boundaries and ran elections, but it could not change the total, which the constitution itself set. Memory tip: 1979 House of Assembly = 3 x House of Reps seats.",
 "|Privatization and commercialization programmes in Nigeria have|increased public utilities|de-emphasized government ownership|reduced government revenue|encouraged corruption": "Privatization means selling all or part of government shares in public enterprises to private investors; commercialization means making the enterprises that remain run like businesses, covering their costs or making profit without subsidies. Nigeria began this under the Structural Adjustment Programme (Decree No. 25 of 1988, with TCPC), and the Bureau of Public Enterprises later took over. The main effect is to reduce the place of government ownership in the economy: B. The trap: privatization often brings government a one-time income from share sales, so \"reduced government revenue\" (C) is not its defining effect. Memory tip: privatize = Private takes over; government steps back.",
 "|The fear of domination in the public service led to the|adoption of population as a formula for revenue allocation|establishment of the Civil Service Commission|introduction of rotational presidency|establishment of the Federal Character Commission": "Nigeria's groups feared that one region or tribe would fill the public service. The 1979 Constitution (s.14(3)) introduced the federal character principle: appointments must reflect the country's diversity so that no group dominates. To enforce it, the Abacha regime set up the Federal Character Commission in 1996, and s.153 of the 1999 Constitution keeps it. So the answer is D. The trap: the Civil Service Commission (B) handles appointments and discipline in the civil service but was not created because of fear of domination. Rotational presidency (C) is a party arrangement about the top political office, not the public service. Memory tip: FEDERAL CHARACTER = Fair Each Region's share.",
 "|In the Igbo political system, the most senior member of the council of elders is the|okpara|obi|eze|ofo": "The Igbo were acephalous: no king, with power shared among the village assembly, age grades, title holders and elders. Each lineage's eldest man was the okpara. He held the ofo, a sacred staff of authority and truth linking the living to the ancestors, and so he was the most senior member of the council of elders: A. The trap: ofo (D) is the staff the okpara holds, not a person. Obi and eze (B, C) are titles of kings found only in a few Igbo communities such as Onitsha and Nri. Memory tip: the OKpara is the OLDest man who keeps the OFO.",
 "|The immediate cause of the 1966 military coup d'etat in Nigeria was|nepotism|political instability|military ambition|corruption": "The First Republic was shaken by the 1962 Action Group crisis and emergency in the West, the disputed 1962–63 census, the 1964 federal election boycott, and the rigged 1965 Western Region election, which led to riots and arson (\"Operation Wetie\"). This breakdown of law and order, political instability, was the immediate trigger of the coup of 15 January 1966 led by Major Chukwuma Kaduna Nzeogwu. So the answer is B. The trap: corruption (D) was a cause the coup plotters named, but it was a long-standing background factor; the immediate spark was the Western crisis. Memory tip: immediate cause = the last straw (the 1965 Western election violence).",
 "|The exclusive list is reserved for legislation by the|regional government|state government|local government|federal government": "A federal constitution divides legislative power into lists. The Exclusive Legislative List (originally 68 items in the 1999 Constitution) covers matters only the federal government may legislate on, such as defence, currency, foreign affairs, citizenship and the police. The Concurrent List is shared by federal and state governments, and residual matters (not on either list) belong to the states. So the exclusive list is for the federal government: D. The trap: the regional government (A) was the second tier before 1967, and it had power over residual matters, not exclusive ones. Memory tip: Exclusive = Excludes everyone but the centre.",
 "|The participation of Nigerians in the Second World War created in the nationalists the awareness that the|Africans were superior|Africans were more qualified to rule|Europeans were not superior|Europeans were less tactful in warfare": "About 100,000 Nigerians served in the British forces during the Second World War (1939–45), many in Burma with the 81st and 82nd West African Divisions. They saw European soldiers wounded, afraid, defeated and dying like anyone else. Returning ex-servicemen and the nationalists drew the lesson that Europeans were not superior beings with a natural right to rule Africa: C. The trap: A and B go too far; nationalists claimed equality and the right to self-rule, not African superiority. Memory tip: WWII broke the \"white superiority\" myth, and the Atlantic Charter (1941) gave nationalists the language of self-determination.",
 "|Which of the following mostly militates against the efficiency and productivity of public corporations in Nigeria?|Incessant interference by government|The management teams are always appointed at will|Boards are often non-professionals|Members of the management team are always at loggerheads": "Public corporations (such as NEPA, the railways and Nigeria Airways) were supposed to run like businesses at arm's length from politics. In practice governments kept interfering: appointing boards as political rewards, fixing prices below cost, forcing over-staffing and directing contracts. This incessant interference is the main reason for their poor performance, and a key argument for privatization: A. The trap: non-professional boards (C) are a real problem, but they are themselves a result of political interference, which is the root cause. Memory tip: too many political hands in the till make a public corporation ill.",
 "|The power to nominate members of the judiciary at the federal level under the 1999 Constitution is vested in the|Federal Judicial Service Commission|Supreme Court|National Judicial Council|Nigerian Bar Association": "Under the 1999 Constitution (Third Schedule), the Federal Judicial Service Commission (FJSC) advises the National Judicial Council (NJC) and supplies names, but it is the NJC that recommends (nominates) persons to the President for appointment as Chief Justice, Justices of the Supreme Court and Court of Appeal, and federal High Court judges. The President then appoints, with Senate confirmation for the top posts. So the power to nominate rests with the NJC: C. The trap: the FJSC (A) only \"advises the NJC in nominating\"; it does not make the nomination itself. Memory tip: FJSC shortlists, NJC nominates, President appoints, Senate confirms.",
 "|Prior to 1976, the local government system practised in Northern Nigeria was|chief-and-council|chief-in-council|prefectorial|council": "Under indirect rule the North was governed through native authorities. At first the emir was a Sole Native Authority with near-absolute power. Reforms in the 1950s (Native Authority Law, 1954) made him rule with a council, as \"chief-in-council\" where his word was final, or \"chief-and-council\" where he had to accept the council's decision. The typical arrangement in the North up to the 1976 reform was chief-in-council: B. The trap: chief-and-council (A) was the weaker form, used where the chief's power was more limited. Memory tip: chief-IN-council = the chief is IN charge; chief-AND-council = the council shares power.",
 "|In the segmented Tiv society, governance was usually confined to the|age grade|chiefdom|family|clan": "The Tiv of the Benue valley were a segmentary, acephalous society: they had no chiefs or central government before colonial rule. The basic unit was the family (compound or minimal lineage) under its eldest man, who settled disputes with the other elders. Larger lineages and clans came together only temporarily, for war or a serious dispute, then split up again. So governance was usually confined to the family: C. The trap: \"chiefdom\" (B) contradicts the very idea of a segmented society; the office of Tor Tiv was created by the British in 1946. Memory tip: Tiv and Igbo are stateless: power stays with family elders.",
 "|Which of the following influenced nationalist activities in Nigeria?|Indian Congress Party|Negro Diaspora Congress|Pan-African Congress|National Congress of British West Africa": "The National Congress of British West Africa (NCBWA) was founded in Accra in 1920 by J.E. Casely Hayford and others from all four British West African colonies. It demanded elected African members in the legislative councils. Its pressure led to the elective principle in the Clifford Constitution of 1922, which in turn led Herbert Macaulay to form the first Nigerian party, the NNDP (1923). So it directly influenced nationalist activity in Nigeria: D. Note: the Pan-African Congress (C), especially the 1945 Manchester Congress, also inspired Nigerian nationalists, so this question is open to argument; D is the expected answer. The trap: C, the Pan-African Congress, also inspired nationalists, but the NCBWA's pressure directly produced the 1922 elective principle and the NNDP. Memory tip: NCBWA (1920) to Clifford (1922) to NNDP (1923).",
 "|The party that did not control any state in the Second Republic was|NAP|PRP|NPP|GNPP": "Five parties contested the 1979 elections: NPN (won 7 states), UPN (5), NPP (3), GNPP (2) and PRP (2), so all of PRP, NPP and GNPP controlled states. The Nigeria Advance Party (NAP), led by lawyer Tunji Braithwaite, was registered only in 1982 for the 1983 elections, and it won no state at all: A. The trap: the PRP (B) was small, but it controlled Kano and Kaduna in 1979. Memory tip: 1979 = 5 parties, 19 states; the late-comer NAP won nothing.",
 "|'Pacification' refers to a process through which some areas in Nigeria were acquired by|military conquest|gunboat diplomacy|signing of treaties|peaceful negotiation": "\"Pacification\" was the British colonial word for bringing resistant peoples under control by force. Officials said they were \"pacifying\" an area when they sent punitive expeditions: the Ijebu expedition (1892), Benin (1897), the Aro Expedition (1901–02) and the conquest of the Sokoto Caliphate (1903). So pacification means acquisition by military conquest: A. The trap: the word sounds like \"peace\", which tempts students to choose peaceful negotiation (D); treaties (C) were a separate method, often signed before force was used. Memory tip: colonial \"pacification\" = peace imposed with the gun.",
 "|Counter-trade was introduced by the government led by|Abdulsalami Abubakar|Olusegun Obasanjo|Muhammadu Buhari|Ibrahim Babangida": "When oil prices fell in the early 1980s, Nigeria ran short of foreign exchange to import essential goods. The Buhari regime (31 December 1983 – 27 August 1985) answered with counter-trade: a barter arrangement exchanging Nigerian crude oil directly for machinery, raw materials and goods from countries such as Brazil, France and Austria, without paying cash. So the answer is C. The trap: Babangida (D), who overthrew Buhari in 1985, is linked with SAP and deregulation, not counter-trade. Memory tip: Buhari bartered: oil for goods.",
 "|A major limitation of the Commonwealth is that it lacks|the power to set formal rules to govern the affairs of member-nations|an organizational structure and a definite identity|enough funds to run its affairs|the power to compel member-nations to comply with its decisions": "The Commonwealth is a voluntary association of 56 states, most of them former British territories. Its decisions are reached by consensus and are not legally binding; it has no army, court or sanctions machinery to enforce them. The most it can do is suspend a member, as it did to Nigeria in 1995 after the execution of Ken Saro-Wiwa. Its limitation is lack of power to compel compliance: D. The trap: the Commonwealth does have a structure and identity (B): a Secretariat in London (since 1965), a Secretary-General and CHOGM summits. Memory tip: the Commonwealth can advise and suspend, but it cannot enforce.",
 "|Which of the following countries has withdrawn her membership of the ECOWAS?|Senegal|Mauritania|Guinea Bissau|Burkina Faso": "ECOWAS was founded by the Treaty of Lagos on 28 May 1975 by 15 states; Cape Verde joined in 1977. Mauritania, a founding member, withdrew in 2000, partly because it was turning towards the Arab Maghreb Union. When this paper was set in 2009, Mauritania was the only country that had left: B. But Burkina Faso, Mali and Niger also left with effect from 29 January 2025, so ECOWAS now has 12 members and Burkina Faso (D) is a correct answer today too. Both B and D are accepted. Senegal (A) and Guinea-Bissau (C) have always remained members; Mauritania left in 2000, and Burkina Faso withdrew with Mali and Niger in January 2025. Memory tip: Mauritania was the first out (2000); the Sahel trio followed in 2025.",
 "|Which of the following is a major threat to OPEC?|Persistence of war in the Middle East|The discovery of crude oil in more countries|Antagonism by the major world powers|Development of viable alternatives to crude oil": "OPEC (founded in Baghdad, 1960; headquarters Vienna) earns its influence from the world's dependence on crude oil. Anything that makes oil less necessary weakens it: nuclear, solar and wind power, biofuels and now electric vehicles. If viable alternatives replace crude oil, OPEC loses its power, so this is the major threat: D. The trap: discovery of oil in more countries (B) increases competition, but new producers can join OPEC or still sell oil, so the market for oil survives; alternatives threaten the market itself. Memory tip: OPEC fears a world that no longer needs oil.",
 "|The AU's right to intervene in a member country in crisis portrays it as|an international organization|a regional organization|a supranational organization|a continental organization": "A supranational organisation has authority over and above its member states, so it can act inside a member even against that member's wishes. Article 4(h) of the AU Constitutive Act (2000) gives the AU the right to intervene in a member state in grave circumstances: war crimes, genocide and crimes against humanity. The OAU, by contrast, stuck to non-interference. This right portrays the AU as supranational: C. The trap: the AU is also international, regional and continental (A, B, D), but those words only describe its membership; only \"supranational\" describes power over states. Memory tip: SUPRA = above; a supranational body stands above its members' sovereignty.",
 "|The permanent membership of the UN Security Council is|ten|fifteen|five|eleven": "The UN Security Council has 15 members. Five are permanent and hold the veto: the USA, Russia (formerly the USSR), China, the United Kingdom and France, the victors of the Second World War. The other ten are elected by the General Assembly for two-year terms (there were six before 1965, when the Council grew from 11 to 15). So permanent membership is five: C. The trap: fifteen (B) is the total membership and ten (A) is the non-permanent members. Memory tip: the \"P5\" = 5 permanent; 5 + 10 = 15.",
 "|An advantage of flexible constitution is that it allows for|political awareness|constitutional stability|reduction of ambiguities|ease of amendment": "A flexible constitution can be amended by the same simple procedure as an ordinary law; Britain's largely unwritten constitution is the classic example. Its chief advantage is ease of amendment: it adapts quickly to changing circumstances or emergencies: D. The trap: constitutional stability (B) is an advantage of a rigid constitution, which needs special procedures (in Nigeria, two-thirds of each house of the National Assembly plus two-thirds of state Houses of Assembly) and so changes rarely. Memory tip: Flexible = easy to Fix; Rigid = Resists change.",
 "|Power refers to the|ability to impose one's will upon others|legal nature of a constitution|duties political parties demand of their registered members|duties an individual imposes on himself": "Power is the ability to influence others or make them act as one wishes, even against their will, by persuasion, reward, threat or force. Authority is power that is accepted as rightful (legitimate). So power refers to the ability to impose one's will upon others: A. The legal nature of a constitution (B) concerns its supremacy, and party or self-imposed duties (C, D) are obligations, not power. Memory tip: Power = can make you; Authority = has the right to; Legitimacy = people accept it.",
 "|Prorogation is the bringing of a session of a parliament to an end through|royal proclamation|final reading|guillotine|impeachment": "Under the Westminster system, a parliament meets in sessions. Prorogation ends a session without dissolving parliament: the monarch, on the Prime Minister's advice, issues a royal proclamation, and unfinished business usually lapses. Parliament then meets again in a new session opened by the King's Speech. So prorogation is by royal proclamation: A. The trap: the guillotine (C) is a motion that cuts short debate on a bill, not a session; dissolution, unlike prorogation, ends the life of parliament and leads to a general election. Memory tip: PROrogation = PROclamation pauses parliament.",
 "|The right of all eligible citizens to vote and be voted for is referred to as|gender equality|political mandate|restricted franchise|universal suffrage": "Universal adult suffrage gives every qualified adult citizen the right to vote (and, if qualified, to stand for election) without regard to sex, wealth, education or religion. In Nigeria it is set at age 18. It came gradually: women in the North could not vote until the late 1970s. So the answer is D. The trap: restricted franchise (C) is the opposite, limiting the vote by income, sex or property, as the 1922 Clifford Constitution did (men with £100 income). Memory tip: Universal = everyone qualified; Restricted = only some.",
 "|Values, beliefs and orientation of a political system are internalized through|political culture|political socialization|political development|political power": "Political culture is the set of values, beliefs and attitudes a society holds about politics. Political socialization is the process by which individuals learn and internalize that culture, through agents such as the family, school, peer groups, religious bodies, the mass media and political parties. So values are internalized through political socialization: B. The trap: political culture (A) is the content that is learnt, not the process of learning it. Memory tip: Culture is WHAT we believe; Socialization is HOW we come to believe it.",
 "|Occupational pressure groups pursue their objectives mainly by|issuing ultimatums and embarking on strikes|alienating the government|embarking on guerrilla warfare|contesting elections": "Occupational (professional or sectional) pressure groups, such as the Nigeria Labour Congress, ASUU, the Nigerian Medical Association and the NUT, exist to protect their members' interests. Their main weapons are negotiation backed by threats: issuing ultimatums and going on strike to force the government to act: A. Contesting elections (D) is what political parties do, and guerrilla warfare (C) is armed rebellion; occupational groups such as unions press their case through ultimatums and strikes. Memory tip: parties want POWER; pressure groups want INFLUENCE (and strike to get it).",
 "|In a cabinet system, the legislature comprises the|prime minister and members of the parliament|prime minister and members of the jury|prime minister and ministers|president and ministers": "In a parliamentary (cabinet) system, as in Britain or Nigeria's First Republic, there is a fusion of powers: the prime minister and all ministers must be members of parliament. The legislature therefore comprises the prime minister and the members of parliament: A. The trap: \"prime minister and ministers\" (C) describes the cabinet (the executive), not the legislature, and \"president and ministers\" (D) is the executive in a presidential system. Juries (B) belong to the courts. Memory tip: cabinet system = the PM sits in parliament with every other MP.",
 "|The committee that scrutinizes budget proposals in the legislature is the|Rules and Business Committee|Public Accounts Committee|Finance Committee|Appropriation Committee": "In Nigeria's National Assembly, the President lays the budget (Appropriation Bill) before both houses, and the Senate and House Committees on Appropriations scrutinise it, consulting the other committees, before it is passed. So the committee is the Appropriation Committee: D. The trap: the Public Accounts Committee (B) checks how money was spent after the year, using the Auditor-General's report; Finance (C) deals with revenue and taxation; and Rules and Business (A) arranges the order of business. Memory tip: Appropriation = before the money is spent; Public Accounts = after.",
 "|Democracy is a system of government that is|constituted by the will of the majority of the citizens|imposed on the majority of the citizens|designed to favour the elite|put in place to favour the party caucus": "Democracy (Greek demos = people, kratos = rule) is government of the people, by the people and for the people (Abraham Lincoln, 1863). Rulers are chosen in free elections and govern with the consent of the majority, while respecting minority rights. So democracy is constituted by the will of the majority: A. Options B, C and D describe imposed government and rule for an elite or party caucus, the opposite of democracy. Memory tip: democracy = majority rule with minority rights.",
 "|Collective ownership of property is associated with|feudalism|communism|mercantilism|capitalism": "Communism, developed by Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels (The Communist Manifesto, 1848), aims at a classless society in which the means of production (land, factories, mines) are owned collectively by the community, and goods are shared \"from each according to his ability, to each according to his needs\". So collective ownership is associated with communism: B. Capitalism (D) rests on private ownership, feudalism (A) on lords owning land worked by serfs, and mercantilism (C) is a trade policy for building national wealth. Memory tip: COMmunism = COMmon ownership.",
 "|The agency responsible for investigating allegations of maladministration in the civil service is the|Federal Civil Service Commission|Code of Conduct Bureau|Public Complaints Commission|Federal Character Commission": "An ombudsman is an official who investigates citizens' complaints against public officers and agencies. Nigeria's ombudsman is the Public Complaints Commission, set up in 1975 (Decree No. 31) by the Murtala Mohammed regime; it investigates injustice and maladministration in the public service. So the answer is C. The trap: the Code of Conduct Bureau (B) deals with public officers' asset declarations and conflicts of interest, not general complaints; the Civil Service Commission (A) appoints and disciplines staff. Memory tip: complain about maladministration to the Public COMPLAINTS Commission.",
 "|A political entity that possesses independence is a|community|nation|society|state": "A state has four elements: population, defined territory, government and sovereignty. Sovereignty means it is independent of outside control and supreme within its borders. A nation is a people bound by common culture, language and history; it may or may not have its own state (for example, the Kurds). Community and society are general terms for groups of people. So the entity with independence is a state: D. The trap: \"nation\" (B) is often used loosely for a country, but strictly it describes a people, not an independent political unit. Memory tip: STATE = Sovereign + Territory + Assembly of people + Government.",
 "|The principle that advocates strict adherence to the fundamental laws of a country is|constitutionalism|individual responsibility|absolutism|collective responsibility": "Constitutionalism means that the constitution is supreme and that both rulers and the ruled obey it; it is government limited by law, with checks on power. It goes with the rule of law and separation of powers. So the principle of strict adherence to a country's fundamental laws is constitutionalism: A. Absolutism (C) means unlimited power, the opposite, and individual and collective responsibility (B, D) are conventions about ministers in a cabinet system. Memory tip: constitutionalism = everyone, even the President, plays by the constitution.",
 "|One advantage of the unitary system over the federal system is its|bureaucratic nature|cost effectiveness|small membership|ratification of appointments": "In a unitary state (for example Britain or France), all power comes from one central government; local units are only its agents. There is no duplication of governors, state legislatures, ministries and civil services, so it is cheaper to run than a federation: B. A unitary system also allows quicker decisions and uniform policies. A bureaucratic nature (A) is a drawback, not an advantage, and ratification of appointments (D) is a legislative check; a unitary system is cheaper because it runs one government. Memory tip: one government, one bill: unitary = cheaper.",
 "|Which of the following is a duty of an electoral commission?|Registration of political parties|Creation of political parties|Establishment of electoral tribunals|Inauguration of the parliament": "An electoral commission (in Nigeria, INEC since 1998) organises and supervises elections. Its duties include registering political parties and monitoring their finances, registering voters, delimiting constituencies, screening candidates' papers, conducting elections and declaring results. So registration of political parties is a duty: A. Parties are created (B) by citizens, and electoral tribunals (C) are set up under the constitution and staffed by the judiciary; electoral commissions register parties. Memory tip: INEC Registers, Conducts and Declares; it does not Create parties or Judge disputes.",
 "|A form of government in which the nobles rule for the benefit of all is|monarchy|gerontocracy|aristocracy|plutocracy": "Aristotle classified governments by who rules and for whom. Rule by one for all is monarchy; by a few for all is aristocracy; by many for all is polity. Their corrupt forms (rulers serving themselves) are tyranny, oligarchy and democracy (mob rule). So rule by nobles for the benefit of all is aristocracy: C. The trap: plutocracy (D) is rule by the rich, gerontocracy (B) is rule by elders, and monarchy (A) is rule by one. Memory tip: aristos = best; aristocracy = the best few ruling for all.",
 "|Fascism as an ideology facilitates|totalitarianism|socialism|communalism|liberalism": "Fascism arose in Italy under Benito Mussolini (in power 1922–1943), and Nazism in Germany under Hitler is a close cousin. It glorifies the state and the nation, worships a single leader, allows only one party, crushes opposition and controls the economy, press and education. A state that controls every aspect of life is totalitarian, so fascism facilitates totalitarianism: A. The trap: socialism (B) also gives the state a large role, but fascism was violently anti-socialist and anti-communist; liberalism (D) defends individual freedom, the opposite of fascism. Memory tip: Fascism = one Leader, one Party, total State.",
 "|A prominent feature of a presidential system of government is|dual executive|vote of no confidence|the rule of law|separation of powers": "In a presidential system (USA; Nigeria since 1979) the three organs of government are separated: the President and ministers are not members of the legislature, each organ has its own powers, and they check each other. So separation of powers is the prominent feature: D. The trap: dual executive (A) and vote of no confidence (B) belong to parliamentary systems; the rule of law (C) applies to every democratic system, so it is not distinctive of the presidential one. Memory tip: presidential = separated; parliamentary = fused.",
 "|The executive checks the excesses of the legislature through|assent to bills|the use of veto power|the ratification of treaties|issuance of orders": "Under checks and balances, each organ restrains the others. The executive checks the legislature mainly by the veto: the President may refuse assent to a bill, and in Nigeria (s.58, 1999 Constitution) the National Assembly can only override with a two-thirds majority in each house. So the answer is B. The trap: assent to bills (A) is the opposite, approving what the legislature has passed; ratification of treaties (C) is usually a legislative check on the executive. Memory tip: VETO is Latin for \"I forbid\".",
 "|The consciousness of a common identity of a people is called|nationalism|integration|indoctrination|emancipation": "Nationalism is the feeling of a people that they belong together as one nation, sharing history, culture or destiny; it often becomes a demand for self-government, as in Nigeria's struggle for independence. So consciousness of a common identity is nationalism: A. The trap: integration (B) is the process of bringing different groups together into one political community; it may produce a common identity but is not the consciousness itself. Indoctrination (C) is one-sided teaching; emancipation (D) is being set free. Memory tip: nationalism = \"we are one people\".",
 "|Citizenship of a state by descent requires that|one is born and bred in the state|one's mother was born in the state|one's grandparents were citizens of that state|one is domiciled in that state": "Citizenship by descent (jus sanguinis, \"right of blood\") is acquired through one's ancestors, not one's place of birth. Section 25 of the 1999 Constitution makes a person a Nigerian by birth if born in Nigeria to parents or grandparents who belong to a community indigenous to Nigeria, or born outside Nigeria to a parent who is a Nigerian citizen. Among the options, only C ties citizenship to ancestors who were citizens: C. The trap: being born and bred in a state (A) is citizenship by birth (jus soli), and domicile (D) leads to naturalisation, not descent. Memory tip: DESCENT = it DESCENDS from your parents and grandparents.",
 "|In order to dispense justice impartially, the judiciary primarily needs|discipline|logistics|motivation|independence": "The judiciary can only judge impartially, without fear or favour, if it is independent: free from pressure by the executive, the legislature, parties or the rich. Independence is secured by security of tenure, appointment through bodies like the NJC, salaries charged on the Consolidated Fund, and immunity for judicial acts. So the primary need is independence: D. Discipline, logistics and motivation (A, B, C) help judges work well, but none guarantees impartiality if judges can be punished for unpopular rulings. Memory tip: no independence, no impartiality.",
 "|A state with a hegemonic political party is one in which|there is one dominant party|there is no opposition party|there is only one party|other parties are officially recognized": "In a dominant-party (hegemonic) system, several parties exist legally and elections are held, but one party is so strong that it wins time after time and controls government, like the ANC in South Africa or Mexico's PRI for most of the twentieth century. So the answer is A. The trap: \"only one party\" (C) describes a one-party state, where other parties are banned, and \"no opposition party\" (B) means the same thing; in a hegemonic system the opposition exists but cannot win. Memory tip: hegemonic = dominant, not alone.",
 "|Nigeria's commitment to the liberation of Africa could best be seen in her policy towards|Namibia and Mozambique|The Congo and Zimbabwe|Sierra Leone and Chad|South Africa and Angola": "Africa was the centrepiece of Nigeria's foreign policy. Nigeria's liberation commitment is best shown by Angola and South Africa. In November 1975 the Murtala regime recognised the MPLA government of Angola, defying the USA and swinging other African states behind it. Against apartheid South Africa, Nigeria led boycotts (e.g. of the 1976 Olympics), nationalised BP's assets in 1979 over oil sales to South Africa, and funded the ANC. So the answer is D. The trap: Nigeria also supported Namibia, Zimbabwe and Mozambique, but Angola and South Africa were the defining cases. Memory tip: \"Angola Recognised, Apartheid Resisted.\"",
 "|The set of policies on the basis of which countries interact with one another is called|constructive engagement policy|socio-economic policy|national policy|foreign policy": "Foreign policy is the set of principles and goals that guide a country's relations with other countries and international organisations, such as Nigeria's Afrocentric policy, non-alignment and economic diplomacy. So the answer is D. The trap: constructive engagement (A) was a specific policy, the Reagan administration's soft approach to apartheid South Africa in the 1980s, not the general term; national and socio-economic policies (B, C) are about domestic affairs. Memory tip: FOREIGN policy faces outward; domestic policy faces inward.",
 "|Nigeria's membership of the UN complements her foreign policy in the area of|bilateral diplomacy|regional diplomacy|sub-regional diplomacy|multilateral diplomacy": "Diplomacy is classified by how many states take part. Bilateral diplomacy is between two states; sub-regional means ECOWAS; regional means the AU; and multilateral means many states together in global bodies like the UN (193 members) or the Commonwealth. Nigeria joined the UN on 7 October 1960 as the 99th member and pursues goals such as peacekeeping and decolonisation there, so UN membership is multilateral diplomacy: D. The trap: the UN is not \"regional\" (B), because it covers the whole world. Memory tip: Bi = 2, Multi = many; the UN is the biggest \"multi\".",
 "|Nigeria teamed up with other developing countries to assert a neutral posture under the platform of|Nationalist Movement|Pan-African Movement|Non-aligned Movement|Negritude Movement": "During the Cold War, the world was split between the Western bloc (USA) and the Eastern bloc (USSR). Developing countries that refused to take sides formed the Non-Aligned Movement at Belgrade in 1961, inspired by the 1955 Bandung Conference and leaders such as Nehru, Nasser, Tito, Sukarno and Nkrumah. Nigeria adopted non-alignment as a principle of its foreign policy after independence. So the platform was the Non-Aligned Movement: C. The trap: the Pan-African Movement (B) united Africans and people of African descent against colonialism; it was not about Cold War neutrality. Memory tip: NAM = No Alignment with Moscow or Washington.",
 "|Nation-state is synonymous with|self-actualization|sovereignty|liberation|nationalism": "A nation is a people bound by common history, culture or language; a state is a political unit with territory, population, government and sovereignty. A nation-state is where the two coincide: a nation that has its own independent state. What makes it a state rather than just a nation is sovereignty, the supreme power to make and enforce laws within its territory free from outside control. So nation-state is synonymous with sovereignty: B. The trap: nationalism (D) is the feeling or movement that seeks a nation-state, not the state itself; liberation (C) is the struggle that may lead to one. Memory tip: nation = people; state = sovereignty; nation-state = a people with sovereignty.",
 "|A fundamental component of political culture is|social values|family values|community structure|economic values": "Political culture, a term made popular by Gabriel Almond and Sidney Verba (\"The Civic Culture\", 1963), is the pattern of beliefs, attitudes and values that members of a society hold towards government and politics. Values about authority, equality, participation and obedience are therefore its basic building blocks, and they grow out of the wider social values of the society. So a fundamental component of political culture is social values: A. The trap: family values (B) and economic values (D) are narrower; each is only one part of the society's values. Community structure (C) is about how society is organised, not what people believe. Memory tip: political culture = what a society values and believes about power.",
 "|A form of oligarchy in which gifted people are at the helm of affairs is|aristocracy|theocracy|plutocracy|gerontocracy": "Oligarchy means rule by a few. Its forms are named by WHO the few are. In an aristocracy (Greek \"aristos\", best) the few are the best: the most gifted, talented, wise or noble. Plato and Aristotle saw it as the good form of rule by the few. So oligarchy of gifted people is aristocracy: A. The trap: plutocracy (C) is rule by the wealthy, gerontocracy (D) rule by the old, and theocracy (B) rule by priests or in God's name. Each is also rule by a few, but the qualification differs. Memory tip: ARISTocracy = the best (ARISTos); PLUTOcracy = the rich (Pluto, god of wealth); GERONTocracy = the old.",
 "|A state that is ruled by an elected citizen is|a monarchy|a plutocracy|a republic|an empire": "A republic is a state whose head is an ordinary citizen chosen by election, directly by the people or by a legislature or electoral college, for a fixed term, rather than someone who inherits the throne. Nigeria became a republic on 1 October 1963, when Nnamdi Azikiwe, elected by Parliament, replaced the Queen as Head of State. So a state ruled by an elected citizen is a republic: C. A monarchy (A) is headed by a hereditary ruler, an empire (D) by a ruler over many conquered peoples, and a plutocracy (B) is rule by the rich; a republic has an elected head. Memory tip: republic = res publica, \"the public thing\"; its head is elected, not born.",
 "|A true democracy in the modern sense exists where the|elected representatives rule|majority of the people rule|majority of the people vote|elite rules": "Democracy is government of the people, by the people, for the people (Abraham Lincoln, 1863). In ancient Athens citizens ruled directly in the assembly, but modern states have millions of people and cannot all meet to decide. So democracy in the modern sense is representative democracy: citizens elect representatives who govern on their behalf and can be voted out at the next election. That is where elected representatives rule: A. The trap: \"majority of the people rule\" (B) describes direct democracy, which is impossible in a large modern state; merely voting (C) is not enough if the voters' choices do not govern; elite rule (D) is oligarchy. Memory tip: modern democracy = ballots choose the rulers.",
 "|In a parliamentary system, when the legislature passes a vote of no confidence on the executive, it means that the|executive is expected to go on suspension|legislature ceases to trust the executive|executive is required to resign|legislature commences legal proceeding against the executive": "In a parliamentary system (Britain, Nigeria 1960–66) the executive, the prime minister and cabinet, comes from the legislature and stays in office only as long as it has the confidence (majority support) of parliament. This is collective responsibility. If parliament passes a vote of no confidence, the government must resign, or the prime minister may ask the head of state to dissolve parliament and call an election. So the executive is required to resign: C. The trap: B is true but is only the reason for the vote, not what it requires. Legal proceedings (D) belong to impeachment in a presidential system. Memory tip: no confidence = no government.",
 "|The legislative body of the United States of America is the|Parliament|National Assembly|Congress|Council": "The legislature of the United States is called Congress. It is bicameral: the Senate has two senators from each of the 50 states, and the House of Representatives has 435 members shared out by population. Nigeria copied the model in 1979 but named it the National Assembly. So the US legislative body is Congress: C. The trap: National Assembly (B) is Nigeria's name for the same kind of body; Parliament (A) is the British (and Commonwealth parliamentary) name. Memory tip: USA = Congress, UK = Parliament, Nigeria = National Assembly.",
 "|Unicameralism is a feature of the legislature in|Israel|the United States|the United Kingdom|Gambia": "A unicameral legislature has only one chamber. Israel's Knesset (120 members) has one chamber, and so does the National Assembly of The Gambia, which has been single-chamber since independence in 1965. By contrast, the United States Congress (Senate and House of Representatives) and the United Kingdom Parliament (House of Lords and House of Commons) are bicameral. So unicameralism is a feature of Israel: A, and of Gambia as well, so D is also accepted. The United States (B) and the United Kingdom (C) are the textbook examples of two-chamber legislatures; Israel's Knesset and The Gambia's National Assembly have one chamber. Memory tip: one chamber is common in small or unitary states (Israel, Gambia, Ghana); two chambers in big federations and old monarchies.",
 "|The upper house in most federal systems is created to|ensure equality of federating units|prevent excesses of the executive|oversee and check the lower house|enable experienced elders make inputs to governance": "Federations join units of very different sizes. If both houses were based on population, the big units would always outvote the small ones. So most federations create an upper house in which every federating unit has equal representation: two senators for each US state, and in Nigeria three senators for each of the 36 states plus one for the FCT. So the upper house exists to ensure equality of federating units: A. The trap: checking the lower house (C) or the executive (B) are things an upper house does, and the House of Lords includes experienced elders (D), but none is the reason federations create one. Memory tip: Senate = equal seats for unequal states.",
 "|In which of the following systems is the power of the component units more than that of the central government?|Monarchical|Federal|Unitary|Confederal": "The three ways of sharing power between centre and units differ in who is stronger. In a unitary state the centre holds all power and delegates some; in a federation the centre and the units are each supreme in their own sphere; in a confederation sovereign states keep most power and give a weak centre only a few tasks, and they can leave. The USA under the Articles of Confederation (1781–1789) and the Senegambia Confederation (1982–1989) are examples. So the units are stronger than the centre in a confederal system: D. The trap: federal (B) gives the units real but not superior power. Memory tip: unitary = strong centre; federal = shared; confederal = strong units.",
 "|One of the general tenets of fascist doctrine is that the leader is|supreme relative to the constitution|weak relative to the constitution|subordinate to the laws of the state|subordinate to the norms of the society": "Fascism, developed by Benito Mussolini in Italy (from 1922) and copied by Hitler's Nazism in Germany, glorifies the nation and the state and places all authority in one leader (Il Duce, Der Führer). The leader is seen as the embodiment of the nation's will, so his word stands above the constitution and the law; there is no rule of law or limited government. So the fascist leader is supreme relative to the constitution: A. B, C and D describe a leader limited by the constitution, laws and norms, which is constitutionalism; fascism rejects it and places the leader above the constitution. Memory tip: fascism = one nation, one party, one leader above the law.",
 "|In a cabinet system of government, executive power is exercised by the|head of government|monarch|president|dominant party": "A cabinet (parliamentary) system separates the head of state from the head of government. The head of state, a monarch as in Britain or a ceremonial president as in Nigeria's First Republic, only reigns. Real executive power is exercised by the head of government, the prime minister, together with the cabinet, who are drawn from parliament and responsible to it. So executive power is exercised by the head of government: A. The trap: the monarch (B) is the nominal executive in whose name things are done, but has no real power; an executive president (C) belongs to a presidential system. Memory tip: in a cabinet system the Queen reigns, the Prime Minister rules.",
 "|The principle of separation of powers is best practised in the|presidential system|parliamentary system|monarchical system|feudal system": "Separation of powers, set out by Montesquieu in \"The Spirit of the Laws\" (1748), means the legislative, executive and judicial powers are held by different organs and different people. The presidential system (USA; Nigeria since 1979) applies it most fully: the president is elected separately from the legislature, ministers may not be members of the legislature, and each organ has its own source of authority. So separation of powers is best practised in the presidential system: A. The trap: in the parliamentary system (B) ministers sit in parliament, so the executive and legislature are fused. Monarchical and feudal systems (C, D) concentrate power in the ruler. Memory tip: presidential = separated; parliamentary = fused.",
 "|A typical form of delegated legislation is|an act|a bill|a decree|a bye-law": "Delegated legislation is law made not by the legislature itself but by another body, such as a minister, a local government or a statutory corporation, using power delegated to it by an Act of the legislature. Bye-laws (local laws made by local governments and public corporations), orders, rules and regulations are its typical forms. So a typical form of delegated legislation is a bye-law: D. The trap: an Act (A) is law made directly by the legislature, and a bill (B) is a proposed law not yet passed. A decree (C) is a law made by a military government that has seized legislative power, not one delegated to it. Memory tip: the parent Act delegates; the bye-law is its child.",
 "|The rights of a citizen can be withdrawn by the state if the person|opposes the government violently|leaves the country permanently|is convicted of a serious crime|is pronounced dead": "Citizens' rights, such as personal liberty, freedom of movement and in some countries the vote, are protected by the constitution. A state may lawfully take them away only through due process: when a court tries a person and convicts him of a serious crime, he may lose his liberty (imprisonment) and other rights. So rights can be withdrawn if the person is convicted of a serious crime: C. The trap: opposing the government violently (A) is a crime, but rights may be withdrawn only after a court convicts, not simply because the government says so. Leaving the country (B) does not end citizenship, and death (D) ends rights naturally, not by state action. Memory tip: no conviction, no withdrawal of rights.",
 "|An electoral process in which candidates are selected for elective offices by party members is|primary election|electoral college|bye election|general election": "Before a general election each party must choose one candidate for each office. A primary election is the contest held inside the party, in which party members (directly, or through delegates as in Nigeria's indirect primaries) choose the party's candidate. So selection of candidates by party members is a primary election: A. The trap: the general election (D) is the contest between candidates of different parties; a bye-election (C) fills a seat that falls vacant between general elections; an electoral college (B) is a body that elects someone on behalf of the voters, like the one that elects the US President. Memory tip: primary = first round, inside the party.",
 "|In theory one major advantage of the one-party system is that it|eliminates intra-party conflict|serves as an instrument of national integration|promotes greater mass participation in government|guarantees social justice": "A one-party state allows only one legal party. African leaders such as Julius Nyerere (Tanzania) and Kwame Nkrumah (Ghana) defended it in the 1960s by arguing that many parties would split their new nations along ethnic and regional lines, while a single party would bring all groups together. In theory, then, its major advantage is that it serves as an instrument of national integration: B. The trap: a single party does not eliminate intra-party conflict (A); the quarrels simply move inside the party. Mass participation (C) and social justice (D) are claims critics deny, since there is no real choice. Memory tip: one party, one nation: that was the argument.",
 "|A tactic employed by pressure groups to achieve their objectives is|memorandum|electioneering|propaganda|lobbying": "Pressure groups (interest groups) try to influence government policy without seeking to form the government. Their methods include lobbying, petitions and memoranda, publicity, strikes and demonstrations, and court action. Lobbying, meeting legislators and officials directly to persuade them, is the most typical tactic. So the tactic is lobbying: D. The trap: electioneering (B), campaigning to win elections, is what political parties do, since they seek power; pressure groups do not field candidates. A memorandum (A) is a document sent, and propaganda (C) is one-sided persuasion, but lobbying is the recognised tactic named in every textbook. Memory tip: parties want the seat; pressure groups want the ear.",
 "|Public opinion can be measured through|negotiation|referendum|strike action|rumour": "Public opinion is the collective view of the people on public issues. It can be measured through opinion polls, elections and referendums. A referendum puts a specific question directly to the electorate, so the result shows exactly what the majority thinks on that issue; Britain's 2016 vote on leaving the European Union is a famous example. So public opinion is measured through referendum: B. Strike action (C) expresses one group's views, rumour (D) is unreliable and negotiation (A) settles disputes; a referendum measures the whole public's opinion. Memory tip: to know what people think, ask them: referendum, poll or election.",
 "|Which of the following is the main function of the civil service?|Implementing government policies|Allocating resources to the federating units|Supporting the party in power|Mobilizing grass root support for government": "The civil service is the body of permanent, professional officials in government ministries and departments. Ministers make policy; civil servants advise them and then carry out, or implement, the policies. It is expected to be neutral (anonymous and non-partisan), serving any party in power equally. So its main function is implementing government policies: A. Supporting the party in power (C) and mobilizing grassroots support (D) are partisan tasks a neutral civil service must avoid, and revenue sharing (B) is done by law and the RMAFC. Memory tip: politicians decide; civil servants deliver.",
 "|Who was the political head of the Old Oyo Empire?|Bashorun|Oyomesi|Aremo|Alaafin": "The Old Oyo Empire was ruled by the Alaafin (the \"owner of the palace\"), the king and political head, believed to be the companion of the gods. His power was checked by the Oyo Mesi, a council of seven kingmakers led by the Bashorun, who could ask him to commit suicide by presenting an empty calabash or parrot's eggs; the Ogboni society in turn checked the Oyo Mesi. So the political head was the Alaafin: D. The trap: the Bashorun (A) was powerful as head of the Oyo Mesi and even acted as regent, but he was not the head; the Aremo (C) was the crown prince. Memory tip: Alaafin rules; Oyo Mesi checks; Ogboni checks the checkers.",
 "|The Igbo political system was based on|age grades|Umunna|family ties|Umuada": "Pre-colonial Igbo society was acephalous (segmentary): there was no central king. The basic unit was the family, and families of common descent formed lineages and villages. Each family was headed by its eldest man, the Okpara, who held the ofo staff, and village decisions were taken by assemblies of family heads and adult men. So the Igbo political system was based on family (kinship) ties: C. The trap: the Umunna (B) is itself the patrilineal kinship group, one expression of these family ties, so it points the same way; age grades (A) and the Umuada (D, married daughters of the lineage) carried out important tasks but were not the base of the system. Memory tip: Igbo politics starts at home, with the family.",
 "|The Aro system in Igboland was|a religious organization|a political organization|a commercial organization|an imperial organization": "The Aro of Arochukwu built their influence on the Ibini Ukpabi oracle, the \"Long Juju\". People from all over Igboland and beyond came to it as the final court of appeal, and Aro agents spread across the land to bring cases and pilgrims to it. Its authority came from religious belief, so the Aro system was a religious organization: A. The British destroyed the oracle in the Aro Expedition of 1901–02. The trap: the Aro also ran a large trade network, including the slave trade (C), but that trade grew from the oracle's religious hold. Memory tip: Aro = oracle = religion.",
 "|France introduced the policy of assimilation in her colonies primarily to|teach them the art of leadership|give them a sound education|change their way of life|discourage them from ritual killings": "France ruled its colonies (Senegal, Ivory Coast and others) through assimilation: Africans were to become \"black Frenchmen\" by adopting the French language, law, religion, dress and culture. An African who did so could become a French citizen (an \"évolué\") and even sit in the French parliament, as Blaise Diagne of Senegal did in 1914. So assimilation was introduced primarily to change their way of life: C. The trap: education (B) was used as a tool of assimilation, but it was the means, not the purpose. Leadership training (A) and stopping ritual killings (D) were not the aim. Memory tip: assimilate = make similar, i.e. make Africans French.",
 "|The foremost British trading company on the West African coast was|Royal Niger Company|United African Company|Lever Brothers|John Holt and Sons": "The United African Company (UAC) was formed in 1929 when the Niger Company (by then owned by Lever Brothers) merged with the African and Eastern Trade Corporation. It became the biggest British trading firm on the West African coast, dominating the export of palm produce and cocoa and the import of manufactured goods throughout the colonial period. So the foremost British trading company was the UAC: B. The trap: the Royal Niger Company (A) was important earlier, ruling the Niger area under a charter from 1886 until 1900, but its trade later passed into the UAC. Lever Brothers (C) was the parent company; John Holt (D) was smaller. Memory tip: RNC ruled, UAC traded.",
 "|Which of these rulers resisted colonial rule and was deported to Calabar?|King Kosoko|King Dosunmu|Oba Ovonramwen|King Jaja": "Oba Ovonramwen Nogbaisi of Benin tried to keep the British out of his kingdom. After the killing of Acting Consul James Phillips's party in January 1897, the British launched a punitive expedition, sacked Benin City in February 1897 and looted the Benin Bronzes. Ovonramwen was tried and deported to Calabar, where he died in 1914. So the ruler deported to Calabar was Oba Ovonramwen: C. The trap: King Jaja of Opobo (D) also resisted and was deported in 1887, but to the Gold Coast and then the West Indies, not Calabar. Kosoko and Dosunmu (A, B) were Lagos kings; Dosunmu ceded Lagos in 1861. Memory tip: Benin's Oba went to Calabar; Opobo's Jaja went overseas.",
 "|Nigerian nationalism was described as two-phased by|John Payne Jackson|Edward Wilmot Blyden|James S. Coleman|David Ricardo": "The American political scientist James S. Coleman wrote \"Nigeria: Background to Nationalism\" (1958), a classic study of the rise of Nigerian nationalism. He divided it into two phases: traditional or primary nationalism, the early resistance of traditional rulers and peoples to British conquest, and modern nationalism, the organised movement for self-government led by educated Nigerians through newspapers and parties. So the scholar was James S. Coleman: C. The trap: Edward Wilmot Blyden (B) was a 19th-century Pan-Africanist thinker who inspired West African nationalists, but he did not analyse Nigerian nationalism in phases. John Payne Jackson (A) edited the Lagos Weekly Record; David Ricardo (D) was a British economist. Memory tip: Coleman wrote the Background.",
 "|One major weakness of the Independence Constitution is that it|failed to provide the country with full sovereignty|gave total independence to Nigeria|gave full powers to the Supreme Court in Nigeria|empowered Britain to continue to rule": "Nigeria became independent on 1 October 1960, but the Independence Constitution left two links with Britain. Queen Elizabeth II remained Head of State, represented in Nigeria by a Governor-General (Nnamdi Azikiwe from November 1960), and the Judicial Committee of the Privy Council in London remained Nigeria's final court of appeal. So it failed to provide the country with full sovereignty: A. These links ended with the 1963 Republican Constitution. The trap: D goes too far: Britain did not continue to rule, since Nigerians governed through the Balewa government. B is the opposite of a weakness, and C is false because the Privy Council stood above the Supreme Court. Memory tip: 1960 independence; 1963 full sovereignty.",
 "|The first law-making body in Nigeria after amalgamation was|Nigerian Council|National Assembly|Legislative Council|Regional Assembly": "After the 1914 amalgamation Lugard set up the Nigerian Council, but it was purely advisory: it could discuss matters but had no power to make laws, and leading members seldom attended. Laws were made by the Lagos Legislative Council (for the Colony) and by the Governor's proclamations. The Clifford Constitution of 1922 then created a new Legislative Council for Lagos and the Southern Provinces, with Nigeria's first elected members. So the first law-making body was the Legislative Council: C. The trap: the Nigerian Council (A) came first in time, but it was not a law-making body. Memory tip: 1914 Nigerian Council talked; the Legislative Council legislated.",
 "|The designation of ministers as chief executives and accounting officers was recommended by a commission headed by|Jerome Udoji|S.J. Cookey|Simeon Adebo|Dotun Phillips": "Nigeria's civil service was reformed by several commissions. The Dotun Phillips panel (1985), put into effect by Decree No. 43 of 1988 under Babangida, made each minister the chief executive and accounting officer of his ministry, renamed the Permanent Secretary Director-General, and made that post political, so it would end with the government that appointed it. So the commission was headed by Dotun Phillips: D. The trap: Jerome Udoji (A) chaired the 1972–74 commission that introduced grade levels 1–17 and the \"Udoji award\"; Simeon Adebo (C) chaired the 1971 wages commission. Memory tip: Udoji = grade levels; Phillips = ministers in charge and Directors-General.",
 "|A major shortcoming of the Ombudsman is|lack of adequate resources|lack of clear-cut mandate|its inability to restrain bureaucratic excesses|lack of executive power to enforce decisions": "The Ombudsman, an office from Sweden (1809), investigates citizens' complaints of injustice or maladministration by public officials. Nigeria's version is the Public Complaints Commission, set up in 1975. It can investigate and recommend that a wrong be put right, but it has no executive power to enforce its decisions: it must rely on the officials or government concerned to act. So its major shortcoming is lack of executive power to enforce decisions: D. The trap: C is the RESULT of this weakness, since bureaucratic excesses go unchecked because it cannot enforce; lack of resources (A) is a problem, but not the defining one. Memory tip: the Ombudsman can bark but cannot bite.",
 "|One of the strong points of the multi-party Nigeria's Fourth Republic is|the provision for a bicameral legislature|wider political participation|government interference|wider anti-democracy campaign": "Nigeria's Fourth Republic began on 29 May 1999. Unlike the two-party arrangement of the aborted Third Republic (SDP and NRC), it allows many political parties to be registered. More parties mean more choices for voters and more avenues for groups to organise, contest elections and take part in politics. So a strong point is wider political participation: B. A bicameral legislature (A) has nothing to do with the number of parties, and government interference (C) and anti-democracy campaigns (D) are weaknesses; multiparty politics widens participation. Memory tip: multi-party = many doors into politics.",
 "|In which of the following is the ceremonial and executive powers fused?|Presidential system of government|Parliamentary system of government|Federal system of government|Unitary system of government": "In a presidential system the president combines two roles: head of state (ceremonial: receiving ambassadors, national symbol) and head of government (executive: running the government). Nigeria's president since 1979 and the US president hold both. In a parliamentary system the roles are separate: a monarch or ceremonial president is head of state and a prime minister is head of government, as in Nigeria in 1963–66 (Azikiwe and Balewa). So the powers are fused in the presidential system: A. The trap: \"fused\" makes people think of the parliamentary system (B), but there it is the EXECUTIVE and LEGISLATURE that are fused, not the ceremonial and executive roles. Memory tip: presidential = two hats, one head.",
 "|A major contentious issue confronting Nigerian federalism is|poverty|education|health care delivery|revenue allocation": "Since the Richards Constitution of 1946, how to share the revenue collected by the federal government among the federal, state and local governments, and by which principles (derivation, population, equality of states, need), has been the most contentious issue in Nigerian federalism. Commissions from Phillipson (1946) and Raisman (1958) to Aboyade (1977) and Okigbo (1980) have tried to settle it, and oil-producing areas still demand \"resource control\". So the contentious issue is revenue allocation: D. Poverty, education and health care (A, B, C) are serious national problems, but they are not disputes about how the federation itself works, as revenue allocation is. Memory tip: in Nigerian federalism, the fight is over the money.",
 "|The main purpose of establishing public enterprises in Nigeria is to|increase government revenue|provide essential services|enrich the elite|compete with the private sector": "Public enterprises (public corporations and government-owned companies) were set up in Nigeria, such as NEPA (electricity), the Nigerian Railway Corporation and NITEL, because essential services needed huge capital, were too important to be left to profit-seeking private firms, or had to reach people who could not pay full cost. So the main purpose was to provide essential services: B. The trap: increasing government revenue (A) can be a by-product, but profit was not the main aim; many ran at a loss and needed subsidies. Competing with the private sector (D) was not the purpose, and enriching the elite (C) is an abuse. Memory tip: public enterprise = public service first.",
 "|Parastatals are established to|enhance entrepreneurial skills|maximize government profits|expand business transactions|render social services": "Parastatals are government-owned agencies and corporations with a degree of independence from the ministries, such as power, water, broadcasting and transport bodies. They are established mainly to render social services, essential services to the public, at reasonable cost, not to make the most money. So parastatals are established to render social services: D. The trap: maximising government profits (B) sounds businesslike, but service, not profit, is their main goal, which is why many were later commercialised or privatised. Enhancing entrepreneurial skills (A) and expanding business (C) are aims of private enterprise. Memory tip: parastatal = state service with a business face.",
 "|The General-Purpose Committee of the local government is the|cabinet of the local government|local government public relations unit|body responsible for supervising self-help projects|body for awarding contracts": "Under the 1976 Local Government Reform each council had a chairman, supervisory councillors and a secretary. Together they formed the General Purpose Committee, the executive arm of the local government. It made policy, prepared the budget, coordinated departments and advised the council, just as the cabinet does at federal level. So the General Purpose Committee is the cabinet of the local government: A. Supervising self-help projects (C) and awarding contracts (D) are tasks the committee may oversee, and public relations (B) is a minor unit; the General-Purpose Committee acts as the council's cabinet. Memory tip: GPC = the local cabinet.",
 "|The highest organ of the state during the Babangida Regime was the|Provisional Ruling Council|Supreme Military Council|Armed Forces Ruling Council|Federal Executive Council": "Each military regime named its supreme ruling body. Gowon and Murtala/Obasanjo had the Supreme Military Council (SMC), and Buhari (1984–85) kept the SMC. When Babangida seized power in August 1985 he replaced it with the Armed Forces Ruling Council (AFRC), which was the highest organ of state until 1993. So under Babangida it was the Armed Forces Ruling Council: C. The trap: the Provisional Ruling Council (A) was Abacha's body (1993–98), kept by Abdulsalami Abubakar; the Supreme Military Council (B) belonged to earlier regimes; the Federal Executive Council (D) was the cabinet below the AFRC. Memory tip: SMC, then AFRC (Babangida), then PRC (Abacha).",
 "|Decree 34 of 1966 was unacceptable to many Nigerians because it was|seen as an instrument of impoverishment|perceived to abolish the federal system|promulgated without consultation with the people|considered as alien": "After the January 1966 coup, Major-General Aguiyi-Ironsi issued the Unification Decree (Decree No. 34) on 24 May 1966. It abolished the four regions, turned them into \"groups of provinces\" and unified the regional civil services, making Nigeria a unitary state. Many Nigerians, especially in the North, feared domination and saw it as the end of the federal system, and riots followed; it helped bring about the counter-coup of 29 July 1966. So it was perceived to abolish the federal system: B. The trap: C is true of all military decrees, so it does not explain why THIS decree was so opposed. Memory tip: Decree 34 unified; Gowon restored federalism (Decree 8 and 12 states, 1967).",
 "|Nigeria broke diplomatic relations with France in 1961 because of|France's atomic test in the Sahara Desert|General de-Gaulle's negative attitude towards her|her poor relations with the Francophone countries|France's diplomatic relations with Israel": "France tested its first atomic bomb in the Sahara (at Reggane, Algeria) on 13 February 1960, with further tests that year, close to West Africa. Nigeria protested strongly, and after the third test in December 1960 the Balewa government broke diplomatic relations with France in January 1961 and closed its ports and airports to French ships and aircraft. So it was because of France's atomic test in the Sahara Desert: A. De Gaulle's attitude (B) and Francophone ties (C) were later issues, such as France's support for Biafra in 1967–70; the 1961 break was over France's atomic tests in the Sahara. Memory tip: 1960 bomb in the Sahara, 1961 break with Paris.",
 "|An attribute that Nigeria shares with most non-aligned countries is|the state of her economy|her heterogeneous population|her large population|her large size": "The Non-Aligned Movement (founded in Belgrade in 1961) is made up mostly of former colonies in Africa, Asia and Latin America. What most of them share with Nigeria is the state of their economies: developing, dependent on exporting raw materials such as oil, cocoa or copper, and seeking economic independence. So the shared attribute is the state of her economy: A. A large population (C) and large size (D) set Nigeria apart from most non-aligned states, many of which are small; what it shares with them is a developing economy. Memory tip: what unites the non-aligned is underdevelopment, not size.",
 "|Nigeria spearheaded the formation of ECOWAS during the regime of|Olusegun Obasanjo|Yakubu Gowon|Murtala Muhammed|Ibrahim Babangida": "The Economic Community of West African States was created by the Treaty of Lagos, signed on 28 May 1975 by 15 West African states. Nigeria's head of state, General Yakubu Gowon, drove the project together with Togo's President Gnassingbé Eyadema, and Nigeria offered to bear much of the cost. Two months later, in July 1975, Gowon was overthrown. So ECOWAS was formed under Gowon: B. The trap: Murtala Muhammed (C) took over in July 1975, after the treaty was signed; Obasanjo (A) followed in 1976 and, much later as civilian president, helped turn ECOWAS into a Commission. Memory tip: Gowon signed in Lagos, May 1975.",
 "|Nigeria was classified as a frontline state for|participating in peacekeeping in the Congo|supporting the liberation efforts in Southern Africa|spearheading the formation of the African Union|helping to end the crisis in Liberia": "The \"frontline states\" were the Southern African countries bordering white-ruled territories (Tanzania, Zambia, Botswana, Mozambique, Angola and later Zimbabwe) which supported the liberation struggle. Nigeria is thousands of kilometres away, but from the 1970s it gave so much money, diplomatic support and help to movements in Angola, Mozambique, Zimbabwe, Namibia and South Africa that it was treated as an honorary frontline state. So it was for supporting the liberation efforts in Southern Africa: B. Peacekeeping in the Congo (A, 1960–64) and Liberia (D, from 1990) and the AU's formation (C) had nothing to do with Southern Africa; frontline status came from backing liberation struggles there. Memory tip: frontline = the front against apartheid.",
 "|The prominent role Nigeria played in the UN in the 70's earned her|non-permanent membership position|membership of the Security Council|permanent representation at the UN|chairmanship of the General Assembly": "The UN Security Council has 5 permanent members and (since 1965) 10 non-permanent members elected for two years. In the 1970s Nigeria was very active at the UN, especially in the fight against apartheid (Nigerians chaired the UN Special Committee against Apartheid from 1972), and it was elected to the Security Council for 1978–1979. So its role earned it membership of the Security Council: B. Option A, a non-permanent membership position, describes the same Security Council seat, so A and B are both accepted. Nigeria had a permanent representative (C) from admission in 1960, as every member has, so that was not earned. Chairmanship of the General Assembly (D) came in 1989 (Joseph Garba). Memory tip: 1970s activism, a Council seat for 1978–79.",
 "|The head of Nigeria's foreign mission in a Commonwealth nation is known as|high commissioner|charge d'affaires|ambassador|attaché": "Commonwealth countries regard one another as members of one family, so they do not exchange ambassadors. The head of a diplomatic mission from one Commonwealth country to another is called a High Commissioner, and the mission is a High Commission. So Nigeria's envoy in London, Accra or New Delhi is a high commissioner: A. The trap: an ambassador (C) is the title for the head of a mission in a non-Commonwealth country, such as Nigeria's ambassador in Paris or Washington. A chargé d'affaires (B) runs an embassy when there is no ambassador, and an attaché (D) is a junior specialist officer. Memory tip: Commonwealth = High Commissioner; everyone else = Ambassador.",
 "|One major function of the Authority of Heads of State and Government of ECOWAS is|organizing international conferences|appointing the Executive Secretary|appointing staff of the Secretariat|preparing the budget of the Community": "Under the 1975 ECOWAS Treaty, the Authority of Heads of State and Government is the supreme organ. It directs the Community's policy and appoints its top officer, which was then the Executive Secretary. So a major function of the Authority was appointing the Executive Secretary: B. Today the Executive Secretariat has been replaced (since 2007) by the ECOWAS Commission, whose President the Authority appoints. The trap: appointing staff of the Secretariat (C) was the job of the Executive Secretary, not of the heads of state; the budget (D) was prepared by the Secretariat and considered by the Council of Ministers. Memory tip: heads of state choose the chief; the chief chooses the staff.",
 "|The tenure of non-permanent members of the Security Council is|5 years|2 years|4 years|6 years": "The UN Security Council has 15 members. Five are permanent (USA, Russia, China, Britain, France), and ten are non-permanent members elected by the General Assembly for two-year terms, five each year, and they cannot be re-elected immediately. So the tenure is 2 years: B. Nigeria has served several such terms, for example 1978–79 and 2014–15. The trap: 5 years (A) is the usual term of the UN Secretary-General; 4 years (C) is the term of Nigeria's president and governors; 6 years (D) is not a UN term. Memory tip: non-permanent means short: two years on the Council.",
 "|The Secretary-General of the United Nations is appointed by the|Security Council acting alone|General Assembly on the recommendation of the Security Council|Permanent members of the Security Council on the recommendation of the General Assembly|General Assembly in plenary session": "Article 97 of the UN Charter says the Secretary-General \"shall be appointed by the General Assembly upon the recommendation of the Security Council\". The Council chooses a candidate (and the five permanent members can veto), then the General Assembly formally appoints. The term is usually five years. So the Secretary-General is appointed by the General Assembly on the recommendation of the Security Council: B. The trap: the Security Council acting alone (A) only recommends; option C reverses the two bodies; the General Assembly alone (D) cannot appoint without a Council recommendation. Memory tip: Council proposes, Assembly appoints.",
 "|The approval of treaties and agreements of the Economic Community of West African States is the responsibility of the|secretariat|ECOWAS Tribunal|Council of Ministers|Assembly of Heads of State and Government": "The ECOWAS institutions under the 1975 treaty were the Authority of Heads of State and Government, the Council of Ministers, the Executive Secretariat, the Tribunal and specialised commissions. The Authority is the supreme organ: it takes the final decisions, including approving the Community's treaties, protocols and agreements, while the Council of Ministers advises it and the Secretariat implements. So approval of treaties and agreements is the responsibility of the Assembly (Authority) of Heads of State and Government: D. The trap: the Council of Ministers (C) prepares and recommends, but it is the heads of state who approve. Memory tip: in ECOWAS the final word is with the heads of state.",
 "|The development of attitudes and beliefs about a political system is|political emancipation|political socialization|political participation|political orientation": "Nobody is born with political views. We learn them, from family first, then school, peer groups, religious bodies, the mass media and political parties. This learning process, by which people come to hold attitudes, beliefs and values about the political system, is called political socialisation, and it goes on all through life. So the development of attitudes and beliefs about a political system is political socialisation: B. The trap: political orientation (D) sounds close, but orientation is the outlook a person ends up with, while socialisation is the process that develops it. Memory tip: socialisation = the \"school\" of politics; culture = what the school teaches.",
 "|Political behaviour is governed by|political socialization|political ideology|political economy|political culture": "Political culture is the set of shared beliefs, attitudes, values and norms that a society holds about government and politics. It tells people what is normal and acceptable: whether to vote, to obey or to protest, how to see rulers. That is why political behaviour, the way people actually act in politics, is governed by political culture: D. The trap: political socialisation (A) is the process by which people learn the culture; it passes the culture on, but the rules that guide behaviour are the culture itself. Memory tip: socialisation is the teacher, culture is the lesson, behaviour is the result.",
 "|In a nation, sovereignty is vested in the|community|state|elite|electorate": "Sovereignty is supreme power within a territory. Lawyers say legal sovereignty lies with the body that makes the final law (parliament or the constitution), but political sovereignty, the power behind it, lies with the people. In a democratic nation the people exercise that power through the electorate: the voters who choose the government and can remove it at the next election. So in a nation sovereignty is vested in the electorate: D. The trap: the state (B) is the political unit that possesses sovereignty in relation to other states, but the question asks where the power ultimately rests inside the nation, and that is with its voters. Memory tip: \"power to the people\" means power to the ballot box.",
 "|Which of the following is a feature of democracy?|Interdependence of states|State responsibilities to society|Power vested in minority parties|Popular consultation": "Democracy means government by the people. Because the people cannot all govern directly, they are consulted: through regular, free and fair elections, referendums, public hearings and free debate in the press. Popular consultation, asking the people and acting on their wishes, is therefore a basic feature of democracy: D. State responsibilities to society (B) exist under every government, even a dictatorship, and power vested in minority parties (C) is the opposite of majority rule; popular consultation marks democracy. Memory tip: democracy asks the people (consults them); dictatorship tells them.",
 "|Private ownership of the means of production is central to|fascism|feudalism|capitalism|communism": "Capitalism is the economic system in which land, factories, banks and other means of production are privately owned, and production is for profit in a market guided by supply and demand. Private ownership is its central feature. So it is capitalism: C. The trap: feudalism (B) also had private holding of land, but it was land held in a chain of obligations from the king downward, not a free market of private owners; communism (D) is the reverse of capitalism, with all means of production owned in common. Memory tip: CAPITALism = private CAPITAL; COMMunism = COMMon ownership.",
 "|A system based on hierarchies of land ownership is|feudalism|totalitarianism|communism|fascism": "Feudalism was the system of medieval Europe. The king owned all land and granted fiefs to great lords in return for military service; the lords granted land to lesser vassals (knights), and at the bottom serfs worked the land in return for protection. Status and power came from one's place in this hierarchy of land holding. So a system based on hierarchies of land ownership is feudalism: A. Totalitarianism (B) and fascism (D) are hierarchies of political control, and communism (C) abolishes private land ownership; feudalism rests on hierarchies of land holding. Memory tip: FEUDalism comes from \"feud\" or \"fief\": land granted in return for service.",
 "|Which of the following performs quasi-legislative functions?|The Judiciary|The Traditional Institutions|The Civil Service|The Executive": "\"Quasi\" means \"as if\" or \"almost\". A body performs quasi-legislative functions when it makes rules that work like laws although it is not the legislature. The executive does exactly this: ministers make regulations and orders under powers delegated by Acts (delegated legislation), and presidents issue executive orders; under military rule the executive even made decrees. So it is the executive: D. The judiciary (A) interprets law rather than making it; the executive performs quasi-legislative functions through delegated legislation and regulations. Memory tip: quasi-legislative = the executive making rules; quasi-judicial = tribunals and panels deciding cases outside the courts.",
 "|A major weakness of confederation is|over-concentration of authority|tendency towards secession|lack of local independence|lack of common currency": "A confederation is a union of sovereign states that create a weak central body for limited common purposes, as in the USA under the Articles of Confederation (1781–89) or the Senegambia Confederation (1982–89). Because the member states keep their sovereignty and the centre depends on them, a member that is unhappy can simply leave. Senegambia broke up when The Gambia pulled away. So a major weakness is the tendency towards secession: B. The trap: over-concentration of authority (A) is the weakness of a unitary state, not a confederation, where power is too scattered. Memory tip: CONfederation = CONvenient exit; the members keep the keys.",
 "|Members of a parliament are required to report the proceedings of the house to their|constituencies|local government chairmen|traditional rulers|political parties": "A legislator is the elected representative of a constituency, the geographical area whose voters chose him or her. Representation is a two-way duty: carrying the people's views to the house, and reporting back to them what the house has debated and decided, often through town-hall meetings. So members of parliament must report proceedings to their constituencies: A. The trap: political parties (D) sponsor candidates and expect loyalty, but a member sits in the house for the people who elected him or her, not for the party. Memory tip: MPs answer to the people who vote; the constituency is the boss.",
 "|Which of the following countries is a unitary state?|Nigeria|India|United States of America|Ghana": "In a unitary state all power belongs to one central government, which may create or abolish regional and local units and give them only the powers it chooses. Ghana is a unitary republic: its 16 regions and its district assemblies have no constitutionally guaranteed share of sovereignty. So it is Ghana: D. The trap: Nigeria (A) may look centralised after years of military rule, but its constitution divides power between the federal government and 36 states, so it is a federation, like India and the USA. Memory tip: Ghana one centre; Nigeria, India, USA: power shared by constitution.",
 "|Ending a session of parliament by royal proclamation means the|expiration of parliament|prorogation of parliament|adjournment of parliament|dissolution of parliament": "In the Westminster system, parliament meets in sessions (usually yearly). Prorogation is the formal ending of a session by royal proclamation, on the prime minister's advice: business stops, but parliament still exists and returns for a new session. Dissolution is different: it ends the life of the parliament itself, all seats become vacant and a general election follows. So ending a session by royal proclamation is prorogation: B. The trap: dissolution (D) is also done by proclamation, but it ends the parliament, not just a session; adjournment (C) is a short break within a session decided by the house itself. Memory tip: adjourn = a break; prorogue = end the session; dissolve = end the parliament.",
 "|A main feature of the parliamentary system is that|the executive consists of all-party members|judges are drawn from the ruling party|electoral commissioners leave at the end of their tenure|the executive is appointed by the legislature": "In a parliamentary system (Britain, and Nigeria in 1960–66) there is no separately elected executive. After an election, the leader of the party with a majority in parliament is appointed prime minister, chooses ministers from among parliament's members, and the cabinet stays in office only while it keeps parliament's confidence. In this sense the executive is made by, and drawn from, the legislature: D. An all-party executive (A) happens only in special coalitions such as a government of national unity; normally the majority party in the legislature forms the cabinet. Memory tip: parliamentary = the executive is born in parliament and dies by a vote of no confidence.",
 "|In a socialist economy, private accumulation of wealth is|prohibited|regulated|limited|encouraged": "Socialism aims at equality. The main means of production (land, factories, mines, banks) are owned by the state on behalf of the people, and income is shared according to work or need. Private accumulation of wealth, building up private fortunes through owning means of production and employing others for profit, is therefore not allowed. So it is prohibited: A. The trap: \"regulated\" (B) or \"limited\" (C) describe a mixed economy or welfare state such as Britain's, where private wealth is permitted but taxed and controlled. Memory tip: capitalism encourages private wealth, a mixed economy regulates it, socialism prohibits it.",
 "|The earliest classification of constitutions was the work of|Aristotle|J.J. Rousseau|K.C. Wheare|Plato": "Aristotle (384–322 BC), the Greek philosopher often called the father of political science, studied the constitutions of about 158 Greek city-states. He classified them by the number who rule and whose interest they serve: monarchy, aristocracy and polity (good forms), and tyranny, oligarchy and democracy (bad forms). This is the earliest classification of constitutions: A. The trap: Plato (D), Aristotle's teacher, wrote about the ideal state in \"The Republic\" but did not make the systematic classification; K.C. Wheare (C) classified constitutions in the twentieth century (written/unwritten, rigid/flexible). Memory tip: Aristotle counted the rulers: one, few, many.",
 "|Constitutionalism refers to|the process of drafting a constitution|amendment of an existing constitution|the process of operating a constitution|strict adherence to a constitution": "A constitution is a document; constitutionalism is a practice. It means that government is limited by the constitution and that rulers and citizens strictly obey its provisions: power is exercised only as the constitution permits, and actions outside it are void. So constitutionalism is strict adherence to a constitution: D. The trap: \"the process of operating a constitution\" (C) sounds similar, but a constitution can be operated badly; constitutionalism requires faithful obedience to it, not merely using it. Memory tip: constitution = the rulebook; constitutionalism = playing by the rulebook.",
 "|An advantage of delegated legislation is that|much time is saved in the process|technical issues are handled by experts|ministers and lawmakers work together|it hastens the implementation of policy": "Delegated legislation is law made by ministers, agencies or local councils under powers given by an Act. Parliament passes the general principle and lets experts write the details. Its advantages are well known: technical matters (drug standards, aviation rules) are handled by experts (B); the legislature saves time for major policy (A); and rules can be made and changed quickly, so policy takes effect faster (D). The answer key gives expert handling of technical issues: B. A and D are also correct, so all three are accepted. \"Ministers and lawmakers work together\" (C) is not an advantage; in delegated legislation the legislature hands detailed rule-making over to experts rather than sharing it. Memory tip: delegated legislation is quick, expert and time-saving, but weakens legislative control.",
 "|One essential duty of a citizen to his state is to|support the government in power|recite the pledge|pay his tax|encourage other citizens": "Citizenship brings rights and duties. A citizen's duties to the state include obeying the law, paying taxes, voting, defending the country and respecting national symbols. Paying tax is essential because tax pays for roads, schools, security and every other service the state provides. So it is to pay his tax: C. The trap: \"support the government in power\" (A) is not a duty; in a democracy citizens may lawfully oppose the government and vote it out. The duty is loyalty to the state, not to whichever party rules. Memory tip: no tax, no state services: tax is the citizen's share of the bill.",
 "|Franchise in an electoral process means the|right to vote|ownership of means of production|sovereignty of a nation|rights and duties of a citizen": "Franchise, also called suffrage, is the right to vote in elections. Universal adult suffrage gives every qualified adult the vote; restricted franchise limits it by sex, property, income or education, as the 1922 Clifford Constitution did (only men in Lagos and Calabar earning £100 a year). So franchise means the right to vote: A. The trap: \"rights and duties of a citizen\" (D) is too broad; voting is only one of a citizen's many rights. Memory tip: franchise = the vote; universal suffrage = the vote for all adults.",
 "|The type of party system in practice is defined by the|relationship between the parties and electorate|structure of the political parties|manner in which the parties operate|number of political parties in a country": "Political scientists classify party systems by counting: one-party (e.g. Tanzania under TANU/CCM until 1992), two-party (USA, Nigeria's Third Republic with SDP and NRC), and multi-party (Nigeria's First, Second and Fourth Republics). What defines the type of party system is the number of political parties in a country that effectively compete for power: D. The trap: the structure of the parties (B) describes how a party is organised internally (branches, executives), not what kind of party system the country has. Memory tip: party SYSTEM = how many; party STRUCTURE = how organised.",
 "|Pressure groups harmonize different individual concerns through|interest formulation|interest manipulation|interest mobilisation|interest aggregation": "Many individuals have many different concerns. Interest aggregation is the process of combining these many demands into a smaller set of common demands or policy positions that can be presented to government with one voice. Pressure groups (and parties) do this: a union turns thousands of workers' complaints into one set of demands. So they harmonise different concerns through interest aggregation: D. The trap: interest mobilisation (C) is rallying people to act (strikes, rallies), which comes after the demands have been harmonised. Memory tip: aggregate = add together; harmonising many voices into one demand is aggregation.",
 "|Opinion polls are organized to find out the|benefits derived by people from government|people's thought about a particular government policy|people's expectations from the government|feelings of people about particular issues and policies": "An opinion poll asks a sample of the population questions in order to measure public opinion: how people feel and think about current issues, government policies, leaders and candidates. Its scope is wide, covering any issue or policy, not one. So opinion polls find out the feelings of people about particular issues and policies: D. The trap: \"people's thought about a particular government policy\" (B) is too narrow; polls cover parties, candidates and social issues as well as single policies. Memory tip: a poll is a sample that takes the public's temperature on issues.",
 "|In pre-colonial Igbo-land, autocratic rule was made difficult by the|fear of dethronement|absence of a centralized system of authority|pressure from age grades|activities of cult societies": "Pre-colonial Igbo society was largely acephalous: most communities had no king or central ruler. Authority was spread among the council of elders (Ofo holders), the village assembly where every adult male could speak, age grades, title societies such as Ozo, and the oracles. With no central throne to seize, no single person could become an autocrat. So autocratic rule was difficult because of the absence of a centralised system of authority: B. The trap: pressure from age grades (C) was real, but age grades were only one of many groups that shared power; the deeper reason was that power was never centralised. Memory tip: no head (acephalous), no dictator.",
 "|The Yoruba traditional system of government was|republican|democratic|monarchical|egalitarian": "The pre-colonial Yoruba kingdoms were monarchies. In Oyo the Alaafin was head of the kingdom, regarded as sacred, but his power was checked: the Oyomesi (seven kingmakers led by the Bashorun) could ask him to commit suicide, the Ogboni society checked the Oyomesi, and the Are-Ona-Kakanfo led the army. This makes it a constitutional monarchy. So the system was monarchical: C. The trap: \"democratic\" (B) is tempting because of the checks on the Oba, but the people did not elect their rulers; the Alaafin came from royal lineages. Memory tip: Oba = king, so Yoruba = monarchy with brakes.",
 "|Under the pre-colonial Sokoto Caliphate system, the next in command to the Sultan was the|Alkali|Galadima|Madaki|Waziri": "After Usman dan Fodio's jihad (1804), the Sokoto Caliphate was ruled by the Sultan (Sarkin Musulmi) through emirs. In the caliphate and each emirate the most senior official after the ruler was the Waziri, the chief adviser and head of administration, like a prime minister. Other officials were the Galadima (in charge of the capital), the Madaki (army commander), the Ma'aji (treasurer) and the Alkali (judge). So it is the Waziri: D. The trap: the Galadima (B) was very senior and governed the capital, but he ranked below the Waziri. Memory tip: Waziri = vizier = second only to the ruler.",
 "|Which of the following societies was classified as acephalous?|Benin|Ibibio|Igbo|Ijaw": "\"Acephalous\" means \"headless\". An acephalous society has no centralised ruler or kingdom; authority is spread among family heads, elders, age grades and assemblies. The Igbo of south-eastern Nigeria are the standard example. So it is the Igbo: C. The trap: B, the Ibibio, and D, many Ijaw communities, were also largely decentralised, but textbooks name the Igbo as the classic acephalous society; Benin (A) was a centralised kingdom. Memory tip: A-cephalous = no head; Igbo, no king (\"Igbo enwe eze\").",
 "|Indirect rule encouraged|communal integration|exploitation and oppression|inter-communal cooperation|the rise of nationalism": "Under indirect rule, the British ruled through traditional rulers, or through warrant chiefs they created where there were no kings, as in Igboland. These rulers now had British force behind them and answered to the British District Officer, not to the old checks of their own people. Many abused this power: forced labour, illegal levies, harsh courts and corruption. The anger it caused led to the Aba Women's Riot of 1929. So indirect rule encouraged exploitation and oppression: B. The trap: \"the rise of nationalism\" (D) is tempting, but indirect rule was designed to slow nationalism by keeping educated Nigerians out of power, especially in the North. Memory tip: warrant chiefs + British backing = oppression.",
 "|The main achievement of the nationalists in Nigeria was|registration of political parties|economic liberation of the nation|political liberation of the nation|building the nation": "The nationalists (Herbert Macaulay, Nnamdi Azikiwe, Obafemi Awolowo, Ahmadu Bello, Tafawa Balewa and others) fought colonial rule through parties, newspapers, strikes and constitutional conferences. Their great achievement was political freedom: independence from Britain on 1 October 1960. So it was the political liberation of the nation: C. The trap: economic liberation (B) did not come with independence; the economy remained tied to foreign firms and markets, which is why later governments tried indigenisation (1972, 1977). Memory tip: 1960 brought the flag (political freedom), not full control of the economy.",
 "|The major external factor that promoted nationalism in Nigeria was|Pan-Africanism|the Yom-Kippur War|the Second World War|Anti-apartheid Movement": "The Second World War (1939–45) was the biggest external push to Nigerian nationalism. Nigerian soldiers fought in Burma and East Africa and saw that Europeans were not invincible; returning ex-servicemen expected rewards and became political. The Atlantic Charter (1941) spoke of the right of all peoples to self-determination, and the war weakened Britain while the USA and USSR opposed colonialism. So it was the Second World War: C. The trap: Pan-Africanism (A) also inspired nationalists, but it was an idea spread over decades; the war was the major event that sped up the movement. Memory tip: after 1945 came the 1946, 1951 and 1954 constitutions and then independence.",
 "|The presidential system of government was introduced in Nigeria with the Constitution of|1989|1999|1960|1979": "Nigeria's First Republic (1960–66) used the British parliamentary system, with a ceremonial head of state and a prime minister. The Constitution Drafting Committee (1975–76) and the Constituent Assembly produced the 1979 Constitution, which introduced an American-style executive presidency with separation of powers. Alhaji Shehu Shagari became the first executive president on 1 October 1979. So it was the 1979 Constitution: D. The trap: 1960 (C) was the independence constitution, which was parliamentary; 1989 and 1999 kept the presidential system but did not introduce it. Memory tip: 1979 = first executive president (Shagari).",
 "|The Action Group crisis of 1963 led to the formation of|UPP|NEPU|NPC|NCNC": "In 1962 the Action Group split: the party leader Obafemi Awolowo clashed with S.L. Akintola, the Western Region premier, over the party's direction. Fighting broke out in the Western House in May 1962, the federal government declared a state of emergency, and Awolowo was later tried for treason (convicted in 1963). Akintola's faction formed the United People's Party (UPP), which later merged into the NNDP. So the crisis led to the formation of the UPP: A. NEPU (B) was founded by Aminu Kano in 1950, and the NPC (C) and NCNC (D) already existed; Akintola's breakaway from the Action Group produced the UPP. Memory tip: Akintola's breakaway = UPP, later NNDP.",
 "|Under the 1963 Republican Constitution, the power of judicial review was vested in the|President|Chief Justice|Supreme Court|Parliament": "Judicial review is the power of the courts to declare laws or government actions void if they conflict with the constitution. The 1963 Republican Constitution made Nigeria a republic and made the Supreme Court the highest court, ending appeals to the Privy Council in London. The power of judicial review therefore rested with the Supreme Court: C. The trap: the Chief Justice (B) heads the Supreme Court, but the power belongs to the court as an institution, not to one judge. Memory tip: 1963 = republic + Supreme Court supreme (no more Privy Council).",
 "|The rules and regulations of the civil service are called|General Order|Bureaucratic Order|Service Order|Administrative Order": "Civil servants are governed by a written code of rules on appointment, promotion, discipline, leave, allowances and conduct. In Nigeria this code is called the General Orders (GO), inherited from the colonial service; it has since been revised as the Public Service Rules. So the rules and regulations of the civil service are called General Orders: A. The trap: \"Service Order\" (C) and \"Administrative Order\" (D) sound official, but they are not the name of the code. Memory tip: GO = General Orders, the civil servant's rulebook.",
 "|The Code of Conduct Bureau was essentially established to|reduce corruption in public life|protect the rights of public servants|enhance probity and accountability in public service|ensure the independence of the public service": "The Code of Conduct Bureau was created by the 1979 Constitution and kept in the Fifth Schedule of the 1999 Constitution. It enforces the Code of Conduct for public officers: declaring assets on taking and leaving office, no foreign bank accounts, no gifts or bribes, no conflict of interest. Its stated aim is to establish and maintain a high standard of morality, probity and accountability in public service: C. The trap: \"reduce corruption in public life\" (A) is a result of its work, but the Bureau's essential purpose, in its own words, is positive: probity and accountability, of which fighting corruption is one part. Memory tip: CCB = assets declared, probity enforced; the Code of Conduct Tribunal tries offenders.",
 "|Which of the following political parties was the first to be formed when the ban on politics was lifted in 1978?|NPP|PRP|NPN|UPN": "General Obasanjo's government lifted the ban on party politics on 21 September 1978. The very next day, 22 September, Chief Obafemi Awolowo announced the Unity Party of Nigeria (UPN). The NPN, NPP, PRP and GNPP were launched over the following weeks, and FEDECO registered all five in December 1978. So the first party formed was the UPN: D. The answer key gives C (NPN); the NPN grew out of groups in the Constituent Assembly, so some books call it first, but as a party the UPN was launched first. The trap: the NPN (C) became the biggest party and won in 1979, which makes it seem first. Memory tip: Awo was ready on day one: UPN, 22 September 1978.",
 "|The principle of federal character was adopted in order to promote equitable allocation of|positions and appointments among people of various regions|appointments between the North and the South|opportunities between the males and females|revenue between groups in the country": "Federal character is a principle in the 1979 and 1999 Constitutions (section 14(3) of 1999). It requires that the composition of government and its agencies, and the conduct of their affairs, reflect the federal character of Nigeria, so that no few states or ethnic groups dominate. The Federal Character Commission enforces it. So it promotes equitable allocation of positions and appointments among people of various regions: A. The trap: \"between the North and the South\" (B) is too narrow; federal character is about all states and ethnic groups, not a two-way division. Memory tip: federal character = fair shares of jobs for every state.",
 "|The component units of the Nigerian Federation comprise|national assembly, military, police and civil service|constituency, ward, emirate and chiefdom|federal, state, local government and federal capital territory|federal capital territory, national assembly, supreme court and civil service": "A federation is made up of levels of government. Nigeria's 1999 Constitution recognises the federal government, 36 states, 774 local government areas, and the Federal Capital Territory, Abuja, which is administered by the federal government but listed as a unit of the federation. So the component units are federal, state, local government and FCT: C. Options A and D list institutions such as the National Assembly, Supreme Court and civil service, which are organs of government, not units of the federation. Memory tip: units are levels (federal, state, local, FCT); organs are arms (legislature, executive, judiciary).",
 "|In Nigeria, privatization and commercialization policies were introduced to|hand over the control of commercial ventures to citizens|increase the asset base of government|divest government major control of commercial ventures|allow government control of the private sector": "By the 1980s many government-owned enterprises (NEPA, NITEL, the railways, refineries) were running at a loss. Under the Structural Adjustment Programme, the Babangida government's 1988 Privatisation and Commercialisation Decree set up TCPC to sell government shares in some enterprises (privatisation) and make others run on commercial lines without subsidy (commercialisation). The aim was to reduce government's control of commercial ventures: C. The trap: \"hand over control to citizens\" (A) sounds similar, but the policy aimed at withdrawing government, with buyers including foreign and corporate investors, not handing ventures to citizens as such. Memory tip: privatise = sell; commercialise = run like a business; both mean government steps back.",
 "|An example of a public corporation in Nigeria is|National Universities Commission|Nigerian Television Authority|National Population Commission|First Bank of Nigeria": "A public corporation is a body created by statute, owned by government, run by a board and expected to provide a service on business lines while serving the public. The Nigerian Television Authority (NTA, 1977) is one, like NNPC, NEPA and Nigerian Railway Corporation were. So it is NTA: B. The trap: the National Universities Commission (A) is a government agency, but it regulates universities rather than running a commercial service, so it is a regulatory body, not a public corporation. First Bank (D) is a private company. Memory tip: public corporation = government business with a board; regulatory agency = government referee.",
 "|Following the reform of the Native Authority system in Northern Nigeria, traditional rulers became|Council|Chief-and-Council|Prefects|Chief-in-Council": "Under colonial indirect rule, many northern emirs were sole native authorities: they made rules, ran treasuries and courts with little check. Reforms from the 1950s (the Native Authority Law of 1954 and later amendments) made them share power with councils, partly elected. Under the Chief-and-Council arrangement the ruler had to act on the decisions of his council, making local government more democratic. The law set up two forms: Chief-and-Council (B), where the ruler had to act on his council's majority decisions, and Chief-in-Council (D), where he presided and could override the council but had to consult it; most large emirates took the second form. Both came from the reform, so B and D are accepted. Prefects (C) belong to the French system of direct rule, not to the reformed native authorities of Northern Nigeria. Memory tip: chief AND council = equal partners; chief IN council = the chief still decides.",
 "|Under whose regime were Akwa-Ibom and Katsina States created?|Gen Murtala Muhammed|Gen Ibrahim Babangida|Gen Sani Abacha|Gen Yakubu Gowon": "Nigeria's states were created in stages: 12 (Gowon, 1967), 19 (Murtala Muhammed, 1976), 21 (Babangida, 1987: Akwa Ibom and Katsina), 30 (Babangida, 1991) and 36 (Abacha, 1996). So Akwa Ibom and Katsina were created under General Ibrahim Babangida: B. The trap: Murtala Muhammed (A) is famous for creating states, but his 1976 exercise gave 19 states, and Akwa Ibom was still part of Cross River. Memory tip: 12-19-21-30-36: Gowon, Murtala, IBB, IBB, Abacha.",
 "|Under the 1999 Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria, the appointment and posting of members of election tribunals on the elections conducted by INEC is the responsibility of the|Chairman, Independent National Electoral Commission|President of Nigeria|Chief Justice of Nigeria|President, Court of Appeal": "Election petitions are heard by tribunals, not by INEC, which conducted the election. Under the Sixth Schedule of the 1999 Constitution, the chairmen and members of the election tribunals (for National Assembly, governorship and state assembly elections) are appointed by the President of the Court of Appeal, in consultation with the Chief Judge of the state for state tribunals. Appeals go to the Court of Appeal and, for governorship, the Supreme Court. So it is the President of the Court of Appeal: D. The trap: C, the Chief Justice of Nigeria, sounds like the natural authority over judicial postings, but the 1999 Constitution gives the President of the Court of Appeal the power to constitute election tribunals. Memory tip: tribunals come from the Court of Appeal.",
 "|Rhodesia was the former name of|Zimbabwe|Swaziland|Zambia|Namibia": "British South Africa Company territories north of South Africa were named after Cecil Rhodes. Southern Rhodesia, called simply Rhodesia after Ian Smith's white-minority Unilateral Declaration of Independence in 1965, became independent under majority rule as Zimbabwe on 18 April 1980. Nigeria strongly supported the liberation struggle there. So Rhodesia was the former name of Zimbabwe: A. The trap: Zambia (C) was Northern Rhodesia, which became independent in 1964; it is never called just \"Rhodesia\". Memory tip: Rhodesia = Southern Rhodesia = Zimbabwe (1980); Northern Rhodesia = Zambia (1964).",
 "|The adoption of non-alignment as a principle of Nigeria's foreign policy was aimed at|promoting Nigeria's leadership aspiration in Africa|attaining equal status with the world powers|fulfilling a basic requirement for acceptance in the UN Security Council|insulating Nigeria against having to take sides in the Cold War": "During the Cold War (1945–91) the world was split between the US-led Western bloc (NATO) and the Soviet-led Eastern bloc (Warsaw Pact). Non-alignment, a principle of Nigeria's foreign policy from 1960, meant Nigeria would not join either military bloc but would judge each issue on its merits and trade with both. Its aim was to keep Nigeria out of the superpower rivalry: D. The trap: \"promoting Nigeria's leadership in Africa\" (A) is a real aim of Nigerian foreign policy (Africa as the centrepiece), but it is a different principle, not the purpose of non-alignment. Memory tip: non-aligned = not tied to East or West.",
 "|In 1979, the non-aligned member states were|21|117|37|19": "The Non-Aligned Movement (NAM) was founded in Belgrade in 1961 by 25 states, led by Tito (Yugoslavia), Nehru (India), Nasser (Egypt), Sukarno (Indonesia) and Nkrumah (Ghana); Nigeria joined at independence. It grew as colonies became free: at the Havana summit of September 1979 it had just over 90 members, and today it has about 120. None of the options fits 1979 exactly; 117 is marked because it is the expected answer and the only figure near the Movement's real size: B. Twenty-one (A) and 19 (D) are too low, since the 1961 Belgrade founding summit already had 25 members; by the 1979 Havana summit there were about 94, so no option is exact. Memory tip: NAM: 25 founders in 1961, about 120 today.",
 "|Which of the following was the secretary-general of OPEC?|Jibril Aminu|Aret Adams|Dalhatu Bayero|Rilwan Lukman": "The Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) was founded in Baghdad in 1960 and has its headquarters in Vienna; Nigeria joined in 1971. Its Secretary General runs the secretariat. Dr Rilwanu Lukman, a Nigerian mining engineer and former petroleum minister, was OPEC Secretary General from 1995 to 2000. So it is Rilwan Lukman: D. The trap: Jibril Aminu (A) was Nigeria's petroleum minister and served as President of the OPEC Conference, a different office; Lukman also held that presidency, but he is the one who became Secretary General. Memory tip: Lukman led OPEC's secretariat (1995–2000); later Mohammed Barkindo (2016–2022) was another Nigerian Secretary General.",
 "|Which of the following countries pioneered the idea of ECOWAS alongside Nigeria?|Liberia|Togo|Cote d'Ivoire|Mali": "ECOWAS began as an idea of two military leaders: General Yakubu Gowon of Nigeria and General Gnassingbé Eyadéma of Togo, who agreed in 1972 to work for a West African economic community and toured the region to win support. The Treaty of Lagos creating ECOWAS was signed by 15 states on 28 May 1975. So the country that pioneered the idea with Nigeria was Togo: B. The trap: Liberia (A) is tempting because President William Tubman had earlier (1964) proposed a West African free trade area, but the Gowon–Eyadéma initiative is the one that produced ECOWAS. Memory tip: ECOWAS = Gowon + Eyadéma, Lagos 1975.",
 "|Which of the following international organizations were in existence before the Second World War?|The UNO|The OAU|The League of Nations|The ECOWAS": "The League of Nations was created by the Treaty of Versailles after the First World War and began work in 1920 in Geneva. It failed to stop the aggression of Japan, Italy and Germany and could not prevent the Second World War (1939–45); it was formally dissolved in 1946. So the organisation that existed before the Second World War was the League of Nations: C. The trap: the UNO (A) replaced the League but was founded only in October 1945, at the end of the war; the OAU (1963) and ECOWAS (1975) came much later. Memory tip: World War I gave the League (1920); World War II gave the UN (1945).",
 "|The organ of the United Nations responsible for the approval of its annual budget is the|Secretariat|Security Council|General Assembly|Economic and Social Council": "The UN General Assembly is the only organ in which all member states sit, each with one vote. Article 17 of the UN Charter gives it the power to consider and approve the budget of the organisation and to share the expenses among members; budget decisions need a two-thirds majority. So the organ that approves the UN budget is the General Assembly: C. The trap: the Secretariat (A) prepares the budget, through the Secretary-General, but it does not approve it; preparing is not approving. Memory tip: the purse belongs to the Assembly, where every nation pays and votes.",
 "|Each member state is represented on the Board of Governors of OPEC for a period of|2 years|3 years|4 years|1 year": "OPEC is run by three main organs: the Conference (oil ministers, the supreme authority), the Board of Governors, and the Secretariat headed by the Secretary General. Each member country nominates one Governor to the Board, and under the OPEC Statute each Governor holds office for a term of two years. So the period is 2 years: A. The trap: 3 years (B) is the term of the OPEC Secretary General, which can be renewed, so the numbers are easily mixed up. Memory tip: Governor 2, Secretary General 3.",
 "|The distinctive attribute of a state is the monopoly of|control|power|violence|justice": "The German sociologist Max Weber (1919) defined the state as the human community that successfully claims the monopoly of the legitimate use of physical force within a given territory. Only the state may lawfully arrest, imprison, wage war or execute; when anyone else uses force, it is a crime. That monopoly of legitimate violence is what marks the state off from families, churches, parties or companies: C. The trap: power (B) is tempting, but every group, from a trade union to a village association, has some power; what only the state holds is the lawful use of force. Memory tip: Weber's state = \"monopoly of legitimate violence\", the police and the army belong to the state alone.",
 "|State as a political entity refers to|an organized group within a definite territory|an association of men in a given society|a branch of a nation|a geographical location": "A state has four elements: a population, a definite territory, an organised government and sovereignty. As a political entity, therefore, the state is an organised group of people living within a definite territory under a government: A. The trap: \"an association of men in a given society\" (B) describes any association, such as a club or a union; it has no territory or sovereignty. A branch of a nation (C) is backwards (a nation is a people; a state is the political organisation), and a geographical location (D) is only one element, territory. Memory tip: state = P-T-G-S (Population, Territory, Government, Sovereignty).",
 "|Political values are acquired in any given society through|political re-orientation|political campaign|political socialization|political indoctrination": "Political socialisation is the process by which people learn and absorb the political values, beliefs and attitudes of their society. It begins in childhood and continues through life, through agents such as the family, school, peer groups, religious bodies, the mass media and political parties. It is how a society's political culture is passed from one generation to the next: C. The trap: political indoctrination (D) is a narrow, one-sided form of teaching that bars questioning, used by totalitarian regimes; re-orientation (A) is a deliberate campaign to change values already held. Neither is the normal way values are acquired. Memory tip: socialisation = learning politics the way a child learns a language, from everyone around.",
 "|In a democratic government, political sovereignty is vested in the|legislature|elite|executive|electorate": "Writers distinguish two kinds of sovereignty. Legal sovereignty belongs to the body whose commands the courts enforce as law (for example the legislature). Political sovereignty belongs to the people whose will ultimately decides who governs. In a democracy that is the electorate, the citizens qualified to vote, who can elect leaders and remove them at the next election: D. The trap: the legislature (A) holds legal sovereignty, not political sovereignty; its members are themselves chosen by and answerable to the electorate. Memory tip: legal sovereignty makes the law; political sovereignty makes the lawmakers, the voters.",
 "|One judicial function performed by the executive is|granting of amnesty|implementing judicial orders|ensuring obedience to the law|appointing judges": "The judiciary decides cases and passes sentences, but the constitution lets the executive alter the result through the prerogative of mercy. Under section 175 of the 1999 Constitution the President (and under section 212 a Governor) may grant a pardon, reprieve, respite or remission of punishment, or an amnesty. Because it changes the outcome of the judicial process, granting amnesty is called a judicial function of the executive: A. The trap: appointing judges (D) involves the courts but is an executive act of appointment; implementing judicial orders (B) and ensuring obedience to the law (C) are ordinary executive duties of enforcement. Memory tip: mercy belongs to the executive; the President's pardon can undo a judge's sentence.",
 "|A governmental system in which constitutional supremacy resides in the center is|federal|confederal|unitary|parliamentary": "In a unitary system all supreme authority is concentrated in one central government. It may create regions or local councils, but they exist at its pleasure and can be changed or abolished by it (Britain, France, Ghana; Nigeria briefly under Ironsi's Decree No. 34 of May 1966). So supremacy resides in the centre: C. The trap: federal (A) is tempting because \"constitutional supremacy\" is a mark of federalism, but in a federation power is shared between the centre and the units by the constitution, so it does not reside in the centre. Because a reader could link the phrase \"constitutional supremacy\" to federalism, this point is debatable. Memory tip: unitary = one boss at the centre; federal = power shared by the constitution.",
 "|A political system which empowers the leader with the ultimate responsibility to execute laws is|autocracy|presidentialism|parliamentarianism|dictatorship": "In a presidential system (USA, Nigeria since 1979) executive power is vested in one person, the president, who is both head of state and head of government, is elected for a fixed term, and bears the final responsibility for executing the laws. Section 5 of the 1999 Constitution vests the executive powers of the Federation in the President. So the system is presidentialism: B. The trap: autocracy (A) and dictatorship (D) also have one powerful leader, but there the leader is not limited by law and also makes the laws; parliamentarianism (C) places executive power in a collective cabinet led by a prime minister. Memory tip: president = one person, final say in executing the law.",
 "|A bill is a draft which is awaiting the consideration of the|executive|party caucus|legislature|judiciary": "A bill is a draft of a proposed law. It goes before the legislature, where it passes first reading, second reading, the committee stage, report stage and third reading in each house. Only after it has been passed and assented to by the executive does it become an Act (law). So a bill awaits the consideration of the legislature: C. The trap: the executive (A) often prepares bills and must sign them, but it cannot pass them; the judiciary (D) interprets laws already made. A party caucus (B) may discuss a bill but has no legal power over it. Memory tip: bill in the legislature, Act after assent.",
 "|The private ownership of the means of production is a feature of|capitalism|socialism|communalism|communism": "Capitalism is the economic system in which land, factories and capital, the means of production, are privately owned, and goods are produced for profit through competition in a free market with limited state interference. Its classic defender was Adam Smith (\"The Wealth of Nations\", 1776). So private ownership of the means of production is a feature of capitalism: A. Socialism (B) and communism (D) place the means of production under public or collective ownership, and communalism (C) is the traditional system of community land ownership. Memory tip: capital-ism = private capital; social-ism = society owns.",
 "|In a feudal system, the two major classes are the serfs and the|masses|vassals|lords|elite": "Feudalism, the system of medieval Europe, was built on land. The king granted land (fiefs) to lords in return for military service; at the bottom, serfs were tied to the lord's land, worked it, and paid him dues in return for protection. Society was therefore split into two main classes: the land-owning lords and the serfs: C. The trap: vassals (B) is tempting, but vassals were themselves nobles holding land from a higher lord, not the class opposite the serfs. \"Masses\" (A) and \"elite\" (D) are general words, not feudal classes. Memory tip: feudalism = lord and serf, land for labour.",
 "|An example of a country with a flexible constitution is|South Africa|Britain|Benin Republic|the United States of America": "A constitution is flexible when it can be amended by the ordinary law-making process, and rigid when it needs a special procedure (such as two-thirds majorities or a referendum). Britain's constitution is largely unwritten, made up of Acts of Parliament, conventions, judicial decisions and works of authority, and Parliament can change any part of it by an ordinary Act because Parliament is sovereign. So Britain is the classic example of a flexible constitution: B. The United States (D) has a famously rigid constitution, amended only 27 times since 1789, and South Africa (A) and Benin (C) also have written, rigid constitutions. Memory tip: unwritten and sovereign Parliament = flexible Britain.",
 "|The rule of law is negation of|equality before the law|supremacy of the law|limited power|absolute power": "The rule of law, explained by A.V. Dicey (1885), has three ideas: the supremacy of the law over arbitrary power, equality of all persons before the law, and protection of rights by the ordinary courts. Because it puts everyone, including rulers, under the law, the rule of law is the direct opposite of absolute power, where a ruler can act as he pleases: D. Equality before the law (A) and supremacy of the law (B) are pillars of the rule of law, and limited power (C) is what it produces; the rule of law negates absolute power. Memory tip: rule of law = nobody above the law, so no absolute ruler.",
 "|To ensure the rights and freedom of citizens, the powers of the arms of government must be|fused|incorporated|separated|rotated": "The French writer Montesquieu (\"The Spirit of the Laws\", 1748) argued that when the powers to make, execute and interpret laws are held by the same person or body, there can be no liberty. Separation of powers gives each power to a different organ (legislature, executive, judiciary), with checks and balances among them, so no single organ can abuse citizens' rights. So the powers must be separated: C. Fusion (A) concentrates power, as in a parliamentary cabinet or a military regime, and \"incorporated\" (B) and \"rotated\" (D) are not constitutional principles. Memory tip: Montesquieu: separate the powers to save liberty.",
 "|Delegated legislation is made by bodies other than the|president|governor|parliament|judiciary": "Parliament (the legislature) is the body that normally makes law, but it cannot provide every detail. So it passes a parent Act giving power to other bodies (ministers, local governments, public corporations, professional bodies) to make detailed rules called bye-laws, orders, regulations or statutory instruments. This is delegated legislation: law made by bodies other than parliament: C. The trap: the president (A) and governors (B) are among the bodies that make delegated legislation, so they cannot be the bodies it is made \"other than\". The judiciary (D) interprets laws and is not the normal law-maker. Memory tip: delegated = parliament hands down (delegates) its law-making power.",
 "|The bringing of a session of a parliament to an end through royal proclamation is known as|political impasse|dissolution of parliament|vote of no confidence|prorogation of parliament": "In the British-style parliamentary system the head of state ends a sitting session of parliament by proclamation. Prorogation suspends parliament's business until the next session, but the parliament itself and its members remain; bills not passed usually lapse. Dissolution is different: it ends the life of the whole parliament so that a general election must be held. So ending a session by royal proclamation is prorogation: D. The trap: dissolution of parliament (B) is also done by proclamation, but it ends the parliament itself, not just a session. A vote of no confidence (C) removes a government, not a session. Memory tip: prorogue = pause the session; dissolve = dismiss the parliament.",
 "|The right of citizens to participate in the affairs of government of their country is called|economic right|civil right|political right|social right": "Rights are usually grouped as civil, political, economic and social. Political rights are the rights that let citizens take part in governing their country: the right to vote, to be voted for, to form or join political parties, to hold public office and to criticise government. So the right to participate in government is a political right: C. The trap: civil rights (B) are the freedoms that protect the individual in society, such as the right to life, fair hearing, religion and free movement; they are close cousins but not about taking part in government. Economic and social rights cover work, property, education and health. Memory tip: political rights = rights to the ballot box and public office.",
 "|The commission charged with the conduct of federal elections in Nigeria is|NEC|FEDECO|INEC|NECON": "Nigeria's federal electoral body has changed its name with each transition: FEDECO, the Federal Electoral Commission (1978–83, Second Republic); NEC, the National Electoral Commission (1987–93, Babangida); NECON, the National Electoral Commission of Nigeria (1995–98, Abacha); and INEC, the Independent National Electoral Commission, set up in 1998 by General Abdulsalami Abubakar and still in charge today (s.153 and Third Schedule, 1999 Constitution). So the commission is INEC: C. The trap: FEDECO (B) is tempting because of the word \"federal\", but it was dissolved in 1983. Memory tip: F-N-N-I: FEDECO, NEC, NECON, INEC, in that order.",
 "|An electoral system in which parties are assigned seats in the parliament commensurate to the number of votes polled is|absolute majority|simple majority|proportional representation|indirect election": "Under proportional representation (PR), seats in the legislature are shared among parties in proportion to the votes each party wins; a party with 30% of the votes gets about 30% of the seats. List PR (used in Israel, the Netherlands and South Africa) is the commonest form. It is fair to small parties but often produces coalition governments. So the answer is proportional representation: C. The trap: simple majority (B), \"first past the post\", gives each seat to the candidate with the most votes, so seat totals often do not match vote shares. Absolute majority (A) requires over 50% of votes. Memory tip: PR = percentage of votes becomes percentage of seats.",
 "|An intra-party activity for the selection of candidates for elective positions is known as|primary election|general election|mid-term election|bye-election": "Before a general election each party must choose its candidates. It does this through a primary election held within the party, by direct primaries (all party members vote), indirect primaries (delegates vote) or consensus. The winner becomes the party's flag-bearer. Because it takes place inside one party, it is called an intra-party activity: A. The trap: a general election (B) is the inter-party contest in which voters choose among the candidates of all parties; a bye-election (D) fills a single vacant seat; a mid-term election (C) is held halfway through a term, as in the USA. Memory tip: primary comes first, inside the party; general comes next, among all parties.",
 "|The primary aim of pressure groups is to|attract people's attention|protect the interest of members|capture political power|fight corrupt officials": "A pressure group is an organised group (such as the NLC, NMA, NBA or ASUU) that tries to influence government policy in favour of its members' interests. It uses lobbying, petitions, strikes, demonstrations and the media, but it does not put up candidates for election. So the primary aim of a pressure group is to protect its members' interests: B. The trap: capturing political power (C) is the aim of a political party, not a pressure group; this is the main difference between them. Attracting attention (A) and fighting corruption (D) may be methods or occasional causes, not the main aim. Memory tip: party wants power; pressure group wants policy for its members.",
 "|Which of the following is used in gauging public opinion?|Constitution|Educational institution|Mass media|Electoral college": "Public opinion is the views held by a large part of the public on issues and leaders. It is gauged (measured) through opinion polls, newspapers, radio and television, letters, phone-ins and today social media; the mass media both report and shape what people think. Elections and referendums also reveal opinion. So the mass media are used in gauging public opinion: C. An electoral college (D) is a small body of chosen electors that does not measure the general public's views, and a constitution (A) and schools (B) do not measure opinion at all. Memory tip: to feel the public's pulse, read the papers and the polls.",
 "|A permanent structure that facilitates continuity and guarantees orderly conduct in governance is|bureaucracy|public corporation|ombudsman|political party": "The bureaucracy, or civil service, is the permanent body of career officials who run government departments. Max Weber described it as hierarchical, rule-based, impersonal and permanent. Ministers, governments and even regimes change, but civil servants remain, keep records, give advice and carry policies forward, providing continuity and orderly administration. So the answer is bureaucracy: A. Political parties (D) win and lose power, so they are not permanent in government; public corporations (B) run business-type services and the ombudsman (C) handles complaints. Memory tip: politicians are temporary; the bureaucracy is permanent.",
 "|In the Hausa pre-colonial political system, a district was headed by|Hakimi|Dagaci|Alkali|Waziri": "In the Hausa-Fulani emirate system the Emir ruled with officials such as the Waziri (chief adviser and head of administration), the Galadima, the Madawaki (army commander) and the Alkali (Islamic judge). For local administration the emirate was divided into districts, each under a Hakimi (district head), who collected taxes through the village heads (Dagaci). So a district was headed by the Hakimi: A. The trap: the Dagaci (B) was also a local head, but of a village, one level below the district. The Alkali judged cases under Sharia, and the Waziri served the Emir at the capital. Memory tip: Emir > Hakimi (district) > Dagaci (village).",
 "|Which of the following ensured the practice of democracy in the pre-colonial Yoruba political system?|Checks and balances|Fusion of power|Individual responsibility|The rule of law": "The old Oyo Empire is the classic Yoruba example. The Alaafin was powerful, but the Oyo Mesi (seven kingmakers led by the Bashorun) could ask him to \"open the calabash\" and commit suicide if he ruled badly; the Ogboni society checked the Oyo Mesi; and the Aare Ona Kakanfo commanded the army outside the capital. Each power was balanced by another. These checks and balances made the system democratic in spirit: A. Fusion of power (B) is the opposite of checks and balances, the rule of law (D) is a modern constitutional idea, and individual responsibility (C) was not a feature; the Oyo Mesi and Ogboni checked the Alaafin. Memory tip: Oyo Mesi checks the Alaafin; Ogboni checks the Oyo Mesi.",
 "|Colonization of Africa was mainly motivated by|security considerations|economic reasons|religious reasons|cultural factors": "The European scramble for Africa, formalised at the Berlin Conference of 1884–85, was driven mainly by economics. The Industrial Revolution created a need for raw materials (palm oil, cocoa, cotton, groundnuts, minerals), markets for manufactured goods, and safe places to invest surplus capital. Trading companies such as the Royal Niger Company paved the way for colonial rule in Nigeria. So the main motive was economic: B. The trap: religious reasons (C), the spread of Christianity, and cultural claims of a \"civilising mission\" (D) were used to justify colonialism but were not the main driving force. Memory tip: the 3 Ms of colonialism were markets, materials and money.",
 "|The French colonial system was underlined by the policy of|assimilation|paternalism|socialism|indirect rule": "France ruled its colonies (Senegal, Côte d'Ivoire, Dahomey, etc.) through assimilation: Africans were to become French in language, culture, religion and law, and those who did, the évolués, could gain French citizenship. The Four Communes of Senegal even sent a deputy to the French parliament. Rule was direct and centralised from Paris. So the French system was underlain by assimilation: A. The trap: indirect rule (D) was the British method of ruling through traditional rulers, developed by Lugard in Northern Nigeria. Paternalism (B) was more the Belgian style, and socialism (C) was not a colonial policy. Memory tip: France assimilates; Britain rules indirectly.",
 "|Radical nationalism in Nigeria is generally attributed to the influence of|Aminu Kano|Herbert Macaulay|Nnamdi Azikiwe|Mbonu Ojike": "Herbert Macaulay founded the NNDP (1923) and is called the father of Nigerian nationalism, but his politics was largely limited to Lagos. The radical, mass-based phase began when Nnamdi Azikiwe returned from the Gold Coast in 1937. Through the West African Pilot and his chain of newspapers, the Nigerian Youth Movement and the NCNC, he spread a militant nationalism across the country, carried further by the Zikist Movement (1946). So radical nationalism is generally attributed to Azikiwe: C. The trap: Macaulay (B) was a fierce critic of colonial rule, but his title is \"father of nationalism\", not the radical, nationwide phase. This point is debatable. Memory tip: Macaulay began it; Zik radicalised it.",
 "|Two foreigners that directly aroused nationalist feelings among Nigerians are|Edward Blyden and Payne Jackson|Casely Hayford and James Horton|W.E.B. Du Bois and H.O. Davies|Marcus Garvey and Casely Hayford": "Early Nigerian nationalism was inspired by black thinkers. Edward Wilmot Blyden (born in the Danish West Indies, a Liberian statesman) lived and lectured in Lagos in the 1890s, preaching African personality and pride. John Payne Jackson, a Liberian, edited the Lagos Weekly Record (founded 1890), which fiercely attacked colonial policies. Because both worked inside Nigeria, they directly aroused nationalist feelings among Nigerians: A. The trap: Marcus Garvey and Casely Hayford (D) inspired Nigerians too, but from a distance; Garvey never came to Nigeria and Hayford worked in the Gold Coast. H.O. Davies (C) was a Nigerian, not a foreigner. Memory tip: Blyden and Jackson were the Liberians in Lagos.",
 "|Members of the Senate in Nigeria's First Republic were|elected directly by the people|elected by electoral college|nominated by regional and federal governments|nominated by the president of the house": "The First Republic Parliament had an elected House of Representatives and a Senate that was not elected by the people. Each region had 12 senators chosen through its regional government and legislature; Lagos had a few seats; and four more were appointed by the Governor-General (later the President) on the Prime Minister's advice. So senators were nominated by regional and federal governments: C. The trap: elected directly by the people (A) describes the House of Representatives in that era, and the Senate since 1979. An electoral college (B) was not used. Memory tip: First Republic Senate = picked by governments; Second Republic onwards = elected by the people.",
 "|In Nigeria, the agency mainly responsible for the maintenance of internal peace and security is the|Army|Navy|Civil Defence Corps|Police": "The Nigeria Police Force is set up by section 214 of the 1999 Constitution. Its duties include preventing and detecting crime, arresting offenders, protecting lives and property and keeping law and order. It is therefore the main agency for internal peace and security: D. The trap: the Army (A) is often deployed inside the country during crises, but its main constitutional duty (s.217) is defending Nigeria against external aggression; the Navy defends the waters, and the Civil Defence Corps assists in protecting infrastructure and in emergencies. Memory tip: Police inside, Army at the border.",
 "|The National Assembly in Nigeria is primarily responsible for|executing laws|interpreting laws|ratifying appointments|making laws": "The 1999 Constitution vests the legislative powers of the Federation in the National Assembly (s.4), made up of the Senate (109 members) and the House of Representatives (360 members). Its main job is making laws for the peace, order and good government of the Federation, though it also controls public funds and oversees the executive. So it is primarily responsible for making laws: D. The trap: ratifying appointments (C) is a real power of the Senate (it confirms ministers, ambassadors and others), but it is a secondary function, not the primary one. Executing (A) belongs to the executive and interpreting (B) to the judiciary. Memory tip: legislature = law; executive = execute; judiciary = judge.",
 "|The major factor militating against the efficient operation of electoral commissions in Nigeria is|inadequate public support|population size|inadequate skilled manpower|excessive political interference": "Nigeria's electoral bodies (FEDECO, NEC, NECON, INEC) have been weakened mostly by political interference: the President appoints and can influence the commission's leaders, governments control its funding, and politicians pressure or bribe its staff. This makes elections appear partial and has led to disputed results, as in 1983. So the major factor is excessive political interference: D. The trap: inadequate skilled manpower (C) is a real problem, but training can fix it; the deeper problem is lack of independence. Population size (B) and public support (A) are not the main issue. Memory tip: an umpire chosen and paid by one team is never trusted.",
 "|A major objective of the Public Complaints Commission is|training and promotion of public servants|settlement of disputes among individuals|addressing the grievances of individuals and groups|fighting corruption and indiscipline": "The Public Complaints Commission was set up in 1975 (by Decree No. 31, after the Udoji Commission's recommendation) and is Nigeria's ombudsman. It receives and investigates complaints from individuals and groups who feel wronged by government officials, ministries and agencies, and recommends remedies. So its major objective is addressing the grievances of individuals and groups: C. The trap: settlement of disputes among individuals (B) sounds close, but disputes between private persons go to the courts; the commission handles complaints against public bodies. Fighting corruption (D) is the work of the EFCC and ICPC. Memory tip: ombudsman = the citizen's complaints office against government.",
 "|The three registered political parties at the inception of Nigeria's Fourth Republic were|PDP, DPP and PPA|PDP, AD and APP|PDP, AD and PPA|PDP, APP and AC": "General Abdulsalami Abubakar's transition (1998–99) allowed many associations to apply, but INEC registered only three parties for the elections: the Peoples Democratic Party (PDP), the Alliance for Democracy (AD) and the All People's Party (APP). Olusegun Obasanjo (PDP) won the presidency in February 1999. So the answer is PDP, AD and APP: B. The trap: AC (D), the Action Congress, was formed only in 2006, and the DPP and PPA (A, C) were registered only in the 2000s. None of the three original parties except the PDP survives under its old name: APP became the ANPP, AD merged into the Action Congress, and both lines ended in the APC (2013). Memory tip: 1999's three: PDP, AD, APP.",
 "|The Sharia legal system was first introduced in the Fourth Republic in|Kano State|Katsina State|Zamfara State|Sokoto State": "In October 1999 Governor Ahmed Sani Yerima of Zamfara State announced that Sharia would apply to criminal as well as civil matters for Muslims; the law took effect on 27 January 2000. Before then, Sharia in Northern Nigeria covered mainly personal and family matters. Eleven other northern states, including Kano, Katsina and Sokoto, soon followed. So Sharia was first introduced in the Fourth Republic in Zamfara: C. The trap: Kano (A) and Sokoto (D) are famous Islamic centres (Sokoto was the seat of the Caliphate), but they followed Zamfara's lead. Memory tip: Z for Zamfara, the first in the Fourth Republic.",
 "|Quota system and federal character principles were entrenched in the 1979 constitution to ensure|loyalty|economic empowerment|equity|even development": "The 1979 Constitution introduced the federal character principle in section 14(3): appointments to government and its agencies must reflect the federal character of Nigeria, \"to promote national unity, and also to command national loyalty\", so that there is no predominance of persons from a few states or groups. The quota system applies the same idea to admissions and recruitment. The core aim is fairness in sharing positions, that is, equity: C. Loyalty (A) is also correct, because the constitution itself names national loyalty as an aim, so both are accepted. The trap: even development (D) is about spreading projects and infrastructure, which is the job of revenue sharing, not appointments. Memory tip: federal character = a fair share of the national cake for every section.",
 "|Workers in the public corporations are known as|civil servants|private employees|public servants|professional employees": "Public corporations (for example NNPC, NPA, NRC) are owned by government but run like businesses under their own boards, created by statute. Their staff are public servants, part of the wider public service paid from public funds. Civil servants are only the career officials in government ministries and extra-ministerial departments. So workers in public corporations are public servants: C. The trap: civil servants (A) is the tempting choice because both groups work for government, but every civil servant is a public servant while not every public servant is a civil servant. Memory tip: public service is the big circle; civil service is the smaller circle of ministries inside it.",
 "|The central objective of privatization in Nigeria is to|reduce the retrenchment of workers|encourage prompt payment of salaries|improve standard of living|improve the efficiency of enterprises": "Privatisation is the sale of all or part of government-owned enterprises to private investors. Nigeria began it under the Structural Adjustment Programme (Decree No. 25 of 1988, the TCPC) and expanded it through the Bureau of Public Enterprises (Public Enterprises Act 1999). The central aim is to make the enterprises efficient and profitable, end waste and corruption, and stop draining the treasury with subsidies: D. The trap: reducing retrenchment (A) is wrong, since privatisation usually leads to job cuts as new owners trim staff. Improving the standard of living (C) is a hoped-for result, not the central objective. Memory tip: privatise to make it efficient.",
 "|Military intervention in Nigeria arose from|perceived incapability of civilians to govern|international pressure for change|the desire for a military government|civilians' desire to relinquish power": "Every Nigerian coup was justified by the claim that civilians had failed: in January 1966 the plotters spoke of corruption and tribalism; in December 1983 Buhari cited economic collapse and rigged elections. Other causes were ethnic rivalry, political crises (Western Region 1962–65) and the soldiers' own ambitions, but the common stated reason was the perceived incapability of civilians to govern: A. The trap: \"the desire for a military government\" (C) describes the soldiers' own ambition and was never the publicly accepted reason; international pressure (B) usually pushed against military rule, not for it. Memory tip: every coup speech says the same thing: the civilians failed.",
 "|The first institution introduced by the military to exercise legislative power was the|Supreme Military Council|Armed Forces Ruling Council|Federal Executive Council|Provisional Ruling Council": "After the coup of 15 January 1966, Major-General Aguiyi-Ironsi's regime issued the Constitution (Suspension and Modification) Decree No. 1 of 1966. It suspended Parliament and vested legislative power in the Supreme Military Council (SMC), which made laws by decree, while the Federal Executive Council handled executive business. Later regimes renamed the body: the Armed Forces Ruling Council (AFRC) under Babangida (1985) and the Provisional Ruling Council (PRC) under Abacha (1993). So the first was the Supreme Military Council: A. The trap: the Federal Executive Council (C) existed under Ironsi, but it was an executive body, not legislative. Memory tip: SMC, then AFRC, then PRC.",
 "|The main focus of Nigeria's foreign policy since independence centers on|South-South cooperation|sub-regionalism|globalism|Afrocentrism": "From Balewa in 1960 onward, Nigeria declared Africa the centrepiece (cornerstone) of its foreign policy. This Afrocentrism shows in support for the OAU and AU, the struggle against colonialism and apartheid, peacekeeping (ECOMOG), the Technical Aid Corps, and aid to African states. So the main focus is Afrocentrism: D. The trap: sub-regionalism (B), focus on West Africa through ECOWAS, is only an inner circle of the same Afrocentric policy, not the main focus. South-South cooperation (A) and globalism (C) are wider, later themes. Memory tip: Africa is the centrepiece; West Africa is the inner circle.",
 "|The country that championed decolonization in Africa was|Nigeria|South Africa|Ghana|Kenya": "Ghana, under Kwame Nkrumah, became independent in March 1957 and at once declared that its independence was meaningless unless linked to the total liberation of Africa. Accra hosted the Conference of Independent African States and the All-African Peoples' Conference (1958), which inspired liberation movements across the continent. So Ghana championed decolonisation: C. Nigeria (A) is also accepted: from 1960 it made decolonisation and the end of apartheid the centrepiece of its foreign policy, funding liberation movements in Angola, Zimbabwe, Namibia and South Africa and acting as a frontline state. South Africa (B) was the last stronghold of white minority rule, a target of decolonization rather than its champion; Nkrumah's Ghana led the campaign, with Nigeria also prominent. Memory tip: Nkrumah began the call; Nigeria funded the finish.",
 "|A major drawback to the NEPAD initiative is its|articulation by few African leaders|affiliation by few African union|inability to empower the youth|reliance on Western donors for funds": "NEPAD, the New Partnership for Africa's Development, was adopted in 2001 (driven by Mbeki, Obasanjo, Bouteflika and Wade). It aims to end poverty through good governance, investment and partnership. Its major weakness is that it depends heavily on aid, loans and investment from Western donors and the G8, so Africa's own development plan relies on outsiders' money and conditions: D. The trap: \"articulation by few African leaders\" (A) is a real criticism, since ordinary Africans were not consulted, but the plan was endorsed by the whole OAU/AU; the dependence on Western funds is the bigger drawback. Memory tip: NEPAD's weak spot is the donor's purse.",
 "|The structures of the African Union include|the court of justice, pan African congress and people's Assembly|pan African parliament, the court of justice and the peace and security council|specialized Technical commission, the court of justice and humanitarian board|people's Assembly, Humanitarian Board and the peace and security council": "The African Union (Constitutive Act 2000, launched 2002) has organs including the Assembly of Heads of State and Government, the Executive Council, the AU Commission, the Pan-African Parliament (in Midrand, South Africa), the Court of Justice, the Peace and Security Council, the Economic, Social and Cultural Council, specialised technical committees and financial institutions. Option B lists three real organs: B. The trap: options A, C and D each mix real organs with invented ones; there is no \"Pan-African Congress\" (that was a series of Pan-Africanist meetings from 1919), no \"People's Assembly\" and no \"Humanitarian Board\". Memory tip: AU = Parliament, Court, Peace and Security Council.",
 "|ECOMOG at the initial stage of its intervention in Liberia was perceived as|neutral|incompetent|partisan|invaders": "The ECOWAS Ceasefire Monitoring Group (ECOMOG), led by Nigeria, landed in Monrovia in August 1990 to stop Liberia's civil war. Charles Taylor's NPFL, which controlled most of the country and was about to capture the capital, rejected ECOMOG as a foreign invading force and attacked it, while President Doe and Prince Johnson's group accepted it. So at the initial stage ECOMOG was perceived as invaders: D. The trap: partisan (C) is close, since Taylor also accused the Nigerian-led force of siding with Doe, a friend of Babangida, but the first reaction was to treat it as an invasion. ECOMOG was clearly not seen as neutral (A). Memory tip: Taylor shouted \"invaders!\" as ECOMOG landed.",
 "|One of the programmes binding members of the Commonwealth is the|food and aid programme|cultural programme|agenda for peace|scholarship scheme": "The Commonwealth links Britain and its former colonies in voluntary cooperation; it has no binding treaty, but members share joint programmes. The best known is the Commonwealth Scholarship and Fellowship Plan, agreed at Oxford in 1959, which lets students from member countries study in other member countries; others include the Commonwealth Games and the Commonwealth Fund for Technical Co-operation (1971). So the answer is the scholarship scheme: D. The trap: \"Agenda for Peace\" (C) was a United Nations report by Secretary-General Boutros Boutros-Ghali in 1992, not a Commonwealth programme. The Commonwealth has 56 members today. Memory tip: Commonwealth = scholarships and the Games.",
 "|The African leader mostly credited for spearheading the formation of the African Union is|Muammar Ghaddafi|Abdelaziz Bouteflika|Abdoulaye Wade|Thabo Mbeki": "In 1999 Libya's leader Muammar Gaddafi invited OAU leaders to Sirte, where they adopted the Sirte Declaration (9 September 1999, 9/9/99) calling for an African Union. The Constitutive Act was adopted at Lomé in 2000 and the AU was launched in Durban, South Africa, in July 2002. Gaddafi pushed and funded the idea and dreamed of a \"United States of Africa\", so he is mostly credited with spearheading it: A. The trap: Thabo Mbeki (D) hosted and chaired the launch of the AU in Durban in 2002 and is linked with NEPAD, but the idea was Gaddafi's. Bouteflika and Wade were NEPAD co-sponsors. Memory tip: Sirte (Libya), 9/9/99, Gaddafi.",
 "|As part of the reforms in the UN, two slots were proposed in the Security Council for|Asia|Africa|America|Europe": "The UN Security Council has 15 members: five permanent with the veto (USA, UK, France, Russia, China) and ten non-permanent members elected for two years. Africa, with over a quarter of UN members, has no permanent seat. In the reform debate, the African Union's Ezulwini Consensus (2005) demands two permanent seats with full rights, including the veto, and five non-permanent seats for Africa. So the two slots were proposed for Africa: B. The trap: Asia (A) also has candidates for new permanent seats (India and Japan), but the famous demand for \"two permanent slots\" is Africa's. No reform has been adopted so far. Memory tip: Africa asks for two seats at the top table.",
 "|The founding members of OPEC are|Algeria, Iran, Iraq, Saudi Arabia and Kuwait|Nigeria, Libya, Iraq and Saudi Arabia|Venezuela, Nigeria, Libya, Iran and Iraq|Saudi Arabia, Iran, Iraq, Kuwait and Venezuela": "The Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) was founded at the Baghdad Conference of 10–14 September 1960 by five countries: Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia and Venezuela: D. Its headquarters is in Vienna, Austria. Others joined later: Libya (1962), Algeria (1969) and Nigeria (1971). Some members have since left, but the five founders are unchanged. The trap: options A, B and C each include a later member, Algeria, Libya or Nigeria; Nigeria is a prominent member but not a founder. Memory tip: founders = the four Gulf states plus Venezuela; Nigeria came eleven years later, in 1971.",
 "|Power that is delegated is exercised|by devolution|directly|by coercion|indirectly": "To delegate power is to let someone else use it on your behalf. Parliament delegates law-making to ministers; a governor delegates duties to commissioners. The power still belongs to the original holder, but he uses it through another person or body, so delegated power is exercised indirectly: D. This is a debatable point, since devolution also involves handing power down, so some readers link the two. The trap: \"by devolution\" (A) looks right because both words mean handing power down, but devolution describes how power is transferred to lower units, not how delegated power is used. Memory tip: delegate = \"do it for me\", so the holder acts through someone else, indirectly.",
 "|De jure sovereign is acquired through|law|grant|treaty|force": "\"De jure\" is Latin for \"by law\". A de jure sovereign is the authority that has the legal right to rule, because the constitution and the laws recognise it. Its opposite is the de facto sovereign (\"in fact\"), which actually holds power whether or not the law backs it, for example a government that seized power in a coup. So de jure sovereignty is acquired through law: A. The trap: force (D) is how a de facto sovereign often gets power; power seized by force becomes de jure only when the law later recognises it. Memory tip: de JURE = JURists and law; de FACTO = FACT of power on the ground.",
 "|A group of people who live together under a common law within a definite territory is a|community|nation-state|nation|state": "A state has four elements: a population, a definite territory, a government that makes and enforces common laws, and sovereignty. A group of people living together under a common law within a definite territory therefore describes a state: D. The trap: nation (C) also means a group of people, but what binds a nation is a shared culture, language, history or descent, not a common law or territory; the Yoruba nation, for example, spreads across Nigeria and Benin. A nation-state (B) is a state whose people are also one nation, which is narrower than the question asks. Memory tip: state = law + land; nation = culture + common feeling.",
 "|Political socialization is associated with|military take-over of civilian government|the transmission of political values|political transition|free choice of party programmes": "Political socialisation is the process by which people learn political values, attitudes and beliefs, and by which a society passes them from one generation to the next. The agents are the family, school, peer groups, religious bodies, the mass media and political parties. So it is associated with the transmission of political values: B. The trap: political transition (C) sounds related, but it means a change from one government or regime to another, such as military to civilian rule, and has nothing to do with how values are learnt. Memory tip: socialisation = schooling in politics; it hands values down.",
 "|According to Aristotle, a form of government in which the few rule for the benefit of all is|diarchy|aristocracy|autocracy|polyarchy": "Aristotle sorted governments by who rules (one, few or many) and for whose benefit. Good forms rule for all: monarchy (one), aristocracy (few) and polity (many). Corrupt forms rule for themselves: tyranny, oligarchy and democracy (in his sense, mob rule). Rule by the few for the benefit of all is therefore aristocracy: B. Autocracy (C) is rule by one person, diarchy (A) is rule by two, and polyarchy (D) is rule by many competing groups; Aristotle called good rule by the few aristocracy. Memory tip: aristos = \"the best\"; the best few rule for everybody.",
 "|Rule by the old people is known as|monarchy|gerontocracy|feudalism|theocracy": "Gerontocracy comes from the Greek \"geron\", old man: it is government by the elders. Pre-colonial Igbo society is the classic Nigerian example, where councils of elders, the ndichie, together with age grades and the village assembly, ran community affairs. So rule by the old people is gerontocracy: B. Monarchy (A) is rule by one hereditary ruler whatever their age, feudalism (C) is a land-based system and theocracy (D) is rule in God's name. Memory tip: GERONTOcracy and geriatrics (care of the old) share the same Greek root.",
 "|As an executive, the commissioner is charged with the responsibility of|implementing laws|writing laws|giving loans|making laws": "Government has three arms: the legislature makes laws, the executive implements them and the judiciary interprets them. A commissioner in a Nigerian state is appointed by the governor, sits in the state executive council and heads a ministry, so as part of the executive his job is to implement laws and policies: A. Writing (B) and making (D) laws belong to the legislature, here the state House of Assembly, and giving loans (C) is not a commissioner's role. Memory tip: executive = execute; commissioners carry out what the Assembly passes.",
 "|Rules adjudication is a primary function of the|judiciary|executive|government|legislature": "Political scientists (after Gabriel Almond) name the outputs of government as rule making, rule application and rule adjudication. Rule adjudication means deciding disputes by interpreting the law and applying it to particular cases, and that is the work of the courts. So it is a primary function of the judiciary: A. The trap: government (C) is too broad: in a narrow sense it means the executive, and in a broad sense it covers all three arms, so it does not single out the organ whose main job is adjudication. Memory tip: adjudicate = judge, so think judiciary.",
 "|The judiciary controls the executive in a federal state through|delegated legislation|judicial overview|judicial review|motions": "Judicial review is the power of the courts to examine laws and executive actions and declare them null and void if they go against the constitution. In a federal state, with a written and supreme constitution, it is the judiciary's main check on the executive, for example when a court sets aside an unconstitutional directive. So it controls the executive through judicial review: C. The trap: B, judicial overview, sounds almost identical to judicial review but is not a recognised term; delegated legislation (A) is rule-making power given to the executive, which courts can review. Memory tip: courts REVIEW, legislatures LEGISLATE, executives EXECUTE.",
 "|One major advantage of the unitary system is that it tends to make government|free of controversy|distant from the people|popular among the masses|strong and stable": "In a unitary system all power is concentrated in one central government; any local units only exercise what the centre gives them. Decisions are quick, policies are uniform, and there is no conflict between levels of government, so government tends to be strong and stable: D. Being distant from the people (B) is a drawback of a unitary system, not an advantage, and no system is free of controversy (A). Memory tip: unitary = one centre, one voice, a strong and stable government.",
 "|The presidential system differs from the parliamentary system of government in that|the principle of collective responsibility applies|executive and legislative powers are fused|powers of the three arms of government are merged|the tenure of office of the president is limited": "In the presidential system the president is elected for a fixed term and can serve only a limited number of terms; in Nigeria, two terms of four years. A prime minister has no fixed tenure and stays in office as long as he keeps a majority in parliament. So the presidential system differs in that the president's tenure is limited: D. The trap: collective responsibility (A) and fusion of executive and legislative powers (B) are real features, but they belong to the parliamentary system, so they are what the presidential system does not have. Memory tip: president = fixed term and term limits; prime minister = no fixed term, as long as he keeps the majority.",
 "|Which of the following advocates equitable distribution of wealth?|Capitalism|Aristocracy|Socialism|Plutocracy": "Socialism holds that the main means of production should be owned by the public (the state or the community) so that wealth is distributed fairly, \"from each according to his ability, to each according to his work\". It therefore advocates equitable distribution of wealth: C. Capitalism (A) leaves distribution to private ownership and the market, which produces inequality, while aristocracy (B) and plutocracy (D) are forms of rule by a privileged few. Memory tip: SOcialism = SHAre; capitalism = CAPital in private hands.",
 "|A constitution that is difficult to amend is|rigid|written|unwritten|flexible": "Constitutions are rigid or flexible according to how easily they can be amended. A rigid constitution needs a special procedure, such as two-thirds majorities in the legislature plus approval by most of the state assemblies, as in Nigeria and the USA. So a constitution that is difficult to amend is rigid: A. The trap: written (B) is tempting because most rigid constitutions are written, but being written describes the form, not how hard it is to change; some written constitutions are flexible. Memory tip: rigid = stiff and hard to bend; flexible = bends with an ordinary law.",
 "|Which of the following constitutions is more suitable for centralization of political power?|Unwritten constitution|Rigid constitution|Written constitution|Flexible constitution": "A flexible constitution can be amended by an ordinary act of the legislature, so the central government can easily take more power to itself. That is why unitary states, where power is centralised, usually have flexible constitutions, while federations need rigid ones to protect the powers of the states. So the constitution more suitable for centralisation is the flexible one: D. The trap: unwritten (A) is close, because the UK's unwritten constitution is flexible and the UK is unitary, but it is the ease of amendment, not whether it is written, that makes power easy to centralise. Memory tip: flexible bends towards the centre; rigid keeps the federal balance.",
 "|The act of transferring autonomous powers to subordinate agencies is|concentration|deconcentration|delegation|devolution": "When the central government transfers powers to subordinate units such as regions or local councils, which then exercise them in their own right with a measure of autonomy, it is devolution: D. The word \"autonomous\" is the clue. Some books treat delegation and devolution loosely as the same, so this point is arguable. The trap: delegation (C) also passes power down, but the person or body receiving delegated power acts on behalf of, and answers to, the one who delegated it, so it is not autonomous. Deconcentration (B) only moves central officials to the field. Memory tip: DEvolution = DEcisions made down there in their own right.",
 "|Which of the following types of citizenship cannot be withdrawn?|Citizenship by conquest|Citizenship by birth|Honorary citizenship|Citizenship by naturalization": "Citizenship by birth is acquired because a person was born to parents who are citizens (and in some countries by being born on the soil). It is a birthright, so it cannot be withdrawn; under the Nigerian Constitution only citizenship by registration or naturalisation can be taken away. So it is citizenship by birth: B. The trap: honorary citizenship (C) sounds permanent and prestigious, but it is a courtesy granted by the state, and what the state grants it can take back. Memory tip: born a citizen, always a citizen; naturalised citizens can lose it.",
 "|The right of citizens to vote is|universal suffrage|nationality suffrage|electoral suffrage|adult suffrage": "Suffrage, also called the franchise, is the right to vote. Universal suffrage is the right of all qualified citizens to vote, without restrictions of sex, wealth, religion, education or ethnic group, normally from a set age (18 in Nigeria). So the right of citizens to vote is universal suffrage: A. The trap: adult suffrage (D) also describes voting rights, but it stresses the age condition only; \"universal\" is the standard term for every citizen's right to vote. Memory tip: universal suffrage = one citizen, one vote, whoever they are.",
 "|A political party is different from a pressure group in its|objective|organization|strategy|source of finance": "Political parties and pressure groups are both organised, both raise funds and both use strategies to influence government. What really separates them is their objective: a party aims to win elections and take control of government; a pressure group aims only to influence government policy in its members' interest, without taking office. So they differ in their objective: A. The trap: organisation (B) is tempting, but both bodies are organised groups with leaders and members, so organisation is shared, not a difference. Memory tip: party wants the power; pressure group wants the policy.",
 "|One of the functions of pressure groups is to|nominate the president|prepare the budget|articulate the opinion of their members|contest elections to serve the people": "A pressure group exists to promote its members' interests, so its main functions are to gather and express (articulate) their opinions, inform government and press for favourable policies, through lobbying, petitions, strikes and the media. So one function is to articulate the opinion of their members: C. The trap: contesting elections (D) sounds public-spirited, but that is the work of political parties; a pressure group that starts contesting elections has become a party. Nominating the president (A) and preparing the budget (B) belong to parties and the executive. Memory tip: pressure groups speak for their members; parties stand for office.",
 "|Public opinion refers to the|aggregate views of groups on particular government activities|views held by the president of a country|views of the chief justice of a country|aggregate of attitudes held by members of the national assembly": "Public opinion is the aggregate, or sum, of the views held by the people or groups in society on public issues and government activities at a given time. It is measured through opinion polls, elections, the press and protests, and it guides and checks government. So it refers to the aggregate views of groups on particular government activities: A. The trap: the aggregate of attitudes held by members of the National Assembly (D) has the right word \"aggregate\", but these are the views of a few hundred office holders, not of the public. Memory tip: PUBLIC opinion belongs to the people, not to office holders.",
 "|The class that oversees the implementation of government decisions and policies is the|executive|clerical|technical|administrative": "In the classic civil service structure there are four classes: administrative (policy advice and formulation), executive, professional/technical, and clerical. The executive class carries out the decisions and policies of government and supervises the day-to-day work of putting them into effect, so the class that oversees implementation is the executive class: A. This point is arguable, because some books say the administrative class supervises the whole ministry. The trap: administrative (D) is tempting because it is the top class, but its special role is advising ministers and formulating policy, while executing it is the executive class's job. Memory tip: administrators advise; executives execute.",
 "|The performance of ritual rites in the Yoruba empire is the responsibility of the|Aare-Ona-Kakanfo|Oba|Ogboni|Oyo-Mesi": "In the Oyo empire, power was balanced between the Alaafin (the Oba), the Oyo Mesi (seven kingmakers led by the Bashorun), the Ogboni and the Aare-Ona-Kakanfo (army commander). The Ogboni was a powerful cult of elders linked to the earth deity; it handled religious and ritual matters and checked both the Alaafin and the Oyo Mesi. So ritual rites were the Ogboni's responsibility: C. The trap: Oyo Mesi (D) is the best-known Yoruba council, but it was the kingmaker and advisory body that could ask the Alaafin to commit suicide, not the ritual cult. Memory tip: Oyo Mesi makes kings; Ogboni keeps the rites; Kakanfo leads the army.",
 "|Under the emirate system, the commander of the army is the|Hakimi|Sarkin Fada|Madawaki|Alkali": "The Hausa-Fulani emir ruled through titled officials: the Waziri (chief adviser, like a prime minister), the Galadima (in charge of the capital), the Madawaki (commander of the army), the Sarkin Fada (head of palace affairs), the Hakimi (district heads) and the Alkali (Islamic judge). So the commander of the army was the Madawaki: C. The trap: Hakimi (A) were important officials, but they were district heads who collected taxes and kept order in the districts, not army commanders. Memory tip: MADawaki leads the MArch to war; Alkali = law; Hakimi = districts.",
 "|The indirect rule system of administration was more successful in Northern Nigeria because|of the existence of an organized structure in the area|the Europeans ensured that the farmlands|the natives showed little or no resistance|the people were mainly interested in being governed indirectly": "Lord Lugard's indirect rule meant ruling through traditional rulers and their institutions. It worked best in the North because the emirates already had a centralised, organised structure: an emir with clear authority, officials, a tax system (zakat and jangali) and Alkali courts. The British simply used this machinery. So it was more successful because of the existing organised structure: A. The trap: \"the natives showed little or no resistance\" (C) is tempting, but the North did resist (Sokoto and Kano fell to British force in 1903); success came from the structure, not passivity. Memory tip: indirect rule needs a ready-made structure, which the North had and the East lacked.",
 "|The earliest nationalist activities in Nigeria were spearheaded by|trade unions|traditional rulers|political parties|educated elite": "Resistance to British rule began with traditional rulers defending their kingdoms and trade: Jaja of Opobo (deported 1887), Nana of Itsekiri (1894) and Oba Ovonramwen of Benin (1897) are famous examples. Many writers treat these early struggles as the earliest nationalist activities, so the answer is traditional rulers: B. This point is debatable, because many textbooks reserve \"nationalism\" for the organised movements of the educated elite after 1920. The trap: educated elite (D) is tempting because people like Herbert Macaulay led modern nationalism from the 1920s, but they came after the traditional rulers' resistance. Memory tip: first the kings resisted, then the scholars organised.",
 "|The first notable nationalist movement in West Africa was the|West African Students' Union|Nigerian Youth Movement|Aborigines' Rights Protection Society|National Congress of British West Africa": "The National Congress of British West Africa (NCBWA) was founded in Accra in 1920 under J. E. Casely Hayford and brought together leaders from Nigeria, the Gold Coast, Sierra Leone and the Gambia. Its petition to London helped win the elective principle in Nigeria's 1922 Clifford Constitution. It is regarded as the first notable nationalist movement in West Africa: D. The trap: the Aborigines' Rights Protection Society (C) was older (1897), but it was a Gold Coast body fighting land laws, not a West Africa-wide nationalist movement. Memory tip: NCBWA 1920 led to the elective principle in 1922.",
 "|In Nigeria's first republic, the prime minister was both the|head of state and commander-in-chief of the armed forces|commander-in-chief of the armed forces and party leader|head of state and party leader|head of government and a lawmaker": "Nigeria's First Republic (1960–1966) used the parliamentary system. The Prime Minister, Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa, was head of government and also an elected member of the House of Representatives, so he was a lawmaker too. So he was head of government and a lawmaker: D. The trap: A and C call the prime minister head of state, but that was the Queen, represented by the Governor-General (Azikiwe from November 1960), and after 1963 the ceremonial President, Azikiwe. Memory tip: in a parliamentary system the PM sits in parliament and runs the government; the head of state is ceremonial.",
 "|Under the 1979 Constitution, statutory allocation of revenue to local government councils is the responsibility of the|House of Assembly|National Economic Council|Federal Legislature|Council of State": "Section 7(6) of the 1979 Constitution (repeated in 1999) shared this duty: (a) the National Assembly provides for statutory allocation of public revenue to local government councils in the federation, and (b) each state House of Assembly provides for statutory allocation to the councils within its state. So the House of Assembly is responsible: A. The Federal Legislature (C) is also correct, so both are accepted. The trap: the National Economic Council (B) sounds expert, but it only advises on economic planning; it does not make allocations. Memory tip: section 7(6): the National Assembly provides at federal level, the state House of Assembly within the state.",
 "|Under Nigeria's Second Republic, the Senate was under the leadership of|J. S. Tarka|Joseph Wayas|Edwin Ume-Ezeoke|John Wash Pam": "In the Second Republic (1979–1983) the National Assembly had two chambers. Joseph Wayas of the NPN, from Cross River, was President of the Senate, with John Wash Pam as Deputy President. In the House of Representatives the Speaker was Edwin Ume-Ezeoke of the NPP. So the Senate was led by Joseph Wayas: B. The trap: John Wash Pam (D) did sit at the top table of the Senate, but as Deputy Senate President, not its leader. J. S. Tarka was an NPN senator and party leader. Memory tip: Wayas held the Senate's ways; Ume-Ezeoke held the House.",
 "|The Revenue Mobilization, Allocation and Fiscal Commission is statutorily empowered to determine the remuneration of|only elected representatives|political office holders|employees of public corporations|all civil servants": "The Revenue Mobilization, Allocation and Fiscal Commission (RMAFC) was set up in 1989 and is in the 1999 Constitution (Third Schedule, Part I). Besides monitoring revenue into the Federation Account and reviewing the allocation formula, it determines the remuneration of political office holders, including the President, governors, legislators, ministers, commissioners and judicial officers. So the answer is political office holders: B. The trap: \"only elected representatives\" (A) is too narrow, because RMAFC also fixes pay for appointed office holders such as ministers and commissioners. Civil servants' pay is set by bodies like the National Salaries, Incomes and Wages Commission. Memory tip: RMAFC pays the politicians; salary commissions pay civil servants.",
 "|The primary function of the Code of Conduct Bureau is to|ensure minimum standard of morality|retain custody of declarations|receive declaration of assets|ensure due process by public officers": "The Code of Conduct Bureau, created by the 1979 Constitution and kept in 1999, enforces the Code of Conduct for public officers. Its first listed function is to receive the declarations of assets that public officers must make on taking office, during their service and when they leave, then examine them and refer breaches to the Code of Conduct Tribunal. So its primary function is to receive declarations of assets: C. The trap: \"retain custody of declarations\" (B) is also one of its duties, but only after it has received and examined them, so it is secondary. Memory tip: CCB = Collect, Check and keep the declarations.",
 "|The party system practised in Nigeria's Third Republic was|two-party|zero-party|one-party|multi-party": "General Ibrahim Babangida's transition programme for the Third Republic decreed that only two parties would exist. In 1989 his government refused all the associations that applied for registration and created two parties itself: the Social Democratic Party (SDP), \"a little to the left\", and the National Republican Convention (NRC), \"a little to the right\". So it was a two-party system: A. The trap: multi-party (D) is the system of the First, Second and Fourth Republics, which students often assume for every republic. Memory tip: Third Republic = two parties, SDP and NRC.",
 "|Nigeria adopted the federal system of government because of|uneven development|the availability of limited resources|the adoption of a state religion|the fear of domination of minorities": "Nigeria is a large country with over 250 ethnic groups, and the smaller ones feared being dominated by the big three (Hausa-Fulani, Yoruba, Igbo). Federalism lets each region or state manage its own affairs while sharing a central government, which eases that fear; this is a main reason Nigeria adopted it. So the answer is the fear of domination of minorities: D. The trap: uneven development (A) was a real feature of colonial Nigeria, but federalism was not adopted because of it; it was adopted to hold diverse groups together. Memory tip: many peoples, one country, so share power to calm fears.",
 "|The highest policy-making body under the Gowon regime was the|Armed Forces Ruling Council|Provisional Ruling Council|Supreme Military Council|Federal Executive Council": "Nigeria's military regimes each had a supreme ruling council. From Ironsi through Gowon (1966–1975) and on to Murtala and Obasanjo, it was the Supreme Military Council (SMC); Buhari kept the SMC. Babangida renamed it the Armed Forces Ruling Council (AFRC) in 1985, and Abacha used the Provisional Ruling Council (PRC). So under Gowon it was the Supreme Military Council: C. The trap: the Federal Executive Council (D) existed under Gowon too, but it was the cabinet that carried out policy, below the SMC. Memory tip: SMC (Gowon to Buhari), AFRC (Babangida), PRC (Abacha and Abubakar).",
 "|Nigeria became a federation of thirty-six states during the era of|Abdulsalami Abubakar|Yakubu Gowon|Ibrahim Babangida|Sani Abacha": "State creation timeline: 12 states (Gowon, 1967), 19 (Murtala, 1976), 21 (Babangida, 1987), 30 (Babangida, 1991) and 36 (Abacha, 1 October 1996). Nigeria has had 36 states, plus the Federal Capital Territory, ever since. So it became a federation of 36 states under Sani Abacha: D. The trap: Ibrahim Babangida (C) created more states than anyone else (21 and then 30), so students often credit him, but he stopped at 30. Memory tip: 12-19-21-30-36 = Gowon, Murtala, Babangida, Babangida, Abacha.",
 "|Which of the following headed the committee that recommended the suitability of Abuja as a new federal capital city?|Justice Baba Ardo|Justice Atanda Fatai Williams|Justice Udo Udoma|Justice Akinola Aguda": "In August 1975 the Murtala Mohammed regime set up the Committee on the Location of the Federal Capital, headed by Justice Akinola Aguda. It recommended a central, neutral site around Abuja, and Decree No. 6 of February 1976 created the Federal Capital Territory; the capital moved there in December 1991. So the committee was headed by Justice Akinola Aguda: D. The trap: Justice Udo Udoma (C) is a famous name too, but he chaired the 1977–78 Constituent Assembly that debated the 1979 Constitution. Memory tip: Aguda found Abuja (both start with A).",
 "|A public corporation is managed by|a minister|a general manager|the board of governors|the board of directors": "A public corporation, such as NNPC or the old NEPA, is owned by government but run like a business. It is managed by a board of directors appointed by government, which makes its policy; the managing director or general manager handles the day-to-day running under the board, and the supervising minister only gives general directions. So it is managed by the board of directors: D. The trap: a minister (A) has oversight and can give general policy directions, but does not manage the corporation; that independence from daily political control is the point of a corporation. Memory tip: a public corporation is like a company, run by its board.",
 "|A major source of revenue in the post-1976 local government in Nigeria is|internally generated revenue|the federation account|grants and loans|the joint state-local government account": "The 1976 local government reform made local government the third tier of government, with a uniform structure across the country and a constitutional right to a share of federally collected revenue. Since then the councils' statutory allocation from the Federation Account has been by far their largest source of revenue. So the major source is the federation account: B. The trap: internally generated revenue (A), from rates, fees, licences and fines, is the source councils are meant to develop, but it is small compared with the federal allocation. Memory tip: since 1976, the third tier gets a share of the national cake.",
 "|The Provisional Ruling Council was the highest ruling body during the regime of|Muhammadu Buhari|Ibrahim Babangida|Murtala Muhammed|Sani Abacha": "General Sani Abacha took power in November 1993 and ruled through the Provisional Ruling Council (PRC) until his death in June 1998; Abdulsalami Abubakar kept the PRC until handing over in May 1999. So the PRC was the highest ruling body under Sani Abacha: D. The trap: Ibrahim Babangida (B) also had a ruling council with a similar ring, but his was the Armed Forces Ruling Council (AFRC), and Buhari and Murtala used the Supreme Military Council. Memory tip: PRC = the last council, used by the last military rulers, Abacha and Abubakar.",
 "|Nigeria's non-alignment policy in the sixties lacked real substance because of her|Afrocentric policy|poor economic potential|partnership with Asian countries|close ties with Britain": "At independence the Balewa government declared non-alignment in the Cold War, but in practice it leaned firmly to Britain and the West. It signed the Anglo-Nigerian Defence Pact (abrogated in 1962 after student protests), kept British officers in key posts and was slow to open relations with the Soviet bloc. So its non-alignment lacked substance because of close ties with Britain: D. The trap: Afrocentric policy (A) was real, but making Africa the centre-piece did not tie Nigeria to either Cold War bloc, so it did not weaken non-alignment. Memory tip: non-aligned on paper, British-aligned in practice.",
 "|Under the Technical Aid Corps, Nigerian experts are deployed to|Africa, the Pacific and the Caribbean|Europe, South America and Asia|the Pacific, the Caribbean and Europe|Asia, Africa and the Pacific": "The Technical Aid Corps (TAC) was set up in 1987, under Babangida, as Nigeria's alternative to foreign aid: instead of money, it sends Nigerian professionals such as doctors, nurses, teachers, lawyers and engineers to serve for a period in developing countries. Its targets are the African, Caribbean and Pacific (ACP) countries. So experts go to Africa, the Pacific and the Caribbean: A. The trap: Asia, Africa and the Pacific (D) has two of the three right, but Asia replaces the Caribbean, which is a main TAC region. Memory tip: TAC follows the ACP: Africa, Caribbean, Pacific.",
 "|The centre-piece of Nigeria's foreign policy covers only|Europe|Africa|Latin America|Asia": "From independence Nigeria declared Africa the centre-piece (cornerstone) of its foreign policy: ending colonialism and apartheid, promoting African unity through the OAU/AU and ECOWAS, peace-keeping (as with ECOMOG in Liberia and Sierra Leone) and aid to African states. So the centre-piece covers Africa: B. Europe (A), especially Britain, matters to Nigeria's trade and history, but Africa is the centre-piece of its foreign policy. Memory tip: Nigeria's foreign policy circles start from the centre, which is Africa.",
 "|Which of the following countries pioneered the establishment of ECOWAS alongside Nigeria?|Ghana|Togo|Algeria|Cameroun": "ECOWAS (the Economic Community of West African States) was created by the Treaty of Lagos, signed on 28 May 1975 by 15 states. The drive came from General Yakubu Gowon of Nigeria and General Gnassingbé Eyadéma of Togo, who toured the region together to win support. So Togo pioneered ECOWAS alongside Nigeria: B. The trap: Ghana (A) is the West African country students most often pair with Nigeria, but it was not the co-founder of the scheme; Algeria and Cameroon are not even ECOWAS members. Memory tip: ECOWAS was born of Gowon and Eyadéma, Nigeria and Togo.",
 "|Nigeria's role in the African Union was most prominent during the regime of|President Olusegun Obasanjo|President Shehu Shagari|President Umaru Yar'adua|President Ibrahim Babangida": "The African Union replaced the OAU at the Durban summit in July 2002. President Olusegun Obasanjo (1999–2007) was among its chief architects, helped create NEPAD and the Peace and Security Council, and served as AU Chairman from 2004 to 2006. So Nigeria's role in the AU was most prominent under Obasanjo: A. The trap: Umaru Yar'Adua (C) did rule during the AU era (2007–2010), but his illness and short tenure meant his role was modest; Shagari and Babangida ruled when the body was still the OAU. Memory tip: Obasanjo saw the AU born and chaired it.",
 "|A representative of a Commonwealth country in another member state is known as|Consul-General|Ambassador|Attaché|High Commissioner": "Commonwealth countries regard one another as a family rather than as foreign states, so they exchange High Commissioners instead of ambassadors. A High Commissioner has the same rank and functions as an ambassador. Nigeria's representative in Ghana or London is therefore a High Commissioner, while its representative in France is an ambassador. So the answer is High Commissioner: D. The trap: B, ambassador, is the usual title for a head of mission, but between Commonwealth member states the head of mission is called a High Commissioner. Memory tip: Commonwealth = High Commission; others = Embassy.",
 "|The organ of UN that promotes voluntary co-operation among member states in diverse areas is the|International Court of Justice|General Assembly|Economic and Social Council|Security Council": "The General Assembly includes every UN member with one vote each. Under Article 13 of the Charter it initiates studies and makes recommendations to promote international co-operation in political, economic, social, cultural, educational and health fields. Its resolutions are only recommendations, so co-operation through it is voluntary. So the answer is the General Assembly: B. This point is arguable, as ECOSOC also works on co-operation. The trap: the Economic and Social Council (C) does co-ordinate economic and social work, but it acts under the authority of the General Assembly and in a narrower range of areas. Memory tip: the Assembly recommends; the Security Council can command.",
 "|The main representative body of the United Nations is the|Security Council|General Assembly|Trusteeship Council|Secretariat": "The General Assembly is the UN's main deliberative and representative organ: all 193 member states sit in it, each with one equal vote, and it discusses any matter within the Charter, admits members, approves the budget and elects non-permanent members of the Security Council. So the main representative body is the General Assembly: B. The trap: the Security Council (A) is the most powerful organ, but with only 15 members (5 permanent), it is not representative of all states. Memory tip: General Assembly = everyone at the table; Security Council = the few with power.",
 "|The AU differs from the OAU in having|more effective tools for decision enforcement|no permanent headquarters|a minimum of divergent viewpoints|no assembly of Heads of State": "The OAU (1963) was criticised as a \"talking shop\": its principle of non-interference meant it could not act against members. The AU (2002) has stronger tools to enforce its decisions: Article 4(h) of its Constitutive Act allows intervention in grave cases such as genocide and war crimes, it suspends governments that take power unconstitutionally, imposes sanctions, and has a Peace and Security Council. So it has more effective tools for decision enforcement: A. The AU still has an Assembly of Heads of State (D) as its supreme organ and a permanent headquarters in Addis Ababa (B); it differs from the OAU in having stronger enforcement tools. Memory tip: OAU talked; AU can act.",
 "|OPEC has strong influence with the|EU|ADB|AU|IMF": "OPEC (1960) coordinates the oil policies of its members. Oil prices affect the balance of payments of almost every country, which is what the IMF watches over, and the huge oil earnings of OPEC members after 1973 made them major lenders to the Fund, which borrowed from them for its 1974 and 1975 oil facilities to help countries hit by high oil prices. So OPEC has strong influence with the IMF: D. OPEC is a global body with no special say in the AU (C), even though several of its members are African. Memory tip: oil money flows through the IMF.",
 "|The necessary attributes of a state are|police, army, sovereignty and custom|resources, population, sovereignty and government|sovereignty, police, army and immigration|definite territory, population, sovereignty and government": "A state is not just any group of people. Political scientists agree it needs four essential elements: a population (people to govern), a definite territory (land with known boundaries), a government (the machinery that makes and enforces decisions), and sovereignty (supreme power at home and independence from outside control). Take any one away and there is no state. So the necessary attributes are definite territory, population, sovereignty and government: D. The trap: B looks almost the same, but \"resources\" replaces territory; a state can be poor in resources and still be a state, yet it cannot exist without land. Memory tip: P-T-G-S, \"People Take Government Seriously.\"",
 "|The process of taking part in political and public affairs can be termed political|socialization|recognition|culture|participation": "Political participation means taking part in the political and public life of the community. It includes voting, contesting elections, joining political parties or pressure groups, attending rallies, writing to representatives and joining public debate. The higher the participation, the healthier a democracy is said to be. So taking part in political and public affairs is political participation: D. The trap: political socialisation (A) also sounds like \"getting involved\", but it is the learning process by which people acquire political attitudes and values, not the act of taking part. Memory tip: socialisation = learning politics; participation = doing politics.",
 "|Membership of a society is|constitutional|conventional|mandatory|voluntary": "A society, in the sense used here, is an association of people who come together for a common purpose: a club, a trade union, a church, a professional body. People join it by choice and can resign whenever they wish. This is the key difference from the state, whose membership is compulsory: you are born into it and are bound by its laws whether you like it or not. So membership of a society is voluntary: D. The trap: mandatory (C) is the word that describes membership of the state, not of a society, which is why students who mix the two pick it. Memory tip: you choose your club, but not your country's laws.",
 "|In a democratic political system, the political sovereign is usually the|legislature|constitution|political parties|electorate": "Sovereignty has two sides. Legal sovereignty belongs to the body whose law is final, such as parliament or the constitution. Political sovereignty is the power that stands behind the law-makers and can change them; in a democracy that power belongs to the people, who use it through the vote. The voters, the electorate, choose those who rule and can remove them at the next election. So the political sovereign in a democracy is the electorate: D. The trap: the legislature (A) is often called the legal sovereign because it makes the laws, but it answers to the voters, who are the political sovereign. Memory tip: legal sovereign makes the law; political sovereign makes the law-makers.",
 "|One basic feature of a monarchical form of government is that|the ruler has fixed tenure and power is absolute|separation of powers is absolute|members of the executive are elected|succession is through heredity": "A monarchy is government headed by a king or queen (or emir, oba, sultan) who inherits the throne. The crown passes within a royal family by birth, usually to the eldest son or nearest heir, and the monarch normally reigns for life. This hereditary succession is the defining mark of monarchy, whether the monarch is absolute (as in Saudi Arabia) or constitutional (as in Britain). So the basic feature is succession through heredity: D. The trap: A attracts students who picture absolute kings, but a monarch has no fixed tenure (he reigns for life), and many monarchs today have little power. Memory tip: monarchy = \"born to the throne.\"",
 "|One main advantage of the bicameral legislature is that it|is not easy to manipulate bills|makes for quick deliberation during emergencies|makes passage of bills easy|is less cumbersome to pass bills": "A bicameral legislature has two chambers, such as Nigeria's Senate and House of Representatives. Every bill must be passed by both houses, so each house checks the other. A bill rushed or manipulated through one chamber can be stopped, delayed or amended in the second. This guards against hasty, careless or self-serving legislation. So the main advantage is that bills are not easy to manipulate: A. Easy (C) and less cumbersome (D) passage of bills and quick emergency deliberation (B) are advantages of a single chamber; two houses slow bills down, making them harder to manipulate. Memory tip: two houses, two locks on the door.",
 "|The court that has the ultimate power to interpret the constitution is the|Court of Appeal|Supreme Court|Magistrate Court|High Court": "In Nigeria the courts form a ladder: magistrate and customary courts at the bottom, then High Courts, then the Court of Appeal, and at the top the Supreme Court. Because the Supreme Court is the final court of appeal, its interpretation of the constitution settles the matter and binds every other court and every arm of government. So the court with the ultimate power to interpret the constitution is the Supreme Court: B. The trap: the Court of Appeal (A) also interprets the constitution, but its rulings can be appealed to the Supreme Court, so its word is not final. Memory tip: \"supreme\" means \"last word.\"",
 "|The unitary system of government is more suitable to a country|with a relatively small area and a homogeneous population|that is sparsely populated|that possesses a strong and modern army|with a robust and dynamic economy": "In a unitary state all power belongs to the central government, which may hand some tasks to local units but can take them back. This works best where the country is small enough to be run from one centre and its people are homogeneous, sharing one language, culture or religion, so there are no strong regional groups demanding self-rule. Britain and Ghana are examples. So unitary government suits a small, homogeneous country: A. The trap: B (sparsely populated) sounds plausible, but a thinly populated country can be huge and varied; it is size and diversity, not density, that decide the matter. Memory tip: small and similar = unitary; big and diverse = federal.",
 "|An example of a country ruled by a constitutional monarch is|Libya|Uganda|Morocco|Italy": "A constitutional monarch is a hereditary king or queen whose powers are limited by a constitution, with an elected parliament and government doing most of the ruling. Morocco is a kingdom of this kind: King Mohammed VI reigns under a written constitution, alongside an elected parliament and a prime minister. Libya, Uganda and Italy are all republics. So the example is Morocco: C. The trap: Libya (A) tempts because it once had a king, but King Idris was overthrown by Gaddafi in 1969 and Libya has had no monarch since. Memory tip: Morocco, like Britain, Spain and Jordan, still has a king who reigns under a constitution.",
 "|The development of a classless society is the goal of|Marxism|conservation|feudalism|liberalism": "Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels (The Communist Manifesto, 1848) argued that history is a story of class struggle between those who own the means of production and those who work for them. They predicted that the workers would overthrow capitalism, pass through socialism, and arrive at communism: a classless, stateless society where property is held in common. So the development of a classless society is the goal of Marxism: A. Liberalism (D) means equal rights before the law but defends the private property and markets that create classes, and feudalism (C) is built on class hierarchy. Memory tip: Marx's end point has no masters and no classes.",
 "|A flexible constitution is one which is|written by the parliament|easily amended|popular with the legislators|known to all the citizens": "Constitutions are classed as flexible or rigid by how easily they can be amended. A flexible constitution can be changed by the ordinary law-making process, a simple majority in the legislature, just like any other law; the British constitution is the classic example. A rigid constitution, like Nigeria's, needs a special procedure such as two-thirds majorities and approval by the states. So a flexible constitution is one that is easily amended: B. The trap: A (written by the parliament) mixes up flexibility with who wrote it or whether it is written; written constitutions are usually rigid, and flexibility is about amendment only. Memory tip: flexible bends easily; rigid needs special force.",
 "|Which of the following is a feature of checks and balances?|Code of conduct|Judicial precedent|Judicial immunity|Judicial review": "Checks and balances means each arm of government can limit the others so that none becomes too powerful. The legislature can impeach and control money; the executive can veto bills; and the judiciary can use judicial review, declaring a law or an executive action unconstitutional and void. Judicial review is therefore the judiciary's main check on the other two arms. So the feature of checks and balances is judicial review: D. The trap: judicial precedent (B) sounds similar, but it is the rule that courts follow earlier decisions; it binds the courts themselves and checks no other arm. Memory tip: review = the court \"re-views\" what the other arms did.",
 "|Laws made by military governments at the state level are called|acts|decrees|bye-laws|edicts": "Under military rule there was no legislature, so the soldiers made laws themselves. At the federal level the Supreme Military Council (later the Armed Forces Ruling Council or Provisional Ruling Council) issued decrees; at state level the military governors or administrators issued edicts. So laws made by military governments at the state level are edicts: D. The trap: decrees (B) were also military laws, which is why students pick it, but decrees came from the federal military government, not the states. Memory tip: Decrees from the Centre; Edicts for Each state.",
 "|Citizenship is acquired by an alien through|naturalization|registration|birth|conferment": "Citizenship can be acquired by birth, by registration or by naturalisation. Naturalisation is the general route for a foreigner (alien): after living in the country for the period the law requires (15 years in Nigeria), showing good character and the intention to settle, he or she applies, is granted a certificate and swears allegiance. So an alien acquires citizenship through naturalisation: A. The trap: registration (B) is also a way into citizenship, but it is a special route for limited groups, such as a foreign woman married to a Nigerian man or a person with a Nigerian grandparent; the general route for an alien is naturalisation. Birth (C) cannot apply to someone already an alien. Memory tip: natural-ise = to make a foreigner \"native.\"",
 "|The officer responsible for announcing the result of an election is known as|electoral officer|ballot officer|presiding officer|returning officer": "Every election has officers with set duties. The presiding officer runs a polling unit on election day and counts the votes there. The results from many units are then collated, and the returning officer for the constituency adds them up and formally declares, or \"returns\", the winner. So the officer who announces the result is the returning officer: D. The trap: the presiding officer (C) also handles votes and may announce the count at a polling unit, but he or she does not declare the final result of the election. Memory tip: the returning officer \"returns\" the name of the winner.",
 "|The ultimate aim of political parties is to|formulate and implement policies|implement people-oriented programmes|acquire and exercise power|increase the political awareness of the electorate": "A political party is an organised group of people with shared ideas who seek to win elections so as to control the government and use its power to carry out their programme. Formulating policies, running programmes and educating voters are all things parties do, but they are means to the ultimate end: gaining and exercising state power. So the ultimate aim of parties is to acquire and exercise power: C. The trap: A (formulate and implement policies) sounds noble, but a party can implement policies only after it has won power, so power is the ultimate aim. Memory tip: parties want to sit in the driver's seat; pressure groups want to shout directions.",
 "|The main objective of pressure groups is to|serve as opposition to the government|promote the interest of political parties|influence legislation for the benefit of their members|protect the interest of the country against foreigners": "A pressure (interest) group is an organised body, such as the NLC, NMA or MAN, that tries to influence government decisions in favour of its members without trying to take over government itself. It lobbies legislators, issues statements, petitions and sometimes strikes, all to shape laws and policies for its members' benefit. So the main objective of pressure groups is to influence legislation for their members' benefit: C. The trap: A (serve as opposition) attracts students because pressure groups often criticise government, but opposition is the role of political parties out of power, and pressure groups may support government when it suits their members. Memory tip: pressure groups push, they do not rule.",
 "|Which of the following is not a dimension of public opinion?|Substance|Polling|Orientation|Intensity": "Public opinion is the views held by a large part of the public on public issues. Writers describe it by its dimensions: its substance or content (what the opinion is about), its orientation or direction (for or against), and its intensity (how strongly people feel). Polling is not one of these; an opinion poll is a method of measuring public opinion, like elections or referendums. So the option that is not a dimension is polling: B. The trap: substance (A) sounds less technical than the others, but it is a real dimension, the content of the opinion. Many writers list substance as a dimension, so polling is the safer answer. Memory tip: dimensions describe the opinion; polls measure it.",
 "|The body that is responsible for the appointment, discipline, promotion and dismissal of civil servants is the|Ministry of Labour and Productivity|Ministry of Establishment|Bureau for Public Service Reforms|Civil Service Commission": "Civil servants must be hired, promoted and disciplined fairly, on merit, and not at the whim of politicians. For this reason the constitution sets up independent Civil Service Commissions: the Federal Civil Service Commission for the federal service and a commission in each state. They appoint, promote, discipline and dismiss civil servants. So the body responsible is the Civil Service Commission: D. The trap: the Ministry of Establishment (B) deals with staff matters such as posts, conditions of service and training, but the power to appoint, promote, discipline and dismiss belongs to the commission. Memory tip: the Commission controls the Career.",
 "|In the pre-colonial Hausa political system, the Madawaki performed the function of|Minister of Works|Minister of Education|Minister of Defence|Minister of Interior": "In the Hausa-Fulani emirate the emir ruled with a council of title-holders, each with a portfolio. The Waziri was the chief adviser (like a prime minister), the Galadima administered the capital, the Ma'aji kept the treasury, the Sarkin Ruwa handled water and fishing, and the Madawaki commanded the army. So the Madawaki performed the function of a Minister of Defence: C. The trap: Minister of Interior (D) tempts students who confuse the Madawaki with the Galadima, who looked after the capital city. Memory tip: Madawaki = Military; Ma'aji = Money; Waziri = Wise adviser.",
 "|In the Old Oyo Empire, the Ajele|ensured the safety of all trade routes|ensured good governance of the districts|mobilized the army|was the head of the army": "The Alaafin of Oyo ruled a large empire. To control the subject towns and districts, he posted resident representatives called Ajele. Each Ajele lived in a provincial town, watched over the local ruler, collected tribute for the Alaafin, settled minor matters and reported back to Oyo, making sure the district was well governed and loyal. So the Ajele ensured good governance of the districts: B. The trap: C and D (the army) tempt because the Ajele represented royal power, but command of Oyo's army belonged to the Are Ona Kakanfo. Memory tip: the Ajele was the Alaafin's \"eyes and ears\" in the districts.",
 "|The General Strike of 1945 was caused primarily by the|disparity in the criteria for employment|harshness in trade laws as it concerns the Africans|government's rejection of a demand for an increase of 50 percent in the cost of living allowance|persistent implementation of discriminatory laws": "During the Second World War prices in Nigeria rose sharply while wages stayed low. Workers demanded a 50 per cent increase in their cost of living allowance (COLA). When the colonial government refused, the workers, led by Michael Imoudu and backed by the NCNC and Zik's newspapers, began a general strike in June 1945 that lasted about six weeks. So the strike was caused by the rejection of the 50 per cent COLA demand: C. The trap: D (discriminatory laws) tempts because nationalists did complain of discrimination, but the immediate trigger of this strike was the COLA demand. Memory tip: 1945 strike = Imoudu + COLA.",
 "|Before 1945, the component units of Nigeria were|regions|districts|provinces|states": "After the 1914 amalgamation, Lugard ran Nigeria as two groups of provinces, the Northern Provinces and the Southern Provinces, each under a Lieutenant-Governor. In 1939 the Southern Provinces were split into Eastern and Western Provinces. Regions (North, West and East) were created only by the Richards Constitution of 1946, and states came in 1967. So before 1945 the component units were provinces: C. The trap: regions (A) is the unit students remember best from colonial history, but regions began only in 1946-47. Nigeria today has 36 states and the FCT. Memory tip: Provinces (1914), Regions (1946), States (1967), in that order.",
 "|National agitation began in Nigeria with the|formation of West African Youth League|Lagos protest against water rate in 1908|introduction of indirect rule|annexation of Lagos in 1861": "Organised political agitation in Nigeria is usually traced to Lagos in 1908, when the colonial government imposed a water rate to pay for the Iju waterworks. Lagosians protested strongly, the People's Union was formed (1908) to oppose it, and young Herbert Macaulay emerged as a political figure. This protest is widely taken as the beginning of nationalist agitation in Nigeria. So national agitation began with the Lagos protest against the water rate in 1908: B. The trap: A tempts because youth leagues were part of nationalism, but the West African Youth League was formed in the Gold Coast in the 1930s by I. T. A. Wallace-Johnson, long after agitation had begun in Nigeria. Memory tip: water rate 1908, \"nationalism began with a bill for water.\"",
 "|The emergence of nationalism was essentially the result of the ills of|imperialism|independence|slavery|colonialism": "Nationalism in Nigeria was a reaction to the hardships of colonial rule: racial discrimination in jobs and pay, exclusion of educated Africans from government, economic exploitation by foreign firms, forced taxation and the indignity of foreign rule. These ills of colonialism pushed educated Africans and workers to demand self-government and, later, independence. So nationalism was essentially the result of the ills of colonialism: D. The trap: imperialism (A) is closely related, but it is the wider policy of empire-building; what Nigerians experienced directly, and rose against, was colonial rule itself. Memory tip: colonialism was the disease, nationalism the cure.",
 "|Which of the following nationalists was the founder of Nigeria's first political party?|Herbert Macaulay|Abubakar Tafawa Balewa|Ahmadu Bello|Nnamdi Azikiwe": "The Clifford Constitution of 1922 introduced the elective principle, giving Lagos three elected seats and Calabar one in the Legislative Council. To contest those Lagos seats, Herbert Macaulay founded the Nigerian National Democratic Party (NNDP) in 1923, Nigeria's first political party. It won the Lagos seats in 1923, 1928 and 1933, and Macaulay is called the father of Nigerian nationalism. So the founder of Nigeria's first party was Herbert Macaulay: A. The trap: Nnamdi Azikiwe (D) is the best-known nationalist, but his NCNC was formed in 1944, more than twenty years after the NNDP. Memory tip: 1922 Clifford, 1923 Macaulay's NNDP.",
 "|The division of powers between the federal and regional governments into exclusive, concurrent and residual lists was done by the|1979 Constitution|1999 Constitution|Independence Constitution|1954 Constitution": "Federalism came to Nigeria with the Lyttelton Constitution of 1954. It made Nigeria a federation of three regions plus Lagos as federal territory, and for the first time divided legislative powers into an exclusive list (matters only the centre could legislate on, such as defence and currency), a concurrent list (shared matters) and residual matters left to the regions. So this division was done by the 1954 Constitution: D. The trap: the 1979 and 1999 Constitutions (A, B) also use exclusive and concurrent lists, which is why students pick them, but they inherited the arrangement; 1954 introduced it. Memory tip: Lyttelton 1954 laid the \"lists\" of federalism.",
 "|The Nigerian Independence Constitution was modified by the|1979 Constitution|1963 Constitution|1999 Constitution|1989 Constitution": "The 1960 Independence Constitution kept the British Queen as head of state, represented by a Governor-General, and kept appeals to the Privy Council in London. The 1963 Republican Constitution, in force from 1 October 1963, modified it: Nigeria became a republic with a ceremonial President (Nnamdi Azikiwe), and the Supreme Court became the final court. Most other features, including the parliamentary system, stayed. So the Independence Constitution was modified by the 1963 Constitution: B. The trap: the 1979 Constitution (A) brought bigger changes, but it replaced the parliamentary system with a presidential one, starting afresh after military rule. Memory tip: 1960 independent, 1963 republican.",
 "|The President of Nigeria was indirectly elected through secret ballot for a period of five years by the senate in|1979|1983|1960|1963": "Under the 1963 Republican Constitution Nigeria got its own President, but he was a ceremonial head of state, not directly elected by the people. He was chosen by secret ballot by the members of Parliament, the Senate and the House of Representatives sitting together, and held office for five years. Real power stayed with the Prime Minister. So the year is 1963: D. The trap: 1979 (A) tempts because it introduced the executive presidency, but the 1979 President was elected directly by the voters for four years. Memory tip: 1963 President chosen by Parliament; 1979 President chosen by the people.",
 "|The main function of the Federal Character Commission in Nigeria is|providing free social services to the citizens|ensuring fair representation of all states in the public service|reviewing unfair administrative decisions|settling disputes among societies": "Nigeria's many ethnic groups fear being shut out of government jobs. The federal character principle, written into the 1979 Constitution and enforced since 1996 by the Federal Character Commission (now in the 1999 Constitution), requires that posts in the public service, the armed forces and federal agencies be shared fairly among the states and local governments. So the Commission's main function is ensuring fair representation of all states in the public service: B. The trap: reviewing unfair administrative decisions (C) is the work of the Public Complaints Commission (the ombudsman), not the Federal Character Commission. Memory tip: federal character = every state gets a fair \"character\" in the service.",
 "|The power of appointing the chairman of the Independent National Electoral Commission is vested in the|Senate|Judicial Council|Council of State|President": "INEC is the body that conducts federal and state elections. Under section 154 of the 1999 Constitution its chairman and members are appointed by the President, after consulting the Council of State, and the appointment must be confirmed by the Senate. The power to appoint itself lies with the President. So the power is vested in the President: D. The trap: the Senate (A) and the Council of State (C) both take part, which is why students choose them, but the Council of State only advises and the Senate only confirms; neither appoints. Memory tip: the Council advises, the President appoints, the Senate confirms.",
 "|The NCNC and the NPC facilitated the creation of the|Eastern Region|Mid-West Region|Northern Region|Western Region": "In the First Republic the NPC (strong in the North) and the NCNC (strong in the East) formed the federal government, while the Action Group ruled the West. After the Action Group crisis of 1962, the coalition pushed through the creation of the Mid-Western Region out of the Western Region, approved by referendum in 1963. This weakened the Action Group and gave the minorities of the Benin and Delta areas a region of their own. So they facilitated the creation of the Mid-West Region: B. The trap: Western Region (D) tempts because the West was involved, but it was the region cut up, not the one created. Memory tip: 1963, \"the Mid-West came from the West.\"",
 "|One of the major problems of Nigerian federalism is|pre-colonial administrative structure among the units of federation|lack of revenue to cater for the demands of the federation|inadequate manpower to fill vacancies of the federation|imbalance in the structure and sizes of units of federation": "A federation works best when no single unit is strong enough to dominate the rest. Nigeria began with a lopsided structure: the Northern Region had more land and more people than the Eastern and Western Regions put together, so it could control the federal parliament. This imbalance caused fears of domination, minority agitation and pressure for state creation. So a major problem is imbalance in the structure and sizes of the units: D. The trap: lack of revenue (B) sounds plausible, but Nigeria's federal problems have come from sharing revenue (allocation), not from having too little of it. Memory tip: one giant among dwarfs makes a shaky federation.",
 "|Which of the following was done during the Gowon administration to reduce regional structural imbalance in the federation?|Formation of political parties|Appointment of ministers|Creation of states|Increase in revenue allocation": "To correct the imbalance of a giant Northern Region and to weaken Biafra's appeal to minorities on the eve of the civil war, General Yakubu Gowon issued Decree No. 14 of 27 May 1967, replacing the four regions with twelve states: six in the North and six in the South. So the Gowon administration reduced structural imbalance by creating states: C. The trap: increase in revenue allocation (D) tempts because money was always an issue, but money cannot change the size of the units; only breaking up the regions could. Nigeria now has 36 states. Memory tip: Gowon, 1967, 12 states.",
 "|A problem of public corporations in Nigeria is|wastage of resources|choice of leadership|public control|emphasis on subsidies": "Public corporations (such as NEPA, NITEL and Nigeria Airways) were set up to provide essential services, but many performed poorly. Common problems were overstaffing, political appointments, corruption, mismanagement and constant losses covered from public money: in short, wastage of resources. These failures led to the privatisation and commercialisation programmes of 1988 and after. So a problem of public corporations is wastage of resources: A. The trap: public control (C) is not a problem but a safeguard; ministers, the legislature and the public are supposed to control public corporations, though political interference within that control was itself a problem. Memory tip: losses and waste led to privatisation.",
 "|One feature of public corporations that was weakened by privatization is|government control|social control|national integration|social harmony": "A public corporation is owned and controlled by government, which appoints its board, gives it directions and funds it. Privatisation means selling some or all of the government's shares in such enterprises to private investors. Once that happens, the government no longer owns or controls them; private shareholders do. So the feature weakened by privatisation is government control: A. The trap: social control (B) sounds similar, but privatisation is specifically about transferring ownership from government to private hands, so what disappears is government control. Memory tip: privatise = take it out of government's hands.",
 "|One of the main duties of the Local Government Service Commission is to|handle requests for the creation of more local governments|supervise and manage the personnel of local governments|conduct elections into Local Council|create an enabling working environment for council workers": "Each state has a Local Government Service Commission that does for local government staff what the Civil Service Commission does for the state civil service: it appoints, posts, promotes, disciplines and manages the senior personnel of the local governments, and handles their conditions of service. So one of its main duties is to supervise and manage the personnel of local governments: B. The trap: conducting council elections (C) belongs to the State Independent Electoral Commission (SIEC), not the Service Commission. Memory tip: Service Commission = staff; Electoral Commission = elections.",
 "|The Option A4 model was used in the conduct of the|1999 elections|2007 elections|1983 elections|1993 elections": "Option A4 was a voting method introduced by the Babangida government's National Electoral Commission (chaired by Humphrey Nwosu) in its transition programme. Instead of a secret ballot, voters lined up openly behind the poster of their preferred candidate and were counted. It was used in the party primaries of 1992-93 and in the presidential election of 12 June 1993, won by M. K. O. Abiola and then annulled. So it was used in the 1993 elections: D. The trap: 1999 (A) tempts because it was the next big transition, but the 1999 elections used the secret ballot. Memory tip: A4 + June 12, 1993.",
 "|The review of Nigerian foreign policy under the Murtala-Obasanjo regime was done by|Phillips Commission|Udoji Committee|Aboyade Committee|Adedeji Committee": "Soon after taking power in 1975, the Murtala-Obasanjo regime set up a panel under Professor Adebayo Adedeji to review Nigeria's foreign policy. Its 1976 report confirmed Africa as the centrepiece of Nigerian foreign policy and supported a more active stance against colonialism and apartheid. So the review was done by the Adedeji Committee: D. The trap: the Udoji Committee (B) also belongs to the 1970s, but it reviewed the public service and wages (1972-74), not foreign policy. Memory tip: Adedeji for Africa; Udoji for public-service pay (the \"Udoji award\").",
 "|Which of the following is a guiding principle of Nigeria's foreign policy?|Decolonisation of all African states|Total opposition to the Cold War|Posting of only career diplomats as envoys|Interference in the affairs of African countries": "Since independence, Nigeria's foreign policy has rested on principles such as Africa as the centrepiece, non-alignment, non-interference in other states' internal affairs, respect for sovereignty and the total liberation of Africa from colonial rule and apartheid. Nigeria supported freedom movements in Angola, Zimbabwe, Namibia and South Africa. So the guiding principle is the decolonisation of all African states: A. The trap: B tempts because of non-alignment, but non-alignment means not taking sides between the Cold War blocs, not total opposition to the Cold War itself. D is the reverse of the non-interference principle. Memory tip: free Africa first.",
 "|The Technical Aid Corps was established during the regime of|Muhammadu Buhari|Olusegun Obasanjo|Sani Abacha|Ibrahim Babangida": "The Technical Aid Corps (TAC) was created in 1987 under General Ibrahim Babangida, on the idea of his Foreign Minister, Professor Bolaji Akinyemi. It sends Nigerian volunteers (doctors, teachers, engineers, lawyers) to serve in other African, Caribbean and Pacific countries, as Nigeria's alternative to giving cash aid. So it was set up during the Babangida regime: D. The trap: Obasanjo (B) tempts because he was strongly Africa-focused, but TAC came in 1987, when Obasanjo was out of office. Memory tip: Babangida + Akinyemi = TAC (1987) and the Concert of Medium Powers.",
 "|The granting of asylum to Charles Taylor by Nigeria was to|control Liberia|protect Nigerians in Liberia|promote peace in Liberia|defy the Western powers": "In 2003 Liberia's civil war had reached Monrovia and rebels demanded that President Charles Taylor leave. As part of an African and international peace deal, Nigeria offered him asylum in Calabar; he stepped down in August 2003, a transitional government took over, and the war ended. So the asylum was granted to promote peace in Liberia: C. The trap: B tempts because Nigerians were in danger in Liberia, but the asylum was a peace arrangement to remove Taylor from the conflict, not a rescue mission. In 2006 Nigeria handed Taylor over, and the Special Court for Sierra Leone convicted him in 2012. Memory tip: Taylor out, peace in.",
 "|Nigeria is regarded as a frontline state because she|sent troops for peacekeeping in Somalia|sent policemen for peacekeeping in Namibia|assisted the liberation struggle in Southern Africa|assisted ECOMOG troops in Liberia": "The frontline states were the Southern African countries bordering apartheid South Africa and white-ruled Rhodesia and Namibia, such as Zambia, Tanzania, Botswana, Angola and Mozambique. Although Nigeria is in West Africa, it gave so much money, diplomatic pressure and support to the liberation movements that it was treated as an honorary frontline state. So Nigeria is regarded as a frontline state because she assisted the liberation struggle in Southern Africa: C. The trap: B (policemen in Namibia) is about one peacekeeping mission after the struggle was won; frontline status came from long support for liberation. Memory tip: frontline = front line of the fight against apartheid.",
 "|The reason behind Nigeria's suspension from the Commonwealth in 1995 was|socio-cultural|legal|political|economic": "In November 1995, during the Commonwealth summit in Auckland, General Sani Abacha's government executed the writer Ken Saro-Wiwa and eight other Ogoni activists. Coming on top of military rule and the annulment of the 1993 election, this broke the Commonwealth's commitment to democracy and human rights (Harare Declaration, 1991), and Nigeria was suspended. Nigeria was readmitted in 1999 after the return to civilian rule. So the reason was political: C. The trap: legal (B) tempts because the executions followed a trial, but the suspension was a response to the political situation, dictatorship and human rights abuses, not to a point of law. Memory tip: 1995, Saro-Wiwa, suspension; 1999, democracy, return.",
 "|Commonwealth nations are represented in other member nations by|attachés|chargés d'affaires|ambassadors|high commissioners": "Because Commonwealth members see themselves as a family, they do not exchange ambassadors with one another. Instead, the head of each Commonwealth country's diplomatic mission in another Commonwealth country is called a High Commissioner, and the mission is a High Commission. Thus Nigeria has a High Commission in London and in Accra, but an Embassy in Washington. So Commonwealth nations are represented in other member nations by high commissioners: D. The trap: ambassadors (C) do the same job and have the same rank, but the title is used for missions in non-Commonwealth countries. Memory tip: Commonwealth = High Commission; others = Embassy.",
 "|The UN succeeded the|League of Nations|Warsaw Pact|NATO|SEATO": "The League of Nations was set up in 1920 after the First World War to keep the peace, but it failed to stop aggression in the 1930s and could not prevent the Second World War. The United Nations was founded in 1945 (Charter signed in San Francisco on 26 June, in force on 24 October) to replace it, and the League was formally dissolved in 1946. So the UN succeeded the League of Nations: A. NATO (C), the Warsaw Pact (B) and SEATO (D) are Cold War military alliances, not world bodies; the UN replaced the League of Nations in 1945. Memory tip: League (1920) failed, UN (1945) followed.",
 "|The number of permanent members of the UN Security Council is|seven|eight|five|six": "The UN Security Council has 15 members. Five are permanent and each holds the veto: the United States, Russia (formerly the Soviet Union), China, the United Kingdom and France, the main victors of the Second World War. The other ten are non-permanent members elected by the General Assembly for two-year terms. So the number of permanent members is five: C. The trap: six (D) tempts students who count the six principal organs of the UN, or who add a non-permanent member; but the permanent seats remain five. Memory tip: the \"P5\" have the veto.",
 "|The Secretary-General of the OAU holds office for a renewable period of|five years|six years|three years|four years": "The Secretary-General headed the OAU's General Secretariat in Addis Ababa. He was appointed by the Assembly of Heads of State and Government for a term of four years, which could be renewed. Salim Ahmed Salim of Tanzania, for example, was appointed in 1989 and re-appointed in 1993 and 1997, serving until 2001. So the term was a renewable four years: D. The trap: five years (A) tempts because it is the term of the UN Secretary-General, but the OAU term was four years. The OAU became the African Union in 2002; the AU Commission chairperson also serves four years, renewable once. Memory tip: OAU and AU both run on four-year terms.",
 "|Former colonies of Britain belong to the association known as|Commonwealth|OECD|NATO|European Union": "The Commonwealth (Commonwealth of Nations) is a voluntary association of Britain and mostly its former colonies, held together by shared history, the English language and common values. The British monarch is its symbolic head. Nigeria joined at independence in 1960. Today it has 56 members, including a few, such as Mozambique and Rwanda, that were never British colonies. So former British colonies belong to the Commonwealth: A. The European Union (D) is a body of European states only, which Britain left in 2020, and NATO (C) and the OECD (B) are military and economic groupings; Britain's former colonies form the Commonwealth. Memory tip: common history, Commonwealth.",
 "|Which of the following countries is a founding member of the Commonwealth?|India|Ghana|South Africa|Zimbabwe": "The Commonwealth grew out of the British Empire. The Statute of Westminster (1931) made the \"dominions\" (Canada, Australia, New Zealand, South Africa, the Irish Free State and Newfoundland) equal partners in the British Commonwealth. The London Declaration of 1949 then created the modern Commonwealth, with eight founding members: Britain, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, South Africa, India, Pakistan and Ceylon. South Africa fits both dates: C. India is also accepted, because it is one of the 1949 founders. Ghana joined in 1957 and Zimbabwe in 1980. Memory tip: South Africa left in 1961 over apartheid and returned in 1994.",
 "|Which of the following is an achievement of OPEC?|Regulation of oil production and price|The issue of quota|Protection of member states|Oil storage": "OPEC, the Organisation of the Petroleum Exporting Countries, was founded in Baghdad in September 1960 and has its headquarters in Vienna; Nigeria joined in 1971. Its aim is to coordinate members' petroleum policies so as to secure fair and stable prices. Its main achievement has been regulating the oil supply, and with it the price, on the world market: A. The trap: \"the issue of quota\" (B) is closely linked, but quotas are only the method OPEC uses to regulate production, not an achievement in themselves. Memory tip: OPEC controls the tap to control the price.",
 "|A political authority which maintains sovereign power over a specific geographical area is a|state|nation|country|community": "A state has four elements: population, a defined territory, a government and sovereignty (supreme power, free of outside control). A political authority that holds sovereign power over a specific geographical area is therefore a state: A. The trap: a nation (B) is a group of people bound by common language, culture and history, and it may have no sovereign power at all, as the Kurds show; \"country\" (C) is an everyday geographical word, not a political term. Memory tip: state = sovereignty + territory; nation = people + culture.",
 "|The head of a crown colony under British rule was the|Secretary of Native Affairs|Governor|Secretary of State|Governor-General": "A crown colony was a territory ruled directly by the British Crown, such as Lagos from 1862. At its head was a Governor appointed by the Crown, who ruled with an Executive Council and a Legislative Council and answered to the Secretary of State for the Colonies in London: B. The trap: Governor-General (D) is a closely related title, but it was used for the head of a larger federation or amalgamated territory, as Lugard was in 1914 and Nigeria's head of government until 1960. The Secretary of State (C) sat in London, not in the colony. Memory tip: one colony, one Governor.",
 "|Nigeria spear-headed the formation and operation of ECOMOG in|Sudan|Liberia|Congo|Egypt": "ECOMOG, the ECOWAS Ceasefire Monitoring Group, was set up in 1990 under the Babangida government, mainly on Nigeria's initiative, to stop the civil war in Liberia. Its troops landed in Monrovia in August 1990, and Nigeria provided most of the men, money and commanders. It later served in Sierra Leone (1997–2000) and Guinea-Bissau: B. Sudan, Congo and Egypt are not in West Africa, so ECOWAS's force never operated there. Memory tip: ECOMOG's first stop was Liberia, Africa's oldest republic.",
 "|NEPAD came into existence in|2004|2001|2003|2002": "NEPAD, the New Partnership for Africa's Development, joined two earlier plans: Mbeki's Millennium Africa Recovery Plan and Wade's Omega Plan. Presidents Obasanjo, Mbeki, Bouteflika, Wade and Mubarak led the effort. The OAU summit at Lusaka adopted the plan in July 2001, and its framework was settled in Abuja in October 2001: B. The trap: 2002 (D) is when the African Union was launched in Durban and took over NEPAD, so students often mix up the two. Memory tip: NEPAD 2001, AU 2002: the plan came one year before the Union.",
 "|The highest organ of the Commonwealth is the|Secretariat|Fund|Conference of Prime Ministers|Trusteeship Council": "The Commonwealth's top body is the regular meeting of its heads of government. It was long called the Commonwealth Prime Ministers' Conference; since 1971 it has been the Commonwealth Heads of Government Meeting (CHOGM), held every two years: C. The Secretariat (A), set up in London in 1965, is the administrative arm that carries out its decisions, while the Trusteeship Council (D) is an organ of the UN, not the Commonwealth. Memory tip: the heads decide, the Secretariat does.",
 "|In which system of government are ceremonial and executive powers fused?|Federal|Parliamentary|Unitary|Presidential": "Every state has a ceremonial head (head of state) and an executive head (head of government). In the parliamentary system these are two people, for example the Queen and the Prime Minister in Britain, or Azikiwe and Balewa in Nigeria in 1963–66. In the presidential system one person holds both roles: the President is head of state and head of government, as in the USA and in Nigeria since 1979: D. The trap: parliamentary (B) is known for fusion of powers, but that is the fusion of the executive and the legislature, not of ceremonial and executive powers. Memory tip: presidential = one person, two hats.",
 "|In the Old Oyo Empire, the Oyomesi was headed by the|Baale|Alaafin|Bashorun|Kakanfo": "In the Old Oyo Empire the Alaafin was king, but his power was checked by the Oyomesi, a council of seven kingmakers. Their head was the Bashorun, who acted as the leader of the council and could present the Alaafin with an empty calabash or parrot's eggs, a sign that he must commit suicide: C. The trap: the Alaafin (B) was the king whom the Oyomesi checked, not its head; the Kakanfo (D) was the commander of the army and the Baale (A) a town or village head. Memory tip: Bashorun = boss of the Oyomesi.",
 "|The principle of non-alignment in Nigeria's foreign policy implies that|Nigeria will be objective during international crises|Nigeria pledges neutrality in international trade relations|Nigeria's foreign policy formulation process must not be influenced|Nigeria is neither committed to the West nor the East": "After 1945 the world was split into a Western bloc (the USA and its allies) and an Eastern bloc (the Soviet Union and its allies). Nigeria, like many new states, joined the Non-Aligned Movement and refused to tie itself permanently to either side; it would judge each issue on its merits: D. The trap: being objective in crises (A) sounds close, but non-alignment is specifically about not belonging to either Cold War bloc, and it did not mean neutrality in trade (B). Memory tip: non-aligned = neither West nor East.",
 "|Which of the following formulates and implements policies?|Police|Judiciary|Executive|Legislature": "Government has three organs. The legislature makes laws; the judiciary interprets them and settles disputes; the executive (the president or prime minister, ministers and the civil service) formulates policies and puts them into effect: C. The police (A) are an agency of the executive, so they enforce law, not form policy. The legislature (D) debates and passes policy into law, but it does not implement it. Memory tip: executive = execute, it both plans and carries out.",
 "|In pre-colonial Nigeria, centralised political administration did not exist among the|Kanuri|Igbo|Yoruba|Hausa": "Pre-colonial Nigeria had centralised states such as the Hausa-Fulani emirates, the Kanem-Bornu (Kanuri) empire and the Oyo and Benin kingdoms, each under a single ruler. Traditional Igbo society was different: it was acephalous (\"headless\"). Each village governed itself through the family heads (ofo holders), the council of elders, the village assembly, the age grades and title societies such as Ozo: B. Memory tip: \"Igbo enwe eze\": the Igbo have no king.",
 "|The Economic and Social Council of the United Nations is made up of|28 members|26 members|25 members|27 members": "The UN Economic and Social Council (ECOSOC) is one of the six principal organs of the UN; it coordinates economic, social and humanitarian work and the specialised agencies. It began in 1945 with 18 members, was enlarged to 27 by a Charter amendment in 1965 and to 54 in 1973. This old question was set during the 27-member period, so the expected answer is 27 members: D. Today ECOSOC has 54 members, elected by the General Assembly for three-year terms. Memory tip: ECOSOC grew 18, 27, 54, doubling at each step.",
 "|One of the agencies established by the military to promote national integration was the|Directorate of Mass Mobilization for Social and Economic Reconstruction|Directorate of Food, Roads and Rural Infrastructure|National Youth Service Corps|National Directorate of Employment": "After the civil war (1967–70), General Yakubu Gowon's military government created the National Youth Service Corps by Decree No. 24 of 22 May 1973. Graduates serve for a year in a state other than their own, so that young Nigerians learn to live with other ethnic groups and build national unity: C. The trap: MAMSER (A), set up by Babangida in 1987, was about political mobilisation for self-reliance, social justice and economic recovery; DFRRI (B, 1986) built rural roads and food projects, and the NDE (D, 1986) fought unemployment. Memory tip: NYSC's motto is \"Service and Humility\", serving outside your home state.",
 "|The French policy of assimilation in West Africa was a form of|monarchy|indirect rule|democratic rule|direct rule": "France ruled its West African colonies directly. Under assimilation French officials (commandants) governed each district, and Africans were expected to adopt the French language, culture and law and might in time become French citizens. Chiefs were made junior agents of the French, not rulers in their own right: D. The trap: indirect rule (B) was the British method of ruling through traditional rulers such as the emirs, the opposite of assimilation. Memory tip: France ran it herself (direct), Britain ran it through chiefs (indirect).",
 "|The international organisation that formed NEPAD is|the Commonwealth|ECOWAS|the UN|the AU": "NEPAD was adopted in July 2001 at the OAU summit in Lusaka and became a programme of the African Union, which replaced the OAU in 2002. Since 2018 it has been the AU Development Agency, AUDA-NEPAD, based in Midrand, South Africa: D. Strictly, the plan was born under the OAU just before it became the AU, but since the OAU is not an option the AU is the answer. The Commonwealth, ECOWAS and the UN are not African-wide bodies of African leaders. Memory tip: NEPAD is the AU's development programme, as the 'African' in its name hints.",
 "|An appropriation bill is|an impeachment bill|a private member bill|a money bill|a state creation bill": "An appropriation bill is the bill that authorises government to spend money from the Consolidated Revenue Fund for the year; the budget is passed as the Appropriation Act. Because it deals with public money it is a money bill: C. A private member bill (B) is one introduced by an ordinary legislator rather than the executive, while appropriation bills are always brought by the executive. Memory tip: appropriate = allocate money.",
 "|An electoral college refers to|direct election|indirect election|the process of educating the electorate|where public opinion is formed": "In a direct election voters choose their representatives themselves. In an indirect election the voters choose an intermediate body, an electoral college, which then elects the official. The US president is chosen this way, and Nigeria's first President (1963) was elected by a joint sitting of Parliament: B. Direct election (A) is its exact opposite. Memory tip: college = a middleman between voters and the winner.",
 "|Government by the few for the interest of the few is known as|autocracy|monarchy|oligarchy|plutocracy": "Aristotle sorted governments by who rules and for whom. Rule by a few for the common good is aristocracy; rule by a few for their own selfish interest is oligarchy: C. The trap: plutocracy (D) is also rule by a few, but specifically by the wealthy; the question says only \"the few\", which is oligarchy in general. Autocracy (A) and monarchy (B) are rule by one person. Memory tip: oligoi = few; ploutos = wealth.",
 "|Which of the following best describes Nigeria's foreign policy between 1960 and 1966?|Truly non-aligned|Total dependence on the eastern bloc|Dynamic and active|Cautious and inactive": "Tafawa Balewa's foreign policy (1960–66) is usually described as cautious, conservative and pro-Western. Nigeria leaned on Britain, signed the Anglo-Nigerian Defence Pact (abrogated in 1962 after protests) and took few bold steps, though it did break relations with France over atomic tests in the Sahara (1961) and helped push South Africa out of the Commonwealth: D. The trap: \"dynamic and active\" (C) describes the Murtala–Obasanjo era, especially the recognition of the MPLA in Angola in 1975. Memory tip: Balewa = careful; Murtala = bold.",
 "|Adjudication is the responsibility of|police|bureaucracy|judiciary|executive": "Adjudication means judging: hearing cases, applying the law to the facts and giving a binding decision. This is the main work of the courts, from the magistrate courts to the Supreme Court, which together form the judiciary: C. The police (A) investigate and arrest, and the executive (D) carries out the court's judgments, but neither decides cases. Memory tip: judge, judiciary, adjudication all share one root.",
 "|Citizenship can be acquired by|naturalisation|indigenization|education|wealth": "Nigeria's 1999 Constitution recognises three ways of becoming a citizen: by birth (section 25), by registration (section 26) and by naturalisation (section 27). A foreigner who is of good character, has lived in Nigeria for at least 15 years and renounces any other citizenship may be naturalised: A. Indigenisation was an economic policy of the 1970s that transferred businesses to Nigerians; education and wealth are not legal routes to citizenship. Memory tip: birth, registration, naturalisation: \"BRN\".",
 "|The major interest for the penetration of Africa by the Europeans was|economic|social|political|religious": "The Scramble for Africa (formalised at the Berlin Conference of 1884–85) was driven chiefly by economic interests. Europe's industrial revolution needed raw materials (palm oil, cotton, rubber, minerals), new markets for manufactured goods and places to invest surplus capital: A. Political prestige (C) and spreading Christianity (D) were real motives but secondary to trade and profit. Memory tip: the three Cs, commerce, Christianity and civilisation, and commerce came first.",
 "|The extent to which citizens accept institutions, officials and activities of government is known as|legitimacy|authority|power|sovereignty": "Legitimacy is the acceptance by citizens that their government, its institutions and officials have the right to rule, so they obey willingly rather than through fear: A. The trap: authority (B) is closely related, being the recognised right to command, but legitimacy is the citizens' acceptance that turns power into authority. Power (C) is the ability to compel and sovereignty (D) is supreme power. Memory tip: legitimacy = the people's \"yes\" to their rulers.",
 "|The twelve-state structure was attained in Nigeria in|1974|1965|1967|1966": "On 27 May 1967, General Yakubu Gowon abolished the four regions (North, East, West and Mid-West) and created twelve states by decree. One aim was to weaken Ojukwu's Eastern Region, which declared itself Biafra three days later: C. Murtala Mohammed raised the number to 19 in 1976, Babangida to 21 (1987) and 30 (1991), and Abacha to 36 (1996). Memory tip: 1967: 12; 1976: 19; 1987: 21; 1991: 30; 1996: 36.",
 "|One of the major defects of the 1999 Constitution was that it was|conceived by the military|influenced by foreign powers|drafted by the state Houses of Assembly|drafted by the National Assembly": "The 1999 Constitution was drafted under General Abdulsalami Abubakar's military regime, based on the 1995 draft and the work of the Niki Tobi committee, and was promulgated by Decree No. 24 of 1999. Critics point out that it was never debated by a constituent assembly or approved in a referendum, yet it begins \"We the people\": A. It was not drafted by the National Assembly (D), which did not yet exist; the Assembly has only amended it since. Memory tip: 1999 = a military gift with a civilian label.",
 "|Local government reforms in Nigeria were carried out in|1979|1966|1976|1967": "The 1976 Local Government Reform, under the Murtala/Obasanjo military government, created a uniform, single-tier local government system across Nigeria, with councils of between 150,000 and 800,000 people, elected councillors and a share of federal revenue. It was later recognised in the 1979 Constitution as the third tier of government: C. 1979 (A) is the year the new constitution entrenched the reform. Memory tip: 1976: one country, one local government pattern.",
 "|The constitution that first introduced bicameral legislature in Nigeria was the|1960 Constitution|1989 Constitution|1963 Constitution|1979 Constitution": "A bicameral legislature has two chambers. Nigeria's first two-chamber federal parliament came with the 1960 Independence Constitution: a Senate (made up largely of members chosen by the regions) and an elected House of Representatives: A. Under the 1951 Macpherson Constitution the Northern and Western regions already had Houses of Chiefs beside their Houses of Assembly, but the central legislature became bicameral in 1960. Memory tip: independence brought Nigeria a Senate.",
 "|The hallmark of capitalism is|restrictive economic policies|private ownership|even development|state ownership": "Capitalism is an economic system in which the means of production (land, factories, banks, firms) are privately owned and run for profit, with prices set by supply and demand in a free market: B. State ownership (D) is the hallmark of socialism, the opposite system. Capitalism tends to produce uneven, not even, development (C). Memory tip: capitalism = private property + profit + free market.",
 "|Nationalism in Africa led to|the formation of economic blocs|decolonization|the formation of a continental army|the creation of new states": "African nationalism was the movement of Africans against colonial rule, led by men such as Azikiwe, Nkrumah and Kenyatta. Its great result was decolonisation, the end of European rule, beginning with Ghana in 1957 and Nigeria in 1960, and continuing across the continent: B. Economic blocs such as ECOWAS (A) and the idea of an African army (C) came after independence and were not the direct product of nationalism. Memory tip: nationalism asked for freedom; decolonisation was freedom granted.",
 "|A government which has the ability to compel its citizens to be obedient is exercising|legitimacy|coercion|right|authority": "Authority is power that is recognised as rightful: the government has the legal and accepted right to make laws and to demand obedience, and it can enforce that obedience: D. The trap: coercion (B) is also about compelling people, but it is force or threat used without consent; a government relying on coercion alone lacks authority. The wording \"compel\" makes this question arguable, and some readers would choose coercion. Memory tip: authority = power + right.",
 "|The newspaper founded by Herbert Macaulay that aided the independence struggle in Nigeria was the|Comet|West African Pilot|Lagos Daily News|Daily Times": "Herbert Macaulay (1864–1946), grandson of Bishop Ajayi Crowther and called the \"father of Nigerian nationalism\", founded the Nigerian National Democratic Party in 1923 and the Lagos Daily News in 1925, which he used to fight colonial injustices: C. The trap: the West African Pilot (B) was Nnamdi Azikiwe's paper (1937), and Azikiwe also owned the Comet (A); the Daily Times (D) was founded in 1926 by a group of businessmen. Memory tip: Macaulay's Lagos, Zik's Pilot.",
 "|The major aim of the United Nations is to|stabilize the price of oil in the world market|co-ordinate and supervise the activities of specialized agencies|maintain international peace and security|promote sports among member nations": "The UN was founded on 24 October 1945, after the Second World War, and Article 1 of its Charter puts first the purpose \"to maintain international peace and security\". Its other aims include friendly relations among nations and cooperation on economic, social and humanitarian problems: C. The trap: coordinating the specialised agencies (B) is a real UN task, but it is the job of ECOSOC, one organ, not the UN's major aim. Stabilising oil prices (A) is OPEC's concern. Memory tip: the UN was born to stop another world war.",
 "|A unitary system of government ensures cohesion because there is|rule of law|concentration of power|separation of power|understanding and harmony": "In a unitary system (Britain, France, Ghana) all power is concentrated in the central government, which may delegate some functions to local units but can take them back. With one centre of power and one set of laws, policy is uniform and the country holds together: B. Separation of powers (C) divides power among organs, which has nothing to do with unitary versus federal structure. Memory tip: unitary = one centre, one voice.",
 "|Checks and balances violates the principle of|individual responsibility|rule of law|collective responsibility|separation of powers": "A strict separation of powers (Montesquieu, 1748) would keep the legislature, executive and judiciary fully apart, each doing only its own work. Checks and balances deliberately lets each organ step into the others' area: the president vetoes bills, the legislature confirms appointments and impeaches, and the courts strike down laws. It therefore modifies, or \"violates\", strict separation of powers: D. Collective responsibility (C) is a rule of parliamentary cabinets that has nothing to do with checks and balances. Memory tip: checks and balances means the three organs touch, so they are not perfectly separated.",
 "|The Northern People's Congress was established in|1978|1944|1977|1951": "The Northern People's Congress began as a cultural association, Jam'iyyar Mutanen Arewa, in 1949 and was turned into a political party in 1951 to contest the elections under the Macpherson Constitution. It was led by Sir Ahmadu Bello, Sardauna of Sokoto, and its deputy leader Abubakar Tafawa Balewa became Prime Minister: D. 1944 (B) is the founding year of the NCNC. Memory tip: AG and NPC were both born in 1951.",
 "|A key characteristic of public opinion is that it is|often reliable|dynamic|not specific|election": "Public opinion is the view held by a large part of the public on an issue of public importance. It is dynamic: it shifts as new events occur, information spreads through the media and circumstances change, which is why opinion polls must be taken again and again: B. It is not always reliable (A), since it can be manipulated or based on rumours. \"Election\" (D) is a way of measuring opinion, not a characteristic of it. Memory tip: public opinion moves like the weather.",
 "|Under monarchy, rulership is attained through|appointment|competition|hereditary|election": "A monarchy is government by a king, queen, emperor or other single ruler who obtains the throne by inheritance, usually through the royal family line, and rules for life: C. Appointment, competition and election are ways of filling offices in republics. Even elective traditional monarchies, like the Oyo kingship, choose only from royal families. Memory tip: monarchy runs in the family.",
 "|Which of the following is an advantage of the federal system?|Fusion of power|Integration of different groups|Collective responsibility|Rigid constitution": "Federalism divides power between a central government and component units. Its main advantage in a country like Nigeria is that it brings together different ethnic, linguistic and religious groups in one nation while letting each unit keep a measure of self-rule: B. Fusion of power (A) and collective responsibility (C) are features of the parliamentary system. A rigid constitution (D) is a feature of federalism, protecting the units' powers, but it is usually counted as a drawback because it makes change slow. Memory tip: federalism = unity in diversity.",
 "|Under which of these constitutions did the Queen of England cease to be Nigeria's Head of State?|1989 Constitution|1960 Constitution|1963 Constitution|1979 Constitution": "At independence on 1 October 1960, Nigeria was a dominion: Queen Elizabeth II was Head of State, represented by the Governor-General, Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe. The Republican Constitution of 1 October 1963 removed the Queen and made Azikiwe the first President, a ceremonial Head of State: C. The trap: the 1960 Constitution (B) is the independence constitution, but under it the Queen was still Head of State. Memory tip: 1960 independence, 1963 republic.",
 "|The foreign policy of Nigeria is mainly determined by her|military strength|national interest|population|geographical location": "National interest is the sum of what a country considers vital to its survival and well-being: security, territorial integrity, economic prosperity and the welfare of its people. Every state, Nigeria included, shapes its foreign policy mainly around it: B. Military strength (A), population (C) and geographical location (D) are factors that influence foreign policy, but they are means and conditions, not the main determinant. Memory tip: foreign policy always starts with \"what is good for Nigeria?\"",
 "|One merit of a one-party system is that it|gives room for opposition to thrive|de-emphasizes the differences among party members|pools together available talents into government|prevents many members from defection": "In a one-party system only one party is allowed, as in Tanzania under Nyerere or China. Its supporters argue that it brings unity: rival ethnic, regional and ideological groups are held in one party, so differences among its members are played down for national stability: B. The trap: pooling the country's best talents into government (C) is also listed as a merit in some textbooks, so it is very close; B is preferred here as the point about unity. \"Gives room for opposition to thrive\" (A) is the opposite, since a one-party state has no legal opposition. Memory tip: one party, one platform, fewer quarrels.",
 "|A form of government in which political power is exercised by a dictator is|plutocracy|fascism|oligarchy|feudalism": "A dictator rules alone with absolute power, usually backed by force and a single party. Fascism, developed by Benito Mussolini in Italy (from 1922), is built on the rule of one supreme leader who controls the state, the economy and the press: B. Plutocracy (A) is rule by the rich and oligarchy (C) by a small group. Feudalism (D) is a system of land holding and lord–vassal ties. Memory tip: fascism = one leader, one party, total control.",
 "|Which of the following does not operate a written constitution?|Britain|Nigeria|Ghana|United States": "Britain is the classic example of an unwritten (uncodified) constitution: there is no single document called \"the Constitution\". Its rules come from Acts of Parliament (such as Magna Carta 1215 and the Bill of Rights 1689), judicial decisions, conventions and authoritative books: A. Nigeria, Ghana and the United States all have written constitutions; the US Constitution of 1787 is the oldest written national constitution still in use. Memory tip: Britain writes laws, not a constitution.",
 "|A characteristic of a pressure group is that members|are in the same labour union|oppose the government|have similar interest|do not believe in dialogue": "A pressure (interest) group is an organised body of people who share a common interest (workers, farmers, lawyers, students) and try to influence government decisions to protect it, without seeking to form the government: C. The trap: members of a labour union (A) do form a pressure group, but not all pressure groups are trade unions. They do not always oppose the government (B), and they work mainly through lobbying and dialogue. Memory tip: pressure group = same interest, outside government.",
 "|The Public Complaints Commission is empowered to investigate complaints and|refer complaints to state governments|refer findings to traditional rulers|report findings to appropriate authorities|prosecute offenders": "The Public Complaints Commission, Nigeria's ombudsman, was set up by Decree No. 31 of 1975 and kept in the 1999 Constitution. It investigates complaints by citizens against government officials and agencies (maladministration) and then reports its findings and recommendations to the appropriate authorities for action: C. The trap: prosecuting offenders (D) sounds like the next step, but the Commission has no power to prosecute; that is done by the Attorney-General and agencies such as the EFCC or ICPC. Memory tip: the ombudsman investigates and reports, it does not judge.",
 "|Which of the following is a major feature of the 1963 Constitution?|Accountability to the parliament|Removal of judges|Introduction of the republican form of government|Introduction of national parliament": "The 1963 Republican Constitution, which came into force on 1 October 1963, made Nigeria a federal republic. The Queen ceased to be Head of State, a Nigerian President (Azikiwe) took over that role and appeals to the Privy Council in London ended, making the Supreme Court the highest court: C. A national parliament (D) had existed since 1954 and was bicameral from 1960; accountability to parliament (A) was already part of the system. Memory tip: 1963 = republic.",
 "|An attribute of a state is|technological advancement|informed citizenry|well-equipped army|defined territory": "The attributes of a state are population, defined territory, government and sovereignty; a state also usually needs international recognition. A defined territory, land with known boundaries over which the state's laws apply, is essential: D. Nigeria's, for example, covers about 923,768 square kilometres. Technological advancement, an informed citizenry and a strong army are good things for a state to have, but many states lack them and are still states. Memory tip: \"P-T-G-S\": People, Territory, Government, Sovereignty.",
 "|One of the first actions of military governments after a take-over is to|appoint new ministers|detain all politicians|give bonus to workers|suspend the constitution": "When soldiers seize power, one of their first acts is to suspend or modify the constitution and rule by decrees. In Nigeria the coup leaders of January 1966, July 1975 and December 1983 all suspended parts of the constitution; the legislatures were dissolved and parties banned: D. They may detain some politicians (B) and appoint ministers (A) later, but suspending the constitution is the basic first step. Memory tip: soldiers in, constitution out.",
 "|The equivalent of the prime minister under the Hausa pre-colonial system was the|Waziri|Dogari|Galadima|Madawaki": "In the Hausa-Fulani emirate system the Emir was helped by titled officials. The Waziri was his chief adviser and head of the administration, the equivalent of a prime minister: A. The trap: the Galadima (C) was also a senior official, but he administered the capital city; the Madawaki (D) commanded the army, and the Dogari (B) were the Emir's police or bodyguards. Memory tip: Waziri = vizier = prime minister.",
 "|A major opposition party at the national level in Nigeria's Second Republic was|NPP|UPN|PRP|GNPP": "The Second Republic ran from 1 October 1979 to the coup of 31 December 1983. Five parties were registered in 1978: the NPN, UPN, NPP, GNPP and PRP. Shehu Shagari's NPN won the presidency and formed the federal government. Obafemi Awolowo's Unity Party of Nigeria came second in the 1979 presidential race, controlled five states and became the main opposition party at the national level: B. The trap: the NPP (A) is tempting because it was a big party, but it first joined the NPN in an \"accord\" to share power and only left it in 1981. Memory tip: Awolowo's UPN = the Unshaken Party of the opposition, 1979–83.",
 "|The exclusion of Africans from colonial administration is associated with|policy of assimilation|indirect rule system|policy of association|indigénat policy": "France's policy of assimilation promised to turn Africans into Frenchmen, but only a small number, the évolués, who learnt French, adopted French ways and met strict conditions, ever became French citizens. Only those citizens could vote, serve in the colonial civil service or, like Blaise Diagne of Senegal, sit in the French parliament. The vast majority were \"subjects\" and were kept out of colonial administration: A. The trap: indirect rule (B) is tempting because it shut out the Western-educated elite, but it actually brought Africans into government, since the British ruled through the emirs, obas and warrant chiefs as native authorities. Memory tip: assimilation opened the door only to those who became French.",
 "|During the 1964 federal election in Nigeria, the United Progressive Grand Alliance comprised the|NNDP, NPC and NDC|NPC, UMBC and BYM|UMBC, AG, NEPU and NCNC|UMBC, BYM, NEPU and NPC": "For the December 1964 federal election, Nigerian parties formed two big alliances. The Nigerian National Alliance (NNA) joined the NPC with Akintola's NNDP and smaller groups. Against it stood the United Progressive Grand Alliance (UPGA): the NCNC and the Action Group in the south, plus their northern allies, Aminu Kano's NEPU and Joseph Tarka's UMBC. So the UPGA comprised the UMBC, AG, NEPU and NCNC: C. The trap: option D (UMBC, BYM, NEPU and NPC) is tempting because it lists northern opposition parties, but it also includes the NPC, the leader of the rival NNA. Memory tip: UPGA = the \"progressives\": the two southern giants plus the northern rebels.",
 "|Which of the following binds Commonwealth nations together?|Uniform political system|Common currency|Common language|Common colonial history": "The Commonwealth (modern form dating from the London Declaration of 1949) is a free association of independent states, nearly all of them former British colonies; Mozambique and Rwanda are the rare exceptions. They keep different political systems, currencies and many languages. What ties them together is their shared history under British colonial rule: D. English is widely used, but members speak many languages, and a common language (C) is not what defines membership. Memory tip: Commonwealth = common history under the British crown.",
 "|A structure of government in which power is shared between the centre and the units is|confederal|oligarchic|unitary|federalism": "Governments are grouped by how power is shared between levels. In a unitary state all power is held by the centre, which may delegate some to local units. In a confederation the units keep sovereignty and the centre is weak. In a federation the constitution shares power between the central government and the component units, each supreme in its own sphere, as in Nigeria, the USA and India. Power shared between the centre and the units is federalism: D. The trap: confederal (A) also involves a centre and units, but there the units hold the real power and can leave; the centre only coordinates. Memory tip: federal = shared; unitary = one; confederal = loose.",
 "|Which of the following performs oversight functions?|Judiciary|Civil Service|Executive|Legislature": "Oversight means watching over how the executive carries out laws and spends money. In Nigeria the National Assembly does this through its committees, public hearings, investigations, summoning ministers and heads of agencies, approving the budget and, in the last resort, impeachment (sections 88 and 89 of the 1999 Constitution give it power to investigate). So the legislature performs oversight functions: D. The judiciary (A) also checks the executive through judicial review, but only when a case is brought before it; routine oversight is the legislature's job. Memory tip: the legislature makes the law and then keeps an eye on how it is used.",
 "|Foreign policy refers to|a policy made by a country when it is at war|the general principles of a state's international behaviour|a policy made by a foreign country|a policy made by a country for foreigners": "Every state has to deal with other states. Its foreign policy is the set of principles, objectives and strategies that guide that behaviour: whom to befriend, which organisations to join, how to protect its citizens and interests abroad. Nigeria's principles, for example, include Afrocentrism, non-alignment and respect for sovereignty. So foreign policy refers to the general principles of a state's international behaviour: B. Foreign policy is about relations with other states, not rules for foreigners living inside a country (D). Memory tip: foreign policy is a nation's \"code of conduct\" abroad.",
 "|\"From each according to his ability, to each according to his needs\" is the principle of|communism|socialism|capitalism|communalism": "Karl Marx described two stages after capitalism. In socialism, the lower stage, the rule is \"from each according to his ability, to each according to his work\". In communism, the final stage, production is so abundant and the state has withered away, so the rule becomes \"from each according to his ability, to each according to his needs\": A. The trap: socialism (B) is tempting because it is also Marxist and shares the first half of the slogan, but under socialism people are paid according to their work, not their needs. Memory tip: socialism pays for WORK; communism provides for NEEDS.",
 "|The maintenance of law and order is a function of the|legislature|military|judiciary|executive": "Government has three arms. The legislature makes laws, the judiciary interprets them and settles disputes, and the executive carries them out. Keeping law and order is part of carrying out the law: the President or Governor directs the police and other security agencies to prevent crime and arrest offenders. So the maintenance of law and order is a function of the executive: D. The military (B) defends the country from external attack; internal law and order is the police's job under the executive. Memory tip: execute = carry out; the executive carries out the law and keeps the peace.",
 "|The development of attitudes and beliefs about a political system is known as|political orientation|political apathy|political culture|political socialization": "Political socialisation is the process by which people develop their political attitudes, beliefs and values, and by which a society passes them from one generation to the next. Its agents are the family, school, peer groups, religious bodies, the mass media and political parties. \"Development of attitudes and beliefs\" describes this process: D. The trap: political culture (C) is very close, but culture is the set of attitudes and beliefs a society already holds, while socialisation is the process that develops and passes them on. Memory tip: socialisation is the teacher, culture is the lesson.",
 "|A nation could be identified through its|military strength|common language and culture|population|constitution": "A nation is a group of people who feel they belong together because they share a common language, culture, history, descent or religion. The Yoruba, Hausa and Igbo are nations in this sense, and a nation may live in more than one state. So a nation is identified by its common language and culture: B. A constitution (D) belongs to a state, a political and legal unit; a nation is a cultural community that may have no constitution or government of its own. Memory tip: nation = native culture; state = laws and borders.",
 "|A network of groups of people in an institution expressing human association is|community|society|nation|state": "A society is the widest network of human relationships: the families, clubs, religious bodies, trade unions, schools and other groups through which people associate and live together, with shared rules and ways of life. So a network of groups of people expressing human association is a society: B. The trap: community (A) is close, but a community is a smaller group of people living in one locality and sharing common interests, such as a village; a society is the whole web of groups and associations. Memory tip: society = the web of all our associations; community = the people next door.",
 "|The Commission of Mediation, Conciliation and Arbitration is an organ of|the United Nations|the Commonwealth|the Organisation of Petroleum Exporting Countries|the African Union": "When the Organisation of African Unity was founded in 1963, its Charter created the Commission of Mediation, Conciliation and Arbitration (its protocol was signed in Cairo in 1964) to settle disputes among member states peacefully. The African Union replaced the OAU in 2002, and today it handles conflicts through its Peace and Security Council instead. Of the options, the body is that of the African Union: D. The United Nations (A) settles disputes through the Security Council and the International Court of Justice, not through this commission. Memory tip: Mediation, Conciliation, Arbitration = \"MCA\", Africa's own peace tool.",
 "|The policy of assimilation was the colonial policy of the|French|British|Portuguese|German": "The policy of assimilation was France's approach to its colonies, such as Senegal, Côte d'Ivoire and Dahomey (now Benin). It aimed to make Africans French in language, culture, religion and law, so that \"civilised\" Africans could become French citizens; the four communes of Senegal even sent deputies to Paris. So it was the colonial policy of the French: A. Portugal had a similar assimilado status, but assimilation as a named colonial doctrine is the French one. The trap: C, the Portuguese, also had a version of it (the assimilado system), but assimilation as a colonial policy is identified with the French; Britain (B) used indirect rule. Memory tip: France Assimilated, Britain ruled Indirectly.",
 "|The appointment, promotion and discipline of civil servants is the responsibility of the|Civil Service Commission|Public Complaints Commission|Judicial Service Commission|Public Service Institute of Nigeria": "To keep the civil service neutral and based on merit, the constitution sets up independent Civil Service Commissions, the Federal Civil Service Commission and a State Civil Service Commission in each state. They appoint, promote, discipline and dismiss civil servants, so that politicians cannot hire or fire them at will. So that responsibility belongs to the Civil Service Commission: A. The trap: the Judicial Service Commission (C) also appoints and disciplines, but only judicial officers and court staff, not the general civil service. Memory tip: each \"service\" has its own commission: civil service, judicial service, police service.",
 "|A country is said to operate an unwritten constitution if the|constitution is not contained in a single document|constitution cannot be written down|existing constitution can be suspended at will|country does not have a printed constitution": "A constitution is unwritten when its rules are not gathered in one document. Britain is the classic example: its constitution is found in Acts of Parliament (such as Magna Carta and the Parliament Acts), court decisions, conventions, and the writings of authorities. Much of it is in fact written down, just in many places. So a country operates an unwritten constitution if its constitution is not contained in a single document: A. The trap: \"constitution cannot be written down\" (B) takes the word \"unwritten\" literally, but most of Britain's constitution is written; it is just scattered. Memory tip: unwritten = uncodified, not unprinted.",
 "|The form of government headed by a king is|monarchy|presidentialism|fascism|republicanism": "A monarchy is a form of government headed by a king or queen (a monarch), who usually inherits the throne and reigns for life. It may be absolute, like Saudi Arabia, or constitutional, like the United Kingdom, where the monarch reigns but elected ministers rule. So the form of government headed by a king is monarchy: A. Republicanism (D) is the opposite: a republic is headed by an elected or appointed president, as in Nigeria since 1963. Memory tip: mono = one; a monarch is one ruler who inherits the crown.",
 "|Citizens exercise their sovereignty by|attending political rallies|voting at elections|registration to vote|their right to sue the state": "Popular sovereignty means that the final power in a democracy belongs to the people. They cannot all govern at once, so they use that power by choosing their representatives at periodic elections, and by removing them through the ballot when they are unhappy. Voting is therefore the main way citizens exercise their sovereignty: B. The trap: registration to vote (C) is a necessary step, but registering only makes a citizen eligible; the power is actually used when the vote is cast. Memory tip: the ballot is the people's sceptre.",
 "|Which of these is an attribute of government?|Political power|Sovereignty|Constitution|Definite territory": "The state is the whole political community; government is its machinery, the agency that makes, enforces and interprets laws for the state. What marks a government is political power: the lawful capacity to make decisions binding on everyone and to enforce them. So political power is an attribute of government: A. The trap: sovereignty (B) is tempting because a government exercises it, but sovereignty, like population and a definite territory (D), is an element of the state, which lasts while governments come and go. Memory tip: the state owns sovereignty; the government wields power.",
 "|The Nigerian judiciary is headed by the|Attorney-General and Minister of Justice|Solicitor-General of the Federation|Chief Justice of the Federation|President, Court of Appeal": "The Nigerian judiciary is headed by the Chief Justice of Nigeria (often called the Chief Justice of the Federation), appointed by the President on the recommendation of the National Judicial Council and confirmed by the Senate. He or she presides over the Supreme Court and chairs the National Judicial Council: C. The trap: the Attorney-General and Minister of Justice (A) is the government's chief law officer, but he is a member of the executive who advises the government and prosecutes, not a judge. Memory tip: the Chief JUSTICE judges; the Attorney-GENERAL advises and prosecutes.",
 "|The modern theory of sovereignty was popularised by|John Locke|Thomas Hobbes|Jean Bodin|Baron de Montesquieu": "The French jurist Jean Bodin set out the modern theory of sovereignty in Six Books of the Commonwealth (1576). He defined sovereignty as the absolute and perpetual power of a state to make laws, a power bound by no human law. Later writers built on him: Hobbes argued for an all-powerful sovereign, and John Austin gave the legal theory its classic form in the 19th century. So the theory was popularised by Jean Bodin: C. The trap: Thomas Hobbes (B) is tempting because his Leviathan (1651) also defends absolute sovereign power, but he came after Bodin and built on his ideas. Memory tip: Bodin = the \"Bod\" (body) of sovereignty theory.",
 "|Public opinion is commonly referred to as the composite reaction of the citizenry on|matters relating to association|legal rights of foreigners|matters affecting government|key issues that affect them": "Public opinion is the combined (composite) view of a large section of the citizens on important public issues that affect them, such as fuel prices, insecurity or a new law. It is formed through the media, discussion and leaders, and expressed through polls, protests, letters and votes. Governments in a democracy watch it closely. So it is the composite reaction of the citizenry on key issues that affect them: D. The trap: \"matters affecting government\" (C) is tempting, but public opinion is about issues that touch the people themselves, not only the internal affairs of government. Memory tip: public opinion = what the public thinks about what touches the public.",
 "|In order to assist fellow developing countries, Nigeria initiated the|New Partnership for African Development|Economic Community of West African States|Concert of Medium Powers|Technical Aid Corps Scheme": "In 1987, under General Babangida, Foreign Minister Bolaji Akinyemi proposed the Technical Aid Corps. It sends young Nigerian graduates and professionals, such as doctors, teachers, nurses and engineers, to serve for about two years in African, Caribbean and Pacific countries, as Nigeria's form of aid to fellow developing countries: D. The trap: the Concert of Medium Powers (C) was also Akinyemi's idea, but it was a plan to unite middle-ranking nations such as Nigeria, Brazil and India into a diplomatic bloc, not an aid scheme. Memory tip: TAC = Technical Aid Corps = Nigeria's \"Peace Corps\".",
 "|In the Igbo traditional system, the highest democratic institution is the|Umunna|Oha-na-eze|Umuada|Ofo title holders": "Pre-colonial Igbo society was acephalous (without a central king) and highly democratic. Each village was run by the elders, age grades, title holders and lineages, but major decisions were taken in the general assembly, the Oha-na-eze, where every adult male could speak and decisions were reached by consensus. So the highest democratic institution was the Oha-na-eze: B. The trap: the Umunna (A) is important, but it is the extended family or lineage group, a smaller unit whose heads meet inside the larger assembly. Memory tip: Oha = the people; Oha-na-eze = the whole people meeting together.",
 "|Separation of powers as an ingredient of the presidential system implies the|distinction of governmental power|demarcation of levels of government|distinction between head of state and head of government|sharing of governmental powers": "Separation of powers, explained by Montesquieu in The Spirit of the Laws (1748), means that the powers of government are shared out among three separate organs: the legislature makes laws, the executive carries them out and the judiciary interprets them, each with its own personnel, so that no one body holds all power. This sharing of governmental powers is central to the presidential system: D. The trap: \"demarcation of levels of government\" (B) describes federalism, which divides power vertically between central and state governments, not horizontally among the three arms. Memory tip: separation of powers is horizontal (three arms); federalism is vertical (levels).",
 "|The major objective of a pressure group is to|change government|form government|influence government|fund elections": "A pressure group, such as the NLC, NBA or ASUU, is an organised group that seeks to influence government policy in favour of its members' interests. It lobbies, petitions, issues statements, goes on strike or demonstrates, but it does not field candidates or try to take power. So its major objective is to influence government: C. The trap: \"form government\" (B) is the aim of a political party, which seeks to win elections and control government; a pressure group wants to influence whoever is in power. Memory tip: parties want POWER; pressure groups want POLICY.",
 "|The foreign policy principle that informed Nigeria's position in the liberation of African states is|economic diplomacy|Afrocentrism|non-alignment|non-interference": "From independence, Nigeria made Africa the centrepiece of its foreign policy. This Afrocentric principle led Nigeria to fund and arm liberation movements, to back the MPLA in Angola in 1975, to nationalise British Petroleum's interests in 1979 to push for Zimbabwe's independence, and to lead the fight against apartheid as a \"frontline state\". So the principle behind its role in liberating African states is Afrocentrism: B. The trap: non-alignment (C) is also a principle of Nigerian foreign policy, but it is about not joining either Cold War bloc, not about freeing African colonies. Memory tip: Afrocentrism = Africa at the centre.",
 "|A type of political association that brings the rich and the poor together is a|broker party|charismatic party|elite party|mass party": "A broker party sets out to bring together people from different classes and interests, such as the rich and the poor, workers and employers, and acts like a broker, reconciling their demands through bargaining and compromise to build a broad coalition. So a political association that brings the rich and the poor together is a broker party: A. The trap: the mass party (D) also has a wide membership, but its defining feature is that it mobilises the masses, the ordinary people, around one programme or ideology, rather than balancing the interests of rich and poor. Memory tip: a broker makes deals between both sides.",
 "|In Nigeria, the foundations of federalism were laid by the|Macpherson Constitution|Lyttelton Constitution|Clifford Constitution|Richards Constitution": "Sir Arthur Richards' constitution (1946) divided Nigeria into three regions, North, West and East, each with a regional House of Assembly, while keeping one central Legislative Council for the whole country. This regionalism laid the foundation of Nigerian federalism. The Macpherson Constitution (1951) strengthened the regions, and the Lyttelton Constitution (1954) made Nigeria a full federation. So the foundations were laid by the Richards Constitution: D. The trap: the Lyttelton Constitution (B) is tempting because it actually established federalism, but it built on foundations already laid in 1946. Memory tip: Richards laid the Regions; Lyttelton lifted them into a federation.",
 "|Which of the following is a form of delegated legislation?|Case law|Convention|Edict|Acts": "Delegated legislation is law made by a body other than the legislature, under power handed down by a higher law-maker: bye-laws, regulations and orders are the usual examples. Under military rule, state military governors could make edicts only within powers given to them by the federal military government's decrees, and federal decrees overrode them. An edict was therefore subordinate law made under delegated power: C. The trap: Acts (D) are made by the legislature itself, so they are primary legislation, not delegated; case law (A) is made by judges through precedent. Memory tip: delegated law is \"borrowed\" power: the maker needs a higher law behind it.",
 "|Franchise can be described as the right to|vote at elections|form political parties|vote and be voted for|contest in elections": "Franchise, also called suffrage, is the right of citizens to take part in elections. Nigerian textbooks usually define it as the right of qualified citizens to vote and be voted for: C. In the strict sense it means only the right to vote (A), so both answers are accepted here. The trap: \"contest in elections\" (D) is only the \"be voted for\" half; on its own it is the right to stand as a candidate, which is called eligibility, not franchise. Memory tip: franchise = your voice at the polls; universal adult suffrage gives every adult (18 and over in Nigeria) that voice.",
 "|A major factor that necessitated the adoption of federalism in Nigeria was the|intention of creating states|diversity of culture and language|abundance of natural resources|low level of literacy": "Nigeria has over 250 ethnic groups and hundreds of languages, several major religions and very different cultures and histories, especially between North and South. Federalism lets these groups live together under one government while each region or state controls its own local affairs; it also eases the minorities' fear of domination. So diversity of culture and language was a major factor: B. States (A) were created later, from 1967, inside a federation that already existed. Memory tip: Nigeria chose federalism because it is diverse: unity in diversity.",
 "|The office of the Prime Minister in Nigeria was created in|1951|1954|1959|1957": "At the 1957 London constitutional conference it was agreed that the federal government should have a Prime Minister. On 30 August 1957 Alhaji Abubakar Tafawa Balewa of the NPC became the first Prime Minister of the Federation, leading a national government that included the NCNC and the AG. He stayed in office until he was killed in the coup of January 1966. So the office was created in 1957: D. The trap: 1959 (C) is tempting because that was the year of the pre-independence federal election, after which Balewa was re-appointed, but the office already existed. Memory tip: 1957 = Balewa becomes PM; 1960 = independence with Balewa still at the helm.",
 "|The Nigeria Police is an agency of the|judicial arm of government|Ministry of Defence|executive arm of government|Ministry of Interior": "The police enforce laws, prevent and detect crime and keep public order, which is part of carrying out the law, the work of the executive. Under the 1999 Constitution the Nigeria Police Force is a federal body under the President, led by the Inspector-General of Police, with the Police Service Commission handling appointments and discipline. So it is an agency of the executive arm of government: C. The police arrest and prosecute suspects, but the courts of the judicial arm (A), which are separate, decide guilt. Memory tip: police = executive enforcers; courts = judges.",
 "|The legislature checks the excesses of the executive through|appointment of civil servants|screening of governors|scrutiny of the budget|nomination of judges": "Under the principle of checks and balances, the legislature controls the executive mainly through the power of the purse. The executive draws up the budget, but no public money can be spent unless the legislature approves it in an Appropriation Act. By examining, amending and passing the budget, and checking how it is spent, the legislature restrains the executive: C. Governors (B) are elected by the people and are not screened by the legislature; the Senate screens ministers and ambassadors. Memory tip: whoever holds the purse holds the executive in check.",
 "|Government by the best citizens is referred to as|aristocracy|democracy|oligarchy|plutocracy": "Aristocracy comes from the Greek aristos, \"best\", and kratos, \"rule\". In Aristotle's scheme it is rule by a few of the best or noblest citizens, governing for the good of all. Its corrupt form is oligarchy, rule by a few for their own benefit. So government by the best citizens is aristocracy: A. The trap: oligarchy (C) is also rule by a few, but by a selfish few governing for themselves, not the best citizens governing for all; plutocracy (D) is rule by the rich. Memory tip: aristocracy = the \"best\" few; oligarchy = the \"selfish\" few; plutocracy = the rich few.",
 "|The charter establishing the AU was signed in|Togo|Libya|South Africa|Ethiopia": "The African Union grew out of the Sirte Declaration made in Libya on 9 September 1999. Its founding charter, the Constitutive Act of the African Union, was adopted and signed at the OAU summit in Lomé, Togo, on 11 July 2000, and came into force on 26 May 2001. The AU was then launched in Durban, South Africa, on 9 July 2002, with its headquarters in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. So the charter was signed in Togo: A. The trap: South Africa (C) is tempting because the AU was formally launched there, but the launch came two years after the signing. Memory tip: Sirte gave the idea, Lomé signed the Act, Durban held the launch.",
 "|In Nigeria, items in the exclusive list are under the jurisdiction of the|regional government|central government|local government|state government": "Nigeria's constitution divides law-making powers into lists. The Exclusive Legislative List (68 items in the 1999 Constitution) covers matters only the central (federal) government can handle, such as defence, foreign affairs, currency, citizenship, the police and the armed forces. The Concurrent List is shared, and residual matters belong to the states. So items on the exclusive list are under the central government: B. State governments (D) legislate only on concurrent and residual matters; they cannot touch exclusive items. Memory tip: exclusive = for the centre alone.",
 "|Which of these factors motivated colonialism in Africa?|Cultural superiority|Scientific superiority|Humanitarianism|Economic interest": "European powers scrambled for Africa in the late 19th century, sharing it out at the Berlin Conference of 1884–85, mainly for economic reasons: the Industrial Revolution created a demand for raw materials such as palm oil, rubber, cotton and minerals, and for markets for European manufactured goods and places to invest capital. So economic interest motivated colonialism: D. Humanitarianism (C), to \"civilise\" Africans and end the slave trade, was the excuse often given, not the main motive. Memory tip: colonialism followed the money: raw materials in, manufactured goods out.",
 "|The responsibility of giving policy guidelines to the board of a corporation rests with the|Board of Directors|Minister|Chairman|Permanent Secretary": "A public corporation, such as the old NEPA or the Nigerian Railway Corporation, is run day to day by its board of directors and management. But because it is owned by the government and serves the public, the supervising minister has the power to give the board general policy directions, and answers for the corporation in the legislature. So that responsibility rests with the Minister: B. The trap: the Board of Directors (A) makes the corporation's internal decisions, but it receives policy guidelines from the minister rather than giving them to itself. Memory tip: the minister sets the direction; the board drives the corporation.",
 "|Which of these institutions makes bye-laws?|Council of State|National Assembly|Local Government Council|State House of Assembly": "Bye-laws are laws made by a local government council for its own area, under powers granted by state law. They cover local matters such as markets, motor parks, sanitation, rates, naming of streets and control of street trading. So bye-laws are made by the Local Government Council: C. The trap: the State House of Assembly (D) does make laws, but its laws are called state laws; it actually gives local councils the power to make bye-laws. Memory tip: \"bye\" comes from the Old Norse word for a town or village; bye-laws are village (local) laws.",
 "|Which of the following commissions was established by the 1979 Constitution?|Economic and Financial Crimes Commission|Federal Character Commission|Federal Road Safety Commission|Federal Electoral Commission": "The 1979 Constitution set up a number of federal executive bodies, including the Federal Civil Service Commission, the Federal Judicial Service Commission, the Police Service Commission, the National Population Commission and the Federal Electoral Commission (FEDECO), which conducted the 1979 and 1983 elections. FEDECO's work is now done by INEC, set up in 1998 and recognised by the 1999 Constitution. So the commission established by the 1979 Constitution is the Federal Electoral Commission: D. The trap: the Federal Character Commission (B) is tempting because the 1979 Constitution introduced the federal character principle, but the commission itself came only in 1996 and is in the 1999 Constitution. Memory tip: FEDECO = 1979; INEC = 1999.",
 "|Federal and state governments legislate on items in the|residual list|legislative list|exclusive list|concurrent list": "In the Nigerian constitution, the Exclusive List is for the federal government alone; the Concurrent List contains matters on which both the federal and state governments can legislate, such as education, agriculture, electricity and industrial development; residual matters belong to the states. Where federal and state laws conflict on a concurrent item, federal law prevails. So federal and state governments legislate on items in the concurrent list: D. The trap: residual list (A) is wrong because residual matters are left to the states alone, not shared. Memory tip: concurrent = \"running together\": both levels share it.",
 "|In Nigeria, public corporations are established by|executive order|a bye-law|an act of parliament|military decree": "A public corporation is created by a statute, an Act of the legislature (or, under military rule, a decree), which sets out its name, functions, powers, board and relationship with its ministry. Examples include the Nigerian Railway Corporation and NNPC (as originally created). So in Nigeria public corporations are established by an act of parliament: C. Military decrees (D) did create corporations under military rule, but that was the exception; the normal constitutional way is an Act of the legislature. Memory tip: statutory corporation = born of a statute.",
 "|In the Hausa pre-colonial political system, the spiritual and political powers were vested in the|Emir|Madawaki|Galadima|Waziri": "In the Hausa-Fulani emirates set up after Usman dan Fodio's jihad (1804), the Emir held both political and spiritual power. As political head he made laws, appointed officials and collected taxes; as religious leader he ruled according to Islamic (Sharia) law and was the Commander of the Faithful in his emirate, owing allegiance to the Sultan of Sokoto or Emir of Gwandu. So spiritual and political powers were vested in the Emir: A. The trap: the Waziri (D) was the most senior official, like a prime minister, but he only advised and served the Emir. Memory tip: the Emir = both mosque and palace.",
 "|Which of the following approves the annual budget of the UN?|The General Assembly|The Secretariat|The Security Council|The Trusteeship Council": "The UN General Assembly, in which every member state has one vote, is the UN's \"parliament\". Under Article 17 of the Charter it considers and approves the budget and decides how expenses are shared among members; budget decisions are \"important questions\" needing a two-thirds majority. So the General Assembly approves the annual UN budget: A. The trap: the Secretariat (B) prepares the draft budget, through the Secretary-General, but it cannot approve it; only the General Assembly can. Memory tip: the Secretariat proposes; the General Assembly disposes.",
 "|Lagos was annexed in|1861|1971|1814|1853": "In 1851 the British bombarded Lagos and replaced Oba Kosoko with Akitoye, claiming to fight the slave trade. Ten years later, in August 1861, Akitoye's son Oba Dosunmu was made to sign the Treaty of Cession, and Lagos was annexed by Britain; it became a crown colony in 1862. This was the start of formal British rule in Nigeria. So Lagos was annexed in 1861: A. The trap: 1853 (D) is close in time, but it was the year Dosunmu became Oba, not the year Lagos was ceded. Memory tip: 1861 = Lagos ceded; 1914 = amalgamation; 1960 = independence.",
 "|A disadvantage of the one-party system is that it|is costly to operate|does not tolerate opposition|does not care about the feelings of the masses|discourages periodic elections": "In a one-party system only one party is allowed by law, as in China or Tanzania under Nyerere. Supporters say it unites the nation, but its great weakness is that no organised opposition is tolerated: critics are silenced, voters have no real alternative, and the system can slide into dictatorship. So a disadvantage of the one-party system is that it does not tolerate opposition: B. Many one-party states do hold regular elections (D); the problem is that voters can choose only within the single party. Memory tip: one party, no opposition.",
 "|At the formation of the OAU, Nigeria belonged to the|Monrovia group|Casablanca group|Lagos group|Brazzaville group": "Before the OAU was formed in May 1963, independent African states split into rival blocs. The radical Casablanca group (Ghana, Guinea, Mali, Egypt, Morocco and others) wanted immediate political union of Africa. The moderate Monrovia group, formed in Liberia in 1961, which Nigeria joined, wanted gradual cooperation among sovereign states with non-interference. So Nigeria belonged to the Monrovia group: A. The trap: the Lagos group (C) is tempting because the Monrovia group held its second meeting in Lagos in 1962, but the bloc is known as the Monrovia group. Memory tip: Monrovia = moderate; Casablanca = radical.",
 "|The ECOWAS Monitoring Group began its operation in|Sierra Leone|Guinea|Gambia|Liberia": "ECOMOG, the ECOWAS Ceasefire Monitoring Group, was created in 1990, mainly at Nigeria's initiative under General Babangida, to end the civil war in Liberia that had broken out after Charles Taylor's invasion in December 1989. Its troops landed in Monrovia in August 1990, its first operation: D. It later intervened in Sierra Leone (1997–2000) and Guinea-Bissau (1998–99). The trap: Sierra Leone (A) is tempting because ECOMOG's operation there to restore President Kabbah in 1998 is well known, but it came years after Liberia. Memory tip: ECOMOG's first stop was Liberia, home of the Monrovia group too.",
 "|The Northern and Southern protectorates were made provinces under the|Macpherson Constitution|Richards Constitution|Clifford Constitution|Lyttelton Constitution": "Lord Lugard amalgamated the Northern and Southern Protectorates on 1 January 1914, and from then on they were administered as the Northern Provinces and the Southern Provinces, each under a lieutenant-governor. The 1922 Clifford Constitution, the first of Nigeria's colonial constitutions, kept that arrangement: its Legislative Council made laws only for Lagos and the Southern Provinces, while the governor made laws for the Northern Provinces by proclamation: C. The trap: the Richards Constitution (B) is tempting because it also divided the country, but in 1946 it created three regions (North, West and East), not two provinces. Memory tip: Clifford 1922 = two provinces; Richards 1946 = three regions; Lyttelton 1954 = federation.",
 "|The legal right to exercise power in a state is referred to as|authority|sovereignty|coercion|influence": "Power is the ability to make others do what you want; authority is the legal, recognised right to use that power and to be obeyed. A police officer directing traffic or a president issuing an order acts with authority because the law gives them that right: A. The trap: sovereignty (B) is close, but it means the supreme and final power of the state itself, not the legal right of an office-holder to exercise power. Coercion is the use of force, and influence is persuading others without any formal right. Memory tip: authority = power + legality (legal right to rule).",
 "|The primary reason for the establishment of political parties is to|attain political power|protect political interest|make laws|lobby the government": "A political party is an organised group of people with common ideas who come together to contest elections so as to win, control and use the power of government. Everything else a party does, such as presenting candidates, educating voters and aggregating interests, serves that main aim: A. The trap: lobbying government (D) is what pressure groups do, since they want to influence government without taking it over. Laws are made by the legislature, not by the party as such. Memory tip: Parties want Power; pressure groups want Policies.",
 "|The head of government under the 1979 Constitution is the|President|Prime Minister|Speaker of the House of Representatives|Senate President": "The 1979 Constitution, drafted in 1975–76 and in force from 1 October 1979, replaced the Westminster parliamentary model with an American-style presidential system. The executive President (Shehu Shagari from 1979) was both head of state and head of government, and he chose ministers from outside the National Assembly: A. The trap: the Prime Minister (B) was head of government under the 1960 and 1963 Constitutions (Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa), not under 1979. The Speaker and Senate President head the two houses of the legislature. Memory tip: 1979 = Presidential; 1960/63 = Prime Minister.",
 "|A system of government in which an individual rules with absolute power is|theocracy|democracy|autocracy|aristocracy": "Autocracy (from the Greek for \"self-rule\") is government by one person who has absolute, unchecked power and is not answerable to the people or to any law; absolute monarchs and many dictators are examples: C. Theocracy is rule by religious leaders or in the name of God; democracy is rule by the people; aristocracy is rule by a few nobles or \"the best\". Memory tip: AUTOcracy = AUTOmatic, one-man rule.",
 "|One of the demerits of the privatisation of public corporations in Nigeria is|scarcity of goods and services|higher cost of goods and services|creation of unemployment|inefficient service delivery": "Privatisation sells government-owned corporations to private investors. Because private owners run the businesses to make profit, they remove subsidies and charge economic prices, so the cost of goods and services such as electricity, telephone and fuel rises for ordinary people: B. Job losses (C) are also often listed as a demerit, since new owners cut excess staff, but higher cost to consumers is the standard answer expected here. Scarcity and inefficient service are the problems privatisation is meant to cure, not ones it creates. Memory tip: private = profit = higher prices.",
 "|In which of the following does the state have absolute control of all aspects of citizens' life?|Autocracy|Democracy|Communalism|Totalitarianism": "Totalitarianism is a system in which the state, usually under one party and one leader, controls every aspect of citizens' lives: politics, the economy, education, religion, culture, the media and even family life. Hitler's Germany and Stalin's Soviet Union are the classic examples: D. The trap: autocracy (A) also means absolute power, but it describes who rules (one person); totalitarianism describes how far the state reaches, into everything. Memory tip: TOTALitarian = TOTAL control of life.",
 "|Early nationalist activities manifested in the form of|discrimination|resistance|participation|disagreement": "The first form of nationalism against colonial rule was resistance. Traditional rulers and peoples fought the British advance by arms or refused to submit: Jaja of Opobo, Nana of Itsekiri, Oba Ovonramwen of Benin (1897) and the Aro, among others. Later came protests, such as the Aba Women's Riot of 1929, and only after that organised parties and constitutional demands: B. The other options do not describe a form of nationalist activity. Memory tip: nationalism grew in three steps: Resist, Protest, then Organise parties.",
 "|One major merit of the unitary system of government is that it|is not democratic|is cheaper to operate|eradicates corruption|eliminates tyranny": "In a unitary state all governmental power belongs to one central government, which may delegate some to local units. With only one legislature, one executive and one civil service, it avoids the many duplicated institutions of a federation, so it is cheaper to operate: B. Being undemocratic is not a merit, and no system eradicates corruption or eliminates tyranny; indeed concentration of power can encourage tyranny. Memory tip: one government, one bill: unitary is cheaper.",
 "|Successive population figures have been contested in Nigeria because of|gender consideration|religious consideration|geographical disparity|political consideration": "In Nigeria population figures decide how many seats each area gets in the legislature, how revenue is shared, how many local governments and constituencies are created and where amenities go. Every region and group therefore has a political interest in a high count, and results are challenged for political reasons, as with the 1962/63, 1973 (cancelled), 1991 and 2006 censuses: D. The trap: geographical disparity (C) sounds plausible, but differences between regions are not why figures are disputed; it is what the figures are used for. Memory tip: in Nigeria a headcount is a power count.",
 "|Akwa Ibom and Katsina States were created in|1987|1985|1988|1990": "On 23 September 1987 General Ibrahim Babangida created two new states, Akwa Ibom (carved out of Cross River) and Katsina (out of Kaduna), raising the number of states from 19 to 21: A. The trap: 1988 (C) and 1985 (B) sit close; 1985 is the year Babangida came to power, not the year he created states. In 1991 he created nine more states to make 30. Memory tip: 1967: 12, 1976: 19, 1987: 21, 1991: 30, 1996: 36.",
 "|The African country that was not colonized is|Sudan|Botswana|Gambia|Ethiopia": "Ethiopia is the African country that was never colonised. Emperor Menelik II defeated the Italians at the Battle of Adwa in 1896; apart from the Italian occupation of 1936–41 it kept its independence, which is why the OAU (now the AU) has its headquarters in Addis Ababa. Liberia, founded by freed American slaves, is the other country usually named. Sudan (Anglo-Egyptian), Botswana (Bechuanaland) and The Gambia were all British: D. Memory tip: Adwa 1896: Ethiopia stood unconquered.",
 "|One of the major sources of a constitution is|party system|conventions|political structure|political party": "A constitution draws on several sources: statutes (Acts of the legislature), judicial precedents, conventions, customs, historical documents and the writings of learned authors. Conventions are unwritten rules of political practice, for example that the British monarch assents to bills passed by Parliament, which are obeyed as if they were law: B. The trap: political party (D) and party system (A) shape how a constitution works in practice, but they are not sources from which constitutional rules come. Memory tip: sources = \"SCJ-CH\": Statutes, Conventions, Judicial precedents, Customs, Historical documents.",
 "|Absence of a charter has been a problem of|ECOWAS|UNO|OAU|Commonwealth": "The UN rests on the UN Charter (1945), the OAU had its Charter (1963) and ECOWAS has the Treaty of Lagos (1975, revised 1993). The Commonwealth, by contrast, grew out of the British Empire as a loose voluntary association and for decades had no founding charter, only declarations such as London (1949), Singapore (1971) and Harare (1991), so its aims and rules were unclear: D. Today this has changed: the Charter of the Commonwealth was adopted in December 2012 and signed in March 2013. Memory tip: the Commonwealth ran on declarations, not a charter, until 2013.",
 "|One of the duties and obligations of the state to the citizens is|loyalty|payment of wages|protection of property|assistance": "The relationship between state and citizen runs both ways. The state's duties to citizens include protection of lives and property, maintenance of law and order, defence against external attack and provision of welfare such as education and health: C. The trap: loyalty (A) is a duty too, but it is one the citizen owes the state, not one the state owes the citizen. Payment of wages is owed by employers to their workers. Memory tip: citizens give loyalty; the state gives protection.",
 "|The appointment and dissolution of the Board of Directors of public corporations fall under|ministerial control|legislative control|political control|judicial control": "Public corporations are controlled in several ways. Ministerial control means the supervising minister appoints the chairman and members of the board, can dissolve the board, issues general policy directives and approves major decisions: A. The trap: legislative control (B) is close, but the legislature controls corporations by passing the law that creates them, voting their funds and investigating them through committees; it does not hire and fire boards. Judicial control means the courts can hear cases against corporations. Memory tip: the Minister makes and breaks the board.",
 "|Which of the following is a process of military disengagement from Nigerian politics?|Increase in military budget|Conduct of free and fair elections|Appointment of army officers into political posts|Strengthening the army": "Military disengagement is the process by which soldiers hand power back to civilians. It normally involves a transition programme: lifting the ban on parties, a new constitution, an electoral body and, at the end, free and fair elections followed by handover to elected leaders, as Obasanjo did on 1 October 1979 and Abubakar on 29 May 1999: B. Raising the military budget, strengthening the army or giving officers political posts would all deepen military involvement, not end it. Memory tip: disengagement ends at the ballot box.",
 "|Which of the following is an advantage of the presidential system of government?|Checks and balances|Absence of opposition|Cost effectiveness|Less red-tapism": "In the presidential system the executive, legislature and judiciary are separate, and each checks the others: the president can veto bills, the legislature can override a veto, confirm appointments, control money and impeach, and the courts can declare acts unconstitutional. This system of checks and balances guards against abuse of power: A. The presidential system is in fact expensive (C is a disadvantage, not an advantage), it still has opposition parties, and it does not reduce red tape. Memory tip: presidential = power separated and checked.",
 "|The chairman of the 1976 Constitution Drafting Committee was|Udo Udoma|Ademola Adetokunbo|Rotimi Williams|Niki Tobi": "The 1979 Constitution was drafted by a 49-member Constitution Drafting Committee (the \"49 wise men\") set up by the Murtala/Obasanjo regime in 1975 and chaired by Chief F.R.A. (Rotimi) Williams; its draft was debated by the Constituent Assembly of 1977–78: C. The trap: Justice Udo Udoma (A) was also part of that process, but he chaired the Constituent Assembly, not the Drafting Committee. Justice Niki Tobi chaired the 1998 Constitution Debate Coordinating Committee. Memory tip: Williams Wrote the draft; Udoma debated it.",
 "|The totality of Nigeria's participation in the international system is known as|international policy|foreign policy|economic policy|social policy": "Foreign policy is the totality of a country's dealings with the rest of the world: the principles, goals and actions that guide its relations with other states and international organisations, such as Nigeria's Afrocentric policy, its membership of the UN, AU and ECOWAS, and peacekeeping: B. The trap: \"international policy\" (A) sounds similar, but it is not the recognised term; economic and social policies are mainly domestic. Memory tip: foreign policy = home country, foreign relations.",
 "|The organ of the UN that served as custodian of some African countries prior to their independence is|the International Court of Justice|the Security Council|the Trusteeship Council|the General Assembly": "The UN Trusteeship Council was one of the six principal organs of the UN. It supervised trust territories, mainly former League of Nations mandates taken from Germany, and prepared them for independence. African trust territories included Tanganyika, British and French Togoland, the British and French Cameroons, Ruanda-Urundi and Italian Somaliland. Once Palau became independent in 1994 its work ended and it has been inactive since: C. The trap: the General Assembly (D) oversaw the system politically, but the custodian body was the Trusteeship Council. Memory tip: Trusteeship = held in trust until independence.",
 "|Grants received by the local government councils in Nigeria are from|National Revenue Allocation Commission|federal and state governments|federal government only|state government only": "Since the 1976 Local Government Reform, local councils in Nigeria have been funded by statutory allocations and grants from both the federal government (their share of the Federation Account) and their state government (a share of state revenue); they also raise internal revenue from rates, licences and fees: B. The trap: federal government only (C) is tempting because the federal allocation is the biggest single source, but state governments also give grants. The National Revenue Allocation Commission recommends formulas; it does not give grants. Memory tip: local councils have two parents paying: federal and state.",
 "|Disputes among member states of ECOWAS are settled by|the technical and specialized commission|the board of trustees|the Community Tribunal|the Council of Ministers": "The ECOWAS Treaty of Lagos (1975) created a Community Tribunal to interpret the treaty and settle disputes between member states. The revised treaty of 1993 renamed it the Community Court of Justice; the Court sits in Abuja and began work in 2001: C. Technical and specialised commissions handle sectors such as trade and agriculture, and the Council of Ministers advises the Authority of Heads of State and Government. Memory tip: tribunal = trial: disputes go to the Community Tribunal, today the ECOWAS Court of Justice.",
 "|Ensuring that appointments into federal establishments in Nigeria reflect geographical spread is the sole function of the|Revenue Mobilization Allocation and Fiscal Commission|Civil Service Commission|Federal Character Commission|Public Complaints Commission": "Federal character means that appointments and the siting of projects should reflect the diversity of Nigeria, so that no few states or groups dominate. The Federal Character Commission, created in 1996 and entrenched in the 1999 Constitution (Third Schedule), monitors and enforces fair spread of posts across states in federal establishments: C. The trap: the Civil Service Commission (B) appoints civil servants, but making sure the appointments reflect geographical spread is the special job of the Federal Character Commission. The RMAFC deals with revenue; the Public Complaints Commission is Nigeria's ombudsman. Memory tip: Federal Character Commission = fair spread of posts.",
 "|A major difference between a pressure group and a political party is that whereas the latter aims at winning power, the former|executes government policies|aims at winning elections|influences government policies|nominates potential leaders for government": "A political party seeks to win elections and form the government. A pressure group, such as the NLC or the NBA, does not want to govern; it seeks only to influence government policies in favour of its members or cause, by lobbying, petitions, strikes and the media: C. The trap: nominating potential leaders (D) and winning elections (B) are what parties do, not pressure groups. Executing policy is the job of the executive. Memory tip: parties want to rule; pressure groups want to sway the rulers.",
 "|The quorum in the OPEC Conference is formed with the attendance of|one third of member states|half of member states|two thirds of member states|three quarters of member states": "The OPEC Conference, made up of the oil ministers of member countries, is OPEC's supreme authority and normally meets twice a year at its Vienna headquarters. Under the OPEC Statute a quorum of three-quarters of the full members must be present for the Conference to meet, and its decisions are normally unanimous: D. The trap: half (B) is the common quorum in many bodies, but OPEC sets a higher bar. Memory tip: OPEC needs three-quarters in the room.",
 "|The characteristic of the civil service which prevents its officers from addressing the press unless directed is known as|immorality|neutrality|anonymity|impersonality": "Anonymity is the principle that civil servants work behind the scenes. They do not take public credit or blame for policies and do not speak to the press unless authorised; the minister answers publicly for the ministry: C. The trap: neutrality (B) is a related principle, but it means civil servants serve every government loyally without taking party sides. Impersonality means treating cases by rules, not personal feelings. Memory tip: anonymous = no name in the news.",
 "|Which of the following emphasizes the rule of law and human rights protection?|Constitutionalism|Delegated legislation|Decentralisation|Centralisation": "Constitutionalism is the idea that government power is limited by a constitution and must be exercised according to it. It goes with the rule of law, separation of powers, an independent judiciary and the protection of fundamental human rights: A. Delegated legislation (law made by bodies under powers given by the legislature), decentralisation and centralisation are about who exercises power, not about limiting it to protect rights. Memory tip: constitutionalism = government under the constitution, citizens under its protection.",
 "|The needs of the citizens are made known to the government through|judiciary|political party|civil service|public opinion": "Public opinion is the views of citizens on public issues, expressed through the press, radio and social media, opinion polls, petitions, demonstrations and voting. It is the main way government learns what the people need and want: D. Political parties (B) also carry demands to government by aggregating interests, so the option is close; but public opinion is the broader channel through which citizens' needs reach government. The judiciary settles disputes, and the civil service carries out policy. Memory tip: public opinion = the voice of the people.",
 "|The tenet of non-alignment connotes|political instability|political neutrality|political alliance|political non-participation": "Non-alignment was the policy of states (Nigeria among them) that refused to join either bloc in the Cold War: the US-led Western bloc (NATO) or the Soviet-led Eastern bloc (Warsaw Pact). It meant political neutrality and independent judgement on each issue, not withdrawal from world affairs; the Non-Aligned Movement was founded in Belgrade in 1961: B. The trap: political non-participation (D) is wrong because non-aligned states took a very active part in world affairs, through the UN for example. Memory tip: non-aligned = neutral, not absent.",
 "|Smaller parties have little chance of winning elections in a|alternative voting system|proportional representation system|absolute majority system|pluralist system": "Under the simple plurality (\"first-past-the-post\") system, the candidate with the most votes in each single-member constituency wins, even without a majority. A small party with support spread thinly across the country can win many votes but few or no seats, so smaller parties have little chance: D. The trap: absolute majority (C) also squeezes small parties, but it at least gives them a role in second rounds; proportional representation (B) is the system that favours small parties, since seats follow vote share. Memory tip: plurality punishes the small; PR protects them.",
 "|The head of the palace in the pre-colonial Hausa system is|Sarkin Dawaki|Sarkin Gida|Sarkin Fada|Sarkin Ruwa": "In the Hausa-Fulani emirate the emir ruled with titled officials. The Sarkin Fada was the head of palace officials and workers and acted as the link between the emir and the other officials: C. The trap: Sarkin Gida (B) sounds right because \"gida\" means house or home, but the palace title is Sarkin Fada (\"fada\" is the palace). The Sarkin Dawaki commanded the cavalry, and the Sarkin Ruwa was in charge of rivers and fishing. Memory tip: Fada = palace, Dawaki = horses, Ruwa = water.",
 "|The main function of the legislature is|law review|law making|law implementation|law interpretation": "The three organs of government each have a main function: the legislature makes laws, the executive implements them, and the judiciary interprets them. The legislature also controls finance, checks the executive and represents the people, but its main function is law making: B. The trap: law review (A) is something legislatures do when they amend laws, but it is part of law making, not its main function. Memory tip: Legislature makes, Executive executes, Judiciary judges.",
 "|One of the demerits of the 1979 Constitution in Nigeria was that of|concentration of power at the centre|impeachment procedure|separation of powers|cost of governance": "The 1979 Constitution brought in an American-style presidential system: an executive President, Vice-President and ministers, a Senate and a House of Representatives, and in each of 19 states an executive governor and a House of Assembly, all with large salaries, aides and elections. The high cost of running this system was one of its major demerits: D. Concentration of power at the centre (A) is also sometimes criticised, but the expense of the presidential system is the standard answer. Impeachment and separation of powers are safeguards, not demerits. Memory tip: 1979 = presidential = pricey.",
 "|A major feature of monarchy is that it is|headed by a king|hereditary|headed by a queen|headed by the richest citizen": "A monarchy is a system headed by a monarch (king, queen, emperor or sultan) who comes to the throne by heredity: the crown passes within a royal family, usually to the eldest son or daughter: B. The trap: \"headed by a king\" (A) is tempting, but many monarchies are headed by queens, and kingship alone does not define the system; it is heredity that does. Memory tip: monarchy is born, not elected.",
 "|The New Partnership for Africa's Development was an attempt to overcome|civil wars in Africa|diseases in Africa|obstacles to African development|coups in Africa": "NEPAD, the New Partnership for Africa's Development, was adopted in 2001 by the OAU and taken over by the African Union. It was driven by Presidents Olusegun Obasanjo of Nigeria, Thabo Mbeki of South Africa, Abdelaziz Bouteflika of Algeria and Abdoulaye Wade of Senegal, to overcome the obstacles to African development: poverty, debt, poor infrastructure and bad governance, partly through the African Peer Review Mechanism: C. Civil wars, disease and coups are only parts of the wider problem. Memory tip: NEPAD's \"D\" is for Development.",
 "|One important agent of political socialisation is the|political party structure|political party|party constitution|party manifesto": "Political socialisation is how people acquire political values, beliefs and attitudes. Its agents include the family, school, peer groups, religious bodies, the mass media, the workplace and political parties; parties educate members through rallies, campaigns and manifestos: B. The trap: political party structure (A), party constitution (C) and party manifesto (D) belong to a party, but they are not themselves agents that socialise people; the party as a whole is the agent. Memory tip: agents of socialisation are people and institutions that teach: family, school, peers, religion, media, parties.",
 "|Bye-laws are made by the|judiciary|legislature|local government|executive": "Bye-laws are rules made by a local government council for its own area, under powers given to it by state law: for example rules on markets, motor parks, sanitation, building and licensing. They are a form of delegated legislation: C. The trap: the legislature (B) makes laws for the whole state or country, not bye-laws; bye-laws are made by local councils under powers delegated by the legislature. Memory tip: BYE-law = local law for the area \"by\" you.",
 "|In the AU, which of the following organs prepares the agenda for meetings?|The Council of Ministers|General Secretariat|The Liberation Committee|The Assembly of Heads of State and Government": "In the OAU the Council of Ministers (foreign ministers) prepared the meetings of the Assembly of Heads of State and Government. The African Union kept the arrangement under a new name: the Executive Council of ministers prepares the sessions and draws up the provisional agenda of the Assembly: A. The trap: the Assembly (D) is the supreme organ that meets on the agenda, but it does not prepare it. The Liberation Committee was an OAU body for freeing colonies, dissolved in 1994. Memory tip: ministers prepare, heads of state decide.",
 "|Which of these is an economic programme designed by the military?|National Youth Service Corps|War Against Indiscipline|Directorate of Food, Roads and Rural Infrastructure|Mass Mobilization for Social Justice": "The Directorate of Food, Roads and Rural Infrastructure (DFRRI) was set up by General Babangida's military government in 1986, during the Structural Adjustment Programme, to develop rural areas through feeder roads, water supply, electricity and food production: an economic programme: C. The others were social or political: NYSC (Gowon, 1973) for national unity, War Against Indiscipline (Buhari–Idiagbon, 1984) for public discipline, and MAMSER (Babangida, 1987) for political mobilisation ahead of the return to civil rule. Memory tip: DFRRI = Food and Roads: the economy in the villages.",
 "|Which of the following is the foremost right of a citizen?|Religious right|Academic right|Right to life|Private life": "Fundamental human rights include the rights to life, dignity, personal liberty, fair hearing, private and family life, freedom of thought, conscience and religion, expression, assembly and movement. The right to life comes first, both in importance and in the 1999 Constitution (section 33), because a person must be alive to enjoy any other right: C. Religious freedom and private life are real rights too, but they depend on life itself; \"academic right\" is not a recognised fundamental right. Memory tip: no life, no other rights, so life comes first.",
 "|Which of the following is a merit of aristocracy?|Leaders must have military experience|The best citizens are in control of government|The organised few control the government|The majority control the government": "Aristocracy comes from the Greek aristos (best) and kratos (rule): government by a small group of the best, most able and most cultured citizens, usually nobles. Its supposed merit is that the state is run by wise and experienced people: B. The trap: \"the organised few control the government\" (C) describes oligarchy, rule by a few for their own selfish interest, which is aristocracy's corrupt form. Majority rule (D) is democracy, and military experience is no part of the idea. Memory tip: aristocracy = rule of the BEST; oligarchy = rule of the FEW for themselves.",
 "|Which of the following is a strategy of foreign policy implementation?|Cultural integration|Democratic elections|Political representation|Propaganda": "Foreign policy states what a country wants from the outside world; it is implemented through instruments such as diplomacy (negotiation through ambassadors), propaganda (spreading information and ideas abroad to win support), economic aid and sanctions, alliances and, as a last resort, war. Propaganda is therefore a recognised strategy of foreign policy implementation: D. Democratic elections and political representation are domestic political processes, and cultural integration is not a tool for dealing with other states. Memory tip: foreign policy tools = \"DPEAW\": Diplomacy, Propaganda, Economic pressure, Alliances, War.",
 "|The Structural Adjustment Programme was introduced under the|Babangida Regime|Obasanjo Regime|Buhari Regime|Abacha Regime": "The Structural Adjustment Programme (SAP) was introduced by General Ibrahim Babangida's military government in July 1986, following IMF and World Bank prescriptions. It devalued the naira through the Second-tier Foreign Exchange Market, deregulated the economy, privatised and commercialised public enterprises and removed subsidies: A. The trap: Buhari's regime (C, 1984–85) also tackled the economic crisis, but through austerity and counter-trade, and it refused the IMF loan conditions; SAP came after Babangida overthrew Buhari in August 1985. Memory tip: Babangida = SAP 1986 (and DFRRI, MAMSER).",
 "|To facilitate the effective achievement of its objectives, ECOWAS is operationally structured in|councils|panels|committees|commissions": "To carry out its work, the 1975 ECOWAS Treaty created, under the Authority of Heads of State and the Council of Ministers, specialised commissions: Trade, Customs, Immigration, Monetary and Payments; Industry, Agriculture and Natural Resources; Transport, Communications and Energy; and Social and Cultural Affairs. These commissions are the operational structure through which its objectives are pursued: D. The trap: councils (A) is close, because ECOWAS has a Council of Ministers, but that is a policy organ, not the working structure. Since 2007 the Executive Secretariat has itself been renamed the ECOWAS Commission. Memory tip: ECOWAS works through Commissions.",
 "|Shortage of trained personnel is a major problem of the|Federal Government|State Government|Regional Government|Local Government": "Local government is the tier closest to the people, yet it suffers most from shortage of trained personnel. It pays less than federal and state services, offers fewer prospects for promotion and training, and is often in rural areas, so qualified doctors, engineers, accountants and administrators avoid it or leave quickly: D. Other well-known problems of local government include inadequate funds, interference by state governments and corruption. A \"Regional Government\" no longer exists in Nigeria; regions were replaced by states in 1967. Memory tip: local government problems = \"FIPS\": Funds, Interference, Personnel shortage, Sleaze.",
 "|The right of a state is known as|authority|power|sovereignty|legitimacy": "Sovereignty is the state's supreme legal right to make and enforce laws over everyone in its territory, and to be free from control by any other state. It is one of the four essential features of a state, with population, territory and government: C. The trap: authority (A) is also a legal right, but it belongs to persons and offices acting within the state, not to the state itself. Legitimacy means the people accept a government as rightful, and power is only the ability to make others obey. Memory tip: sovereignty = the state's final say.",
 "|The popularity of a political party in a given democracy rests on its|constitution|manifesto|ideology|number of branches": "A party's manifesto is its published programme: the policies it promises to carry out if elected (free education, jobs, roads, security). Voters compare manifestos, so a party's popularity in a democracy rests mainly on how attractive and believable its manifesto is: B. The trap: ideology (C) is close, since the manifesto grows out of the party's ideology, but most voters respond to concrete promises rather than abstract beliefs, and many Nigerian parties have no clear ideology at all. A party constitution only governs its internal affairs. Memory tip: Manifesto = what the voters Measure.",
 "|A system of local council that allows for rotational leadership is known as|single executive|dual executive|multi executive|collegiate executive": "In a collegiate (plural) executive, executive power belongs to a council or committee of equals rather than to one person, and the chairmanship rotates among the members. The Swiss Federal Council, whose seven members take turns as president for one year, is the classic example; some local councils have been run the same way: D. The trap: multi executive (C) sounds similar, but it is not the recognised term; dual executive means a ceremonial head plus a real head (as in parliamentary systems). Memory tip: Collegiate = Colleagues taking turns.",
 "|A type of constitution that is difficult to amend is described as|written and flexible|rigid and written|unwritten and rigid|flexible and rigid": "A rigid constitution can only be amended by a special, difficult procedure, unlike an ordinary law. Nigeria's 1999 Constitution, for example, needs a two-thirds majority in each house of the National Assembly and approval by at least two-thirds (24) of the State Houses of Assembly. Rigid constitutions are written in one document, so the best description is \"rigid and written\": B. The trap: \"unwritten and rigid\" (C) mixes the wrong pair, because unwritten constitutions such as Britain's are flexible and can be changed by ordinary Acts of Parliament. Memory tip: Rigid goes with wRitten; unwritten goes with flexible.",
 "|The political achievement of the UN is the promotion of|economic development|educational development|international peace and security|democratic institutions": "The UN was founded on 24 October 1945, after the Second World War, and its first purpose under Article 1 of the Charter is to maintain international peace and security. Through the Security Council, peacekeeping missions, mediation and disarmament efforts it has helped to settle many conflicts and prevent another world war; this is its main political achievement: C. Economic and educational development are the work of its economic and social agencies (UNDP, UNESCO), and the UN does not itself create democratic institutions. Memory tip: UN = peace first.",
 "|The elective principle in Nigeria was first introduced by the|Richards Constitution|Macpherson Constitution|Lyttelton Constitution|Clifford Constitution": "The 1922 Clifford Constitution, named after Governor Sir Hugh Clifford, introduced the elective principle in Nigeria: for the first time Nigerians could elect members of the Legislative Council, three for Lagos and one for Calabar, with the vote limited to adult males earning at least £100 a year. This led Herbert Macaulay to form the NNDP in 1923, Nigeria's first political party: D. The trap: Richards (A, 1946) and Macpherson (B, 1951) widened elected representation, but they built on what Clifford had begun. Memory tip: Clifford = first elections; Richards = regions; Lyttelton = federalism.",
 "|The three major political parties of the First Republic can be said to have had|national outlook|regional and ethnic undertones|government funding|religious and sectional appeals": "The three major parties of the First Republic (1960–66) were each rooted in one region and its main ethnic group: the Northern People's Congress (Hausa-Fulani North, led by Sir Ahmadu Bello), the Action Group (Yoruba West, Chief Obafemi Awolowo) and the NCNC (Igbo East, Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe). They therefore had regional and ethnic undertones: B. The trap: \"religious and sectional appeals\" (D) is close, since the parties were sectional, but they were organised mainly on regional and ethnic lines, not religious ones. Memory tip: First Republic parties = one region, one tribe each.",
 "|Development of the Civil Service relies on all of the following except|impartiality|anonymity|promotability|neutrality": "The civil service works by well-known principles: impartiality (serving all citizens fairly), anonymity (civil servants stay out of the limelight and ministers take public responsibility), political neutrality (serving any government in power without taking sides), permanence and hierarchy. \"Promotability\" is not one of these principles; promotion is a career matter, not a principle on which the service is built: C. Memory tip: civil service principles = \"PAIN\": Permanence, Anonymity, Impartiality, Neutrality.",
 "|The chief executive system is associated with|federalism|presidentialism|parliamentarism|unitarianism": "The presidential system is built on a single chief executive: one elected president who is both head of state and head of government, holds all executive power, and appoints ministers who answer to him. That is why it is called a chief (single) executive system, as in the USA and Nigeria since 1979: B. The trap: parliamentarism (C) has a plural executive instead, with a ceremonial head of state and a prime minister who governs together with a collectively responsible cabinet. Federalism and unitarianism describe how power is shared between levels of government, not the type of executive. Memory tip: presidential = one person at the top.",
 "|Public corporations are mainly funded through|foreign aid|shareholders' funds|internally generated funds|government subventions": "Public corporations are created by law and owned by government. Government provides their initial capital and keeps funding them through grants, subsidies and loans, which are called subventions: D. The trap: internally generated funds (C) are real, since public corporations charge for their services (fares, tariffs), but these rarely cover their costs, so government subvention remains their main source. Shareholders' funds belong to private companies, and foreign aid is not their usual source. Memory tip: public corporation = public money.",
 "|Which of the following is the oldest public corporation in Nigeria?|Power Distribution Company of Nigeria|Nigeria Mining Corporation|Nigerian Railway Corporation|Nigerian Postal Services": "The Nigerian Railway Corporation was established in 1955 to take over the government railway, which had been built from 1898 (Lagos to Ibadan, 1901). It is the oldest of the four corporations listed: C. The trap: Nigerian Postal Services (D) is tempting because postal services in Nigeria go back to the 19th century, but NIPOST became a separate corporation only in 1985, after being split from the Posts and Telecommunications department. The Nigerian Mining Corporation dates from 1972, and the power distribution companies were formed in 2005 and privatised in 2013. Memory tip: railway first, 1955.",
 "|In 1987, Nigeria attained a federation of|19 states|12 states|21 states|30 states": "On 23 September 1987 General Babangida created two new states, Akwa Ibom (out of Cross River) and Katsina (out of Kaduna), raising the number of states from 19 to 21: C. The trap: 19 states (A) is the figure Nigeria had before 1987 (since Murtala's 1976 exercise), and 30 states (D) came in 1991. Today Nigeria has 36 states, created by Abacha in 1996, plus the FCT. Memory tip: 1967: 12, 1976: 19, 1987: 21, 1991: 30, 1996: 36.",
 "|The ombudsman aims at|offering qualitative educational services|rendering alternative dispute resolution services|providing qualitative job opportunities|entertaining complaints on abuse of public office": "The ombudsman is an official who receives and investigates complaints by citizens against injustice, maladministration and abuse of office by public officials, and seeks redress for them. The office began in Sweden in 1809; Nigeria's version is the Public Complaints Commission, set up in 1975: D. The trap: alternative dispute resolution (B) sounds similar, but ADR (mediation and arbitration) settles disputes between private parties, while the ombudsman deals with complaints against government. Memory tip: ombudsman = the people's complaint office against officials.",
 "|The apex body under the military regime of Yakubu Gowon was the|Supreme Military Council|Federal Executive Council|Armed Forces Ruling Council|National Council of State": "Under General Yakubu Gowon (1966–75), the highest decision-making body was the Supreme Military Council (SMC), made up of the head of state and senior officers; below it were the Federal Executive Council and the military governors. Murtala and Obasanjo kept the SMC: A. The trap: the Armed Forces Ruling Council (C) did the same job, but it was Babangida's name for it (1985–93); Abacha's equivalent was the Provisional Ruling Council. The National Council of State is an advisory body. Memory tip: Gowon to Obasanjo = SMC; Babangida = AFRC; Abacha and Abubakar = PRC.",
 "|One of the problems of the Commonwealth of Nations is lack of|finance|administrative structures|cultural heterogeneity|capacity to enforce decisions": "The Commonwealth is a voluntary association of independent countries, most of them former British colonies. Its decisions are not legally binding and it has no army or sanctions machinery, so it lacks the capacity to enforce its decisions; its strongest step is to suspend a member, as it did Nigeria in 1995 after the execution of Ken Saro-Wiwa: D. It has a Secretariat in London (administrative structure) and members pay contributions; cultural heterogeneity, as the diversity of its members, is not something it lacks. Memory tip: the Commonwealth can advise and suspend, not enforce.",
 "|A major objective of the Public Complaints Commission is|creating fair opportunities for all government employees|training and promotion of public servants|addressing grievances of individuals and groups|creating an efficient work environment": "The Public Complaints Commission, established by Decree No. 31 of 1975, is Nigeria's ombudsman. Its main objective is to receive, investigate and address grievances of individuals and groups against government ministries, agencies and officials, so that ordinary people get justice without costly court cases: C. Training, promotion and working conditions of public servants are matters for the Civil Service Commission and the ministries, not for the PCC. Memory tip: PCC = Public Complaints Corrected.",
 "|Which of the following is a major feature of democracy?|Capacity to influence people|Existence of political office holders|Decision making|Consent of the people": "Democracy is \"government of the people, by the people and for the people\" (Abraham Lincoln). Its central feature is that government rests on the consent of the people, expressed through free and periodic elections, majority rule with respect for minority rights, and the people's power to change their rulers: D. Every form of government, democratic or not, has office holders, makes decisions and influences people, so options A to C do not distinguish democracy. Memory tip: democracy = consent; no consent, no democracy.",
 "|The struggle for self-government from foreign rule is known as|imperialism|nationalism|patriotism|neo-colonialism": "Nationalism is the feeling of oneness among a people and the organised struggle to end foreign rule and govern themselves. In Nigeria it ran from early resistance through the NNDP (1923), the Nigerian Youth Movement (1936) and the NCNC (1944) to independence on 1 October 1960: B. The trap: patriotism (C) is close, but it means love of and loyalty to one's country, not specifically the struggle against colonial rule. Imperialism and neo-colonialism are what nationalists fought against. Memory tip: Nationalists wanted the Nation for themselves.",
 "|Territorial defence in the Yoruba pre-colonial system was the responsibility of the|Bashorun|Oyomesi|Aremo|Are-Ona-Kakanfo": "In the Old Oyo Empire the Alaafin ruled with the Oyomesi, the council of seven kingmakers led by the Bashorun. Military affairs and defence of the empire's territory were the duty of the Are-Ona-Kakanfo, the generalissimo of the army, who by custom lived outside the capital: D. The trap: the Bashorun (A) was very powerful and was once also a war leader, but he was the prime minister and head of the Oyomesi, not the army commander. The Aremo was the Alaafin's eldest son and crown prince. Memory tip: Kakanfo = Commander.",
 "|An effective means of measuring public opinion is|referendum|radio interview|letters to government|telephone calls": "Public opinion is the views held by the people on public issues. A referendum puts a question directly to the whole electorate, so its result shows what the majority actually thinks; that makes it a more effective measure than radio interviews, letters or phone calls, which only reach a few self-selected people: A. Opinion polls and elections are other ways of measuring it. Memory tip: to know what everyone thinks, ask everyone: referendum.",
 "|Universal Adult Suffrage permits all|citizens to vote|qualified males to vote|qualified citizens to vote|females to vote": "Universal adult suffrage means the right to vote is given to every adult citizen who meets basic legal qualifications, whatever their sex, wealth, education or religion. In Nigeria a voter must be a citizen aged 18 or above, registered and of sound mind: C. The trap: \"all citizens\" (A) leaves out the word that matters, because children, people of unsound mind and some others are not qualified to vote. Votes for males only or females only would not be universal. Memory tip: universal adult suffrage = every QUALIFIED adult citizen.",
 "|In the pre-colonial emirate system, the Emir of Gwandu controlled the|Central section|Southern section|Eastern section|Western section": "After Usman dan Fodio's jihad (from 1804), the Sokoto Caliphate was divided for administration in about 1812: his son Muhammad Bello at Sokoto oversaw the eastern emirates (such as Kano, Katsina, Zaria and Adamawa), while his brother Abdullahi at Gwandu oversaw the western emirates (such as Ilorin and Nupe). The Sultan of Sokoto remained the overall leader: D. The trap: Eastern section (C) is what Sokoto controlled, so students who mix up the two capitals fall for it. Memory tip: GWandu = West.",
 "|Both federal and state governments derive power from the|residual list|concurrent list|Exclusive list|regional list": "Nigeria's constitution shares legislative powers in lists. The Exclusive List (defence, foreign affairs, currency) is for the federal government alone; the Concurrent List (education, health, agriculture, electricity) is for both the federal and state governments; residual matters (such as chieftaincy) are left to the states. Both levels therefore derive power from the concurrent list: B. The trap: the residual list (A) belongs to the states (and local governments) only, not the federal government. Federal law prevails where federal and state laws on a concurrent matter conflict. Memory tip: Concurrent = shared by both levels.",
 "|A social group consisting of two or more people who interact and identify with one another is a|nation|society|government|state": "A society is a group of people, two or more, who live together, interact regularly and share a sense of belonging to one another, with common rules and culture. It is the widest and most basic social grouping: B. The trap: nation (A) also involves identification, but a nation is a large group bound by common descent, language, history and culture, not any group of two or more people. A state needs territory, population, government and sovereignty, and government is the machinery that runs the state. Memory tip: society = people interacting; state = society with sovereignty.",
 "|Which of the following Nigerian presidents initiated and facilitated the creation of NEPAD?|Goodluck Jonathan|Olusegun Obasanjo|Umaru Musa Yar'Adua|Muhammadu Buhari": "NEPAD, the New Partnership for Africa's Development, was launched in 2001 and adopted by the African Union. President Olusegun Obasanjo of Nigeria initiated and promoted it together with Thabo Mbeki (South Africa), Abdelaziz Bouteflika (Algeria) and Abdoulaye Wade (Senegal): B. Yar'Adua (2007–10), Jonathan (2010–15) and Buhari (2015–23) came to power after it was set up. Since 2018 NEPAD has been transformed into the African Union Development Agency (AUDA-NEPAD). Memory tip: Obasanjo = NEPAD 2001 and the OAU-to-AU change.",
 "|Nigeria became a Republic with the|1979 Constitution|1989 Constitution|1960 Constitution|1963 Constitution": "At independence on 1 October 1960, Nigeria was a dominion with the British Queen as head of state, represented by a Governor-General. The 1963 Republican Constitution, in force from 1 October 1963, removed the Queen: Dr Nnamdi Azikiwe became the first President (ceremonial) and the Supreme Court replaced the Privy Council as the final court of appeal: D. The trap: the 1960 Constitution (C) gave independence, but not republican status. The 1979 Constitution introduced the presidential system, not the republic. Memory tip: 1960 independence, 1963 republic.",
 "|Which of the following is a function of the Civil Service Commission?|Enforcement of law and order|Payment of civil servants' salaries|Discipline of erring civil servants|Protection of lives and properties": "The Civil Service Commission is the body that manages the civil service on behalf of government: it appoints, promotes, transfers and disciplines civil servants, so that these decisions are made fairly and kept away from politicians. Disciplining erring civil servants is therefore one of its functions: C. Enforcing law and order and protecting lives and property are police duties, and salaries are paid through the ministries and the Office of the Accountant-General. Memory tip: CSC = hire, promote, discipline.",
 "|The concentration of power in the units is a merit of the|quasi-federal system|confederal system|federal system|unitary system": "In a confederation, sovereign states come together for limited purposes but keep most of their power; the centre is weak and depends on the members. Because power is concentrated in the units, each member keeps its identity, independence and freedom to leave, which is counted as a merit of the confederal system: B. The trap: the federal system (C) also gives the units power, but it divides power between the centre and the units, and the centre is usually stronger. A unitary system concentrates power at the centre. Memory tip: confederal = strong units, weak centre.",
 "|In fascism, the leader is|supreme|democratic|rich|religious": "Fascism, practised by Benito Mussolini in Italy (from 1922) and in a similar form by Hitler's Nazi Germany, glorifies the nation and the state above the individual. Its leader (Il Duce, Der Führer) is supreme: his word is law, opposition is crushed, and there is only one party: A. A fascist leader is the very opposite of democratic, and fascism is defined neither by wealth nor by religion. Memory tip: fascism = one leader Supreme, one party, no opposition.",
 "|Delegated legislation is the|limitation of responsibilities to agencies|transfer of responsibilities to agencies|deterring of responsibilities to agencies|facilitation of responsibilities of agencies": "The legislature cannot make every detailed rule itself, so it passes an enabling Act that transfers law-making responsibility to other bodies: ministers (regulations, orders), local councils (bye-laws) and public corporations. Laws made in this way are called delegated legislation: B. The trap: limitation of responsibilities (A) points to how delegated legislation is controlled (the parent Act limits it, and courts can strike down rules that go beyond it), but the thing itself is a transfer of power. Memory tip: delegate = hand over; the parent Act hands law-making to agencies.",
 "|Which of the following countries significantly contributed to the formation of ECOMOG?|Ghana|Gambia|Liberia|Nigeria": "ECOMOG, the ECOWAS Monitoring Group, was formed in August 1990 to intervene in the Liberian civil war, and later served in Sierra Leone and Guinea-Bissau. Nigeria was the driving force: General Babangida pushed for it, and Nigeria supplied most of its troops, money, equipment and field commanders: D. The trap: Ghana (A) did contribute troops and its General Arnold Quainoo was the first field commander, but Nigeria's contribution was by far the largest. Liberia was where ECOMOG was sent, not a contributor. Memory tip: ECOMOG = Nigeria's men and money.",
 "|An important element of the doctrine of separation of powers is|delegation of power|checks and balances|rule of law|concentration of powers": "The doctrine of separation of powers, developed by Montesquieu (1748), divides government into three organs, the legislature, the executive and the judiciary, each with its own personnel and functions. Its essential companion is checks and balances, by which each organ can limit the others (veto, impeachment, judicial review) so that none abuses power: B. The trap: rule of law (C) is a related principle of constitutional government, but it means the supremacy of law and equality before it, not the division of powers. Concentration of powers is the opposite of separation. Memory tip: separate the powers, then let them check each other.",
 "|Nigeria's actions towards the external environment are embedded in her|state policy|party policy|government policy|foreign policy": "Foreign policy is the set of principles, goals and actions that guide a country in its dealings with other states and international organisations, its external environment. Nigeria's Afrocentric policy, its membership of the UN, AU and ECOWAS, and its peacekeeping are all part of its foreign policy: D. The trap: government policy (C) is very broad and usually means domestic policies on matters such as education or the economy; the specific term for policy towards the outside world is foreign policy. Party policy is only a party's programme. Memory tip: foreign policy = home country, foreign relations.",
 "|Fusion of power is an attribute of a|democratic society|presidential government|monarchical state|parliamentary state": "In a parliamentary system, as in Britain or Nigeria's First Republic (1960–66), the Prime Minister and ministers are members of the legislature and stay in office only while they keep its confidence. The same people therefore make laws and carry them out, so legislative and executive powers are fused: D. A presidential government is the opposite: it is built on separation of powers, with an executive President who is not a member of the legislature. Democracy and monarchy describe who rules, not how the organs are joined. Memory tip: Parliamentary = fusion (the same people in both); Presidential = separation.",
 "|In the pre-colonial system, checks and balances were prominent in the|Igbo system|Tiv system|Yoruba system|Hausa-Fulani system": "The Old Oyo (Yoruba) empire was a constitutional monarchy. The Alaafin was powerful, but the Oyo Mesi (seven kingmakers led by the Bashorun) could demand his removal by presenting an empty calabash or parrot's eggs; the Ogboni society checked the Oyo Mesi; and the Are-Ona-Kakanfo commanded the army outside the capital. Power was spread and each body limited the others: C. The Hausa-Fulani emirate was highly centralised under the Emir, while the Igbo and Tiv were acephalous (no central ruler), so there was no strong central power to check. Memory tip: Oyo = Alaafin checked by Oyo Mesi, Oyo Mesi checked by Ogboni.",
 "|Foreign policy is relevant because it|encourages rivalry among nations|encourages interference in other countries' domestic policy|makes foreign countries aggressive|helps to foster good relationship between nations": "Foreign policy is the set of principles and goals a state follows in its dealings with other states and international bodies. A good foreign policy protects the national interest and promotes peace, cooperation, trade and friendly relations among nations; Nigeria's own principles include good-neighbourliness, peaceful settlement of disputes and non-interference in other states' affairs: D. Rivalry, interference and aggression are what a sound foreign policy tries to avoid, not reasons for having one. Memory tip: foreign policy builds bridges, not walls.",
 "|Emirate system is associated with|Yoruba pre-colonial system|Hausa-Fulani pre-colonial system|Igbo pre-colonial system|Tiv pre-colonial system": "The emirate system came from Usman dan Fodio's jihad, begun in 1804, which overthrew the Hausa kings and set up emirates under the Sokoto Caliphate (with Gwandu overseeing the western emirates). Each emirate was ruled by an Emir, the political and religious head, helped by officials such as the Waziri, Madawaki, Galadima and Alkali: B. The Yoruba had kingdoms under Obas (such as the Alaafin of Oyo), while the Igbo and Tiv were acephalous, run by councils of elders and age grades. Memory tip: Emir, Emirate, Islam = Hausa-Fulani.",
 "|The major reason against open balloting is that it|is costly|is crude|can lead to intimidation|is simple to operate": "In open balloting voters show their choice in public, by queuing behind a candidate's poster or raising hands, as in Nigeria's 1993 \"Option A4\" elections. Because everyone can see how each person votes, the powerful can threaten, punish or bribe voters, so people may not vote freely: C. That is why the secret ballot is preferred in democracies. Open balloting is in fact cheap and simple, so \"costly\" is wrong and \"simple to operate\" is an advantage, not a reason against it. Memory tip: open ballot = open to intimidation; secret ballot = free choice.",
 "|The first aborted coup in Nigeria was staged by|Gen Olusegun Obasanjo|Gen Murtala Mohammed|Lt. Col Buka Suka Dimka|Gen Tunde Idiagbon": "On 13 February 1976 Lt. Col. Buka Suka Dimka led a coup attempt in Lagos. General Murtala Mohammed, the Head of State, was ambushed and killed in his car, but loyal troops crushed the coup within hours, Lt. Gen. Olusegun Obasanjo took over, and Dimka was caught and executed in May 1976: C. The trap: Murtala Mohammed (B) is linked with this coup, but he was its victim, not its leader. Idiagbon was Buhari's deputy (1984–85), not a coup plotter in a failed coup. Memory tip: 13 Feb 1976: Dimka struck, Murtala died, the coup failed.",
 "|Political parties are expected to|control the government|dictate to government|manipulate public officials|control public officials": "A political party is an organised group of people who share ideas and seek to win elections so as to control and run the government and carry out their programme. Taking control of government is the very reason parties exist; that is what separates them from pressure groups, which only try to influence government: A. The trap: \"control public officials\" (D) sounds similar, but parties aim at the whole government through elections, not at controlling individual officials, and dictating or manipulating are not legitimate aims. Memory tip: Parties want Power (to control government); pressure groups want Policies.",
 "|The lapses of the Clifford Constitution necessitated its replacement with the|Lyttelton Constitution|Richards Constitution|Macpherson Constitution|Lugard Constitution": "The Clifford Constitution (1922) left the North out of the Legislative Council, gave Africans few seats and only four elected members (three for Lagos, one for Calabar). These lapses led to the Richards Constitution of 1946 (in force 1947), which brought the North into a central Legislative Council for the whole country and created three regional Houses: B. The trap: the Macpherson Constitution (C) is also colonial, but it came in 1951 to replace Richards, not Clifford; Lyttelton came in 1954. There was no \"Lugard Constitution\". Memory tip: Clifford 1922 → Richards 1946 → Macpherson 1951 → Lyttelton 1954.",
 "|In the Hausa/Fulani political systems, the administrative functions were carried out by the|Madawaki|Hakimi|Dogari|Waziri": "In the emirate system the Emir was helped by titled officials. The Waziri was the chief adviser and head of the administration, rather like a prime minister, coordinating the government of the emirate: D. The trap: the Hakimi (B) also did administrative work, but only as district heads collecting taxes and keeping order in their districts under the central officials. The Madawaki commanded the army and the Dogari were the Emir's guards or police. Memory tip: Waziri = \"Prime Minister\"; Madawaki = army; Alkali = judge; Ma'aji = treasurer.",
 "|What differentiates a public corporation from a private enterprise?|Financing|Proclamation|Ownership|Membership": "A public corporation (such as the Nigerian Ports Authority or NNPC in its early form) is set up by law and owned by the government on behalf of the people, while a private enterprise is owned by private individuals or shareholders who invest their own money: C. Both may raise funds in different ways and both may be set up by legal documents, so financing and proclamation do not separate them, and \"membership\" does not apply. Memory tip: public corporation = public ownership; private enterprise = private ownership.",
 "|One of the major problems of ECOWAS is|lack of military force|the presence of CEAO as a competitor|overlapping functions of her agencies|political instability in the region": "ECOWAS, set up by the Treaty of Lagos in May 1975, aims at economic integration of West Africa. Its work has been badly disrupted by political instability: coups and civil wars in Liberia, Sierra Leone, Guinea-Bissau, Guinea, Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger, the last three of which announced their withdrawal in 2024 (effective 2025): D. Rivalry from the French-speaking CEAO (B) was also a problem in the early years, so this is arguable, but the CEAO was dissolved in 1994 (replaced by UEMOA). ECOWAS does have a military force, ECOMOG. Memory tip: no peace, no progress: instability is ECOWAS's biggest headache.",
 "|A public corporation becomes commercialised when|it is expected to make profit|the edict for its establishment is repealed|its board of directors is statutorily constituted|it no longer provides essential services": "Commercialisation means the government keeps owning the corporation but tells it to operate like a business: charge economic prices, cover its costs and make a profit, without depending on government subsidies. Nigeria's 1988 Technical Committee on Privatisation and Commercialisation classified corporations into those to be fully or partly commercialised: A. Repealing the law that set it up would end the corporation, and every public corporation has a board set up by statute. Commercialised firms still provide essential services such as power or water. Memory tip: commercialise = government owns, but profit rules.",
 "|In the presidential system, the executive council comprises the president and the|ministers|head of service|ambassadors|special advisers": "In the presidential system the executive council (in Nigeria the Federal Executive Council) consists of the President, the Vice-President and the ministers the President appoints with the Senate's confirmation; it decides government policy: A. The trap: special advisers (D) work closely with the President and may attend meetings, but they are aides, not members of the council. The Head of Service heads the civil service and ambassadors represent the country abroad. Memory tip: FEC = President + Vice-President + Ministers.",
 "|Delegated legislation becomes expedient because of the need|to control appointees|for parliamentary control|for judicial control|for convenience": "Delegated legislation is law made by a body other than the legislature (ministers, agencies, local councils) under powers given by an Act. It became necessary because parliament lacks the time and technical knowledge to make every detailed rule, and because some rules must be made quickly in emergencies. Letting experts fill in the details is a matter of convenience: D. Parliamentary and judicial control are ways of checking delegated legislation, not reasons for having it. Memory tip: parliament makes the frame; delegated bodies fill in the details for convenience.",
 "|The reason for the first military intervention in Nigeria includes which of the following?|Kano riot|Western Region election crisis|Hatred for the politicians|Dispute over census result": "Nigeria's first coup, on 15 January 1966, came after a series of crises. The most serious was the Western Region crisis: the AG split of 1962 and then the rigged regional election of October 1965, which led to riots, arson and killings (\"Operation Wetie\") and a breakdown of law and order that the soldiers cited: B. The disputed census of 1962–63 (D) is also one of the standard causes listed, and the question asks for reasons in general, so D is also accepted. The trap: the Kano riot (A) was a real crisis, but it happened in 1953, long before the coup. Memory tip: \"Wetie\" in the West, then the soldiers struck in 1966.",
 "|The capacity to affect another person's behaviour by the threat of sanction is|authority|power|sovereignty|legitimacy": "Power is the ability or capacity to make others behave as you wish, even against their will, backed by the threat of sanctions such as punishment, loss or force. Max Weber and Robert Dahl both define it this way: B. The trap: authority (A) is close, but authority is power that is legal and accepted as rightful; the question stresses threat of sanction, which is the mark of power itself. Sovereignty is the supreme power of the state, and legitimacy is acceptance by the people. Memory tip: power = can make you; authority = has the right to make you.",
 "|The judicial administration of issues bothering on the code of conduct is the responsibility of the|Code of Conduct Tribunal|Code of Conduct Service|Code of Conduct Commission|Code of Conduct Bureau": "The Code of Conduct for public officers (Fifth Schedule of the 1999 Constitution) requires them to declare their assets and forbids foreign accounts and conflicts of interest. The Code of Conduct Tribunal is the court that tries breaches of the code and can punish offenders, for example by removal from office or disqualification: A. The trap: the Code of Conduct Bureau (D) is the real body that receives and checks asset declarations, but it only investigates and refers cases; judging them is the Tribunal's job. There is no Code of Conduct \"Service\" or \"Commission\". Memory tip: the Bureau collects; the Tribunal judges.",
 "|Nigeria's membership of ECOWAS has been beneficial to her because it|ensured Nigeria's return to democratic rule in 1999|has assisted in the fight against Boko Haram insurgency|has helped to eliminate obstacles to free movement of persons|has tied Nigeria's economic fortunes to West Africa": "The ECOWAS Protocol on Free Movement of Persons, Residence and Establishment (1979) lets citizens of member states enter other member states without a visa for up to 90 days, and later phases cover residence and business. Nigerians trade, study and work across West Africa because of it, and the ECOWAS passport and removal of many border barriers followed: C. Nigeria's return to democracy in 1999 came from internal events, the fight against Boko Haram is led by the Multinational Joint Task Force of the Lake Chad states, and being tied to the region is not a benefit. Memory tip: ECOWAS 1979 = visa-free movement.",
 "|Nigeria's foreign policy implementation is the exclusive responsibility of the|Ministry of Budget and Planning|Department of Immigration|Ministry of External Affairs|Nigerian Institute of International Affairs": "Nigeria's foreign policy is made at the top (the President and the Federal Executive Council) and carried out by the ministry in charge of external relations, through its embassies, high commissions and consulates: C. Today that ministry is called the Ministry of Foreign Affairs. The trap: the Nigerian Institute of International Affairs (D), founded in 1961 in Lagos, studies foreign affairs and advises government, but it is a research body and does not carry out policy. Immigration controls entry into Nigeria, and the budget ministry plans the economy. Memory tip: NIIA thinks; the Foreign Ministry acts.",
 "|Bourgeoisie is a class in|socialism|communism|capitalism|feudalism": "In Karl Marx's analysis, every society is divided into classes according to their relation to the means of production. Under capitalism the bourgeoisie is the owning class (factories, land, capital), while the proletariat are workers who sell their labour for wages: C. Marx said socialism would remove the bourgeoisie as a ruling class and communism would be classless. Under feudalism the classes were lords and serfs. Memory tip: Bourgeoisie = Big business owners = capitalism.",
 "|In appointing the chairman of the Independent National Electoral Commission, the president must consult with the|National Council of State|state governors|National Assembly|National Judicial Council": "Section 154 of the 1999 Constitution says the chairman and members of INEC are appointed by the President, subject to confirmation by the Senate, and section 154(3) adds that before appointing them the President must consult the Council of State, which includes former Heads of State, former Chief Justices, the Senate President, the Speaker and all state governors: A. The trap: the National Assembly (C) is close, because its Senate confirms the appointment, but that comes after; consultation is with the Council of State. Governors sit on the Council but are not consulted on their own. Memory tip: INEC chief: consult Council of State, then Senate confirms.",
 "|Checks and balances guarantee|superiority of the legislature|balance of the three arms|superiority of the executive|subordination of the judiciary": "Checks and balances means each arm of government can limit the others: the legislature can impeach and control money, the executive can veto bills and appoints judges, and the judiciary can declare laws and actions unconstitutional. The result is that no arm dominates and the three stay in balance: B. Superiority of the legislature or of the executive, or subordination of the judiciary, are exactly what checks and balances are meant to prevent. Memory tip: checks and balances = a three-legged stool; each leg holds the others up.",
 "|A popular technique employed by pressure groups to achieve their objectives is|gerrymandering|campaign|coercion|protests": "Pressure groups try to influence government policy without seeking to form the government. Their methods include lobbying, petitions, press campaigns, strikes, and protests or demonstrations, such as the NLC's protests against fuel price increases and the #EndSARS protests of 2020: D. \"Campaign\" (B) is a broad word also used for party electioneering, and the key accepts protests as the specific technique. Gerrymandering is redrawing constituency boundaries to favour a party, and coercion (force) is not a lawful method. Memory tip: pressure groups LPS: Lobby, Petition, Strike or protest.",
 "|The Unity Party of Nigeria in its manifesto emphasised|class struggle|mixed economy|dictatorship of the proletariat|socialist principles": "The Unity Party of Nigeria (UPN) was formed in 1978 by Chief Obafemi Awolowo and came second in the 1979 presidential election. Its manifesto was built on democratic socialism, with four cardinal programmes: free education at all levels, free health care, integrated rural development and full employment: D. Class struggle and the dictatorship of the proletariat are Marxist-communist ideas the UPN did not preach, and a mixed economy was not its emphasis. Memory tip: UPN = Awo's \"four cardinal programmes\" of welfare socialism.",
 "|Monarchy remains a natural institution that believes in|rulership by the people|rulership of a kingdom|competitive selection of leaders|equitable power distribution": "A monarchy is a system in which a king or queen (or emperor) is head of state, usually inheriting the throne and ruling for life over a kingdom. It is called a natural institution because it grew out of the traditional family and clan headship. So monarchy believes in the rulership of a kingdom by a monarch: B. Rule by the people and competitive selection of leaders describe democracy, and equitable distribution of power describes federalism or constitutional government, not monarchy. Memory tip: mono = one: one hereditary ruler over a kingdom.",
 "|The 1999 Constitution of Nigeria is said to be supreme because|its provisions must be respected by all and sundry|the president has sovereign immunity|the Supreme Court retained the power of judicial review|it enhances fundamental human rights": "Section 1(1) of the 1999 Constitution says: \"This Constitution is supreme and its provisions shall have binding force on all authorities and persons throughout the Federal Republic of Nigeria.\" Section 1(3) adds that any law inconsistent with it is void. So it is supreme because everyone, from the President to the ordinary citizen, must obey it: A. The trap: judicial review by the Supreme Court (C) is how supremacy is enforced, but it is not why the constitution is supreme. Immunity for the President (s.308) is a limit on the rule of law. Memory tip: supreme = binding on all and sundry.",
 "|The armed forces are classified as|public servants|federal civil servants|civil servants|political appointees": "Public servants are all who work for government and are paid from public funds: civil servants, staff of public corporations, the police, the armed forces, teachers in public schools and so on. Soldiers therefore belong to the public service: A. The trap: civil servants (C) and federal civil servants (B) are close, but the civil service is only the staff of government ministries and departments; soldiers are uniformed military personnel, not civil servants. Political appointees are chosen by politicians, while soldiers are career staff. Memory tip: every civil servant is a public servant, but not every public servant is a civil servant.",
 "|Statutory age is a requirement for obtaining citizenship through|naturalisation|duality|marriage|birth": "Under the 1999 Constitution citizenship is by birth (s.25), registration (s.26) or naturalisation (s.27). For naturalisation a person must be of full age, which section 29(4) sets at 18 years and above, as well as of good character, with 15 years' residence and an intention to live in Nigeria: A. Citizenship by birth has no age requirement, and a foreign woman married to a Nigerian man gets citizenship by registration without any age limit. \"Duality\" means holding two citizenships and is not a way of obtaining one. Memory tip: naturalisation = for adults only (18+).",
 "|\"Africa as the centrepiece of Nigeria's foreign policy\" could be traced back to the administration of|Sir Ahmadu Bello|Alhaji Shehu Shagari|Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa|Gen Yakubu Gowon": "In his first speeches after independence in 1960, Prime Minister Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa declared that Africa would be the cornerstone of Nigeria's foreign policy, supporting decolonisation and African unity: C. The Adedeji review panel set up under Murtala Mohammed formally restated it in 1976 as \"Africa as the centrepiece\", and later leaders such as Gowon and Shagari kept it. Sir Ahmadu Bello was Premier of the Northern Region and never ran the federal government. Memory tip: Balewa 1960 laid the cornerstone; Adedeji 1976 named it the centrepiece.",
 "|A president of a state performs a judicial function through|dismissal of judges|assent to bills|prerogative of mercy|appointment of judges": "Judicial functions are about deciding cases and punishing offenders. Through the prerogative of mercy (s.175 of the 1999 Constitution) the President, advised by the Council of State, can pardon convicted persons, grant reprieves, and reduce or replace sentences, which directly changes a court's judgment: C. The trap: appointment of judges (D) touches the judiciary, but it is an executive act of choosing officials, not a judicial function. Assent to bills is a legislative function, and the President cannot simply dismiss judges. Memory tip: mercy = the President's \"judge's hat\".",
 "|The highest and most powerful organ of OPEC is the|Conference|Secretariat|Council of Ministers|Board of Governors": "OPEC, founded in Baghdad in September 1960, has three main organs. The Conference, made up of the oil ministers of member countries, is the supreme authority: it decides policy and production levels and meets at least twice a year: A. The trap: the Board of Governors (D) is a real OPEC organ, but it only manages the organisation and carries out the Conference's decisions; the Secretariat in Vienna handles day-to-day work. OPEC has no Council of Ministers. Memory tip: OPEC: Conference decides, Board directs, Secretariat does.",
 "|Which of these is the first political party in Nigeria?|NYM|NNDP|NCNC|NPC": "The Nigerian National Democratic Party (NNDP) was founded by Herbert Macaulay on 24 June 1923 to contest the elective seats in Lagos created by the Clifford Constitution of 1922. It is regarded as Nigeria's first political party: B. The trap: the Nigerian Youth Movement (A) is older than the NCNC and NPC, and is often called the first party with a national outlook, but it began only in 1934 (as the Lagos Youth Movement, renamed in 1936). The NCNC was formed in 1944 and the NPC in 1949 (as a party from 1951). Memory tip: NNDP 1923 → NYM 1936 → NCNC 1944 → NPC 1949/51.",
 "|The military regime abolished the state ministries of local government in Nigeria in|1985|1957|1990|1989": "Under General Babangida's local government reforms, the state ministries of local government, through which state governments had controlled councils, were abolished in 1989. Councils then received their allocations directly from the Federation Account, and the reforms went on to the 1991 executive-chairman system: D. The trap: 1985 (A) is the year Babangida came to power, not the year of this reform; 1990 is a year too late. There was no military rule in 1957. Memory tip: IBB's local government ladder: 1989 ministries go, 1991 executive chairmen and 589 councils.",
 "|Government by the best men is referred to as|democratic|autocratic|aristocratic|totalitarianism": "Aristocracy comes from the Greek \"aristos\" (best) and \"kratos\" (rule), so it means government by a few of the best, wisest or noblest people, often a hereditary upper class. Aristotle saw it as the good form of rule by the few, which turns into oligarchy when the few rule for themselves: C. Democracy is rule by the people, autocracy is rule by one person with absolute power, and totalitarianism is a state controlling every part of life. Memory tip: ARISTocracy = the ARISTs (the best) rule.",
 "|Human rights are usually enshrined in a constitution with a view to|avoid tenure elongation|promote peaceful co-existence|protect political office holders|protect the rights of individuals in a state": "Fundamental human rights, such as the rights to life, dignity, personal liberty, fair hearing, and freedom of expression and movement, are written into constitutions (Chapter IV of Nigeria's 1999 Constitution) so that they cannot be taken away easily and can be enforced in court, even against the government. Their purpose is to protect the rights of individuals in the state: D. They do not exist to protect office holders or to stop tenure elongation, and peaceful co-existence is at most a side effect. Memory tip: entrenched rights = shield for the individual.",
 "|The political culture that has to do with the loyalty of the people towards the government is|evaluative orientation|cognitive orientation|societal orientation|affective orientation": "Almond and Verba described three orientations in political culture. Cognitive orientation is knowledge and beliefs about the political system; affective orientation is feelings towards it, such as attachment, loyalty, pride or rejection; evaluative orientation is judgements and opinions about how it performs: D. The trap: evaluative orientation (A) sounds close, but it is about judging the system's performance, not feeling loyal to it. \"Societal orientation\" is not one of the three. Memory tip: Cognitive = Know, Affective = Feel (love, loyalty), Evaluative = Judge.",
 "|The existence of the OAU was seriously threatened in 1982–1983 because of a conflict which led to the departure of|Rwanda and Angola|Namibia and South Africa|Morocco|Somalia and Ethiopia": "In 1982 the OAU admitted the Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic (Western Sahara), which Morocco claims as its own territory. The quarrel split the organisation: the 1982 Tripoli summit failed to reach a quorum twice, and the OAU seemed close to collapse. When the SADR delegation was seated in 1984, Morocco withdrew from the OAU: C. Morocco stayed out for over 30 years and rejoined the African Union, which replaced the OAU in 2002, in January 2017. South Africa and Namibia were not yet free; Somalia and Ethiopia fought the Ogaden war but did not leave. Memory tip: Western Sahara drove Morocco out (1984); back in 2017.",
 "|A factor that can cause hindrance to the effectiveness of the civil service in Nigeria is|executive control|low incentive|legal control|registration of civil servants": "A civil service works well only when its staff are motivated. In Nigeria low incentives (poor salaries, delayed promotions, poor working conditions and few rewards for good work) lead to low morale, absenteeism, moonlighting and corruption, all of which reduce efficiency: B. Executive control and legal control are normal ways of keeping the civil service accountable, not hindrances, and registration of civil servants is not a problem. Memory tip: low pay, low morale, low output.",
 "|Which of the following commissions is responsible for sharing revenue based on an agreed formula in Nigeria?|Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission|National Planning Commission|National Human Rights Commission|Federal Civil Service Commission": "The Revenue Mobilisation Allocation and Fiscal Commission (RMAFC), set up in 1989 and now a federal executive body under section 153 of the 1999 Constitution, monitors the money flowing into the Federation Account and its sharing among the federal, state and local governments according to the agreed formula, and it reviews and proposes that formula: A. The National Planning Commission plans development, the National Human Rights Commission protects rights, and the Federal Civil Service Commission appoints and disciplines civil servants. Memory tip: Revenue sharing = RMAFC.",
 "|Public opinion is NOT formed through|mass media|symposia and lectures|documentary|advertisement": "Public opinion is the views held by many people on public issues. It is formed through mass media (newspapers, radio, television, social media), symposia, lectures and seminars, documentaries, political parties, pressure groups, family and religious bodies. Advertisement is mainly commercial: it persuades people to buy goods and services, so it is not a means of forming opinion on public affairs: D. Documentaries inform viewers about real events and issues, and so do shape opinion. Memory tip: advertisements sell products; media, lectures and documentaries shape opinions."
};
